Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"4faf50d9-c7db-4742-921a-c5a629ce1f22","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"759d921c-3445-4d20-bf6c-64bf23bda927","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016nEjF2F9TZ5mKmiQ2D4Kqs","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5-follow-ups.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"322cdc64-1623-4aca-9077-efd79e21078b","timestamp":"2026-10-30T08:10:49.821Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2A.5-follow-ups.md","oldString":"## Suggested next","newString":"## 2B plan (commit `2ae86fa`, after the EM's yes)\n\n`Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md` section 2B now includes:\n- The reel-to-batch map. It is validated by hiding the batch on pages that have one, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged. This is also a 2B gate item.\n- No loc.gov lookups for the 25,595 uncovered candidates until the reader makes them reports. Those go through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s, with each answer cached, since `page_service` doesn't cache today.\n\n## Suggested next (superseded by the plan above)","originalFile":"# 2A.5 follow-ups: DONE\n\nCommit `9d56340` on `phase-2a-archive`, pushed. It follows eval A's `ba247a0`. The PR is still held until the 2A gate. Decisions 28 and 29 have the detail.\n\n## 1. The last five headline mis-drops\n\n**The rule** (`paperghosts/ingest/headline.py`). `display_headline` and `masthead_lines` now take the record's edition date.\n\n- **Datelines:**\n  - Each weekday and month on the line must be the issue's.\n  - The issue's weekday then settles it, because OCR misreads digits far more often than words. The Sun's `SUNDAY. DECEMBER 9.` is its masthead of the 6th.\n  - Without a weekday, the day after a month and the year must also be the issue's, with I, l and O read as digits.\n  - A line of only numbers holding a year must hold the issue's year.\n- **`In` followed by the paper's place** is never a masthead.\n- **Number lines** go only when the leading lines hold the paper's title or a `Vol.` line. That covers a bare number, and `No.`, `Number`, `Whole No.` or `Page` with a number.\n\n**On the same 200 judged lines** (`uv run python scripts/headline_drops.py --same`):\n\n- Mis-drops still made: **0 of 5**.\n- Correct drops lost: **37 of 195**:\n  - 22 numbers with neither the title nor a volume beside them (`NO. 40`, `NUMBER 81.`, `No. 36.`, a bare `2` and `81`)\n  - 5 `WHOLE NO.` lines\n  - 3 lines of stray marks with a digit (`*%%~*+<<%+***~%~4 #`)\n  - 4 datelines with no weekday whose digits don't match the record's: `DELAWARE, OHIO, AUGUST 4, 1866.` (issue 1865-08-04), `September 6. 1905.` (09-08), `RICHMOND, SEPTEMBER 90. I8e5.` and `MONTPELIER, DECEMBER 20, 1871.` (issue 1841)\n  - 3 datelines whose weekday or month isn't the record's: `SATURDAY, March 16'` in a Thursday 14th issue, `THURSDAY MORNING, MAY ?, 1870` in a June 2 issue, and `Friday. July ~.` in a Saturday issue\n- Precision on the 158 lines still dropped: 158 of 158 (95% Wilson interval 97.6–100%).\n- **Over every candidate headline:** 7,509 drops, down from 10,287, and **none that the old rule kept** (checked by running both rules). So the 158 remain a fair sample of the new drops.\n  - Of the 2,778 lines no longer dropped, split as in the sample, about 2,450 are masthead lines left showing, or 0.2% of candidates.\n  - About 330 are headline lines no longer hidden.\n\n**Interpretation calls, all yours to overrule:**\n- **\"Matches the issue's own date, year or weekday.\"** I read it as: no weekday or month may contradict the issue, and a matching weekday settles the line. Requiring every digit to match as well keeps the same 0 mis-drops but loses 54 correct drops instead of 37. The extra 17 are datelines such as `THURSDAY MORNING. JULY 10, 1877.` in the Thursday 19th issue.\n- **`WHOLE NO.` follows your number-line rule.** Counting it with the volume would win back 5, with no mis-drop in the sample.\n- **Place lines don't count as \"the title\"** for a number line.\n\n**Tests** (`tests/test_headline.py`, 45 real cases, all now passing the issue date):\n- The 5 mis-drops are keeps.\n- New drops: a whole number beside the title (Indiana State sentinel, 1863), a page number beside the title (Blue-grass blade, 1908), a weekday settling a misread day (Detroit times `Saturday. July 80.`, 1910), and the issue's year alone (`1900.`).\n- Four old drops are now keeps, by design: two whole numbers alone, the National Era's misread dateline, and the Portland gazette's Saturday in an issue that LoC dates to a Tuesday.\n\nThe callers `crop_check.py`, `load_skeleton.py` and `headline_drops.py` pass the date. The skeleton's stored headline comes out the same under the new rule (its `New Series-VOl. 4-NO. 40.` is a volume line), so Atlas needs no reload.\n\n## 2. Missing scans\n\n- **335,907 candidates (32.0%) have no batch path.** `eval_a.json` has the totals in `totals.no_batch_path` and `share_no_batch_path`, and each year in `by_year[].no_batch_path`. `make eval` prints it too.\n  - By decade: 15% in the 1820s and 18% in the 1830s, then 29–37% from the 1840s to the 1910s, peaking at 37% in the 1850s. From the 1920s on it is 24–28%.\n  - The largest yearly counts are 1909–1914, at about 6,600–7,000 a year.\n- **Not every one of them is a lost clipping.**\n  - In the crop check, loc.gov named the IIIF service for 12 of the 13 picks with no batch path. The 13th was #50.\n  - Offline, these candidates sit on 283,546 pages and 19,115 reels. **92.4% of them are on a reel whose batch another candidate's batch path names.** The service is `batch + LCCN + reel + issue + seq`, so it can be built without any lookup.\n  - That leaves **25,595 candidates (2.4% of all) on 1,292 reels** needing loc.gov, one lookup per reel.\n  - 220 reels hold more than one batch: 57 are a batch and its reissue, and 163 are split between batches. So a reel-to-batch map needs a crop check before we trust it. It isn't built.\n  - At the ghost-report rate, that's roughly 900 reports needing a lookup, and an unknown share of those with no clipping at all.\n- **loc.gov rate limit, my mistake:**\n  - loc.gov's JSON API allows **20 requests a minute** and blocks for **an hour** past that ([Working Within Limits](https://www.loc.gov/apis/json-and-yaml/working-within-limits/)). Image services allow 150.\n  - The crop code throttled everything to one a second.\n  - I ran a 100-page lookup probe at that rate, and it got 429s at 04:03. This machine is likely blocked from loc.gov's JSON API until about 05:03. tile.loc.gov wasn't touched afterwards.\n  - Fix: `crops.get` now spaces www.loc.gov requests 3.5 s apart, with a test.\n  - The probe script is deleted, because a reel-based check is the better probe.\n  - Per-page lookups for all 283,546 pages would take 11.5 days at that rate, so the site can't resolve them on demand.\n- **Crop 21's stray box** is recorded in decision 29 as a known limitation, with no fix.\n\n## Suggested next\n\nIn 2B (storage and loading), build the reel-to-batch map from the batch paths in candidates and samples. Crop-check a seeded sample of the services it infers, through tile.loc.gov info.json (150 a minute). Then look up the remaining ~1,292 reels at 17 a minute, about 75 minutes.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":60,"oldLines":6,"newStart":60,"newLines":12,"lines":["   - Per-page lookups for all 283,546 pages would take 11.5 days at that rate, so the site can't resolve them on demand."," - **Crop 21's stray box** is recorded in decision 29 as a known limitation, with no fix."," ","-## Suggested next","+## 2B plan (commit `2ae86fa`, after the EM's yes)"," ","+`Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md` section 2B now includes:","+- The reel-to-batch map. It is validated by hiding the batch on pages that have one, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged. This is also a 2B gate item.","+- No loc.gov lookups for the 25,595 uncovered candidates until the reader makes them reports. Those go through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s, with each answer cached, since `page_service` doesn't cache today.","+","+## Suggested next (superseded by the plan above)","+"," In 2B (storage and loading), build the reel-to-batch map from the batch paths in candidates and samples. Crop-check a seeded sample of the services it infers, through tile.loc.gov info.json (150 a minute). Then look up the remaining ~1,292 reels at 17 a minute, about 75 minutes."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4faf50d9-c7db-4742-921a-c5a629ce1f22","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
