{"parentUuid":"b69f4e83-c7c9-425d-931a-7b5be0653321","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYZXiFyvuykfnaEXVK7z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011EuiaLq1EPZ81xhEC7XGqV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","offset":59,"limit":72},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":840,"cache_read_input_tokens":175050,"output_tokens":234,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":840,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":234,"cache_read_input_tokens":175050,"cache_creation_input_tokens":840,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":840},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011EuiaLq1EPZ81xhEC7XGqV":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","offset":59,"limit":72}},"serverClassifierRequest":"bdda54c6-10fc-411b-8a63-9d9e2a261446","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgYZXhNAFq9sxGDGh7ZGL","requestRef":"a5d2ff78-835f-426d-bd0d-d7324f5921d2","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"e7f58f09-29ac-4eb7-b5f6-2cd1c5eecece","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:14:55.209Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"e7f58f09-29ac-4eb7-b5f6-2cd1c5eecece","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"efbe7dae-4a3a-47ef-9f27-91c90fdaed9c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011EuiaLq1EPZ81xhEC7XGqV","type":"tool_result","content":"59\t## 4. Phase 2: database, schemas and data models\n60\t\n61\t### 2A: archive read and EDA\n62\t**Persona:** data engineer, with a systems specialist for throughput.\n63\t- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants and unit tests, plus a near-miss lexicon.\n64\t- `IngestArchiveWorkflow` over all 180 files: candidates, counts, samples and manifests, with concurrency set from Phase 1's measurements.\n65\t- Kill test 1, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after.\n66\t- The crop check on 50 random candidates spread across batches.\n67\t- Eval A, with charts:\n68\t  - per year: pages, articles and candidates, and candidates per million articles\n69\t  - by state, the term mix and the OCR-quality distribution\n70\t  - the reprint signal, and candidate lengths\n71\t- Prefilter recall: the random-sample bound and the near-miss pool. If misses are found, lexicon v2 and a re-run.\n72\t\n73\t**Gate**\n74\t- All 180 years are done, or each failure is listed with its reason.\n75\t- Disk use is reported.\n76\t- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces the 1850 counts exactly.\n77\t- Kill-test evidence exists, and every crop-check failure is explained.\n78\t- The EM spot-checks 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their sources.\n79\t\n80\t### 2B: schemas and data layer\n81\t**Persona:** data architect.\n82\t- Pydantic models, with Atlas validators generated from them.\n83\t- `make db-init` creates every collection and index, and can be re-run safely.\n84\t- A storage budget built from real document sizes, and a written decision on what goes into Atlas.\n85\t- The GeoNames geocoder and its manifest. The LCCN title parser, tested on at least 200 distinct titles.\n86\t- The reprint-clustering design, and its evaluation set.\n87\t- The reel-to-batch map (decision 29):\n88\t  - Build it from the batch paths in the candidates and samples, so a page with no batch path gets its IIIF service from local data. That's about 310,000 candidates.\n89\t  - Validate it: hide the batch on pages that have one, and count how often the map gets it back.\n90\t  - Flag every reel that maps to more than one batch, such as a reissued ver01 and ver02.\n91\t- No loc.gov lookups yet for the 25,595 candidates whose reels the map doesn't cover. At 20 a minute they would take about a day, and most won't be reports.\n92\t  - Resolve only the ones the reader makes reports, through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s.\n93\t  - Cache each answer, which `page_service` doesn't do yet, so no page is looked up twice.\n94\t\n95\t**Gate**\n96\t- Schema tests pass, and `make db-init` can run twice.\n97\t- The budget is written down.\n98\t- Geocoder accuracy is measured on a sample.\n99\t- The reel-to-batch map's hit rate on hidden batches is reported, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged.\n100\t\n101\t### 2C: the reader\n102\t**Persona:** ML specialist.\n103\t1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n104\t2. Build the labelling page.\n105\t3. Label the gold set before seeing any model output.\n106\t4. Teacher labels for the training pool, with the leakage check passing.\n107\t5. Baselines: a keyword rule, and base 4B both zero-shot and few-shot.\n108\t6. LoRA training on Tinker, iterating on the dev split until improvements stop. Each iteration is recorded.\n109\t7. Eval B, ablations B1 to B10, and edge-case slices E1 to E14.\n110\t8. The failure log, and `eval/MODEL_CARD.md`.\n111\t9. The local export and the offline proof.\n112\t10. The full pass over all triaged candidates.\n113\t\n114\t**Gate**\n115\t- The gold hash hasn't changed since the freeze, and the leakage check passes.\n116\t- The eval JSON and charts include confidence intervals.\n117\t- At least one failure is written up with its fix.\n118\t- The offline proof passes, and the full-pass counts are recorded.\n119\t- The EM audits 30 random reader outputs against their clippings.\n120\t\n121\t### 2D: load, cluster and harvest\n122\t**Persona:** data engineer.\n123\t- Geocode, cluster, embed, and load Atlas.\n124\t- `make db-verify` runs geo, fuzzy and vector queries and checks their expected results and latencies.\n125\t- The top-case rule, and the follow-up harvest as a second Temporal pass.\n126\t\n127\t**Gate**\n128\t- Atlas counts match the local counts, and storage is within the limit.\n129\t- `db-verify` passes.\n130\t- The harvest has written output for every case."}]},"uuid":"25332a02-31c1-4068-bf02-82ff72943b9b","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:14:55.222Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","content":"## 4. Phase 2: database, schemas and data models\n\n### 2A: archive read and EDA\n**Persona:** data engineer, with a systems specialist for throughput.\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants and unit tests, plus a near-miss lexicon.\n- `IngestArchiveWorkflow` over all 180 files: candidates, counts, samples and manifests, with concurrency set from Phase 1's measurements.\n- Kill test 1, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after.\n- The crop check on 50 random candidates spread across batches.\n- Eval A, with charts:\n  - per year: pages, articles and candidates, and candidates per million articles\n  - by state, the term mix and the OCR-quality distribution\n  - the reprint signal, and candidate lengths\n- Prefilter recall: the random-sample bound and the near-miss pool. If misses are found, lexicon v2 and a re-run.\n\n**Gate**\n- All 180 years are done, or each failure is listed with its reason.\n- Disk use is reported.\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces the 1850 counts exactly.\n- Kill-test evidence exists, and every crop-check failure is explained.\n- The EM spot-checks 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their sources.\n\n### 2B: schemas and data layer\n**Persona:** data architect.\n- Pydantic models, with Atlas validators generated from them.\n- `make db-init` creates every collection and index, and can be re-run safely.\n- A storage budget built from real document sizes, and a written decision on what goes into Atlas.\n- The GeoNames geocoder and its manifest. The LCCN title parser, tested on at least 200 distinct titles.\n- The reprint-clustering design, and its evaluation set.\n- The reel-to-batch map (decision 29):\n  - Build it from the batch paths in the candidates and samples, so a page with no batch path gets its IIIF service from local data. That's about 310,000 candidates.\n  - Validate it: hide the batch on pages that have one, and count how often the map gets it back.\n  - Flag every reel that maps to more than one batch, such as a reissued ver01 and ver02.\n- No loc.gov lookups yet for the 25,595 candidates whose reels the map doesn't cover. At 20 a minute they would take about a day, and most won't be reports.\n  - Resolve only the ones the reader makes reports, through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s.\n  - Cache each answer, which `page_service` doesn't do yet, so no page is looked up twice.\n\n**Gate**\n- Schema tests pass, and `make db-init` can run twice.\n- The budget is written down.\n- Geocoder accuracy is measured on a sample.\n- The reel-to-batch map's hit rate on hidden batches is reported, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged.\n\n### 2C: the reader\n**Persona:** ML specialist.\n1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n2. Build the labelling page.\n3. Label the gold set before seeing any model output.\n4. Teacher labels for the training pool, with the leakage check passing.\n5. Baselines: a keyword rule, and base 4B both zero-shot and few-shot.\n6. LoRA training on Tinker, iterating on the dev split until improvements stop. Each iteration is recorded.\n7. Eval B, ablations B1 to B10, and edge-case slices E1 to E14.\n8. The failure log, and `eval/MODEL_CARD.md`.\n9. The local export and the offline proof.\n10. The full pass over all triaged candidates.\n\n**Gate**\n- The gold hash hasn't changed since the freeze, and the leakage check passes.\n- The eval JSON and charts include confidence intervals.\n- At least one failure is written up with its fix.\n- The offline proof passes, and the full-pass counts are recorded.\n- The EM audits 30 random reader outputs against their clippings.\n\n### 2D: load, cluster and harvest\n**Persona:** data engineer.\n- Geocode, cluster, embed, and load Atlas.\n- `make db-verify` runs geo, fuzzy and vector queries and checks their expected results and latencies.\n- The top-case rule, and the follow-up harvest as a second Temporal pass.\n\n**Gate**\n- Atlas counts match the local counts, and storage is within the limit.\n- `db-verify` passes.\n- The harvest has written output for every case.","numLines":72,"startLine":59,"totalLines":226}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e7f58f09-29ac-4eb7-b5f6-2cd1c5eecece","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"25332a02-31c1-4068-bf02-82ff72943b9b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYZXiFyvuykfnaEXVK7z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014cP2JRutWqH2stALTEs5rV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":840,"cache_read_input_tokens":175050,"output_tokens":234,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":840,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":234,"cache_read_input_tokens":175050,"cache_creation_input_tokens":840,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":840},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014cP2JRutWqH2stALTEs5rV":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"bdda54c6-10fc-411b-8a63-9d9e2a261446","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgYZXhNAFq9sxGDGh7ZGL","requestRef":"a5d2ff78-835f-426d-bd0d-d7324f5921d2","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"24ae94c4-813e-45cb-9c01-35fc1f0e7788","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:14:55.929Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"24ae94c4-813e-45cb-9c01-35fc1f0e7788","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"efbe7dae-4a3a-47ef-9f27-91c90fdaed9c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014cP2JRutWqH2stALTEs5rV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Resume\n2\t\n3\tWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n4\t\n5\tHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n6\t\n7\t## Next step\n8\t\n9\t**Phase 2A: the archive read and EDA** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #1 is merged.\n10\t\n11\tNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n12\t- the Render deploy\n13\t- domain DNS\n14\t\n15\t## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n16\t\n17\tBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n18\t\n19\tBuilt:\n20\t- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n21\t- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n22\t- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n23\t- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n24\t- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n25\t- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n26\t\n27\tMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n28\t- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n29\t- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n30\t- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n31\t- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n32\t- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n33\t- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n34\t- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n35\t- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n36\t- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n37\t- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n38\t\n39\tUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n40\t- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n41\t- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n42\t- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n43\t- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n44\t\n45\tRisks and open questions:\n46\t- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n47\t- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n48\t- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n49\t- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n50\t- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n51\t\n52\t## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n53\t\n54\t- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n55\t- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n56\t- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n57\t- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n58\t- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n59\t"}]},"uuid":"2dcf655d-ab4b-470b-9e6a-6c37978963b2","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:14:55.946Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","content":"# Resume\n\nWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n\nHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n\n## Next step\n\n**Phase 2A: the archive read and EDA** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #1 is merged.\n\nNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n- the Render deploy\n- domain DNS\n\n## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n\nBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n\nBuilt:\n- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n\nMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n\nUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n\n## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n\n- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n","numLines":59,"startLine":1,"totalLines":59}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"24ae94c4-813e-45cb-9c01-35fc1f0e7788","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-archive","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","hash":"f685563aa42a13439569e1dff1135bd8d1423c8d53f629ed9325d7b035b19d23","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011EuiaLq1EPZ81xhEC7XGqV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","offset":59,"limit":72},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014cP2JRutWqH2stALTEs5rV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","hash":"750f567fb63d4a3173b518b9d3b7115b8e8de38162f4286d7b917bb527fdd962","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011EuiaLq1EPZ81xhEC7XGqV","type":"tool_result","content":"59\t## 4. Phase 2: database, schemas and data models\n60\t\n61\t### 2A: archive read and EDA\n62\t**Persona:** data engineer, with a systems specialist for throughput.\n63\t- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants and unit tests, plus a near-miss lexicon.\n64\t- `IngestArchiveWorkflow` over all 180 files: candidates, counts, samples and manifests, with concurrency set from Phase 1's measurements.\n65\t- Kill test 1, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after.\n66\t- The crop check on 50 random candidates spread across batches.\n67\t- Eval A, with charts:\n68\t  - per year: pages, articles and candidates, and candidates per million articles\n69\t  - by state, the term mix and the OCR-quality distribution\n70\t  - the reprint signal, and candidate lengths\n71\t- Prefilter recall: the random-sample bound and the near-miss pool. If misses are found, lexicon v2 and a re-run.\n72\t\n73\t**Gate**\n74\t- All 180 years are done, or each failure is listed with its reason.\n75\t- Disk use is reported.\n76\t- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces the 1850 counts exactly.\n77\t- Kill-test evidence exists, and every crop-check failure is explained.\n78\t- The EM spot-checks 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their sources.\n79\t\n80\t### 2B: schemas and data layer\n81\t**Persona:** data architect.\n82\t- Pydantic models, with Atlas validators generated from them.\n83\t- `make db-init` creates every collection and index, and can be re-run safely.\n84\t- A storage budget built from real document sizes, and a written decision on what goes into Atlas.\n85\t- The GeoNames geocoder and its manifest. The LCCN title parser, tested on at least 200 distinct titles.\n86\t- The reprint-clustering design, and its evaluation set.\n87\t- The reel-to-batch map (decision 29):\n88\t  - Build it from the batch paths in the candidates and samples, so a page with no batch path gets its IIIF service from local data. That's about 310,000 candidates.\n89\t  - Validate it: hide the batch on pages that have one, and count how often the map gets it back.\n90\t  - Flag every reel that maps to more than one batch, such as a reissued ver01 and ver02.\n91\t- No loc.gov lookups yet for the 25,595 candidates whose reels the map doesn't cover. At 20 a minute they would take about a day, and most won't be reports.\n92\t  - Resolve only the ones the reader makes reports, through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s.\n93\t  - Cache each answer, which `page_service` doesn't do yet, so no page is looked up twice.\n94\t\n95\t**Gate**\n96\t- Schema tests pass, and `make db-init` can run twice.\n97\t- The budget is written down.\n98\t- Geocoder accuracy is measured on a sample.\n99\t- The reel-to-batch map's hit rate on hidden batches is reported, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged.\n100\t\n101\t### 2C: the reader\n102\t**Persona:** ML specialist.\n103\t1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n104\t2. Build the labelling page.\n105\t3. Label the gold set before seeing any model output.\n106\t4. Teacher labels for the training pool, with the leakage check passing.\n107\t5. Baselines: a keyword rule, and base 4B both zero-shot and few-shot.\n108\t6. LoRA training on Tinker, iterating on the dev split until improvements stop. Each iteration is recorded.\n109\t7. Eval B, ablations B1 to B10, and edge-case slices E1 to E14.\n110\t8. The failure log, and `eval/MODEL_CARD.md`.\n111\t9. The local export and the offline proof.\n112\t10. The full pass over all triaged candidates.\n113\t\n114\t**Gate**\n115\t- The gold hash hasn't changed since the freeze, and the leakage check passes.\n116\t- The eval JSON and charts include confidence intervals.\n117\t- At least one failure is written up with its fix.\n118\t- The offline proof passes, and the full-pass counts are recorded.\n119\t- The EM audits 30 random reader outputs against their clippings.\n120\t\n121\t### 2D: load, cluster and harvest\n122\t**Persona:** data engineer.\n123\t- Geocode, cluster, embed, and load Atlas.\n124\t- `make db-verify` runs geo, fuzzy and vector queries and checks their expected results and latencies.\n125\t- The top-case rule, and the follow-up harvest as a second Temporal pass.\n126\t\n127\t**Gate**\n128\t- Atlas counts match the local counts, and storage is within the limit.\n129\t- `db-verify` passes.\n130\t- The harvest has written output for every case."},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014cP2JRutWqH2stALTEs5rV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Resume\n2\t\n3\tWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n4\t\n5\tHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n6\t\n7\t## Next step\n8\t\n9\t**Phase 2A: the archive read and EDA** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #1 is merged.\n10\t\n11\tNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n12\t- the Render deploy\n13\t- domain DNS\n14\t\n15\t## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n16\t\n17\tBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n18\t\n19\tBuilt:\n20\t- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n21\t- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n22\t- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n23\t- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n24\t- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n25\t- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n26\t\n27\tMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n28\t- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n29\t- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n30\t- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n31\t- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n32\t- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n33\t- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n34\t- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n35\t- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n36\t- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n37\t- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n38\t\n39\tUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n40\t- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n41\t- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n42\t- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n43\t- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n44\t\n45\tRisks and open questions:\n46\t- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n47\t- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n48\t- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n49\t- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n50\t- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n51\t\n52\t## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n53\t\n54\t- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n55\t- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n56\t- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n57\t- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n58\t- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n59\t"}]}}
