an and lady, as well as their families, were firm believers in the doctrines of the spiritualists and Notwithstanding the death of the form er, it was determined that the marriage should take place between the disembodied spirit of the young man and the living, breathing body of his affianced bride. Accordingly, on Sunday the marriage ceremony was performed between the clay cold corpse and the warm, blooming bride. 1t is under stood that this was in compliance with the directions of the spirit of the bridegroom. The devotion of the lady to the spirit of the memory of her lover carried her through this ceremony without faltering, but it must lead to unhappiness, for she no doubt considers herself as the wife of one whom she shall meet iii the body never more. Her heart lies buried in the grave with him who shold have been her guide and protector. Among all the singular things recorded of the spirit ualists we have met nothing parallel to this. Trenton Gazette, August 9Th.\n\n### 5_1907-09-21_p2_sn84020645_0051368423A_1907092101_1022.json | 1907-09-21 | The Montgomery advertiser. | 419 words\nHEADLINE: TORTURED TO DEATH /  / I'VE PARHEMITEs ARE ARRESTED /  /  FOR THE CRIME. /  / An old Woman Who Had Been a lq Cripple For Over Twenty Years, /  /  Dies in Most Horrible A.k Am0nfNa.\n;AChicago, Sept, 20-Five people, mem hArs of the sect of Parhemites, are, Inder arrest in Zion City accused of @rturing to death Mrs. Letitia Green hAulgh. e4 years old. a cripple for twenty years, to show their belief in the religion they profess AThe people under arrest are Walter aud Jennie Greenhaulgh. the son and daughter of the woman. Harold Mitchell. Mrs Harold Mitchell and Mrs. Smith. All are accused of man. slaughter. Mrs. Greenhaulgh had been f9r twenty years an invalid, suffering ffbm paralysis and rheumatism. ~The sect of Parhamites was founded about a year ago by Charles Parham apd numbers about two hundred per sons. The members of the sect origin ally belonged to Dowie's church and hslieve in the gift of tongues, and especially in bibolical possession. @The condition of Mrs. Greenhaulgh c1nvinced her son and daughter and tte three persons arrested with them that she must be possessed of the devil and they determined to exercise the exil spirit. The five knelt by the bed- side and after praying commenced their work. The arms Of Mrs. Greenhaulgh. stiff fened by Rheumatism, were twisted about in order that the devil might be dTAFeH OQl The cries of the aged woman were considered to be the evil spirit leaving and were greeted with triumph and hopes. After a course of the violent tr&atment, Mrs. Greenhaulgh not only beeame so weak that she could not use heF limbs, but became incapable of mak- tnB any motions. Then her neck was twisted, and for some time this treatment was kept up. walter Greenhaulgh testified at the coroner's in quest that Mitchell and his wife had a vision in which Mitchell was ordered to quit work and devote his time in casting out the demons from the sick. Young Greenhaulgh declared that his mothers consent was obtained before the treatment was commenced. Mrs Smith shared in the treatment. according to the testim0ny before the coroner's jury, which consisted in rais- ing Mrs. Greenhaulgh's head and hold ing her. while Mitchell and his wife attempted to straighten the woman's limbs which had been twisted by years ofsrheumatism. Greenhaulgh broke down and cried asFhe told his story. He declared that for some time his mother bore the tr6atment bravely, but at last the agony became so great that she could not restrain herself and she cried, \"Oh Lord help me-\" a number of times. The case will be further investigated by the authorities of Lake County in which Zion City is situated.\n\n### 11_1919-11-15_p22_sn83030214_00206532452_1919111501_0468.json | 1919-11-15 | New-York tribune. | 640 words\nHEADLINE: \n| Of fact she mildly and ambguously | proceeded thus: Through that vast vale black. pitlike. | Wouldst thou hold a taper? | Woudsk thou lend thy hand unto them that seek? Is that yearning. born ot truth | Eating thy heart in hungry anticipation | That thou shoudst leave beacon upon the sands? Yet: o had thou beheld the regal bark OF | day Floating upon the sea of eternity And no beacon alisht. no chart! ! In benedictlon do bow before thee. DIR. Hyslop Looks Worried This was received with polite murmurs of surprise as Dr. Hys op made his way back to the fireplace. He listened to the rest of the sayings Of Patience with slightly worried, ! enlgmatical expression. He refused to commit himself one way or the ohcr, , saying that he was perfectly open minded about it. He admitted it to be an extraordinary psychological phcnomcnon, but SAID he would be unable to spare the time to give the examina! tlons and tests necessary to establish anything definite about the perform ances of Patience. He insisted that mis- accusations were against her pub I fishers and editors and not against Mrs. Curran personally. AS Ethel Barrymore seated herself at the table Mrs. Curran proceeded to / explain why Patience chose her as a , medium for her messages to this world. \"She explained to me once that she sought to communicate through me because my nature was attuned to hers\" she declared. mo other spirit ever bothers me and I manage to live quite healthily and .wholesomely, not wthstanding Patience. 1t is really a miracle that has happened to me- Don't attach any Importance to the ouija board. 1t is only means of concentration and signifies nothing\" Verse to Ethel Barrymore Patience then addressed herself to Ethel Barrymore as follows: Great Cod With mine eyes uprascd un fearfully Il commune with the shadows-the lowering shadows Which the gifts of thy being! l am thrce-fold blessed, for within the flesh of man I may find but egotry and the chaHs But there is Il deeper cup which Is ninel Yea. may lay hold with mae own hands Upon the cups presented from the pit of mans soul Filled with the wine of imagination, And | may drink and become drunk upon the sup. Dreaming dreams that cling like robes and lifting my head in strange clements Yea Mans station 1 may discard as a glove, Become a dolt. a lout Or a noble at wishing. Within paint pot l may dip And wreck havoc with Thy creations Masqueradrng with sure faith in the folly, For l present stuff which is q food unto the earth. Yes, emotion cloak within a cloth and metals -s'Al walk before the eyes Of man en LA ~tins him. is this :. - or is 1t not attainment? Fore God So day is tattercd torn May not patch =; with the wizardy Thou hast delivered me? Renewing man In dreams SO. when ne leaves the tabernacle of dreams He stanGeth. wondcring-wondering Is the day so gray or is it transformed In the new drink he hath supped? \"Flowers\" for MIR. Rathom Mr.: Rathom got the most flowery out pourings of Patience. She talked to him about \"plucking purple lilies and sleeping them within his dreams and weaving these DER the tapestries Of his songs and much more in a vein of vouptuous metaphor. 1t seems that sometimes Patience is not entirely polite to her human medium. For instance. she continually calls her \"Follytop,' and yesterday Mrs. Curran vowed she heard herself called a dullard. Although she has never had q concrete vision of Patience she saws she hears her sweet, low-toned voice continually in her ears. The last of the pubic writings\" viii be given at Mrs. Behr's home TODAY.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"2fe4ea6a-4333-4462-94bf-c546c58b4df0","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-archive","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":6,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":1},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remo