{"parentUuid":"6d076064-a5f4-463f-8acb-9120fe5a5348","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"efbe7dae-4a3a-47ef-9f27-91c90fdaed9c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AJs4XSMbrywNMxzHUwCX9J","type":"tool_result","content":"### 8_1898-01-20_pNone_sn84029386_00295871477_1898012001_0669.json | 1898-01-20 | Abilene weekly reflector. | 123 words\nHEADLINE: \"CONTROLLED BY SPIRITS\"\nThis VIII he the Defense OF Chris Merry, Chicago Thief aud Wife Murderer CHICAGO, Jan. l3.-The defense OF Chris Merry, now on trial for the MUIR der of his wife, is to be novel one. 1t is to be urged by his attorneys that Merry, being under the control OF spirits, was not responsible for his actions If he did commit the crime OF which he Is accused. Spiritualistic mediums are to BE- called to testify in the case, it is said, although his attorney denies 1t. An effort will he made to show that Merry is degener ate. Experts on sociology, criminol' ogy and the lines of heredity will be called. This indicates that the defense will admit Merry killed his wife.\n\n### 1_1900-10-23_pna_sn84028140_00237283661_1900102301_0725.json | 1900-10-23 | Akron daily Democrat. [volume] (Akron, Ohio) 1892-1902 | 223 words\nHEADLINE: WHEN A PAPoOsE DIES. /  / Its Spirit II Thought ny the Motbe, / TO Frequent Its Places /  /  / In Life.\nThe Indian mother, when her baby dies, does not believe that swift angels bear IL into the sunshine Of the spirit. land; but she has a beautiful dream 70 solace her bereavement. The cruel empty places which everywhere meet the white mothers eye are unknown 10 her. for TO her tender fancy little spirit-child fills them. II is not uncommon. says the author OF Little Folks Of Many Lands, TO see In Mexico, or in Canada pair Of elaborate tiny moccasins above little In dian grave. A mothers fingers have made them, q mothers hand has hung ' them there. TO help baby's feet over the long, rough road that stretches between his fathers wiRwam and the Great Chiefs happy hunting-grounds. Indians believe that baby's spirit cannot reach the spirit-land until the child, II living. would have been old enough fnd strong enough to walk. Until that time the little spirit hovers about it's mother. And often II grows tired-oh. so very ed!--so he tender mother carries a papoose's cradle on her back that the baby spirit may ride and rest when 1t will. The cradle is filled with the softest featbers-for spirits rest more com fortably upon feathers, hard things bruise them-and all papoose's old toys dangle from it's hood, for dead papoose may like to play even as living papoose did,\n\n### 4_1906-09-07_p4_sn86002159_00513680405_1906090701_0534.json | 1906-09-07 | The Dolores star. | 139 words\nHEADLINE: VISITS PLANET OF MARS; /  /  / FINDS 0NE-EYED GIANTS /  / Syracuse IN. W Man Brings Back / Queer Account Of Our Nearest /  /  / Terrestrial Neighbor.\nSyracuse. N. Y.-Sackville G. Ley son, president of the Society for Psychical Research. says he recently paid visit to Mars. Although the dis tance is 141,000,OOO miles his spirit went there and back in 40 minutes while his body lay in his residence. In describing his visit he said: When approached Mars it looked like big globe Of fire. and it seemed as If l were about to plunge into a molten mass. 1t was surrounded by blood red clouds mixed with others OF greenish hue. There are two tribes of people on Mars-one SO large 1 only came up tc their knees and the other SO small that they only came up to my knees. None wore clothing. All were covered with hair. The largest species had huge ears Z nose like lion. and only one eye. in\n\n### 8_1910-06-25_pna_sn96077142_00271764352_1910062501_0115.json | 1910-06-25 | The L&#39;Anse sentinel. (L&#39;Anse, L.S., Mich.) 18??-current | 831 words\nHEADLINE: JIM POLES / TAVERN\nIl had been up against the hote problem to such an extent In that par Of North Carolina\" said John Gilbert the travelling groceryman, that hat become shy Of tackling 1t. and ONT day meeting on the road darby whs was on his way somewhere driving { bull hitched to wabbly wagon OF which were loaded dozen or SO stick Of lightwood. thought 1t might b well to see II he couldn't give me some information as to the probable fate that might be In store for me al the inn lying in wait at the mountain town I was approaching. | stopped him and asked him II there was q good hotel there. \"The darby leaned forward with his elbows on his knees, looked at mecooked one eye up toward the sky as II he was pondering on the subject had brought forward and then coming back to earth gave his bull whack with the CAD he carried and exclaimed: .W'oa. 20 p'intedly Of hoodoo.' \"The bull hadn't stirred since the darby stopped him In the road and he didn't stir under the whack OF the cad; so I assumed that this little show Of disciplining was but divert fng habit of the bulls owner. was On the point OF pressing my question when the darby rested his elbows on his knees again. and turning to me said: I reckon yo' nevuh did know oil ledge Gillian, wat kim frum ovuh yon n de Chowan kentryT II replied that had never known Judge Gillian. but co'se yo' done heah bout himT said the darby. mo, said l. I never heard about him either That seemed to surprise the darby not little Shoot he exclaimed after staring at me a while. 'Whah 20 done come rum, sah?' II told him I was from the north, and even that didn't seem to excuse me for not having ever heard of old Judge Gillian. The darby sat In a bordering attitude half minute or SO, and then looking at me again as II ] was something OF q curiosity. he gathered up his lines preparatory to starting on his way and said: Jim Pole, he done kip hotel up yon. subj ' But hold on said 4 don't care who keeps the hotel. Is 1t a good one? And what has old Judge Gillian got to do with it?' .'The darby leaned forward with his elbows on his knees again and he grinned as he said: II nevuh did stop at Jim Poles. sub, In my life. but OIL ledge Gillian sne time he kim ovuh hyuh to lay down de law du'in' de cote time, an he stow hesef away at Jim Poles. One O de baddes' nigguhs dat bean roun' hyuh den he bean fotch up befo' de oil ledge. Dat nigguh he bean a bad un, too, an dey fine him guilty de wuss kyne. \"'Or ledge Gillian he bean stoppin' den. two, free days at Jim Poles. He lookin' desput sot In he mind wen dey fotch in dat po' nigguh wat dey toun' guilty. \"\"Stan up dab, 20 nigguh!\" he say. He stan up. mos skeeuh to def \"Nigguh,\" de Of jedge say, dis hyuh a monst'us ting 20 done bean foun' guilty To-! My haht it bean des lack stone ag'in you an | mighty re. gretful to find dat de law an de cons1tution, dey standin' in de way o. my fiictin' on yo' te'ble punishment des' pinin' to giveeyo' \"\"Ef de law an de constitution dey didn't stan. in de way o. my doin' 1t, I shore done sentence yo' to stop one whole week at Jim Poles tavuhn, des sho's yo' bon! Ez case 1t done stan, dahfo',\" de OIL jedge lay 1t down to dat po' shiv'n nigguh. Il CANT do 1t, an de wus' ] kin do to yo' is to sen yo' to de pen'tenshry TO ten yeahs!\" de Of ledge say, an dey done took dat bad nigguh away THE darby on his load of light wood rolled his eyes up at me. while his grin spread wide across his face, and continuing, he said: Goose. nevuh did stop at Jim Poles in my life, sub, an so CANT say wevvuh de hotel bean good un or no. but heah folks declar. many times, dat OIL ledge Gillian he sutton. ~ do let dat nigguh on mighty lucky! With that the darky started up his Bull and went ON his way. As gazed after him he glanced back and heard him say: 'Nevuh did heah of OIL ledge Gillaml Sho! Dat shore bean mon. Sous queeuhi' There was another tavern In the place, and I didn't stop at Jim Poles, But. relying ON the darky's tale Of old Judge Gtllam's opinion, II this one wasn't branch Of Jim Poles, then HE proprietor must have learned no tel keeping from Jim Pole, and I'D have been willing to bet on It.\"\n\n### 42_1912-12-10_pna_sn95066012_00202199549_1912121001_0355.json | 1912-12-10 | The Bennington evening banner. (Bennington, Vt.) 19??-1961 | 855 words\nHEADLINE: A Cabman' /  /  / Experience\nupon the marvellous electric ulSpidy But soon he heard his cab door open and shut and a mans voicesay: Wake upr' John Burns turned and looked through the glass behind him upon the features of very old man. The only part of his apparel that was not GOV cred by his cloak was his hat, which struck the cabman as something remarkable. 1t was what is called bell crown, with large curled brim and covered with short fur. The chang ing electric displays lighted the wear er's face, showing a countenance not only old, but there was an eve twinkle In the eye. \"Northward.\" said the passenger. \"On what street\" THE Bloomlngdale road\" \"The whatT \"That one\" The speaker pointed to Broadway Cabby put on the power and, turning at the corner, entered that part of Broadway lying north OF Forty-second street and now the live Hest part of upper New York. After reaching a less crowded portion of the thoroughfare he asked the old gen tleman behind him just where he should take him and received orders to turn into One Hundred and Fortieth street. From that time the passenger kept pointing out the way till he pull ed up before an old fashioned house situated among modern edifices. The flare\" opened the cab door and, sup porting himself with a staff, tottered NJ the walk to the front door. From this point John Burns in tell ing the story appears confused. He doesn't remember whether the house was lighted or in darkness. He can't say that he saw the old man go into the house. His impression is that there were no lights in the windows and If his fare went inside he must have gone right In through the closed door. The only thing John Burns re members distinctly Is that the wind blew aside the mans cloak. which reached to his ankles and exposed q pair of legs no bigger than those of a skeleton. However this may be, the cabman who, it has been said, was in need OF rest soon began to doze In his seat He was awakened by blaze of light coming through the doorway of the house before which he was stopping and saw distinctly two persons there The one was his passenger, the other a lady. The latter was dressed very much In the present mode of high waist. panniers and cllnging skirts The man who was bowling himself out flourIshed his bell crown hat. bowed very low and scraped excessively with his feet After number of such caperings, which made John Burns wonder. he came down the walk, turn ing to throw a kiss to the lady who still stood in the doorway. Then the Illumination seemed to go out all of 1 sudden. and the passenger stepped int the cab. closed the door and said: Down the Bloomingdale road\" Having learned that the Blooming dale road meant Broadway. cabby ask ed no questions. but motOred south ward. He received no order to leave the thoroughfare or to 20 to any par ticular place, so he drove on past the junction at Broadway and Thirty third street and Madison and Unior squares and thence straight south ward through what is now lower Broadway. The life of the city grew became the midnight OF great city Ii llvened up as they passed the City Hall park. adjoining which most OF the newspaper offices linger. but died out as they approached Trinity church at the head Of Wall street Burns was directed to turn into Rector street bordering the churchyard on the south \"Stopr' said the stranger. John Burns drew UP TO the curb. II am under deep obligations to You. said the passenger in a tone that sounded to John Burns very pompous \"You have taken me to call on my wife at her residence. and | nm now goine to pay visit to a gentleman whom was reluctantly Obliged 20 punish FOL some annoying remarks he made about me. Good evening\" with a flourish OF tie bell crown hat in lieu OF fare. the strange maj seemed to fit up the side Of stone wall. through an iron railing. and dis appeared beneath n monument located but a few feet beyond the rail. Whether John Burns awoke from q dream or was so astonished that he didn't know whether he was asleep OF awake he fails 10 make Il appear As to what he did the next day &c clear up the matter he Is explicit He motored up TO the dwelling where he had taken the stranger and asked OF one passing who lived there. Don't know\" was the reply. THAT: the Jumel house. She lived In the early part Of the last century and mar ried Aaron Burr in his old age\" who was Aaron Burr Former vice president OF the United States. He killed his political rival in a duel\" From the Jumel house John Burns drove 10 Trinity church and. entering the chnrchyard. w\n\n### 9_1918-04-21_p1_sn83030214_00206532257_1918042102_0461.json | 1918-04-21 | New-York tribune. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 1866-1924 | 435 words\nHEADLINE: SOLDIER died in New York /  /  / last week who probably knew / as much about No Mans Land\nas any soldier in the war. This was Captain R. H. Knyvett, of the 18th Australian Infantry, who BIAS been lecturng in this country. Nearly year ago ne was wounded by German bomb in a night encounter among the boys and she'll holes of the desert between the lines. Now he is dead, of tuberculosis of the blood; but the record he has left as scout in No Mans Land is almost unexampled. This familiarity Of Captain Knyvett's with the dread land between the lines came as the result of his being made a front line scout and intelligence officer. de did his most distinguished work in the region of the Somme, his duties gen. orally taking him into No Mans Land just before an assault in investigations OF German positions. Yet only once was he wounded in this most dangerous of tasks, being bombed near Bapaume after many desperate encounters. Mr Knyvett has written a book called Over There with the Australians\" Particularly timely is an article OF his in \"scribner's\" dealing with this work. Here in the space of few pages one will find the outlines of No Mans Land pretty we'll indicated. Take, for example, his remarks regarding sounds IN No Mans Land not human sounds, for such carry far the beat Of hammer on a post, the sharp wang Of unrolling barbed wire as it catches ana then springs away-voices even come a. through mcgaphone in the eerie silence odt these are long drawn signs that peneaa'e the inner consciousness and hushed \"afmurs that fall on the ear of the soul. nave felt touch on the shoulder as .\"'aen one would speak TO me when there nas been no one by. 1t is the grave of ten thousand unburied dead, but the grinning a'is ard auivering jelly or the few rags mat hutter in,thc wind are not the COM RACES that We knew. think their spirits mover near, for they cannot 20 to their ss'oing place till victory has been won. 1hsy are ever seeking to pierce the veil of .snse 10 that they may add their strength :, our arms, and these mako for us of No S\"\"S Land mo strange place. and give to saf sensries encouragement until the land ~\" Man vanishes and our possession 4 lsaenes TO the barrier OF the enemy barbed wire\" naturally, in this land of barbed wire. so1pSes, reeking shell holes and tapping s'senine guns, with occasional star shells ssn.'nx up every nook and corner with 10y light, strange encounters were 81 hound 70 occur ners vas one night when cot 4 huge","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"0d2bd09d-ff7c-46e7-aa88-bc72d0f68e53","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:21:32.048Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"### 8_1898-01-20_pNone_sn84029386_00295871477_1898012001_0669.json | 1898-01-20 | Abilene weekly reflector. | 123 words\nHEADLINE: \"CONTROLLED BY SPIRITS\"\nThis VIII he the Defense OF Chris Merry, Chicago Thief aud Wife Murderer CHICAGO, Jan. l3.-The defense OF Chris Merry, now on trial for the MUIR der of his wife, is to be novel one. 1t is to be urged by his attorneys that Merry, being under the control OF spirits, was not responsible for his actions If he did commit the crime OF which he Is accused. Spiritualistic mediums are to BE- calle