roubles. The clutch may slip, drag, bite or knock Then again the gears may be hard to\nWINDOW: refuses to start or is hard TO start: II knocks, misses. lacks power or over heats Then there are clutch troubles. The clutch may slip, drag, bite or knock Then again the gears may be hard to shift or they may grind or hum By classifying these different symptoms It is a comparatively easy matter in\n\n=== c-002 | 1901-06-21 | Phillipsburg herald. [volume] (Phillipsburg, Kan.) 1882-1905 | 538 words | flags: knocking\nHEADLINE: \nOPENING: runaway husband into her home again after Il years. A telegram had come the day before telling her he was dead, and his last request had been that his body should be sent to her for burial. 1t touched her deeply. To think that she had been upon the point of yielding to Pennington Brush's repeated plead ings the very hour she had received the intelligence of Brewster's death and his belated remembrance of her self.\nWINDOW: e. He hurried out of doors and sped swiftly up the path towards Mrs. Brewster's. He stepped lightly upon the porch, determined not to brighten her by knocking, if it had been but a trick of his mag nation, she asleep and everything quiet within. He peeped cautiously through the parlor window, and th\n\n=== c-003 | 1918-11-05 | Daily capital journal. (Salem, Oregon) 1903-1919 | 205 words | flags: spirits\nHEADLINE: The Wife /  /  / BY JANE PHELPS /  / ALTHOUGH RUTH HAS GUESTS /  /  / BRIAN GOES TO SCHOOL\" 80\nOPENING: But Ruth's gay spirits soon made him forget Mollie and his own passive deception. She told him as much of her business matters as she thought would interest him, then of her trip down on the train, repeating little incidents in so entertaining a manner that the time passed quickly. Passed, too, with out as much as n thought straying Mollie.ward. The tension so often present, was not felt between them. In fact it\nWINDOW: But Ruth's gay spirits soon made him forget Mollie and his own passive deception. She to