ritualistic, medium(s), disembodied, ouija board, and control of spirits.","+  - Not counted:","+    - living witches' shapes in testimony from 1692","+    - the devil driven out of a woman","+    - a psychical researcher's own spirit visiting Mars","+    - the belief that a dead baby's spirit stays with its mother","+    - a fictional cabman's passenger who is Aaron Burr","+    - a dialect sketch","+- Estimates (`eval_a.json`, `recall.screen`; `recall.svg` now shows them):","+  - **Non-candidates:** 4 in 38,900, so 0.010% (0.0040–0.026%), or about 38,000 (14,800–97,600) of 369,263,454 articles.","+  - **Near-miss pool:** 4 in 1,013 (the 713 drawn and eval A's 300), so 0.39% (0.15–1.0%), or about 27,100 (10,600–69,500) of 6,872,539.","+  - **Recall:**","+    - The candidates hold about 36,800 reports (decision 27), so lexicon v1 finds about 49% of them.","+    - With the most reports the interval allows outside the candidates, it finds at least 27%.","+    - Across the candidates' own interval (17,900–74,000), the floor runs from 16% to 43%.","+  - Counting only the 2 clear reports gives about 19,000 missed (5,200–69,200), so recall would be about 66%, and at least 35%.","+- **Caveat:** the screen sees only the reports that hold a flag word or a near-miss term.","+  - With their lexicon terms masked, 5 of eval A's 7 reports still hold one (71%, 36–92%).","+  - The two that don't are the 1904 courtroom spectre and the 1916 Peking legation, which calls its ghost a \"shade\".","+  - So the screen may miss about a third of the reports outside the candidates, and these estimates are low by about that much.","+- **Lexicon v2, proposed to the EM and not run.** Each candidate term was measured on the 38,900 judged articles:","+","+  | Term | Articles in 38,900 | Candidates added | Reports caught |","+  |---|---|---|---|","+  | spiritualism, spiritualist(s), spiritualistic | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 |","+  | spirit(s) and medium(s) in one article | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 |","+  | spirit wor