writes \"I have felt a touch on the shoulder as when one would speak to me when there has been no one by\", and that the spirits of No Man's Land's unburied dead \"hover near\". It is a first-person claim of the dead's presence. The reader agent called it a close call.\n42\t\n43\tNot counted, all of them close calls first:\n44\t- 1692 witch-trial testimony reprinted in 1841: the \"shapes\" are of living witches\n45\t- 1907, Zion City: an \"evil spirit\", the devil, is tortured out of a woman\n46\t- 1906: a psychical researcher's own spirit goes to Mars\n47\t- 1900: the belief that a dead papoose's spirit stays with its mother, which is folklore in general\n48\t- 1912: a cabman's passenger who turns out to be Aaron Burr, which reads as syndicated fiction ending at Hamilton's monument\n49\t- 1910: a dialect sketch about a tavern, where \"hoodoo\" is OCR noise\n50\t\n51\t## Estimates (`eval_a.json`, `recall.screen`)\n52\t\n53\t| | Reports | Rate (95% Wilson) | Implied articles |\n54\t|---|---|---|---|\n55\t| Non-candidates (the 38,600 and eval A's 300) | 4 of 38,900 | 0.010% (0.0040–0.026%) | ~38,000 (14,800–97,600) of 369.3M |\n56\t| Near-miss pool (713 drawn and eval A's 300, deduplicated by id) | 4 of 1,013 | 0.39% (0.15–1.0%) | ~27,100 (10,600–69,500) of 6.87M |\n57\t\n58\t- **Recall:** the candidates hold about 36,800 reports, so lexicon v1 finds **49%** of them.\n59\t  - It finds **at least 27%** with the most reports that non-candidates can hold.\n60\t  - Across the candidates' own interval (17,900–74,000), that floor runs from 16% to 43%.\n61\t- With only the 2 clear reports: about 19,000 missed (5,200–69,200), so recall is about 66%, and at least 35%.\n62\t- `recall.svg` now shows the screen's figures, and small shares keep two significant figures (0.0040%–0.026%), so the interval doesn't read as 0.0%. I rendered it and checked it in both themes.\n63\t\n64\t## The screen's caveat (mask test)\n65\t\n66\t- With their lexicon terms masked, **5 of eval A's 7 reports** still hold a flag word or near-miss term (71%, 36–92%). They hold mysterious and spirit; in white and witchcraft; spiritualism and spirits; in white; and mysterious.\n67\t- The two that don't:\n68\t  - the 1904 courtroom spectre, whose only supernatural word is \"spectre\"\n69\t  - the 1916 Peking legation, which says ghost, haunted and spooks, and also calls its ghost \"the shade of an officer\". \"Shade\" isn't a flag word.\n70\t- So the screen may miss about 2 in 7 of the reports outside the candidates. If it does, the true count is about 1.4 times the estimates above.\n71\t- `composition.json` gains a `flags_without_terms` field for each of its 200 items, which is the input to this test. It adds 328 lines and removes none.\n72\t\n73\t## Lexicon v2: a proposal for your call, not run\n74\t\n75\tEach term was measured on the 38,900 judged articles, with no re-read (scratch `v2.py`):\n76\t\n77\t| Term | Articles in 38,900 | Candidates added | Reports caught |\n78\t|---|---|---|---|\n79\t| spiritualism, spiritualist(s), spiritualistic | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 (1856, 1898) |\n80\t| spirit(s) and medium(s) in one article | 8 | ~76,000 | 2 (1919, 1898) |\n81\t| spirit world, land, control, communication, message, manifestation, circle, photograph or medium; control of or by spirits; disembodied; spirits of the dead or departed | 5 | ~47,000 | 2 (1856, 1898) |\n82\t| ouija, planchette | 1 | ~9,500 | 1 (1919) |\n83\t| séance, moved from the near-miss lexicon | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n84\t\n85\t- **Together:** 17 articles, so about **161,000 more candidates (+15%)**, and they catch **3 of the 4**. About 18% of what they add are reports (6–41%), against 3.5% for v1's candidates.\n86\t- Their other matches are spiritualism mentioned in passing, announced spiritualist meetings, a stage exposé of mediums, and \"medium\" in other senses.\n87\t- Left out: materialize, which matched 7 articles and no report. No term catches the 1918 scout's \"spirits\".\n88\t- **Cost:** a full re-read, 118.6 min and 340.4 GB at four processes (decision 25), because the near-miss pool is stored only as samples. The reader's job would grow from 1.05 million candidates to about 1.21 million.\n89\t- The estimates rest on 3 reports in 17 articles, so the intervals are wide.\n90\t\n91\t## Interpretation calls, yours to overrule\n92\t\n93\t- I counted the 1898 spirit-control defence and the 1918 felt presence as reports. Without them, the figures are the \"2 clear reports\" line above. I added both kinds of claim to the 2C `POLICY.md` item.\n94\t- The screen reads near-miss terms as well as flag words.\n95\t- The non-candidate estimate pools the 38,600 screened with eval A's 300, which were read in full. The near-miss estimate pools the 713 with eval A's 300.\n96\t\n97\t## Plan items added (`BUILD-PLAN.md`)\n98\t\n99\t- **2B reprint evaluation set:** sample per candidate as well as per cluster. Eval A's check drew clusters uniformly, so 11 of its 20 pairs came from 2-printing clusters, and none of the 157 clusters of 50 or more printings was checked. I verified both numbers. The largest clusters also get a hand check for chaining.\n100\t- **2C `POLICY.md`:** one rule for delusions and hallucinations, with your 1904 and 1909 examples (I verified both verdicts), and also for spirit control and a felt presence of the dead.\n101\t- **2C eval B:** the reader screens the random reservoirs, for a bound that doesn't rest on flag words. The Tinker credit check sets its size.\n102\t\n103\t## Also\n104\t\n105\t- `eval_a.json` has `ocr_quality.dictionary`: sha256 `be41ad97963bf8dabedd5871d5d691596175269d540956b0f9965a885c2bbab9`, 235,976 words (`wc -l`).\n106\t- These files are byte-identical to before:\n107\t  - `recall.json`\n108\t  - `reprint-check.json`\n109\t  - the other seven charts\n110\t- The Phase 2A STATUS block in `RESUME.md` cites your spot check, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\". \"Next step\" now points at 2B, with v2 waiting on you.\n111\t- Staged-diff scans:\n112\t  - Commit 1 named `ULTRAMARINE_BASE_URL`. The match was the word \"instruments.\" in the 1913 White Plains court report in `screen.json`, which shares 12 characters with that URL's domain, so it's a false positive. I checked it without printing the value.\n113\t  - Commit 2 was clean.\n114\t  - Machine name: 0 in both.\n115\t- No loc.gov requests in this step.\n116\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
