{"parentUuid":"9eee1bb7-49a8-4c4e-ba40-bdcd11149860","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"400ad373-d924-47cf-8e2d-d61155c6ff44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FK3LAj48UwvQvsRVPRHhcj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Resume\n2\t\n3\tWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n4\t\n5\tHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n6\t\n7\t## Next step\n8\t\n9\t**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #2 is merged. Lexicon v2 (decision 30) waits on the EM. If it is accepted, its re-read runs before anything is loaded into Atlas.\n10\t\n11\tNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n12\t- the Render deploy\n13\t- domain DNS\n14\t\n15\t## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read and EDA (2026-10-30)\n16\t\n17\tBranch `phase-2a-archive`, PR #2.\n18\t\n19\tBuilt:\n20\t- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n21\t- The full read of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6` (decision 25).\n22\t- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n23\t- Eval A (`make eval`, 42 s, 4.0 GB at peak). It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n24\t\n25\tMeasured:\n26\t- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages, giving 1,050,459 candidates (2,837 per million) and 6,872,539 near-miss articles.\n27\t- The read took 118.6 min for 340.4 GB, at 47.8 MB/s with four years at a time.\n28\t- 3.5% of candidates are ghost reports (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800. Reprints put 31.4% of candidates in 80,770 clusters, which leaves 801,071 distinct stories.\n29\t- Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27%. That rests on 4 reports found among 38,900 non-candidates (decision 30).\n30\t- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n31\t- 32.0% of candidates have no batch path, and 92.4% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decision 29).\n32\t\n33\tGate:\n34\t- All 180 years are done, and none failed. Six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n35\t- Disk: the outputs take 1.94 GB (`data/candidates` 1.3 GB, `data/samples` 565 MB, counts 956 KB, manifests 720 KB), and 39 GiB are free.\n36\t- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the committed manifest.\n37\t- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n38\t- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n39\t- The EM's spot check, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n40\t  - every candidate's term is in its text\n41\t  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n42\t  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n43\t\n44\tRisks and open questions:\n45\t- **Lexicon v2 is the EM's call** (decision 30). Spiritualist terms would add about 161,000 candidates (+15%) and catch 3 of the 4 missed reports. Adding them takes a full re-read: 118.6 min and 340.4 GB.\n46\t- The recall estimate rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Its screen would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports once their lexicon terms are masked.\n47\t- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle delusions, hallucinations, spirit control and a felt presence of the dead.\n48\t- 25,595 candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n49\t\n50\t## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n51\t\n52\tBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n53\t\n54\tBuilt:\n55\t- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n56\t- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n57\t- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n58\t- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n59\t- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n60\t- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n61\t\n62\tMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n63\t- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n64\t- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n65\t- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n66\t- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n67\t- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n68\t- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n69\t- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n70\t- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n71\t- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n72\t- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n73\t\n74\tUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n75\t- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n76\t- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n77\t- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n78\t- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n79\t\n80\tRisks and open questions:\n81\t- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n82\t- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n83\t- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n84\t- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n85\t- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n86\t\n87\t## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n88\t\n89\t- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n90\t- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n91\t- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n92\t- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n93\t- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n94\t"}]},"uuid":"5ef471e3-49d2-4642-8bfd-e2cb468b1782","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:51:49.246Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","content":"# Resume\n\nWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n\nHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n\n## Next step\n\n**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #2 is merged. Lexicon v2 (decision 30) waits on the EM. If it is accepted, its re-read runs before anything is loaded into Atlas.\n\nNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n- the Render deploy\n- domain DNS\n\n## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read and EDA (2026-10-30)\n\nBranch `phase-2a-archive`, PR #2.\n\nBuilt:\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n- The full read of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6` (decision 25).\n- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n- Eval A (`make eval`, 42 s, 4.0 GB at peak). It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n\nMeasured:\n- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages, giving 1,050,459 candidates (2,837 per million) and 6,872,539 near-miss articles.\n- The read took 118.6 min for 340.4 GB, at 47.8 MB/s with four years at a time.\n- 3.5% of candidates are ghost reports (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800. Reprints put 31.4% of candidates in 80,770 clusters, which leaves 801,071 distinct stories.\n- Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27%. That rests on 4 reports found among 38,900 non-candidates (decision 30).\n- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n- 32.0% of candidates have no batch path, and 92.4% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decision 29).\n\nGate:\n- All 180 years are done, and none failed. Six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n- Disk: the outputs take 1.94 GB (`data/candidates` 1.3 GB, `data/samples` 565 MB, counts 956 KB, manifests 720 KB), and 39 GiB are free.\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the committed manifest.\n- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n- The EM's spot check, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n  - every candidate's term is in its text\n  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- **Lexicon v2 is the EM's call** (decision 30). Spiritualist terms would add about 161,000 candidates (+15%) and catch 3 of the 4 missed reports. Adding them takes a full re-read: 118.6 min and 340.4 GB.\n- The recall estimate rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Its screen would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports once their lexicon terms are masked.\n- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle delusions, hallucinations, spirit control and a felt presence of the dead.\n- 25,595 candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n\n## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n\nBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n\nBuilt:\n- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n\nMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n\nUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n\n## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n\n- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n","numLines":94,"startLine":1,"totalLines":94}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9eee1bb7-49a8-4c4e-ba40-bdcd11149860","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
