"704cc272-23b5-4dee-b3e7-9407905ed8ce","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"9bb8b879-a404-4002-b6f8-124b6907d74c","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:58:51.996Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"9bb8b879-a404-4002-b6f8-124b6907d74c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018eFou5QCb14LegyksBuWTk","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 1-003 | 1873-06-15 | New Orleans Republican. [volume] (New Orleans, La) 1867-1878 | terms ['spirit+medium']\nHEADLINE: Heard From.\nOur reporter called on medium in good standing, yesterday, to inquire into the present whereabouts of Pelagie Brown. After striking an attitude and the tab lie in the style usual on such occasions. the \"shade\" manifested its presence. Are you happy!\" inquired the medium. No answer. \"Do you know you are wanted in New OrleansE' Eyes, but can not come\" \"Why not!'' because the lawyers land there are plenty Of them herel say my evidence would be ruled out\" where are you, then!' No answer. ''Do you know anything OF the great con. trcversy now pending ON earth between Jacob Hawkins, plaintiff, and the New Ocleans Picayune Company, defendant?' lExcitedly.! \"'Fore Ged, doesn't know what you're tslkin' about. spec the white folks all done gone crazy Here the medium announced that tho spirit had left in disgust and refused to make any further manifestations. Other spirits were questioned as to the habits of Pelagie, who stated that she has strange fear of courts. and does not, as rule. as. sociate much with the lawyers. by whom she is now surrounded. \n\n##### 1-039 | 1888-12-05 | St. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 | terms ['spirit+medium', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: England's Poet Laureate Is a / Spiritualist and His Own /  /  / Medium. /  / In an Autograph Letter He / Admits Possession of a /  /  / Peculiar Power. /  / lie Can Transfer Conscious / ness From the Body Into /  /  / Spirit. /  / a New Light Upon a Noted / Poetic Production Of /  /  / His Pen.\nCHICAGO, Dec. 1.-The following is published here: To-day it is well known that among the higher scientific circles of England spiritualism made, a few years ago, rapid and remarkable progress. Wallace, the co-discoverer with Darwin of the principles of nat ural selection; Prof. Crookes, the emf Nent chemist, on whom the French academic DIES sciences conferred gold medal with an honorarium of 5000 francs tor his discoveries in molecular physics; SERGEANT Cox. the noted psyehist, and Prof. Huxley, him. self a skeptic of skeptics. were concerned in q series of experiments, chiefly with the medium Home, which attracted wide attentinn at the time. None of the scientists could explain the phenomena produced by the medium, and the report made by Prof. Crookes is held by the professors of the spirit ualiStic belief an overwhelming testimOny to its genuineness. The name of the poet TennySOn has never before been connected with spiritualism. A letter written by him has come into the possession Of the Tribune. which shows that he holds the conviction that conseriousness may pass from the body and hold COMMUNION WITH TIE DEAD. This is essentially spiritualism, but in TennySon'S case, at least so far as the letter indicates, he is his own medium. The statement he makes is curious. The letter is in the poets handwriting. 1t is dated Farringford, Freshwater, dsie of Wight, May, z, 1874. 1t was written to a gentleman who cOmmuniGated to him certain strange expert ences he had when passing from under the effect of an@StheticS. Tennyson writes: 81 have never had any reyelalions through an8sthetics. but kind of waking trance this for lack of a better nance l have frequently had, quite up from boyhood, when l have been all alone. This has often come upon me through repeating my own name to my self, silently, till, at once, as it were, out Of the consciousness Of Individual ity, the individuality itself seemed to dissolve and fade away into boundless being; and this not confused state, but the clearest of the clearest, the sureSt Of the surest, utterly beyond words, where death was an almost aaughable impossibility, the loss of perSonality Of so it Were) seeming 40 eXtlnction, but THE ONLY TRUE LIFE\" AS if conscious of the Incredible sig niticance of the statement just COM pleted, he adds: Il am ashamed of my feeble description. Have L not said the state was utterly beyond words\" This is not a vulgar table tipping spiritualiSm. 1t is the most emphatic declaration that the spirit of The writer is capable of transferring it. self into another existence; it is not only real, clear, simple, but that it is infinite in vision and eternal in duration. For he continues that when he returns to sanity\" he is ready to fight for the truth\" of his experience and that he holds 1.-THE spirit, whose separate existence he thus repeatedly tests-\"will last for icons and eons.\" 1t is pointed out by Prof. Thomas Davidson, who has seen the letter, that the same convicton, If not the same experience, only Withanother. is described in ''In Mem Oriam,\" XCV. The StanZaS are gen orally passed over as referring to a mere poetic frenzy of grief But reading them in the light of the calmly penned prose puts an entirely different aspect Oh the incident contained in the lines: : s. And in the house light after light Went. out and was all alone. A hunger seized my heart; read OF that glad year which once had been In those falln leaves which kept their green, The noble letters of the dead. And strangely on the silence broke The silent-speaking words. and strange Was Lore's dumb cry defying change To te: t his worth, and strangely spoke The faith, the vigor. bold to dwell On doubts that drive the coward back: And keen through wOrdy snares to track Suggestion to her Utmost cell; So word by word and line by line The dead man touched me from the past; And all at once, it seemed, at last His living soul was flashed on mine. And mine III his was bound and whirled About empyreal heights OF thought And came on that which is and caught The deep puIsations Of the world. .H1onian music measuring out The steps of time-the shocks of chance The blows Of death g The idea of the actuality of the unseen is conveyed in the letter in terms which correspond nearly to those in the Thythmic expression. That (which isl is certainly a confirmation of the state t'sureSt of the surest,' and the lasting for Icons upon icons\" finds its counter part in ''feOnian music\" AS TennySon has never been connected in any way with psychic science or spiritualism, the latter, not to mention the now clearer reading OF the poem, will create not a ion \n\n##### 1-048 | 1913-06-27 | The Birmingham age-herald. | terms ['spirit+medium']\nHEADLINE: BULWER's DEAFNESS\nFrom Chronicle and Comment in the July Bookman. If Byron was possessed of an enormous vanity Sir Edward Bulwer-Lytton was quite his equal in that respect. There was a medium oy the name of Home who in tho course of an cventful life held spirit ualistic meetings In the United States, England, and on the continent of Europe; was expelled from Rome as a sorcerer married twice, each time to Russian lady of rank, and published various books. Home was particularly disliked by Charles Dickens, who applied to him all the hard names of which he could think. Mr. Bigelow once remarked to Dickens that Bulwer had faith in the medium. Oh yes\" Dickens replied. but you see Bulwer is deaf and does not like to have II remarked; so Home would say. Do you hear those raps?' ard Bulwer would say, oh yes, heard them perfectly. \n\n##### 1-009 | 1923-02-02 | Evening star. | terms ['spirit_phrase', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: TELLS OF HIS MESSAGES /  /  / FROM THE SPIRIT WORLD /  / Rev. G. Vale Owen Speaks Of a / Faith That Dispels Fear of Death /  /  / and Consoles the Bereaved.\nThe bitterness and sadness Of being parted from loved ones does not exist in the minds of believers in spiritual ism. This declaration was made yes terday afternoon at the National Theater by Rev. G.. Vale Owen, Eng lish clergyman. and co-investigator of the spirit realms along with Sir oliver Lodge, Sir Arthur Conan Doyle and other eminent Englishmen. Rev. Mr.. owen IS still connected with the Church of England. but has for nearly thirty years been strong believer in phychic phenomena. He is conducting a lecture tour through out the United States, and made his only scheduled appearance at the Ma tional yesterday. 1t was noticed that audience present who were clad in deep mourning, and many others IN Semi 1t was to these, who evidenced the recent loss of some loved one. that the clergyman's lee ture seemed most appealing. The lecturer divided his address into the various stages of communication from departed spirits. He explained the knocking, the voice, spirit photography, the materializaand automatic writing. Each one he explained and cited from the Bible 4 similar happening. As an example, he told the story of the child. Sam ueL who awoke one night thinking that he heard his father, Eli. calling. Eli told Samuel that he had not called, but that If the voice came again to say, Lord. here am t. He also cited David drawing the plans of the temple, which was later built by Solomon. These plans were drawn by David, but under the guiding hand of God, the clergyman stated. Mr.. Owen stated that the spirit that has returned to earth in making the man if.a fat rhor i cles or atoms from the air and forms them into the shape of a body, or ar ranges them into a throat from which a voice can be heard. In reality they are a magnetic emanation. he declared. He asserted that he had been in direct communication with hundreds Of persons. including his father and mother, who had passed beyond, and his little daughter. Ruby, who died a few years after birth. He gave graphic instances of their return. and related messages they had brought him. From the messages I have received, | know that every one who has passed from this life is about the same age the lecturer said. The young who die reach maturity, and the old who die again grow young. All infirmities pass away. Death is only short, sweet sleep\" In speaking Of the British soldiers who saw the battalions in the sky at the retreat from the Marne, Rev Mr.. Owen liked the happening to the Israelites. who saw vast armies and chariots in the air fighting for them 'Spiritualism,\" he continued, helps one to bear up under the trials of this life. it dispels the fear of death. and brings happiness to those who have lost some one by death. The trouble with too many persons is that they think their religion is taking them TO heaven. the best.Way We all get there. Just lead Il humble. faith full life. trying to do what good we can. That is all that is necessary Christ has told us: In my ather S house there are many, many man sionS.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"cad9999e-5d98-4ac3-bc25-2ba0019fdf49","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:58:53.509Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 1-003 | 1873-06-15 | New Orleans Republican. [volume] (New Orleans, La) 1867-1878 | terms ['spirit+medium']\nHEADLINE: Heard From.\nOur reporter called on medium in good standing, yesterday, to inquire into the present whereabouts of Pelagie Brown. After striking an attitude and the tab lie in the style usual on such occasions. the \"shade\" manifested its presence. Are you happy!\" inquired the medium. No answer. \"Do you know you are wanted in New OrleansE' Eyes, but can not come\" \"Why not!'' because the lawyers land there are plenty Of them herel say my evidence would be ruled out\" where are you, then!' No answer. ''Do you know anything OF the great con. trcversy now pending ON earth between Jacob Hawkins, plaintiff, and the New Ocleans Picayune Company, defendant?' lExcitedly.! \"'Fore Ged, doesn't know what you're tslkin' about. spec the white folks all done gone crazy Here the medium announced that tho spirit had left in disgust and refused to make any further manifestations. Other spirits were questioned as to the habits of Pelagie, who stated that she has strange fear of courts. and does not, as rule. as. sociate much with the lawyers. by whom she is now surrounded. \n\n##### 1-039 | 1888-12-05 | St. Paul daily globe. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn.) 1884-1896 | terms ['spirit+medium', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: England's Poet Laureate Is a / Spiritualist and His Own /  /  / Medium. /  / In an Autograph Letter He / Admits Possession of a /  /  / Peculiar Power. /  / lie Can Transfer Conscious / ness From the Body Into /  /  / Spirit. /  / a New Light Upon a Noted / Poetic Production Of /  /  / His Pen.\nCHICAGO, Dec. 1.-The following is published here: To-day it is well known that among the higher scientific circles of England spiritualism made, a few years ago, rapid and remarkable progress. Wallace, the co-discoverer with Darwin of the principles of nat ural selection; Prof. Crookes, the emf Nent chemist, on whom the French academic DIES sciences conferred gold medal with an honorarium of 5000 francs tor his discoveries in molecular physics; SERGEANT Cox. the noted psyehist, and Prof. Huxley, him. self a skeptic of skeptics. were concerned in q series of experiments, chiefly with the medium Home, which attracted wide attentinn at the time. None of the scientists could explain the phenomena produced by the medium, and the report made by Prof. Crookes is held by the professors of the spirit ualiStic belief an overwhelming testimOny to its genuineness. The name of the poet TennySOn has never before been connected with spiritualism. A letter written by him has come into the possession Of the Tribune. which shows that he holds the conviction that conseriousness may pass from the body and hold COMMUNION WITH TIE DEAD. This is essentially spiritualism, but in TennySon'S case, at least so far as the letter indicates, he is his own medium. The statement he makes is curious. The letter is in the poets handwriting. 1t is dated Farringford, Freshwater, dsie of Wight, May, z, 1874. 1t was written to a gentleman who cOmmuniGated to him certain strange expert ences