e noble letters of the dead. And strangely on the silence broke The silent-speaking words. and strange Was Lore's dumb cry defying change To te: t his worth, and strangely spoke The faith, the vigor. bold to dwell On doubts that drive the coward back: And keen through wOrdy snares to track Suggestion to her Utmost cell; So word by word and line by line The dead man touched me from the past; And all at once, it seemed, at last His living soul was flashed on mine. And mine III his was bound and whirled About empyreal heights OF thought And came on that which is and caught The deep puIsations Of the world. .H1onian music measuring out The steps of time-the shocks of chance The blows Of death g The idea of the actuality of the unseen is conveyed in the letter in terms which correspond nearly to those in the Thythmic expression. That (which isl is certainly a confirmation of the state t'sureSt of the surest,' and the lasting for Icons upon icons\" finds its counter part in ''feOnian music\" AS TennySon has never been connected in any way with psychic science or spiritualism, the latter, not to mention the now clearer reading OF the poem, will create not a ion \n\n##### 1-048 | 1913-06-27 | The Birmingham age-herald. | terms ['spirit+medium']\nHEADLINE: BULWER's DEAFNESS\nFrom Chronicle and Comment in the July Bookman. If Byron was possessed of an enormous vanity Sir Edward Bulwer-Lytton was quite his equal in that respect. There was a medium oy the name of Home who in tho course of an cventful life held spirit ualistic meetings In the United States, England, and on the continent of Europe; was expelled from Rome as a sorcerer married twice, each time to Russian lady of rank, and published various books. Home was particularly disliked by Charles Dickens, who applied to him all the hard names of which he could think. Mr. Bigelow once remarked to Dickens that Bulwer had faith in the medium. Oh yes\" Dickens replied. but you see Bulwer is deaf and does not like to have II remarked; so Home would say. Do you hear those raps?' ard Bulwer would say, oh yes, heard them perfectly. \n\n##### 1-009 | 1923-02-02 | Evening star. | terms ['spirit_phrase', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: TELLS OF HIS MESSAGES /  /  / FROM THE SPIRIT WORLD /  / Rev. G. Vale Owen Speaks Of a / Faith That Dispels Fear of Death /  /  / and Consoles the Bereaved.\nThe bitterness and sadness Of being parted from loved ones does not exist in the minds of believers in spiritual ism. This declaration was made yes terday afternoon at the National Theater by Rev. G.. Vale Owen, Eng lish clergyman. and co-investigator of the spirit realms along with Sir oliver Lodge, Sir Arthur Conan Doyle and other eminent Englishmen. Rev. Mr.. owen IS still connected with the Church of England. but has for nearly thirty years been strong believer in phychic phenomena. He is conducting a lecture tour through out the United States, and made his only scheduled appearance at the Ma tional yesterday. 1t was noticed that audience present who were clad in deep mourning, and many others IN Semi 1t was to these, who evidenced the recent loss of some loved one. that the clergyman's lee ture seemed most appealing. The lecturer divided his address into the various stages of communication from departed spirits. He explained the knocking, the voice, spirit photography, the materializaand automatic writing. Each one he explained and cited from the Bible 4 similar happening. As an example, he told the story of the child. Sam ueL who awoke one night thinking that he heard his father, Eli. calling. Eli told Samuel that he had not called, but that If the voice came again to say, Lord. here am t. He also cited David drawing the plans of the temple, which was later built by Solomon. These plans were drawn by David, but under the guiding hand of God, the clergyman stated. Mr.. Owen stated that the spirit that has returned to earth in making the man if.a fat rhor i cles or atoms from the air and forms them into the shape of a body, or ar ranges them into a throat from which a voice can be heard. In reality they are a magnetic emanation. he declared. He asserted that he had been in direct communication with hundreds Of persons. including his father and mother, who had passed beyond, and his little daughter. Ruby, who died a few years after birth. He gave graphic instances of their return. and related messages they had brought him. From the messages I have received, | know that every one who has passed from this life is about the same age the lecturer said. The young who die reach maturity, and the old who die again grow young. All infirmities pass away. Death is only short, sweet sleep\" In speaking Of the British soldiers who saw the battalions in the sky at the retreat from the Marne, Rev Mr.. Owen liked the happening to the Israelites. who saw vast armies and chariots in the air fighting for them 'Spiritualism,\" he continued, helps one to bear up under the trials of this life. it dispels the fear of death. and brings happiness to those who have lost some one by death. The trouble with too many persons is that they think their religion is taking them TO heaven. the best.Way We all get there. Just lead Il humble. faith full life. trying to do what good we can. That is all that is necessary Christ has told us: In my ather S house there are many, many man sionS.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ccac72f4-f141-4381-9a18-e827843ee7e3","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":363,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":4},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remot