{"parentUuid":"a8924e46-52e4-4d4c-8453-076cb4d0317a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016y7pgKJYMhHt7zLK5rxRmv","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 4-047 | 1891-08-11 | The Wichita daily eagle. [volume] (Wichita, Kan.) 1890-1906 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: q NEW PROP8FTFCC\npTTTSBCRG, Pa, August l0-Ihe hamlet of Economy, where the remaining mem beIs of the peculiar economic society are spending their last days, is excited over Inc advent and teachings Of a woman VINO says her name is Francisco r. Emmertz. and who claims to be spiritualist Mrs EmmertZ first made her appearance at Economy two years ago, but did not re main long \\ few weeks ago she returned and il Is not denied that she has many converts 90 her views Among these are Trustee DuSs, who has been chosen 10 gov ern the community after the death of Jacob Renrlchi, the present chief trustee. Mr. HenrichI, who i over 80 years old. and who Is finely educated man. Is also said 10 be completely under tue influence OF the woman. although he does not admit this. IL is not de MED. however. that lac received her as an houored guest az the Great House\" where the officers of the society live, aud that he has allowed her 10 deliver addresses III he Economic meeting house where no stranger is ever known To have been admitted before. Mrs Emmertz left Economy ou FRIDAY, TO be absent week. and the news of her work ia the COM munity was not brought to Pittsburgh until yesterday. Trustee Du>s talks voubly concerning her, however He says she il 40 years old, % woman of wealth and broad culture and q resident OI New York city, although she has spent COM paratiVely lltJle time there IN recent wears lie does not pretend Ia base this IuformaZION on anything accept what the woman has told him. Ee accepts her t-achinz~ which lSBore the idea OF punishment after death for Siu, and assert belief Iu the sec Oud coming OF Christ. Oj her views i'm material things. as accepted by Mr. DGss. this = a fair sample. We are told be said. \"thlt God is everywhere We are told that the ear. IS round and THAI we live On the outSida Now, imagine a God who can iiil this IL limitable space. The idea - absurd We know that the earth l. round. that has been conclusively proven: ont it has NOT been proven that We hive GA the Our side hy any means The fact Is that the eaTth is a hollow bail 2uu we live Oh the inside Computing scientifically. the crust Is 10 miles in thickness. On the TOD or outside part we find the different formations are all mixed up bat on going deeper every thine is in exact layers. The sun is in tie center. Lines of force are continually rushing from the earth 70 the center, they fuse there aud form our sun, which has q dark side. Lines rush back and are met by other forces, and the meeting OF these form our stars. Just on tue same principle As electric light is caused. Each one Of Us has his own particular star. There are no tJanets. AS the old system tells rs; simply these points Of light. We have proof from the Testament for this belief IN \"a parJicuLar star for each of Us. Did not Christ have his starr We can alp prove from the Bible that we live within tue earth and not On the outside. \n\n##### 4-043 | 1908-10-29 | The Bamberg herald. [volume] (Bamberg, S.C.) 1891-1972 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: How the Russian Throne Got its Heir\nA curious light on superstition IN high places is related in the Woman's S Home Companion for November. In this issue Kellogg Durland, writing tof the Tsaritsa of All the Russlas' tells the following: = \"When three children had been born, and no heir, the Tsaritsa turn ed an open ear to various men who claimed supernatural power and controll. Before the birth of Anastasle the aid of eminent medical and sclentific men had been sought to lnf1uence the sex of the child, but all to no avail. And so, when science fall ed, religion was appealed to. sHThe example of their trust IN miracle working which history has been called upon to remember, is that of the dead and buried priest who was supposed to have finally brought them a. son, and who was eanonized by the Emperors The day of St. Seraphim is now one OF the hottest of days in Russia and is celebrated throughout the empire. ,\"Seraphim was an old priest of Saroff in the interior of Russia, who according to local tradition. was a kind of sooth-sayer and local medlcine man. His particular power is said to have beer in endowing bar >ren women with children. ttSeraphim had been dead seventy years, but the Tsar was now anxious to leave no stone unturned to pro cure a son, and encouraged by the Tsaritsa, Seraphim was canonized: \"When the remains Of the old priest was unearthed it was found that the body was badly decomposed and to canonize a man whose Dooy Yields to the influence Of decomposlton is contrary to the traditions and cnstoms of the church. The Orthodox Bishop Dmitry of Tambof ven: tured to call attention to this and protest the canonization to the Tsar, hut for his temerity he was deprived of his see and exiled to Vlatka Nicholas held the preservation OF bones, hair and teeth sufficient quail fication for saintship. $50 Seraphim was canonlzed Wltn great pomp and ritualistic solemnlty and the next child born to the Tsar' lisa was a son! If anything were needed to fasten the belief of the Tsar and Tsaritsa in these extreme forms of religion, it was an answer to their prayers at this time. The boy was called Alexis, and he was born on July 80, 1904, according te the Russian calendar, and ever since the Tsar and Tsaritsa have beer given more and more to the pursul of spiritualistic religion.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c83d2482-a933-4901-b499-bbd02852c1f6","timestamp":"2026-10-30T16:59:57.760Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 4-047 | 1891-08-11 | The Wichita daily eagle. [volume] (Wichita, Kan.) 1890-1906 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: q