ef":"fc0fdb49-8e3a-48de-8469-c6eeed72f76e","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"945fc0fd-867f-4630-b756-9c977a1869b0","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:00:09.737Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"945fc0fd-867f-4630-b756-9c977a1869b0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LacvAKMNJPTDDMxEyd9N1E","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 2-043 | 1857-04-02 | The Carolina Spartan. [volume] (Spartanburg, S.C.) 1852-1896 | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: \nEven OH the bed of death, and with the full knowledge that he never should quit j, alive, Antoine Allut, Notwithstanding my urgent entreates, would not tell me by what means he obtained information of those facts in his narrative of which he was not personally cognizant, or who had told him of The crimes and fortune Of Picaud. Only one hour before his death he said to me-'Won Iere. no mans faith can be more lively than mine. for have seen and spoken with soul separated from its body when he said this, there was nothing to indicate that he was suffering under de lIrium. He appeared 10 be simply making a confession Of his faith, and TO lie in the full possession of his mental faculties\" The letter terminated with few words improving the occasion, aud tie usual com. pllmen;s; but it was said that the sagaciou. preiet, albeit sufficiently good Catholic, dropped few words sicnificative of his thought that Allut might have picked up the stirring events that marked Picaud' 5 m'stortunes and crimes without the aid of \"A disembodied spirit. \n\n##### 2-020 | 1880-09-18 | Louisiana capitolian. | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: A HIDDEN CRIME. /  / A MURDERER DYING IN THE WILDS OF / ARKANSAS ON THE Eve OF /  /  / DETECTION.\nSpecial Correspondence GlODe-Dem0crat.l PocAHoNrAs, Ark, Sept 1.-This county (Raudolph) is very thinly settled and it is not a rare thing to find families living ten or twelves apart, and who see no human faces except the members of their own family circle FROM one months end to another Hence ever and anon many thrilling events occur in these isolated regions that never reach the out. side world. One of these strange and sim gular life histories has recently been reported to your correspondent, together with facts which form 9 feast of horror, and would afford material for . dime novel of the most blood-curdling character. We shall give these briefly: Three years ago a man and his wife settled on piece of land in q desolate stretch of country some eighteen or twenty miles from here. He said he came from Mont gomery county, No., and that his name was Thos. Graham. Living in little cabin, tendinga patch of corn and cot. ton, he neither sought ror declined COM panionship, and SO the few neighbors around left him to himself. Thus affairs went on nearly two years, at the end of which time no one knew anything more about Graham than when he first located in the neighborhood Silently he went about his work he asked no questions, he answered none if be could help it, and, in short, WAS A LIVING ENIGMA to bis neighbors, who by this time had begun to feel deep interest in him. His wife was Spiritualist-a medium-and upon any other subject of but Spiritual ism would not or could not converse. Presently it was limited, then said boldly, that Graham h 4 murdered man in Missouri, and that there was big re. ward offered for his capture. The greed of gain is strong everywhere and it found no exception ia this isolated sec. tion. Three of his neighbors, arming themselves with double barreled SHOT, guns, laid plan to take him, and wen, to the cabin, ostensibly to invite him to hunt deer with them, but in reality in tending to seize him whenever a. good opportunity presented itself. They put their plan into effect ten days since Quietly surrounding the hut, they callet for Graham to come out. His wife open ed the door. She took in the situation a, a glance, and her sallow features Lighted up with strange smile as she bockoned the men toward her. When they som' what reluctantly drew nearer she Yer! significantly remarked. \"Men, yer tot later' Asked for an explanation, sh' pointed over her shoulder, and peerint into the house the men discovered Ora ham stretched npon bed, apparentl! asleep. Acloser examination disclosed the fact that he was dead. He had evi IDENTIFY been ACORPSE FOR MANY HOURS. , The woman related the manner of hi! death in a matter-ot-fact way. She sai' he had for years been troubled will , heart disease, and that on the nigh previous, while pulling off his boots, HI had suddenly dropped oD the floor. Sh' lifted him up, but found life extinct \n\n##### 2-001 | 1902-11-24 | The evening times. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1895-1902 | terms ['seance']\nHEADLINE: Suspense ar a SeaHce.\nHEN matters get personal in village there's always Wv considerable unpleasantness and excitement. and this the during second sight seance\" by Mrs Vail the hypnotic subject OF her husband Old Bridge n j. the other night You know how the people OF small be wrought up\" by a touch the terrible and the don't you? Wen. that the way Old dge folk they asked the hypnotzed about robbery and murder mystery that puzzling years Upon which Mrs told the stll living village murderer among the audience-and to give names. awoke her \n\n##### 2-013 | 1903-07-09 | Rosebud County news. [volume] (Forsyth, Mont.) 1901-1906 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nf.. h. Etland, the big Norwegian contined in the Carbon county Jail for the shooting of Edward Trusdale near Rridger, claims to be a spiritualist. He says he can \"hear but can't see 1t was evidently an evil spirit he heard about the time of the shooting. \n\n##### 2-030 | 1923-10-20 | The Washington times. | terms ['seance']\nHEADLINE: \nAmong the spirit convives is Eschylus who, when caught repeating as his own verses given by Shakespeare as q preceding seance, nonchalantly replied: Shakespeare and /, we are col laborators! Evidently they are quick at a turn, these spirits. Most curious Of all, however, are what we might call spirits Of the abstract, which II they are ever embodied must divide them selves into millions of individual sparks. For instance, the spirit Of \"la Critique\" (criticism), and that of \"I'Idee\" Qdea or thought >, take their turns at cryptic writ ing, but they seem to be singularly lacking in both ideas and judgment. M. Souday in his review relieves Victor Hugo of all responsibility for the utterances col lected, and throws 1t upon the shoulders Of the poets son, Charles Hugo, whose presence at the table seems to have been in dispensable to it's effective work ing. OF him M. Souday says. He was in a literary sense very capable of acting as parlor pytho. ness and Of initiating his fathers manner of writing\" One is glad to be assured that Hugo used to point out the contradictions and utilities of the communications and that he often left the room before the seance was finished. Twenty years afterward, says M. Souday, when questioned by r. Lesclide, the poet replied: \"Croyez-en ce que vous voudrez.\" cBelieve as much as you like of it.'O It makes in teresting reading, but IL the spiritists really think that it is a document calculated to aid them, ! comniserate their judgment.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8c4e6a9d-5ac9-4215-9f95-c640b5a3e2c1","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:00:12.113Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 2-043 | 1857-04-02 | The Carolina Spartan. [volume] (Spartanburg, S.C.) 1852-1896 | terms ['spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: \nEven OH the bed of death, and with the full knowledge that he never should quit j, alive, Antoine Allut, Notwithstanding my urgent entreates, would not tell me by what means he obtained information of those facts in his narrative of which he was not personally cognizant, or who had told him of The crimes and fortune Of Picaud. Only one hour before his death he said to me-'Won Iere. no mans faith can be more lively than mine. for have seen and spoken with soul separated from its body when he said this, there was nothing to indicate that he was suffering under de lIrium. He appeared 10 be simply making a confession Of his faith, and TO lie in the full possession of his mental faculties\" The letter terminated with few words improving the occasion, aud tie usual com. pllmen;s; but it was said that the sagaciou. preiet, albeit sufficiently good Catholic, dropped few words sicnificative of his thought that Allut might have picked up the stirring events that marked Picaud' 5 m'stortunes and crimes without the aid of \"A disembodied spirit. \n\n##### 2-020 | 1880-09-18 | Louisiana capitolian. | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: A HIDDEN CRIME. /  / A MURDERER DYING IN THE WILDS OF / ARKANSAS ON THE Eve OF /  /  / DETECTION.\nSpecial Correspondence GlODe-Dem0crat.l PocAHoNrAs, Ark, Sept 1.-This county (Raudolph) is very thinly settled and it is not a rare thing to find families living ten or twelves apart, and who see no human faces except the members of their own family circle FROM one months end to another Hence ever and anon many thrilling events occur in these isolated regions that never reach the out. side world. One of these strange and sim gular life histories has recently been reported to your correspondent, together with facts which form 9 feast of horror, and would afford material for . dime novel of the most blood-curdling character. We shall give these briefly: Three years ago a man and his wife settled on piece of land in q desolate stretch of country some eighteen or twenty miles from here. He said he came from Mont gomery county, No., and that his name was Thos. Graham. Living in little cabin, tendinga patch of corn and cot. ton, he neither sought ror declined COM panionship, and SO the few neighbors around left him to himself. Thus affairs went on nearly two years, at the end of which time no one knew anything more about Graham than when he first located in the neighborhood Silently he went about his work he asked no questions, he answered none if be could help it, and, in short, WAS A LIVING ENIGMA to bis neighbors, who by this time had begun to feel deep interest in him. His wife was Spiritualist-a medium-and upon any other subject of but Spiritual ism would not or could not converse. Presently it was limited, then said boldly, that Graham h 4 murdered man in Missouri, and that there was big re. ward offered for his capture. The greed of gain is strong everywhere and it found no exception ia this isolated sec. tion. Three of his neighbors, arming themselves with double barreled SHOT, guns, laid plan to take him, and wen, to the cabin, ostensibly to invite him to hunt deer with them, but in reality in tending to seize him whenever a. good opportunity presented itself. They put their plan into effect ten days since Quietly surrounding the hut, they callet for Graham to come out. His wife open ed the door. She took in the situation a, a glance, and her sallow features Lighted up with strange smile as she bockoned the men toward her. When they som' what reluctantly drew nearer she Yer! significantly remarked. \"Men, yer tot later' Asked for an explanation, sh' pointed over her shoulder, and peerint into the house the men discovered Ora ham stretched npon bed, apparentl! asleep. Acloser examination disclosed the fact that he was dead. He had evi IDENTIFY been ACORPSE FOR MANY HOURS. , The woman related the manner of hi! death in a matter-ot-fact way. She sai' he had for years been troubled will , heart disease, and that on the nigh previous, while pulling off his boots, HI had suddenly dropped oD the floor. Sh' lifted him up, but found life extinct \n\n##### 2-001 | 1902-11-24 | The evening times. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1895-1902 | terms ['seance']\nHEADLINE: Suspense ar a SeaHce.\nHEN matters get personal in village there's always Wv considerable unpleasantness and excitement. and this the during second sight seance\" by Mrs Vail the hypnotic subject OF her husband Old Bridge n j. the other night You know how the people OF small be wrought up\" by a touch the terrible and the don't you? Wen. that the way Old dge folk they asked the hypnotzed about robbery and murder mystery that puzzling years Upon which Mrs told the stll living village murderer among the audience-and to give names. awoke her \n\n##### 2-013 | 1903-07-09 | Rosebud County news. [volume] (Forsyth, Mont.) 1901-1906 | terms ['spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: \nf.. h. Etland, the big Norwegian contined in the Carbon county Jail for the shooting of Edward Trusdale near Rridger, claims to be a spiritualist. He says he can \"hear but can't see 1t was evidently an evil spirit he heard about the time of the shooting. \n\n##### 2-030 | 1923-10-20 | The Washington times. | terms ['seance']\nHEADLINE: \nAmong the spirit convives is Eschylus who, when caught repeating as his own verses given by Shakespeare as q preceding seance, nonchalantly replied: Shakespeare and /, we are col laborators! Evidently they are quick at a turn, these spirits. Most curious Of all, however, are what we might call spirits Of the abstract, which II they are ever embodied must divide them selves into millions of individual sparks. For instance, the spirit Of \"la Critique\" (criticism), and that of \"I'Idee\" Qdea or thought >, take their turns at cryptic writ ing, but they seem to be singularly lacking in both ideas and judgment. M. Souday in his review relieves Victor Hugo of all responsibility for the utterances col lected, and throws 1t upon the shoulders Of the poets son, Charles Hugo, whose presence at the table seems to have been in dispensable to it's effective work ing. OF him M. Souday says. He was in a literary sense very capable of acting as parlor pytho. ness and Of initiating his fathers manner of writing\" One is glad to be assured that Hugo used to point out the contradictions and utilities of the communications and that he often left the room before the seance was finished. Twenty years afterward, says M. Souday, when questioned by r. Lesclide, the poet replied: \"Croyez-en ce que vous voudrez.\" cBelieve as much as you like of it.'O It makes in teresting reading, but IL the spiritists really think that it is a document calculated to aid them, ! comniserate their judgment.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"13bba162-02e4-48e3-9970-1a55fb3793a7","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":363,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":4},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"945fc0fd-867f-4630-b756-9c977a1869b0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
