{"parentUuid":"b035b0e3-9d81-4bf3-9223-f54d139945d3","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014t6o6uX3MyER9sMWZ3ZfZq","type":"tool_result","content":"##### 2-035 | 1857-07-29 | The Middlebury register. [volume] (Middlebury, Vt.) 1850-1882 | terms ['spirit+medium']\nHEADLINE: Home Items.\nof the delusive nature Of the weather, large amount was cut, (gen orally Of the best quality and is all damaged more or less, On Saturday, fields Of from five to fifteen acres, in this condition were to be seen in every direction. We have never before known SO much hay damaged at one time. MlppLEsuRY COLLEGE Commence. ment will occur the present year, on Wednesday August 18th. On Tuesday P. M .orations will be delivered before the College Societies by Rev. DR.. Cheever, and G. M. Curtis Esq, of New York. The usual address before the Associated Alumni by Rev Dr. Hall Of Auburn N. Y, and poem by Baxter Perry Esq, of Boston, on We'd nesday P. NJ. We are glad to learn that the Super. indendant of tho R. & B. Rail Road will allow persons attending Commence ment to 60 and come for fare one way. Tickets will be good from Tuesday till rh\".a~y ef that WOOL. We learn that scarlet fever prevails extensively in West Cornwall. SPIRITUAL ENTHUSIASTICALLY 10 notice posted up in various cor ners about town, Union Hall was the receptacle of spirit by the name Of Judd, whose outer tabernacle was one rather thin.faced, whiskersd, moustached nervous individual from town in Mass., by the name of Fairfield. After paying ten cents to the tabernacle who sat OF the door, we were admitted-took q seat of which We had our choice, There being about twenty persons presently. We looked about to sec who was to be favor. ed by the spirit aforesaid. Among the crowd we noticed the Chief Engineer of arm wri ting, and tho soft-shelled. dish. water female preacher and promulga,or Of spiritual manifestations generally The balance Of the audience was made up Of men who wanted to see what the fellow could do or say, aud shrewd young fellows too wise to get their feet into any such trap. Soon the tabernacle commissioned another honest looking person to receive the ten cent pieces\" to defray expenses and he took bis seat 10 front of the \"mass Of mind present. The tabernacle shut his eyes, aud very soon slight witches Of the hands might be clearly seen with the \"naked eye\" Soon the tabernacle arose and presented itself be. fore tho assemblage, and the spirit broke loose and commenced to utter words loud enough to be heard by all present. We shall not attempt TO give the spirits speech entire. but will give an outline of the summing up of the whole in few words He said that the Mosaic dispensation was dispensa.ioD Of war violence and force, and that no person could read the history as given in the bible, without coming to the con. clusiop that God had nothing to DO with the transactions of those days. He said Moses was Spiritual Medium, and was most certingly far in advance of his age. That tho next grand era was tho usher. ing in Of Christh dispensatiop-that Jesus was also Q medium, and this era was far in advance of that of Noses That \"Old John\" was very excellent medium, but after all tho present and and Sd grand era was far 10 advance Of all former dispensations-t that Jesus had commissioned mediums that was to fol. low him TO raise the dead, heal the sick and do woudsrous things, aud that this spirit Of Jaderism was prosecuting tho mediums now as in the days Of Christ that the dispensation Of Nose. was that of\"War voilence and force\" He said this was q time long to be remsm bsred on account Of the wonderful Onfolding that were daily coming to light. That Moses dispepsation was . dispensation of \" War, violence aud force.n He said there were great many things be should like to say, yet the tabernacle was weary. yet if there were sufficient nuAber present that would like to hear what God was, and where he was, what the devil was and where he was, where hell and heaven were situated, he would speak to them at the same place on Sunday at two O'Clock. The au. dience manifested their desire to hear him again by pnanimoasly set. ting still and saying nothing, and he con. cluded to speak at his friends house u at the came hour, which we suppose he did from certain sounds not unlike an \n\n##### 2-048 | 1922-05-02 | The Washington times. | terms ['ouija', 'spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: A. ACCOMMODATE . /  /  / R|D|CULED BY / . dc. SCIENTIST /  / DR.. C. E. Seashore Calls All His / Spirit Proofs Result of /  /  / Mere Trickery.\nOF the National Research Council and member OF the National Academy of Sciences, today character lied Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's proofs OF spirits communication as ..the result of trickery\" and ''crass evidence which is, certain to be exposed\" DIR. Seashore, one OF the foremost psychologists OF the United States, attended Sir Arthur's lectuye here Sunday night, and after seeing his spirit photographs\" and hearing his statements, declared that the mentality that offered them to the public is different\" from that which created Sherlock Holmes. He asserted that every type of evidence brought forth by the Brit ish author had been Investigated and exposed. and said that the rec. lures are having dangerous ef feet on simple-minded people. When asked to give his impressions of Sir ATthur's message. DIR. Seashore authorized the following statement: Scientists Puzzled. 'The psychologist listening to Sir Arthur Conan Doyle is naturally puzzled in the effort to explain the man. How can a man Of his ex. perience maintain the stand that he does in this lecture? This queston is difficult to answer. The mentality exhibited is certainly diff ferent from that which produced | Sherlock Holmes. The impression is rather painful \"A scientific demonstration of spirit communication other than Through the channels OF sense would be Of more interest and value | than any discovery OF material sci ence. But those who are best quail fled to judge the evidence have | found no conclusive evidence of such communication. MANY assert that they have such | evidence, but II is in a field sat urated with trickery, and the type of proof employed does not COM mend itself to the scientific thinker. Every type of evidence which | Sir Arthur brings forth has been investigated and exposed as either the result OF trlckery. as in trick photography and sleight of hand, or as normal, though little known wonderful. but natural manifesto tions of the human mind. ITO the latter class belong auto matte writing, whether II takes the form Of writing with the pencil, | through the Ouija board or plan chette, in table tipping, or in the latest fad by means OF the type writer. \"These may be genuine phenomena, revealing many wonderful powers of the human mind. The same Is true OF illusions and hallucinatiofs, the wonderful realization of which has scarcely any bounds. Common Fallacies. \"One of the commonest fallacics in this field is the assumption that II the observer cannot see how it is done by natural means. then II is done by spiritual action. THE impression that Sir Arthur leaves upon the audience is one which is very dangerous, because he speaks on a subject in which the audience, as a rule. is not qualified TO Judge. Those who believe in spirits find comfort in their belief, and those who do not, find no evidence. These doctrines are very danger. Gus to serious, often religious but simple-minded people who base their religious faith on such Grass evil dence which is certain to be exposed\" DR.. Seashore's statement Is the first denunciation of Sir Arthur's campaign in this country which has come from an official source, al though the psychologist said that he spoke only for himself and not fer either the National Research Council, which is a Government institution, or the National Academy Of Sciences. the foremost scientific organization in the United States. His attitude, however, is general ly held by his colleagues In both the council and the academy. DR.. G. c. Abbot, astrophysical eIpert Of Smithsonian Institution and home secretary OF the National Academy OF Sciences. said today that Doyle's \"ectoplasm,\" which he de scribes as the link between material and spiritual bodies, has no scientific standing before the academy. Sir Arthur left Washington today for Philadelphia. \n\n##### 2-027 | 1922-04-14 | The Bismarck tribune. | terms ['seance', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: CONAN DOYLE, / SPIRITUALIST. /  /  / TO RAID US. /  / Distinguished Author, on Step / ping Ashore, Declared He Had /  /  / Learned The Truth /  / TO TOUR LARGE CITIES\nConan Doyle, creator of the material istic Sherlock Holmes, but now sin cere believer in things spiritual, ar rived here last night to raid\" AM erica. II propose to make raid on Amer ican skepticism,\" he said in explain ing the purpose of his proposed. lee .ture tour. Il propose to raid church and laty alike\" Stepping on American shores for the first time in seven years, the dis tinguished author declared he had Learned the truth\" Spirtualism today, said Sir Arthur, is nothing but religion. 1t is a great er religion than anything we have ever known. Fifty years from today this world is going to be a spiritual world in which leaders of thought are going to laugh at our puny at tempts to fathom the future. Says Dead Are Happier. \"Spiritualism teaches a definite knowledge of the life after so-called death. 1t teaches us not to fear death and that the passing of heart beats is merely promotion. ''You see, a so-called dead man goes to happier plane, there is no crime, no sordidness and it is many, many times happier. You always have a difficult task proving to a man on that plane that he, not you, is really dead. \"But suppose man passes who has been something of an unsavory individual here. Does he go to hell? No, he goes to a sort of hospital. That is gray and very unhappy, unpleasant sort of place. He must remain there, however, until his own voluntary acts show him fit for the other plane. 1t is simple and beau tiful But it Is not materialistic.\" That is what Sir Arthur is going to teach here. His lecture tour, or raid, is not for money, he claims, Whatever he gets, goes to the cause, although he admits he probably will take it away from the United States because we \"already have all the money in the world: Sir Arthur declared that the Church OF England is adopting most of the tenets of spiritualism, although it cannot admit 1t. He said churchmen the world over are leaning that way in their services, because there is nothing else they can do and be truthful\" SCOTS at Mediums. Sir Arthur does not believe in So called mediums who use silver or glass globes, and cards of various descriptions, weirdly moving tables characterizing such things as fakes\" spiritualism isn't that Spiritualism, he explained, is the truth of philosophy and religion COM bined which cannot be escaped. II have many times spoken with my SON, Kingsley Conan, Doyle\" he continued, \"but that is, not strange I wanted to talk to him, he wanted to talk to me. We talked. Kingsley isn't dead-and it is, interesting to note that from such seances the truth about the after life is becoming more and more apparent \"Simplified sRiritualism is merely a great white truth of the Bible and religion. It is pristine purity OF thought and action combined. There is nothing mysterious about it. It Is clear and open. All you have to do is to think of a life after death, a life of enviable beauty-and realize that there can be no fear Of death because with spiritualism you know exactly what is going to happen to you\" sWill Vlyify Religion\" \"F'ar from being antagonistic to religion, this psyhic movement is destined to vivify religion, which has long been decaying and becoming a mere formality. This new knowledge makes it real and sure. and enables one for the first time to understand the actions and views Of the early Christians and of their great founder Without this psyhic knowledge, much of the new testament is incomprelensible. With it one has renewed as surance of its essential truth. 1T unites real science and real religion, each supporting the other\" Sir Arthur and his family will be in America three months. They will visit Philadelphia, Boston, Washington, St. Louis, Chicago, and possibly other cities Sir Arthur does not intend to seek to organize spiritualism In this country, he said, but merely to explain","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8107a769-cf73-4e30-93b9-507660bb02e8","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:00:38.630Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### 2-035 | 1857-07-29 | The Middlebury register. [volume] (Middlebury, Vt.) 1850-1882 | terms ['spirit+medium']\nHEADLINE: Home Items.\nof the delusive nature Of the weather, large amount was cut, (gen orally Of the best quality and is all damaged more or less, On Saturday, fields Of from five to fifteen acres, in this condition were to be seen in every direction. We have never before known SO much hay damaged at one time. MlppLEsuRY COLLEGE Commence. ment will occur the present year, on Wednesday August 18th. On Tuesday P. M .orations will be delivered before the College Societies by Rev. DR.. Cheever, and G. M. Curtis Esq, of New York. The usual address before the Associated Alumni by Rev Dr. Hall Of Auburn N. Y, and poem by Baxter Perry Esq, of Boston, on We'd nesday P. NJ. We are glad to learn that the Super. indendant of tho R. & B. Rail Road will allow persons attending Commence ment to 60 and come for fare one way. Tickets will be good from Tuesday till rh\".a~y ef that WOOL. We learn that scarlet fever prevails extensively in West Cornwall. SPIRITUAL ENTHUSIASTICALLY 10 notice posted up in various cor ners about town, Union Hall was the receptacle of spirit by the name Of Judd, whose outer tabernacle was one rather thin.faced, whiskersd, moustached nervous individual from town in Mass., by the name of Fairfield. After paying ten cents to the tabernacle who sat OF the door, we were admitted-took q seat of which We had our choice, There being about twenty persons presently. We looked about to sec who was to be favor. ed by the spirit aforesaid. Among the crowd we noticed the Chief Engineer of arm wri ting, and tho soft-shelled. dish. water female preacher and promulga,or Of spiritual manifestations generally The balance Of the audience was made up Of men who wanted to see what the fellow could do or say, aud shrewd young fellows too wise to get their feet into any such trap. Soon the tabernacle commissioned another honest looking person to receive the ten cent pieces\" to defray expenses and he took bis seat 10 front of the \"mass Of mind present. The tabernacle shut his eyes, aud very soon slight witches Of the hands might be clearly seen with the \"naked eye\" Soon the tabernacle arose and presented itself be. fore tho assemblage, and the spirit broke loose and commenced to utter words loud enough to be heard by all present. We shall not attempt TO give the spirits speech entire. but will give an outline of the summing up of the whole in few words He said that the Mosaic dispensation was dispensa.ioD Of war violence and force, and that no person could read the history as given in the bible, without coming to the con. clusiop that God had nothing to DO with the transactions of those days. He said Moses was Spiritual Medium, and was most certingly far in advance of his age. That tho next grand era was tho usher. ing in Of Christh dispensatiop-that Jesus was also Q medium, and this era was far in advance of that of Noses That \"Old John\" was very excellent medium, but after all tho present and and Sd grand era was far 10 advance Of all former dispensations-t that Jesus had commissioned mediums that was to fol. low him TO raise the dead, heal the sick and do woudsrous things, aud that this spirit Of Jaderism was prosecuting tho mediums now as in the days Of Christ that the dispensation Of Nose. was that of\"War voilence and force\" He said this was q time long to be remsm bsred on account Of the wonderful Onfolding that were daily coming to light. That Moses dispepsation was . dispensation of \" War, violence aud force.n He said there were great many things be should like to say, yet the tabernacle was weary. yet if there were sufficient nuAber present that would like to hear what God was, and where he was, what the devil was and where he was, where hell and heaven were situated, he would speak to them at the same place on Sunday at two O'Clock. The au. dience manifested their desire to hear him again by pnanimoasly set. ting still and saying nothing, and he con. cluded to speak at his friends house u at the came hour, which we suppose he did from certain sounds not unlike an \n\n##### 2-048 | 1922-05-02 | The Washington times. | terms ['ouija', 'spirit_phrase']\nHEADLINE: A. ACCOMMODATE . /  /  / R|D|CULED BY / . dc. SCIENTIST /  / DR.. C. E. Seashore Calls All His / Spirit Proofs Result of /  /  / Mere Trickery.\nOF the National Research Council and member OF the National Academy of Sciences, today character lied Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's proofs OF spirits communication as ..the result of trickery\" and ''crass evidence which is, certain to be exposed\" DIR. Seashore, one OF the foremost psychologists OF the United States, attended Sir Arthur's lectuye here Sunday night, and after seeing his spirit photographs\" and hearing his statements, declared that the mentality that offered them to the public is different\" from that which created Sherlock Holmes. He asserted that every type of evidence brought forth by the Brit ish author had been Investigated and exposed. and said that the rec. lures are having dangerous ef feet on simple-minded people. When asked to give his impressions of Sir ATthur's message. DIR. Seashore authorized the following statement: Scientists Puzzled. 'The psychologist listening to Sir Arthur Conan Doyle is naturally puzzled in the effort to explain the man. How can a man Of his ex. perience maintain the stand that he does in this lecture? This queston is difficult to answer. The mentality exhibited is certainly diff ferent from that which produced | Sherlock Holmes. The impression is rather painful \"A scientific demonstration of spirit communication other than Through the channels OF sense would be Of more interest and value | than any discovery OF material sci ence. But those who are best quail fled to judge the evidence have | found no conclusive evidence of such communication. MANY assert that they have such | evidence, but II is in a field sat urated with trickery, and the type of proof employed does not COM mend itself to the scientific thinker. Every type of evidence which | Sir Arthur brings forth has been investigated and exposed as either the result OF trlckery. as in trick photography and sleight of hand, or as normal, though little known wonderful. but natural manifesto tions of the human mind. ITO the latter class belong auto matte writing, whether II takes the form Of writing with the pencil, | through the Ouija board or plan chette, in table tipping, or in the latest fad by means OF the type writer. \"These may be genuine phenomena, revealing many wonderful powers of the human mind. The same Is true OF illusions and hallucinatiofs, the wonderful realization of which has scarcely any bounds. Common Fallacies. \"One of the commonest fallacics in this field is the assumption that II the observer cannot see how it is done by natural means. then II is done by spiritual action. THE impression that Sir Arthur leaves upon the audience is one which is very dangerous, because he speaks on a subject in which the audience, as a rule. is not qualified TO Judge. Those who believe in spirits find comfort in their belief, and those who do not, find no evidence. These doctrines are very danger. Gus to serious, often religious but simple-minded people who base their religious faith on such Grass evil dence which is certain to be exposed\" DR.. Seashore's statement Is the first denunciation of Sir Arthur's campaign in this country which has come from an official source, al though the psychologist said that he spoke only for himself and not fer either the National Research Council, which is a Government institution, or the National Academy Of Sciences. the foremost scientific organization in the United States. His attitude, however, is general ly held by his colleagues In both the council and the academy. DR.. G. c. Abbot, astrophysical eIpert Of Smithsonian Institution and home secretary OF the National Academy OF Sciences. said today that Doyle's \"ectoplasm,\" which he de scribes as the link between material and spiritual bodies, has no scientific standing before the academy. Sir Arthur left Washington today for Philadelphia. \n\n##### 2-027 | 1922-04-14 | The Bismarck tribune. | terms ['seance', 'spiritualism']\nHEADLINE: CONAN DOYLE, / SPIRITUALIST. /  /  / TO RAID US. /  / Distinguished Author, on Step / ping Ashore, Declared He Had /  /  / Learned The Truth /  / TO TOUR LARGE CITIES\nConan Doyle, creator of the material istic Sherlock Holmes, but now sin cere believer in things spiritual, ar rived here last night to raid\" AM erica. II propose to make raid on Amer ican skepticism,\" he said in explain ing the purpose of his proposed. lee .ture tour. Il propose to raid church and laty alike\" Stepping on American shores for the first time in seven years, the dis tinguished author declared he had Learned the truth\" Spirtualism today, said Sir Arthur, is nothing but religion. 1t is a great er religion than anything we have ever known. Fifty years from today this world is going to be a spiritual world in which leaders of thought are going to laugh at our puny at tempts to fathom the future. Says Dead Are Happier. \"Spiritualism teaches a definite knowledge of the life after so-called death. 1t teaches us not to fear death and that the passing of heart beats is merely promotion. ''You see, a so-called dead man goes to happier plane, there is no crime, no sordidness and it is many, many times happier. You always have a difficult task proving to a man on that plane that he, not you, is really dead. \"But suppose man passes who has been something of an unsavory individual here. Does he go to hell? No, he goes to a sort of hospital. That is gray and very unhappy, unpleasant sort of place. He must remain there, however, until his own voluntary acts show him fit for the other plane. 1t is simple and beau tiful But it Is not materialistic.\" That is what Sir Arthur is going to teach here. His lecture tour, or raid, is not for money, he claims, Whatever he gets, goes to the cause, although he admits he probably will take it away from the United States because we \"already have all the money in the world: Sir Arthur declared that the Church OF England is adopting most of the tenets of spiritualism, although it cannot admit 1t. He said churchmen the world over are leaning that way in their services, because there is nothing else they can do and be truthful\" SCOTS at Mediums. Sir Arthur does not believe in So called mediums who use silver or glass globes, and cards of various descriptions, weirdly moving tables characterizing such things as fakes\" spiritualism isn't that Spiritualism, he explained, is the truth of philosophy and religion COM bined which cannot be escaped. II have many times spoken with my SON, Kingsley Conan, Doyle\" he continued, \"but that is, not strange I wanted to talk to him, he wanted to talk to me. We talked. Kingsley isn't dead-and it is, interesting to note that from such seances the truth about the after life is becoming more and more apparent \"Simplified sRiritualism is merely a great white truth of the Bible and religion. It is pristine purity OF thought and action combined. There is nothing mysterious about it. It Is clear and open. All you have to do is to think of a life after death, a life of enviable beauty-and realize that there can be no fear Of death because with spiritualism you know exactly what is going to happen to you\" sWill Vlyify Religion\" \"F'ar from being antagonistic to religion, this psyhic movement is destined to vivify religion, which has long been decaying and becoming a mere formality. This new knowledge makes it real and sure. and enables one for the first time to understand the actions and views Of the early Christians and of their great founder Without this psyhic knowledge, much of the new testament is incomprelensible. With it one has renewed as surance of its essential truth. 1T unites real science and real religion, each supporting the other\" Sir Arthur and his family will be in America three months. They will visit Philadelphia, Boston, Washington, St. Louis, Chicago, and possibly other cities Sir Arthur does not intend to seek to organize spiritualism In this country, he said, but merely to explain","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"f1225863-5c46-4676-aacb-bc48a3093bd6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":363,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":4},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToo