{"parentUuid":"3de7aa3f-fd82-4200-a9cc-7bbe4e89b296","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4acd64b9-626e-4904-824b-5a9167cf4217","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016q3UUDbpoFes1UX3guay7m","type":"tool_result","content":"60\t\n61\t### 2A: archive read and EDA\n62\t**Persona:** data engineer, with a systems specialist for throughput.\n63\t- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants and unit tests, plus a near-miss lexicon.\n64\t- `IngestArchiveWorkflow` over all 180 files: candidates, counts, samples and manifests, with concurrency set from Phase 1's measurements.\n65\t- Kill test 1, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after.\n66\t- The crop check on 50 random candidates spread across batches.\n67\t- Eval A, with charts:\n68\t  - per year: pages, articles and candidates, and candidates per million articles\n69\t  - by state, the term mix and the OCR-quality distribution\n70\t  - the reprint signal, and candidate lengths\n71\t- Prefilter recall: the random-sample bound and the near-miss pool. If misses are found, lexicon v2 and a re-run.\n72\t\n73\t**Gate**\n74\t- All 180 years are done, or each failure is listed with its reason.\n75\t- Disk use is reported.\n76\t- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces the 1850 counts exactly.\n77\t- Kill-test evidence exists, and every crop-check failure is explained.\n78\t- The EM spot-checks 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their sources.\n79\t\n80\t### 2B: schemas and data layer\n81\t**Persona:** data architect.\n82\t- Pydantic models, with Atlas validators generated from them.\n83\t- `make db-init` creates every collection and index, and can be re-run safely.\n84\t- A storage budget built from real document sizes, and a written decision on what goes into Atlas.\n85\t- The GeoNames geocoder and its manifest. The LCCN title parser, tested on at least 200 distinct titles.\n86\t- The reprint-clustering design, and its evaluation set:\n87\t  - Sample per candidate as well as per cluster. Eval A's check drew clusters uniformly, so 11 of its 20 pairs came from clusters of 2 printings, and none of the 157 clusters of 50 or more was checked.\n88\t  - Hand-check the largest clusters for chaining, where shared runs join stories that aren't the same.\n89\t- The reel-to-batch map (decision 29):\n90\t  - Build it from the batch paths in the candidates and samples, so a page with no batch path gets its IIIF service from local data. That's about 310,000 candidates.\n91\t  - Validate it: hide the batch on pages that have one, and count how often the map gets it back.\n92\t  - Flag every reel that maps to more than one batch, such as a reissued ver01 and ver02.\n93\t- No loc.gov lookups yet for the 25,595 candidates whose reels the map doesn't cover. At 20 a minute they would take about a day, and most won't be reports.\n94\t  - Resolve only the ones the reader makes reports, through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s.\n95\t  - Cache each answer, which `page_service` doesn't do yet, so no page is looked up twice.\n96\t\n97\t**Gate**\n98\t- Schema tests pass, and `make db-init` can run twice.\n99\t- The budget is written down.\n100\t- Geocoder accuracy is measured on a sample.\n101\t- The reel-to-batch map's hit rate on hidden batches is reported, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged.\n102\t\n103\t### 2C: the reader\n104\t**Persona:** ML specialist.\n105\t1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n106\t   - `POLICY.md` settles delusions and hallucinations with one rule. Eval A counts a 1904 defendant pointing at an \"imaginary\" spectre in court as a report, and a 1909 lunacy hearing's spooks as other. Its interval barely moves either way.\n107\t   - It also settles claims of spirit control and of a felt presence of the dead. Decision 30 counts two such articles as borderline reports, and they are half of the reports it found.\n108\t2. Build the labelling page.\n109\t3. Label the gold set before seeing any model output.\n110\t4. Teacher labels for the training pool, with the leakage check passing.\n111\t5. Baselines: a keyword rule, and base 4B both zero-shot and few-shot.\n112\t6. LoRA training on Tinker, iterating on the dev split until improvements stop. Each iteration is recorded.\n113\t7. Eval B, ablations B1 to B10, and edge-case slices E1 to E14.\n114\t   - Eval B also has the reader screen the random reservoirs, for a recall bound that doesn't rest on flag words (decision 30). The Tinker credit check sets how many it reads.\n115\t8. The failure log, and `eval/MODEL_CARD.md`.\n116\t9. The local export and the offline proof.\n117\t10. The full pass over all triaged candidates.\n118\t\n119\t**Gate**\n120\t- The gold hash hasn't changed since the freeze, and the leakage check passes.\n121\t- The eval JSON and charts include confidence intervals."}]},"uuid":"63e5b1f0-5b7e-4213-96b6-1dbcd772dd18","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:07:09.650Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","content":"\n### 2A: archive read and EDA\n**Persona:** data engineer, with a systems specialist for throughput.\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants and unit tests, plus a near-miss lexicon.\n- `IngestArchiveWorkflow` over all 180 files: candidates, counts, samples and manifests, with concurrency set from Phase 1's measurements.\n- Kill test 1, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after.\n- The crop check on 50 random candidates spread across batches.\n- Eval A, with charts:\n  - per year: pages, articles and candidates, and candidates per million articles\n  - by state, the term mix and the OCR-quality distribution\n  - the reprint signal, and candidate lengths\n- Prefilter recall: the random-sample bound and the near-miss pool. If misses are found, lexicon v2 and a re-run.\n\n**Gate**\n- All 180 years are done, or each failure is listed with its reason.\n- Disk use is reported.\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces the 1850 counts exactly.\n- Kill-test evidence exists, and every crop-check failure is explained.\n- The EM spot-checks 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their sources.\n\n### 2B: schemas and data layer\n**Persona:** data architect.\n- Pydantic models, with Atlas validators generated from them.\n- `make db-init` creates every collection and index, and can be re-run safely.\n- A storage budget built from real document sizes, and a written decision on what goes into Atlas.\n- The GeoNames geocoder and its manifest. The LCCN title parser, tested on at least 200 distinct titles.\n- The reprint-clustering design, and its evaluation set:\n  - Sample per candidate as well as per cluster. Eval A's check drew clusters uniformly, so 11 of its 20 pairs came from clusters of 2 printings, and none of the 157 clusters of 50 or more was checked.\n  - Hand-check the largest clusters for chaining, where shared runs join stories that aren't the same.\n- The reel-to-batch map (decision 29):\n  - Build it from the batch paths in the candidates and samples, so a page with no batch path gets its IIIF service from local data. That's about 310,000 candidates.\n  - Validate it: hide the batch on pages that have one, and count how often the map gets it back.\n  - Flag every reel that maps to more than one batch, such as a reissued ver01 and ver02.\n- No loc.gov lookups yet for the 25,595 candidates whose reels the map doesn't cover. At 20 a minute they would take about a day, and most won't be reports.\n  - Resolve only the ones the reader makes reports, through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s.\n  - Cache each answer, which `page_service` doesn't do yet, so no page is looked up twice.\n\n**Gate**\n- Schema tests pass, and `make db-init` can run twice.\n- The budget is written down.\n- Geocoder accuracy is measured on a sample.\n- The reel-to-batch map's hit rate on hidden batches is reported, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged.\n\n### 2C: the reader\n**Persona:** ML specialist.\n1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n   - `POLICY.md` settles delusions and hallucinations with one rule. Eval A counts a 1904 defendant pointing at an \"imaginary\" spectre in court as a report, and a 1909 lunacy hearing's spooks as other. Its interval barely moves either way.\n   - It also settles claims of spirit control and of a felt presence of the dead. Decision 30 counts two such articles as borderline reports, and they are half of the reports it found.\n2. Build the labelling page.\n3. Label the gold set before seeing any model output.\n4. Teacher labels for the training pool, with the leakage check passing.\n5. Baselines: a keyword rule, and base 4B both zero-shot and few-shot.\n6. LoRA training on Tinker, iterating on the dev split until improvements stop. Each iteration is recorded.\n7. Eval B, ablations B1 to B10, and edge-case slices E1 to E14.\n   - Eval B also has the reader screen the random reservoirs, for a recall bound that doesn't rest on flag words (decision 30). The Tinker credit check sets how many it reads.\n8. The failure log, and `eval/MODEL_CARD.md`.\n9. The local export and the offline proof.\n10. The full pass over all triaged candidates.\n\n**Gate**\n- The gold hash hasn't changed since the freeze, and the leakage check passes.\n- The eval JSON and charts include confidence intervals.","numLines":62,"startLine":60,"totalLines":231}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3de7aa3f-fd82-4200-a9cc-7bbe4e89b296","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
