and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.","new_string":"- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31)."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"306975d1-01a7-44fd-9ab5-a95e35353b81","apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CgYoQYK8KUfWwuHm9Yag9","requestRef":"967a277c-949b-4b4b-8aeb-f813aaf29287","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"2ab5a1bc-62bd-42ab-9d78-1a35968a965d","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:17:04.085Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"043ce19a-9cdd-4726-a1b2-98d95e9fe41e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"53915d29-3b9f-4873-b1be-8f20126b960f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gw75VXRr8kqfVNSFKnjupW","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"11eaaa02-24f4-4a7a-acc6-8b493804e751","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:17:04.111Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","oldString":"  - 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875's Eddys, 1922's Conan Doyle, 1923's Vale Owen and 1924's Chicago meeting.","newString":"  - 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875's Eddys, 1922's Conan Doyle, 1923's Vale Owen and 1924's Chicago meeting.\n  - Two of the single-rule reports are the borderline ones: Allut (spirit_phrase) and Hodgson (spiritualism). Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3.","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":323,"oldLines":6,"newStart":323,"newLines":7,"lines":["   | ouija | 0: 0% (0–1.9%) | at most 3,500 | 3 | 0% (0–56.2%) of 5,124 |"," ","   - 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875's Eddys, 1922's Conan Doyle, 1923's Vale Owen and 1924's Chicago meeting.","+  - Two of the single-rule reports are the borderline ones: Allut (spirit_phrase) and Hodgson (spiritualism). Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3.","   - In 2A.7's screen, spirit+medium caught no report that another rule missed. Here it alone catches two: 1857's Middlebury trance medium and 1859's Plymouth convention.","   - spirit+medium is also the largest rule, with the lowest precision. Its candidates holding no other rule are reports at 3.5%, the same rate as v1's candidates.","   - `lexicon_v2.json` gives each rule's `marginal` (over the 200) and `only` (over its sampled articles holding only it)."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"043ce19a-9cdd-4726-a1b2-98d95e9fe41e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"41ce0e4f-e3a6-4628-bb22-1614a4be2fe2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"53915d29-3b9f-4873-b1be-8f20126b960f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014QNbNfumgBgSKWsVJKfK3s","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"dcc9b8d9-1c82-461b-b950-ae1b3eda63fd","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:17:04.138Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","oldString":"once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. Whether to drop the ouija rule is the EM's call (decision 31). Dropping it takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.","newString":"once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. The EM keeps or drops each new rule from its marginal yield (decision 31). Dropping one takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.","originalFile":"# Resume\n\nWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n\nHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n\n## Next step\n\n**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. Whether to drop the ouija rule is the EM's call (decision 31). Dropping it takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.\n\nNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n- the Render deploy\n- domain DNS\n\n## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2 (2026-10-30)\n\nBranches `phase-2a-archive` (PR #2, merged) and `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` (PR #3, step 2A.8).\n\nBuilt:\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n- Lexicon v2, which adds five spiritualist rules and records in each candidate's `terms` the rules that matched it (decision 31).\n- Two full reads of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6`:\n  - v1's (decision 25), now archived in `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`\n  - v2's (decision 31)\n- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n- Eval A (`make eval`, 93 s, 4.7 GB at peak):\n  - It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n  - It then compares v2 with v1, and measures a judged sample of v2's added candidates (decision 31).\n  - `make eval-v1` reproduces v1's record from the archive.\n\nMeasured:\n- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages. Lexicon v2 gives 1,236,122 candidates (3,338 per million) and 6,695,792 near-miss articles. v1 gave 1,050,459 (2,837 per million).\n- Each read takes about two hours for 340.4 GB, with four years at a time: 118.6 min for v1 and 120.6 min for v2.\n- Ghost reports: 3.5% of v1's candidates (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800, and 7.0% of the 185,663 that v2 adds (4.2–11.4%), about 13,000 more.\n- Reprints put 30.3% of v2's candidates in 91,301 clusters, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n- Recall:\n  - Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27% (decision 30).\n  - Lexicon v2 finds about 84%, and at least 48%. **v2's figures are in-sample:** its rules were chosen from that screen's misses.\n  - 2C's eval B gives the independent figure (decision 31).\n- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n- 32.0% of v2's candidates have no batch path, and 93.0% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decisions 29 and 31).\n\nGate:\n- All 180 years are done in both reads, and none failed.\n  - In v1's read, six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n  - In v2's read, every year finished at the first attempt, all at code version `2d6ff0faaf81`.\n- v1 ⊆ v2: all 1,050,459 v1 candidates are still candidates, and none of the 185,663 added holds a v1 term.\n- Disk: the v1 archive and v2's outputs take 4.1 GB, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n  - `data/archive/lexicon-v1`: 1.8 GiB\n  - v2's `data/candidates`: 1.5 GiB\n  - v2's `data/samples`: 563 MiB\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850 under both lexicons. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the manifest.\n- `make eval-v1` reproduces eval A's v1 record byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`.\n- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n- The EM's spot check of v1, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n  - every candidate's term is in its text\n  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports.\n- v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline.\n- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle these cases:\n  - delusions and hallucinations\n  - spirit control and a felt presence of the dead\n  - decision 31's two borderline kinds\n- 27,817 of v2's candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n- The reader's job is 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's. The Tinker credit check weighs that before the full pass.\n\n## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n\nBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n\nBuilt:\n- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n\nMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n\nUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n\n## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n\n- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":6,"oldLines":7,"newStart":6,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," ## Next step"," ","-**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. Whether to drop the ouija rule is the EM's call (decision 31). Dropping it takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.","+**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. The EM keeps or drops each new rule from its marginal yield (decision 31). Dropping one takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read."," "," Needs the owner's yes when we get there:"," - the Render deploy"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"41ce0e4f-e3a6-4628-bb22-1614a4be2fe2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"2ab5a1bc-62bd-42ab-9d78-1a35968a965d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"53915d29-3b9f-4873-b1be-8f20126b960f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K4E9G8Tf3NtYJ8k8Eb5n2T","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"bd4edf27-b47a-440e-9602-74928a270002","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:17:04.157Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","oldString":"- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.","newString":"- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31).","originalFile":"# Resume\n\nWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n\nHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n\n## Next step\n\n**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. The EM keeps or drops each new rule from its marginal yield (decision 31). Dropping one takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.\n\nNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n- the Render deploy\n- domain DNS\n\n## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2 (2026-10-30)\n\nBranches `phase-2a-archive` (PR #2, merged) and `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` (PR #3, step 2A.8).\n\nBuilt:\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n- Lexicon v2, which adds five spiritualist rules and records in each candidate's `terms` the rules that matched it (decision 31).\n- Two full reads of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6`:\n  - v1's (decision 25), now archived in `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`\n  - v2's (decision 31)\n- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n- Eval A (`make eval`, 93 s, 4.7 GB at peak):\n  - It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n  - It then compares v2 with v1, and measures a judged sample of v2's added candidates (decision 31).\n  - `make eval-v1` reproduces v1's record from the archive.\n\nMeasured:\n- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages. Lexicon v2 gives 1,236,122 candidates (3,338 per million) and 6,695,792 near-miss articles. v1 gave 1,050,459 (2,837 per million).\n- Each read takes about two hours for 340.4 GB, with four years at a time: 118.6 min for v1 and 120.6 min for v2.\n- Ghost reports: 3.5% of v1's candidates (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800, and 7.0% of the 185,663 that v2 adds (4.2–11.4%), about 13,000 more.\n- Reprints put 30.3% of v2's candidates in 91,301 clusters, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n- Recall:\n  - Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27% (decision 30).\n  - Lexicon v2 finds about 84%, and at least 48%. **v2's figures are in-sample:** its rules were chosen from that screen's misses.\n  - 2C's eval B gives the independent figure (decision 31).\n- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n- 32.0% of v2's candidates have no batch path, and 93.0% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decisions 29 and 31).\n\nGate:\n- All 180 years are done in both reads, and none failed.\n  - In v1's read, six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n  - In v2's read, every year finished at the first attempt, all at code version `2d6ff0faaf81`.\n- v1 ⊆ v2: all 1,050,459 v1 candidates are still candidates, and none of the 185,663 added holds a v1 term.\n- Disk: the v1 archive and v2's outputs take 4.1 GB, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n  - `data/archive/lexicon-v1`: 1.8 GiB\n  - v2's `data/candidates`: 1.5 GiB\n  - v2's `data/samples`: 563 MiB\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850 under both lexicons. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the manifest.\n- `make eval-v1` reproduces eval A's v1 record byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`.\n- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n- The EM's spot check of v1, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n  - every candidate's term is in its text\n  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports.\n- v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline.\n- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle these cases:\n  - delusions and hallucinations\n  - spirit control and a felt presence of the dead\n  - decision 31's two borderline kinds\n- 27,817 of v2's candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n- The reader's job is 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's. The Tinker credit check weighs that before the full pass.\n\n## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n\nBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n\nBuilt:\n- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n\nMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n\nUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n\n## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n\n- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":60,"oldLines":6,"newStart":60,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," Risks and open questions:"," - **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.","+- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31)."," - v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports."," - v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline."," - `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle these cases:"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2ab5a1bc-62bd-42ab-9d78-1a35968a965d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2a-lexicon-v2","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","hash":"409da6a2918ab91c47761103ea2484b20b8f419723944789f5a03ec36758b253","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Two of the single-rule reports are the borderline ones, which matters for the keep/drop call. I'm adding that."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Gw75VXRr8kqfVNSFKnjupW","name":"Edit","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","old_string":"  - 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875's Eddys, 1922's Conan Doyle, 1923's Vale Owen and 1924's Chicago meeting.","new_string":"  - 10 of the 14 reports hold a single rule. Each of the other 4 holds two or more: 1875's Eddys, 1922's Conan Doyle, 1923's Vale Owen and 1924's Chicago meeting.\n  - Two of the single-rule reports are the borderline ones: Allut (spirit_phrase) and Hodgson (spiritualism). Without them, spirit_phrase alone catches 1 and spiritualism alone catches 3."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014QNbNfumgBgSKWsVJKfK3s","name":"Edit","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","old_string":"once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. Whether to drop the ouija rule is the EM's call (decision 31). Dropping it takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.","new_string":"once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. The EM keeps or drops each new rule from its marginal yield (decision 31). Dropping one takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K4E9G8Tf3NtYJ8k8Eb5n2T","name":"Edit","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","old_string":"- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the E