{"parentUuid":"08911aba-9276-4d8a-a5c8-8c7f348fd91d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYp5Uy3wM95v4kGaJpbQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018Q4hJWoJ8cowp15za6CdwP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/ARCHITECTURE.md","offset":100,"limit":160},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3712,"cache_read_input_tokens":350086,"output_tokens":491,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":347},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3712,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":491,"cache_read_input_tokens":350086,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3712,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3712},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018Q4hJWoJ8cowp15za6CdwP":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/ARCHITECTURE.md","offset":100,"limit":160}},"serverClassifierRequest":"bf22c122-4a30-486b-813b-39ba9cd8391a","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgYp5U3EwH2UjfAyyceaa","requestRef":"0fe9872f-941b-46f8-a308-2ebe9896b533","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"517232b9-7c68-4e9c-9fb7-8c386b996cc8","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:25:44.681Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"517232b9-7c68-4e9c-9fb7-8c386b996cc8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cfc7125e-bacb-48e4-917e-c396bc7253de","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Q4hJWoJ8cowp15za6CdwP","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t- **Gold set:**\n101\t  - 150–300 candidates, stratified by decade and lexicon term and including near-miss items.\n102\t  - Frozen before any training: the split and its hash are committed under `eval/gold/`.\n103\t  - The build agent labels it before seeing any model output, from the OCR text and the scan crop where the OCR is unclear.\n104\t  - The labelling rules for hard cases (séance reports, reprinted fiction, hoax exposures, humour columns) are written in `eval/gold/POLICY.md` before labelling starts.\n105\t- **Training data:** the teacher labels a separate pool. No gold article, and no reprint of a gold article, may appear in it.\n106\t- **Model selection** happens on a dev split. The gold set is touched only for final evaluations.\n107\t- **Full pass:** the tuned student reads every triaged candidate and writes `data/reader/{year}.jsonl.zst`. Every field either passes the quote verifier or becomes \"not stated\".\n108\t\n109\t### 3.3 Geocode, cluster, embed, load\n110\t- Places are resolved against GeoNames. Every location records its precision (town, street or building) and its source. Street or building coordinates exist only when a source supplies them.\n111\t- Printings of the same story are grouped. Counts on the site say which they count: stories or printings.\n112\t- Stories get one embedding each, from the earliest legible printing.\n113\t- The loader upserts by stable IDs, so re-running it is safe.\n114\t\n115\t### 3.4 Follow-up harvest\n116\t- The top 30–60 cases are picked by a written, versioned rule: a verified place, named people or a specific place, and a legible clipping.\n117\t- The years those cases need are grouped, and each year file is streamed once.\n118\t- From each stream, the harvest pulls articles that match a case's named people, its specific place, or its town within a date window.\n119\t- Output goes to `data/harvest/{case_id}.jsonl`.\n120\t\n121\t### 3.5 Investigator (the main Temporal story)\n122\t- `InvestigateCaseWorkflow(case_id, model)` runs a loop: plan, call tools, read, write claims, verify.\n123\t  - Every model call and every tool call is an activity, with an idempotency key made from the workflow ID and the step number.\n124\t  - Temporal records each result in the workflow history. After a crash the workflow replays and reuses those results, so no completed model call or web search is repeated.\n125\t  - A call that is in flight when the worker dies has no recorded result, so Temporal retries it. The idempotency key lets a provider that supports one drop the duplicate, and the kill test counts these retries rather than claiming there are none.\n126\t- **Tools:**\n127\t  - `search_archive` over the harvested and candidate articles, using a local full-text index and Atlas Search\n128\t  - `get_article`\n129\t  - `find_death_notices(name, place, window)`\n130\t  - `web_search` through SerpApi, with a budget counter that persists across runs\n131\t  - `wikipedia` for the \"now\" side when the SerpApi budget runs out (the card says so)\n132\t- **Guardrails in code, not just in the prompt:**\n133\t  - A claim is kept only if it cites at least one clipping and its quote verifies against that clipping. Dropped claims are logged with the reason.\n134\t  - A cause of death is stated only as printed.\n135\t  - The link between a ghost and a death is graded strong, possible or weak, with its reasons listed (name match, place match, date gap).\n136\t- **Output:** a `cases` document, plus one Sentry trace per case with a span per step carrying model, tokens, cost and latency.\n137\t- **Budgets:** a maximum number of steps per case, a maximum number of web searches per case, and a global SerpApi counter (the free plan is 250 searches a month).\n138\t\n139\t```mermaid\n140\tsequenceDiagram\n141\t  participant W as InvestigateCase workflow\n142\t  participant M as Model activity\n143\t  participant T as Tool activities\n144\t  W->>M: step 1, plan (result recorded)\n145\t  W->>T: step 2, search_archive and find_death_notices (recorded)\n146\t  Note over W: worker killed here\n147\t  W-->>W: replay from history: steps 1 and 2 reuse recorded results\n148\t  W->>M: step 3, write claims\n149\t  W->>T: step 4, verify quotes and save the case file\n150\t```\n151\t\n152\t### 3.6 Static export and the site\n153\t- `export_static` reads Atlas and writes:\n154\t  - `front.json`: printed-on-this-night candidates, ticker headlines and totals\n155\t  - `towns/index.json`: a compact town index for search on the device\n156\t  - `towns/{place_id}.json` and `cases/{report_id}.json`\n157\t  - `reel.json`: the archive chart\n158\t- The API contract is in `SRS.md` section 6.\n159\t- Both services are defined in `render.yaml`. Nothing deploys until the owner says yes.\n160\t\n161\t## 4. Data model (MongoDB Atlas, database `paperghosts`)\n162\t\n163\tPydantic models in the Python package are the single source of truth. Atlas JSON Schema validators are generated from them.\n164\t\n165\t**`reports`:** one document per story.\n166\t- `_id`, plus `primary_article_id`\n167\t- `printings[]`: article ID, LCCN, paper title, publication place, state, date, scan location, `bbox_list`, crop URLs\n168\t- `headline` and `text` (the OCR of the primary printing, untouched), `display_headline` (the headline without the masthead lines that lead it), `date` (earliest printing), `year`, `month_day`\n169\t- `kind` (always `report` in this collection)\n170\t- `fields`: each is `{value, quote, verified, score}`:\n171\t  - `place`\n172\t  - `people[]`\n173\t  - `seen`\n174\t  - `outcome`: unexplained, explained, hoax or unknown\n175\t  - `specific_place`\n176\t  - `place_type`: house, church, cemetery, inn or hotel, road or bridge, public building, ship, or other\n177\t  - `precision`: town, street or building\n178\t- `location`: GeoJSON point, with `location_precision` and `location_source`\n179\t- `place_id`, `town`, `state`\n180\t- `embedding`\n181\t- `reader`: model, adapter version, prompt version\n182\t- `lexicon_terms[]`\n183\t- `case_id`, when an investigation exists\n184\t\n185\t**`places`:** GeoNames ID, name, alternate names, state, location, report count, first and last year.\n186\t\n187\t**`cases`:**\n188\t- `report_id`\n189\t- `claims[]`: each with its text, the clippings it cites (article ID, quote, verified) and a confidence\n190\t- `followups[]`: each with its relation (follow-up, debunking, arrest, moved out, inquest or death notice), date, paper, quote and crop URLs\n191\t- `person`: name, death-notice article, link grade and reasons\n192\t- `then_now`: the \"then\" side; the \"now\" items, each with source, URL, retrieval date and quote; and differences, each backed by both quotes\n193\t- `run`: model, provider, steps, tokens, cost, latency, Sentry trace ID and Temporal workflow ID\n194\t\n195\t**`stats`:** one document per year and per state: pages, articles, candidates, stories, printings, and reports per million articles.\n196\t\n197\t**Indexes:**\n198\t- `reports`:\n199\t  - a 2dsphere index on `location`\n200\t  - compound `{place_id, date}` and `{month_day}`\n201\t  - an Atlas Search index on headline, text, field values and town, used with fuzzy matching\n202\t  - a Vector Search index on `embedding`, with filters on state and decade\n203\t- `places`: 2dsphere, plus Atlas Search autocomplete on names\n204\t- `cases`: `{report_id}`\n205\t\n206\t**Storage budget:** if the cluster is the free tier (512 MB), only stories, places, cases and stats go to Atlas. Candidates and reader outputs stay local, and the article says so. The real per-document size is measured in Phase 2B before anything is loaded.\n207\t\n208\t## 5. Repo layout\n209\t\n210\t```\n211\tMakefile                 setup, keys, temporal, worker, smoke, ingest, eval, db-init, db-verify, api, web, app, test, lint, numbers\n212\tpyproject.toml           one uv project, Python pinned in .python-version\n213\tpaperghosts/             the Python package\n214\t  config.py              reads .env; never prints a value\n215\t  schemas/               Pydantic models (single source of truth)\n216\t  ingest/                streaming reader, lexicon, prefilter, samples\n217\t  reader/                prompts, Tinker training and sampling, local runner, quote verifier\n218\t  geo/                   GeoNames loader, LCCN title parser, geocoder\n219\t  cluster/               reprint clustering, embeddings\n220\t  store/                 Atlas loader, indexes, validators, static export\n221\t  investigator/          agent loop, tools, guardrails\n222\t  workflows/             Temporal workflows, activities, worker\n223\t  observability/         Sentry setup and model-call spans\n224\tapi/                     FastAPI app (read-only)\n225\tweb/                     React + Vite + TypeScript site and design system\n226\teval/\n227\t  gold/                  split, hash, labelling policy (committed)\n228\t  results/               JSON and charts for evals A to D (committed)\n229\tscripts/                 one-off checks: keys, crop check, offline proof, numbers sheet\n230\ttests/                   unit, integration (Temporal test env), API contract\n231\tdata/                    local only, gitignored except small manifests and per-year counts\n232\tDocs/                    BRIEF, ARCHITECTURE, PRD, SRS, BUILD-PLAN, DECISIONS, RESUME, UI-IDEAS (IDEAS.md stays private)\n233\t.github/workflows/       CI\n234\trender.yaml              deploy blueprint (used only after the owner's yes)\n235\t```\n236\t\n237\t## 6. Environments and delivery\n238\t- **Local:** `make temporal`, `make worker`, `make api` and `make web` run everything on the owner's machine.\n239\t- **CI (GitHub Actions):** lint, offline tests, a web typecheck and build, and a secret scan, on every push and pull request. CI needs no secrets; tests that need the network are marked and skipped there.\n240\t- **CD (Render):** a Blueprint with an API web service and a static site. It auto-deploys from `main` only after the owner approves the first deploy.\n241\t  - Render environment variables are set by the owner.\n242\t  - Atlas network access must allow Render's servers; that change also needs the owner's yes.\n243\t- **Releases:** Sentry releases are tagged with the git SHA.\n244\t\n245\t## 7. Security and privacy\n246\t- **Secrets:**\n247\t  - Secrets reach code only through environment variables.\n248\t  - `config.py` redacts them in `repr`, logs and errors.\n249\t  - The Ultramarine key is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` in `.env`; config also accepts `ULTRAMARINE_API_KEY`.\n250\t- **Sentry:** `send_default_pii=False`, and a `before_send` hook strips coordinates and query strings.\n251\t- **Near me:** coordinates are rounded to about 1 km on the device and again on the server, used for that one query, and never logged.\n252\t- **Secret scanning:** a scanner runs in CI.\n253\t  - Entire is on with telemetry off, and its checkpoints sync to the private `origin`.\n254\t  - Before the repo goes public, the Entire checkpoint refs and the recorded sessions are scanned for secrets.\n255\t- **The people in the archive:** nobody is named except as the papers printed them, and nothing about how they died is guessed.\n256\t\n257\t## 8. Architecture risks and what answers them\n258\t\n259\t| Risk | Answer |"}]},"uuid":"a77b35bd-f73e-4eca-a671-d9b0d53750eb","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:25:44.709Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/ARCHITECTURE.md","content":"- **Gold set:**\n  - 150–300 candidates, stratified by decade and lexicon term and including near-miss items.\n  - Frozen before any training: the split and its hash are committed under `eval/gold/`.\n  - The build agent labels it before seeing any model output, from the OCR text and the scan crop where the OCR is unclear.\n  - The labelling rules for hard cases (séance reports, reprinted fiction, hoax exposures, humour columns) are written in `eval/gold/POLICY.md` before labelling starts.\n- **Training data:** the teacher labels a separate pool. No gold article, and no reprint of a gold article, may appear in it.\n- **Model selection** happens on a dev split. The gold set is touched only for final evaluations.\n- **Full pass:** the tuned student reads every triaged candidate and writes `data/reader/{year}.jsonl.zst`. Every field either passes the quote verifier or becomes \"not stated\".\n\n### 3.3 Geocode, cluster, embed, load\n- Places are resolved against GeoNames. Every location records its precision (town, street or building) and its source. Street or building coordinates exist only when a source supplies them.\n- Printings of the same story are grouped. Counts on the site say which they count: stories or printings.\n- Stories get one embedding each, from the earliest legible printing.\n- The loader upserts by stable IDs, so re-running it is safe.\n\n### 3.4 Follow-up harvest\n- The top 30–60 cases are picked by a written, versioned rule: a verified place, named people or a specific place, and a legible clipping.\n- The years those cases need are grouped, and each year file is streamed once.\n- From each stream, the harvest pulls articles that match a case's named people, its specific place, or its town within a date window.\n- Output goes to `data/harvest/{case_id}.jsonl`.\n\n### 3.5 Investigator (the main Temporal story)\n- `InvestigateCaseWorkflow(case_id, model)` runs a loop: plan, call tools, read, write claims, verify.\n  - Every model call and every tool call is an activity, with an idempotency key made from the workflow ID and the step number.\n  - Temporal records each result in the workflow history. After a crash the workflow replays and reuses those results, so no completed model call or web search is repeated.\n  - A call that is in flight when the worker dies has no recorded result, so Temporal retries it. The idempotency key lets a provider that supports one drop the duplicate, and the kill test counts these retries rather than claiming there are none.\n- **Tools:**\n  - `search_archive` over the harvested and candidate articles, using a local full-text index and Atlas Search\n  - `get_article`\n  - `find_death_notices(name, place, window)`\n  - `web_search` through SerpApi, with a budget counter that persists across runs\n  - `wikipedia` for the \"now\" side when the SerpApi budget runs out (the card says so)\n- **Guardrails in code, not just in the prompt:**\n  - A claim is kept only if it cites at least one clipping and its quote verifies against that clipping. Dropped claims are logged with the reason.\n  - A cause of death is stated only as printed.\n  - The link between a ghost and a death is graded strong, possible or weak, with its reasons listed (name match, place match, date gap).\n- **Output:** a `cases` document, plus one Sentry trace per case with a span per step carrying model, tokens, cost and latency.\n- **Budgets:** a maximum number of steps per case, a maximum number of web searches per case, and a global SerpApi counter (the free plan is 250 searches a month).\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n  participant W as InvestigateCase workflow\n  participant M as Model activity\n  participant T as Tool activities\n  W->>M: step 1, plan (result recorded)\n  W->>T: step 2, search_archive and find_death_notices (recorded)\n  Note over W: worker killed here\n  W-->>W: replay from history: steps 1 and 2 reuse recorded results\n  W->>M: step 3, write claims\n  W->>T: step 4, verify quotes and save the case file\n```\n\n### 3.6 Static export and the site\n- `export_static` reads Atlas and writes:\n  - `front.json`: printed-on-this-night candidates, ticker headlines and totals\n  - `towns/index.json`: a compact town index for search on the device\n  - `towns/{place_id}.json` and `cases/{report_id}.json`\n  - `reel.json`: the archive chart\n- The API contract is in `SRS.md` section 6.\n- Both services are defined in `render.yaml`. Nothing deploys until the owner says yes.\n\n## 4. Data model (MongoDB Atlas, database `paperghosts`)\n\nPydantic models in the Python package are the single source of truth. Atlas JSON Schema validators are generated from them.\n\n**`reports`:** one document per story.\n- `_id`, plus `primary_article_id`\n- `printings[]`: article ID, LCCN, paper title, publication place, state, date, scan location, `bbox_list`, crop URLs\n- `headline` and `text` (the OCR of the primary printing, untouched), `display_headline` (the headline without the masthead lines that lead it), `date` (earliest printing), `year`, `month_day`\n- `kind` (always `report` in this collection)\n- `fields`: each is `{value, quote, verified, score}`:\n  - `place`\n  - `people[]`\n  - `seen`\n  - `outcome`: unexplained, explained, hoax or unknown\n  - `specific_place`\n  - `place_type`: house, church, cemetery, inn or hotel, road or bridge, public building, ship, or other\n  - `precision`: town, street or building\n- `location`: GeoJSON point, with `location_precision` and `location_source`\n- `place_id`, `town`, `state`\n- `embedding`\n- `reader`: model, adapter version, prompt version\n- `lexicon_terms[]`\n- `case_id`, when an investigation exists\n\n**`places`:** GeoNames ID, name, alternate names, state, location, report count, first and last year.\n\n**`cases`:**\n- `report_id`\n- `claims[]`: each with its text, the clippings it cites (article ID, quote, verified) and a confidence\n- `followups[]`: each with its relation (follow-up, debunking, arrest, moved out, inquest or death notice), date, paper, quote and crop URLs\n- `person`: name, death-notice article, link grade and reasons\n- `then_now`: the \"then\" side; the \"now\" items, each with source, URL, retrieval date and quote; and differences, each backed by both quotes\n- `run`: model, provider, steps, tokens, cost, latency, Sentry trace ID and Temporal workflow ID\n\n**`stats`:** one document per year and per state: pages, articles, candidates, stories, printings, and reports per million articles.\n\n**Indexes:**\n- `reports`:\n  - a 2dsphere index on `location`\n  - compound `{place_id, date}` and `{month_day}`\n  - an Atlas Search index on headline, text, field values and town, used with fuzzy matching\n  - a Vector Search index on `embedding`, with filters on state and decade\n- `places`: 2dsphere, plus Atlas Search autocomplete on names\n- `cases`: `{report_id}`\n\n**Storage budget:** if the cluster is the free tier (512 MB), only stories, places, cases and stats go to Atlas. Candidates and reader outputs stay local, and the article says so. The real per-document size is measured in Phase 2B before anything is loaded.\n\n## 5. Repo layout\n\n```\nMakefile                 setup, keys, temporal, worker, smoke, ingest, eval, db-init, db-verify, api, web, app, test, lint, numbers\npyproject.toml           one uv project, Python pinned in .python-version\npaperghosts/             the Python package\n  config.py              reads .env; never prints a value\n  schemas/               Pydantic models (single source of truth)\n  ingest/                streaming reader, lexicon, prefilter, samples\n  reader/                prompts, Tinker training and sampling, local runner, quote verifier\n  geo/                   GeoNames loader, LCCN title parser, geocoder\n  cluster/               reprint clustering, embeddings\n  store/                 Atlas loader, indexes, validators, static export\n  investigator/          agent loop, tools, guardrails\n  workflows/             Temporal workflows, activities, worker\n  observability/         Sentry setup and model-call spans\napi/                     FastAPI app (read-only)\nweb/                     React + Vite + TypeScript site and design system\neval/\n  gold/                  split, hash, labelling policy (committed)\n  results/               JSON and charts for evals A to D (committed)\nscripts/                 one-off checks: keys, crop check, offline proof, numbers sheet\ntests/                   unit, integration (Temporal test env), API contract\ndata/                    local only, gitignored except small manifests and per-year counts\nDocs/                    BRIEF, ARCHITECTURE, PRD, SRS, BUILD-PLAN, DECISIONS, RESUME, UI-IDEAS (IDEAS.md stays private)\n.github/workflows/       CI\nrender.yaml              deploy blueprint (used only after the owner's yes)\n```\n\n## 6. Environments and delivery\n- **Local:** `make temporal`, `make worker`, `make api` and `make web` run everything on the owner's machine.\n- **CI (GitHub Actions):** lint, offline tests, a web typecheck and build, and a secret scan, on every push and pull request. CI needs no secrets; tests that need the network are marked and skipped there.\n- **CD (Render):** a Blueprint with an API web service and a static site. It auto-deploys from `main` only after the owner approves the first deploy.\n  - Render environment variables are set by the owner.\n  - Atlas network access must allow Render's servers; that change also needs the owner's yes.\n- **Releases:** Sentry releases are tagged with the git SHA.\n\n## 7. Security and privacy\n- **Secrets:**\n  - Secrets reach code only through environment variables.\n  - `config.py` redacts them in `repr`, logs and errors.\n  - The Ultramarine key is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` in `.env`; config also accepts `ULTRAMARINE_API_KEY`.\n- **Sentry:** `send_default_pii=False`, and a `before_send` hook strips coordinates and query strings.\n- **Near me:** coordinates are rounded to about 1 km on the device and again on the server, used for that one query, and never logged.\n- **Secret scanning:** a scanner runs in CI.\n  - Entire is on with telemetry off, and its checkpoints sync to the private `origin`.\n  - Before the repo goes public, the Entire checkpoint refs and the recorded sessions are scanned for secrets.\n- **The people in the archive:** nobody is named except as the papers printed them, and nothing about how they died is guessed.\n\n## 8. Architecture risks and what answers them\n\n| Risk | Answer |","numLines":160,"startLine":100,"totalLines":269}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"517232b9-7c68-4e9c-9fb7-8c386b996cc8","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
