{"parentUuid":"dd84daf3-1340-48a7-a858-f5aaffde1b41","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cfc7125e-bacb-48e4-917e-c396bc7253de","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AjMb8Cf2NdfgSvBFS2xFYS","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"5f0cec71-57f9-4f87-9d19-d324b8be9627","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:31:08.685Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/DECISIONS.md","oldString":"  - `lexicon_v2.json` gives each rule's `marginal` (over the 200) and `only` (over its sampled articles holding only it).","newString":"  - `lexicon_v2.json` gives each rule's `marginal` (over the 200) and `only` (over its sampled articles holding only it).\n- **The EM's call (2026-10-30): keep all five rules.**\n  - Each of spiritualism, seance, spirit_phrase and spirit+medium catches reports that no other rule does.\n  - ouija's 0 of 6 rules nothing out (0–39%), and it costs only 5,124 candidates, 0.4% of the reader's job. 2C's term strata measure it.","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":327,"oldLines":6,"newStart":327,"newLines":9,"lines":["   - In 2A.7's screen, spirit+medium caught no report that another rule missed. Here it alone catches two: 1857's Middlebury trance medium and 1859's Plymouth convention.","   - spirit+medium is also the largest rule, with the lowest precision. Its candidates holding no other rule are reports at 3.5%, the same rate as v1's candidates.","   - `lexicon_v2.json` gives each rule's `marginal` (over the 200) and `only` (over its sampled articles holding only it).","+- **The EM's call (2026-10-30): keep all five rules.**","+  - Each of spiritualism, seance, spirit_phrase and spirit+medium catches reports that no other rule does.","+  - ouija's 0 of 6 rules nothing out (0–39%), and it costs only 5,124 candidates, 0.4% of the reader's job. 2C's term strata measure it."," - The 14 reports:","   - 1857, Middlebury: a spirit called Judd speaks through a trance medium at Union Hall.","   - Borderline, 1857: in the Picaud (\"real Monte Cristo\") narrative, printed as true, the dying Allut says he has seen and spoken with a soul separated from its body."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dd84daf3-1340-48a7-a858-f5aaffde1b41","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"bbfe8f19-dbf5-4e73-ba62-12b391e500ca","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cfc7125e-bacb-48e4-917e-c396bc7253de","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2oPbBmEPfEMzVeM4C2XYH","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"30b92697-15d9-4ab0-a61e-bce38073650c","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:31:08.706Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","oldString":"**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. The EM keeps or drops each new rule from its marginal yield (decision 31). Dropping one takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.","newString":"**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), on branch `phase-2b-data-layer`. Step 2B.1 covers the models, `make db-init` and the storage budget. Step 2B.2, the geocoder and LCCN parser, follows once the EM has reviewed it. Lexicon v2 keeps all five rules (decision 31).","originalFile":"# Resume\n\nWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n\nHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n\n## Next step\n\n**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. The EM keeps or drops each new rule from its marginal yield (decision 31). Dropping one takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.\n\nNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n- the Render deploy\n- domain DNS\n\n## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2 (2026-10-30)\n\nBranches `phase-2a-archive` (PR #2, merged) and `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` (PR #3, step 2A.8).\n\nBuilt:\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n- Lexicon v2, which adds five spiritualist rules and records in each candidate's `terms` the rules that matched it (decision 31).\n- Two full reads of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6`:\n  - v1's (decision 25), now archived in `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`\n  - v2's (decision 31)\n- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n- Eval A (`make eval`, 93 s, 4.7 GB at peak):\n  - It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n  - It then compares v2 with v1, and measures a judged sample of v2's added candidates (decision 31).\n  - `make eval-v1` reproduces v1's record from the archive.\n\nMeasured:\n- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages. Lexicon v2 gives 1,236,122 candidates (3,338 per million) and 6,695,792 near-miss articles. v1 gave 1,050,459 (2,837 per million).\n- Each read takes about two hours for 340.4 GB, with four years at a time: 118.6 min for v1 and 120.6 min for v2.\n- Ghost reports: 3.5% of v1's candidates (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800, and 7.0% of the 185,663 that v2 adds (4.2–11.4%), about 13,000 more.\n- Reprints put 30.3% of v2's candidates in 91,301 clusters, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n- Recall:\n  - Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27% (decision 30).\n  - Lexicon v2 finds about 84%, and at least 48%. **v2's figures are in-sample:** its rules were chosen from that screen's misses.\n  - 2C's eval B gives the independent figure (decision 31).\n- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n- 32.0% of v2's candidates have no batch path, and 93.0% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decisions 29 and 31).\n\nGate:\n- All 180 years are done in both reads, and none failed.\n  - In v1's read, six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n  - In v2's read, every year finished at the first attempt, all at code version `2d6ff0faaf81`.\n- v1 ⊆ v2: all 1,050,459 v1 candidates are still candidates, and none of the 185,663 added holds a v1 term.\n- Disk: the v1 archive and v2's outputs take 4.1 GB, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n  - `data/archive/lexicon-v1`: 1.8 GiB\n  - v2's `data/candidates`: 1.5 GiB\n  - v2's `data/samples`: 563 MiB\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850 under both lexicons. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the manifest.\n- `make eval-v1` reproduces eval A's v1 record byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`.\n- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n- The EM's spot check of v1, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n  - every candidate's term is in its text\n  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31).\n- v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports.\n- v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline.\n- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle these cases:\n  - delusions and hallucinations\n  - spirit control and a felt presence of the dead\n  - decision 31's two borderline kinds\n- 27,817 of v2's candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n- The reader's job is 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's. The Tinker credit check weighs that before the full pass.\n\n## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n\nBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n\nBuilt:\n- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n\nMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n\nUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n\n## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n\n- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":6,"oldLines":7,"newStart":6,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," ## Next step"," ","-**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), once PR #3 (lexicon v2) is merged. The EM keeps or drops each new rule from its marginal yield (decision 31). Dropping one takes a filter on `terms`, not a re-read.","+**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), on branch `phase-2b-data-layer`. Step 2B.1 covers the models, `make db-init` and the storage budget. Step 2B.2, the geocoder and LCCN parser, follows once the EM has reviewed it. Lexicon v2 keeps all five rules (decision 31)."," "," Needs the owner's yes when we get there:"," - the Render deploy"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bbfe8f19-dbf5-4e73-ba62-12b391e500ca","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"56178a9b-56de-4fac-80f8-a3b41fe49c16","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cfc7125e-bacb-48e4-917e-c396bc7253de","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017zdyeBQCdUwfL5ASad2fHe","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a56ba699-9869-4377-8674-8e592f87c3f7","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:31:08.733Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","oldString":"- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31).","newString":"- **The ouija rule stays.** None of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). The EM kept it, and 2C's term strata measure it (decision 31).\n- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31).","originalFile":"# Resume\n\nWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n\nHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n\n## Next step\n\n**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), on branch `phase-2b-data-layer`. Step 2B.1 covers the models, `make db-init` and the storage budget. Step 2B.2, the geocoder and LCCN parser, follows once the EM has reviewed it. Lexicon v2 keeps all five rules (decision 31).\n\nNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n- the Render deploy\n- domain DNS\n\n## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2 (2026-10-30)\n\nBranches `phase-2a-archive` (PR #2, merged) and `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` (PR #3, step 2A.8).\n\nBuilt:\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n- Lexicon v2, which adds five spiritualist rules and records in each candidate's `terms` the rules that matched it (decision 31).\n- Two full reads of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6`:\n  - v1's (decision 25), now archived in `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`\n  - v2's (decision 31)\n- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n- Eval A (`make eval`, 93 s, 4.7 GB at peak):\n  - It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n  - It then compares v2 with v1, and measures a judged sample of v2's added candidates (decision 31).\n  - `make eval-v1` reproduces v1's record from the archive.\n\nMeasured:\n- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages. Lexicon v2 gives 1,236,122 candidates (3,338 per million) and 6,695,792 near-miss articles. v1 gave 1,050,459 (2,837 per million).\n- Each read takes about two hours for 340.4 GB, with four years at a time: 118.6 min for v1 and 120.6 min for v2.\n- Ghost reports: 3.5% of v1's candidates (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800, and 7.0% of the 185,663 that v2 adds (4.2–11.4%), about 13,000 more.\n- Reprints put 30.3% of v2's candidates in 91,301 clusters, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n- Recall:\n  - Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27% (decision 30).\n  - Lexicon v2 finds about 84%, and at least 48%. **v2's figures are in-sample:** its rules were chosen from that screen's misses.\n  - 2C's eval B gives the independent figure (decision 31).\n- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n- 32.0% of v2's candidates have no batch path, and 93.0% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decisions 29 and 31).\n\nGate:\n- All 180 years are done in both reads, and none failed.\n  - In v1's read, six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n  - In v2's read, every year finished at the first attempt, all at code version `2d6ff0faaf81`.\n- v1 ⊆ v2: all 1,050,459 v1 candidates are still candidates, and none of the 185,663 added holds a v1 term.\n- Disk: the v1 archive and v2's outputs take 4.1 GB, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n  - `data/archive/lexicon-v1`: 1.8 GiB\n  - v2's `data/candidates`: 1.5 GiB\n  - v2's `data/samples`: 563 MiB\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850 under both lexicons. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the manifest.\n- `make eval-v1` reproduces eval A's v1 record byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`.\n- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n- The EM's spot check of v1, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n  - every candidate's term is in its text\n  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.\n- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31).\n- v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports.\n- v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline.\n- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle these cases:\n  - delusions and hallucinations\n  - spirit control and a felt presence of the dead\n  - decision 31's two borderline kinds\n- 27,817 of v2's candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n- The reader's job is 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's. The Tinker credit check weighs that before the full pass.\n\n## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n\nBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n\nBuilt:\n- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n\nMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n\nUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n\n## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n\n- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":59,"oldLines":7,"newStart":59,"newLines":7,"lines":["   - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%"," "," Risks and open questions:","-- **The ouija rule is flagged** (decision 31): none of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). Dropping it is the EM's call, and a filter on `terms` would remove 5,124 candidates.","+- **The ouija rule stays.** None of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). The EM kept it, and 2C's term strata measure it (decision 31)."," - Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31)."," - v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports."," - v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"56178a9b-56de-4fac-80f8-a3b41fe49c16","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"b60c8d26-8a98-41e0-9f58-08fd2b567529","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cfc7125e-bacb-48e4-917e-c396bc7253de","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HLPcf1djXAHLhiMVZ57JTF","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"06f9a9f6-94cf-45c0-ba9c-dd8ef2cee521","timestamp":"2026-10-30T17:31:08.757Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","oldString":"1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.","newString":"1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n   - The gold set's term strata include each of lexicon v2's five rules. That's how ouija, kept with 0 reports in its 6 sampled candidates, gets measured (decision 31).","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":103,"oldLines":6,"newStart":103,"newLines":7,"lines":[" ### 2C: the reader"," **Persona:** ML specialist."," 1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.","+   - The gold set's term strata include each of lexicon v2's five rules. That's how ouija, kept with 0 reports in its 6 sampled candidates, gets measured (decision 31).","    - `POLICY.md` settles delusions and hallucinations with one rule. Eval A counts a 1904 defendant pointing at an \"imaginary\" spectre in court as a report, and a 1909 lunacy hearing's spooks as other. Its interval barely moves either way.","    - It also settles claims of spirit control and of a felt presence of the dead. Decision 30 counts two such articles as borderline reports, and they are half of the reports it found.","    - And decision 31's two borderline kinds: a claim inside a narrative whose truth is doubtful, and a particular claim told inside a general essay. It confirms or overrules counting a first-person claim of communication with a named dead person, made in a lecture or an interview."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b60c8d26-8a98-41e0-9f58-08fd2b567529","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
