{"parentUuid":"a89d63df-91e2-46d4-85a0-29760f1dc49d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYroEpfBnKFCqMrPm4bX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CWjg2tfK85GhSC2T6QLYuw","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","offset":80,"limit":63},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1054,"cache_read_input_tokens":591666,"output_tokens":143,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1054,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":143,"cache_read_input_tokens":591666,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1054,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1054},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CWjg2tfK85GhSC2T6QLYuw":{"file_path":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","offset":80,"limit":63}},"serverClassifierRequest":"ca8f737b-a84f-479b-bfb3-37615b7f04f4","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgYroDtLxeKCBQtDR9Qft","requestRef":"f5e0ab19-3f7e-45b4-b24e-39622cc1a256","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"addbc8cf-ce79-4e54-a72f-517e297c9d6d","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:01:23.324Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"addbc8cf-ce79-4e54-a72f-517e297c9d6d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d9847e6d-1ff5-4ddf-ae83-97f1a4dc7124","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CWjg2tfK85GhSC2T6QLYuw","type":"tool_result","content":"80\t### 2B: schemas and data layer\n81\t**Persona:** data architect.\n82\t- Pydantic models, with Atlas validators generated from them.\n83\t- `make db-init` creates every collection and index, and can be re-run safely.\n84\t- A storage budget built from real document sizes, and a written decision on what goes into Atlas.\n85\t- The GeoNames geocoder and its manifest. The LCCN title parser, tested on at least 200 distinct titles.\n86\t- The reprint-clustering design, and its evaluation set:\n87\t  - Sample per candidate as well as per cluster. Eval A's check drew clusters uniformly, so 11 of its 20 pairs came from clusters of 2 printings, and none of the 157 clusters of 50 or more was checked.\n88\t  - Hand-check the largest clusters for chaining, where shared runs join stories that aren't the same.\n89\t- The reel-to-batch map (decision 29):\n90\t  - Build it from the batch paths in the candidates and samples, so a page with no batch path gets its IIIF service from local data. That's about 367,000 of lexicon v2's candidates (decision 31).\n91\t  - Validate it: hide the batch on pages that have one, and count how often the map gets it back.\n92\t  - Flag every reel that maps to more than one batch, such as a reissued ver01 and ver02.\n93\t- No loc.gov lookups yet for the 27,817 candidates whose reels the map doesn't cover. At 20 a minute they would take about a day, and most won't be reports.\n94\t  - Resolve only the ones the reader makes reports, through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s.\n95\t  - Cache each answer, which `page_service` doesn't do yet, so no page is looked up twice.\n96\t\n97\t**Gate**\n98\t- Schema tests pass, and `make db-init` can run twice.\n99\t- The budget is written down.\n100\t- Geocoder accuracy is measured on a sample.\n101\t- The reel-to-batch map's hit rate on hidden batches is reported, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged.\n102\t\n103\t### 2C: the reader\n104\t**Persona:** ML specialist.\n105\t1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n106\t   - The gold set's term strata include each of lexicon v2's five rules. That's how ouija, kept with 0 reports in its 6 sampled candidates, gets measured (decision 31).\n107\t   - `POLICY.md` settles delusions and hallucinations with one rule. Eval A counts a 1904 defendant pointing at an \"imaginary\" spectre in court as a report, and a 1909 lunacy hearing's spooks as other. Its interval barely moves either way.\n108\t   - It also settles claims of spirit control and of a felt presence of the dead. Decision 30 counts two such articles as borderline reports, and they are half of the reports it found.\n109\t   - And decision 31's two borderline kinds: a claim inside a narrative whose truth is doubtful, and a particular claim told inside a general essay. It confirms or overrules counting a first-person claim of communication with a named dead person, made in a lecture or an interview.\n110\t2. Build the labelling page.\n111\t3. Label the gold set before seeing any model output.\n112\t4. Teacher labels for the training pool, with the leakage check passing.\n113\t5. Baselines: a keyword rule, and base 4B both zero-shot and few-shot.\n114\t6. LoRA training on Tinker, iterating on the dev split until improvements stop. Each iteration is recorded.\n115\t7. Eval B, ablations B1 to B10, and edge-case slices E1 to E14.\n116\t   - Eval B also has the reader screen the random reservoirs, for a recall bound that doesn't rest on flag words (decision 30). The Tinker credit check sets how many it reads.\n117\t   - That screen gives lexicon v2's independent recall. Decision 31's 84% is in-sample.\n118\t8. The failure log, and `eval/MODEL_CARD.md`.\n119\t9. The local export and the offline proof.\n120\t10. The full pass over all triaged candidates.\n121\t   - Lexicon v2 makes the reader's job 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's (decision 31). The Tinker credit check weighs that before the pass starts.\n122\t\n123\t**Gate**\n124\t- The gold hash hasn't changed since the freeze, and the leakage check passes.\n125\t- The eval JSON and charts include confidence intervals.\n126\t- At least one failure is written up with its fix.\n127\t- The offline proof passes, and the full-pass counts are recorded.\n128\t- The EM audits 30 random reader outputs against their clippings.\n129\t\n130\t### 2D: load, cluster and harvest\n131\t**Persona:** data engineer.\n132\t- Geocode, cluster, embed, and load Atlas.\n133\t- `make db-verify` runs geo, fuzzy and vector queries and checks their expected results and latencies.\n134\t- The top-case rule, and the follow-up harvest as a second Temporal pass.\n135\t\n136\t**Gate**\n137\t- Atlas counts match the local counts, and storage is within the limit.\n138\t- `db-verify` passes.\n139\t- The harvest has written output for every case.\n140\t\n141\t## 5. Phase 3: backend and APIs\n142\t"}]},"uuid":"3b5d64d8-fe53-467b-b10b-0c7555932113","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:01:23.338Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md","content":"### 2B: schemas and data layer\n**Persona:** data architect.\n- Pydantic models, with Atlas validators generated from them.\n- `make db-init` creates every collection and index, and can be re-run safely.\n- A storage budget built from real document sizes, and a written decision on what goes into Atlas.\n- The GeoNames geocoder and its manifest. The LCCN title parser, tested on at least 200 distinct titles.\n- The reprint-clustering design, and its evaluation set:\n  - Sample per candidate as well as per cluster. Eval A's check drew clusters uniformly, so 11 of its 20 pairs came from clusters of 2 printings, and none of the 157 clusters of 50 or more was checked.\n  - Hand-check the largest clusters for chaining, where shared runs join stories that aren't the same.\n- The reel-to-batch map (decision 29):\n  - Build it from the batch paths in the candidates and samples, so a page with no batch path gets its IIIF service from local data. That's about 367,000 of lexicon v2's candidates (decision 31).\n  - Validate it: hide the batch on pages that have one, and count how often the map gets it back.\n  - Flag every reel that maps to more than one batch, such as a reissued ver01 and ver02.\n- No loc.gov lookups yet for the 27,817 candidates whose reels the map doesn't cover. At 20 a minute they would take about a day, and most won't be reports.\n  - Resolve only the ones the reader makes reports, through `crops.get` at one request every 3.5 s.\n  - Cache each answer, which `page_service` doesn't do yet, so no page is looked up twice.\n\n**Gate**\n- Schema tests pass, and `make db-init` can run twice.\n- The budget is written down.\n- Geocoder accuracy is measured on a sample.\n- The reel-to-batch map's hit rate on hidden batches is reported, and every reel with more than one batch is flagged.\n\n### 2C: the reader\n**Persona:** ML specialist.\n1. Write `POLICY.md`, build the gold set, freeze it and hash it.\n   - The gold set's term strata include each of lexicon v2's five rules. That's how ouija, kept with 0 reports in its 6 sampled candidates, gets measured (decision 31).\n   - `POLICY.md` settles delusions and hallucinations with one rule. Eval A counts a 1904 defendant pointing at an \"imaginary\" spectre in court as a report, and a 1909 lunacy hearing's spooks as other. Its interval barely moves either way.\n   - It also settles claims of spirit control and of a felt presence of the dead. Decision 30 counts two such articles as borderline reports, and they are half of the reports it found.\n   - And decision 31's two borderline kinds: a claim inside a narrative whose truth is doubtful, and a particular claim told inside a general essay. It confirms or overrules counting a first-person claim of communication with a named dead person, made in a lecture or an interview.\n2. Build the labelling page.\n3. Label the gold set before seeing any model output.\n4. Teacher labels for the training pool, with the leakage check passing.\n5. Baselines: a keyword rule, and base 4B both zero-shot and few-shot.\n6. LoRA training on Tinker, iterating on the dev split until improvements stop. Each iteration is recorded.\n7. Eval B, ablations B1 to B10, and edge-case slices E1 to E14.\n   - Eval B also has the reader screen the random reservoirs, for a recall bound that doesn't rest on flag words (decision 30). The Tinker credit check sets how many it reads.\n   - That screen gives lexicon v2's independent recall. Decision 31's 84% is in-sample.\n8. The failure log, and `eval/MODEL_CARD.md`.\n9. The local export and the offline proof.\n10. The full pass over all triaged candidates.\n   - Lexicon v2 makes the reader's job 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's (decision 31). The Tinker credit check weighs that before the pass starts.\n\n**Gate**\n- The gold hash hasn't changed since the freeze, and the leakage check passes.\n- The eval JSON and charts include confidence intervals.\n- At least one failure is written up with its fix.\n- The offline proof passes, and the full-pass counts are recorded.\n- The EM audits 30 random reader outputs against their clippings.\n\n### 2D: load, cluster and harvest\n**Persona:** data engineer.\n- Geocode, cluster, embed, and load Atlas.\n- `make db-verify` runs geo, fuzzy and vector queries and checks their expected results and latencies.\n- The top-case rule, and the follow-up harvest as a second Temporal pass.\n\n**Gate**\n- Atlas counts match the local counts, and storage is within the limit.\n- `db-verify` passes.\n- The harvest has written output for every case.\n\n## 5. Phase 3: backend and APIs\n","numLines":63,"startLine":80,"totalLines":235}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"addbc8cf-ce79-4e54-a72f-517e297c9d6d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
