{"parentUuid":"4a8827da-17c3-464e-81cd-ec9b844698b6","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"3c21d421-8439-4ef3-a792-2ee8b64888f0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<tools-used-before-summary>Agent, Bash, Edit, Read, SendMessage, Skill, TaskStop, ToolSearch, WebSearch, Write, mcp__Claude_Browser__browser_batch, mcp__Claude_Browser__computer, mcp__Claude_Browser__find, mcp__Claude_Browser__get_page_text, mcp__Claude_Browser__javascript_tool, mcp__Claude_Browser__navigate, mcp__ccd_pr__get_status, mcp__ccd_session__mark_chapter, mcp__ccd_turn__update_plan, mcp__ccd_turn__wrap_up</tools-used-before-summary>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n\n   **Setup and standing rules.** This is a child session of the EM session `local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e`, on the Paper Ghosts project (repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts`, at `/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts`). Hand-over notes go to `/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-X.md`.\n\n   Standing rules, still in force (verbatim where possible):\n   - \"never print, log or echo secrets from `.env`, and check keys by shape only\"\n   - \"No global installs and no Homebrew\"\n   - \"Stop and ask before anything public (repo visibility, deploys, DNS, posting) or any spending beyond free tiers and existing credits\"\n   - \"Make small commits on phase branches and open pull requests into `main`; I merge them\"\n   - \"Record numbered decisions in `Docs/DECISIONS.md`, and end each milestone with a STATUS block in `Docs/RESUME.md`\"\n   - \"Entire capture is enabled through `.claude/settings.json`; don't touch it\"\n   - \"Don't run brainstorming Q&A with the owner; put questions in your reply to me\"\n   - \"Start every reply to me with `<step>: DONE` or `<step>: BLOCKED — reason`, then 150 words at most\"\n   - Scope pgrep and ps to my uid, and never print the hostname. Filter push output with `grep -v -i -F \"$(hostname -s)\"`.\n   - Before every commit, scan the staged diff with `uv run python $S/scan_staged.py .env <diff>` (names only), and count machine-name matches. Check the scan's output before committing.\n   - loc.gov's JSON API: at most one request every 3.5 s in all, never in parallel.\n   - \"Never touch sample_mflix or any other database\". The EM is taking `sample_mflix` to the owner.\n   - \"check claims like 'pushed' with `ls-remote` before you report them\"\n   - Commits end with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. PR bodies end with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`.\n   - Scratch directory: `S=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad`.\n\n   **Completed this stretch:**\n   - 2A.8: PR #3, merged at `718de62`.\n   - 2B.1: passed the EM's review.\n\n   **Current: Phase 2B.2,** on branch `phase-2b-data-layer`, covering titles, title records and the geocoder. It started with the EM's 2B.2 brief (step 0 budget scenario, parser, LoC records, then-and-now, GeoNames geocoder, accuracy). Then came the EM's latest review, which changes the plan (quoted in section 6).\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - **Tooling:** uv with Python 3.12, ruff (`make lint`), pytest (`make test`; the `atlas` marker skips without `MONGODB_URI`).\n   - **Stats:** Wilson intervals via `paperghosts.stats.wilson(hits, n)` and `eval_a.rate`.\n   - **Pydantic models** in `paperghosts/schemas`:\n     - Report, Place, Case, Stat, Printing, Stated, Versions, Title.\n     - Validators are generated by `paperghosts/store/validators.py`, handling $ref, const, integer, prefixItems, `not`, and `json_schema_extra` anyOf.\n     - Stored with `model_dump(by_alias=True, exclude_none=True)`.\n   - **Atlas M0:**\n     - Counts dataSize plus indexSize from dbStats against \"0.5 GB\"; planned at 500,000,000 bytes.\n     - Search indexes aren't in dbStats.\n     - Cap of 3 Search indexes cluster-wide; `sample_mflix.movies` holds `default`, so `places_search` is pending.\n   - **Title parser** (`paperghosts/geo/titles.py`):\n     - outputs title, place, county, state as printed, supplied_place, supplied_state, years, first_year, last_year\n     - `state_name`; the `then_and_now` display\n     - ambiguous territories (W.T., N.T., I.T., D.T., Dakota) map to None\n   - **LoC title records** (`paperghosts/geo/loc.py`):\n     - `www.loc.gov/item/{lccn}/?fo=json`, cached in `data/loc/titles/{lccn}.json` with url, retrieved, status and body\n     - a 404 is cached; 5xx is not cached and is counted as an error\n     - `COUNTS` holds requests, cache_hits and errors\n   - **GeoNames:**\n     - US, PR and VI dumps, plus admin1/admin2 codes, in `data/geonames/`; the committed manifest has URLs, dates, sha256s and the CC BY 4.0 licence\n     - names compared space-free with `name_key` and `flat`\n     - tie-break rule: county, living, name over alternate, seat, population, ID\n     - ghost towns are class S \"(historical)\" features, accepted only with the record's county\n   - **Record check** (`paperghosts/geo/papers.py`, `paper_place`): the printed place is geocoded and checked against the record's county. On a mismatch or no result, the record's place is used, then a ghost town, else no point.\n   - **Accuracy draw:** walk the papers in seeded order (seed \"2B geocoder\") to 200 distinct (place, state) pairs; answers checked by hand in `eval/results/phase2b/geocoder_expected.json`.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/papers.py`** (new, uncommitted). The record check; 6 tests pass.\n     ```python\n     def towns(title): [supplied_place, place] deduped\n     def modern_state(title): title.modern_state or state_name(supplied_state or state)\n     def printed_place(gazetteer, title): tries towns in modern state with title county\n     def paper_place(gazetteer, title, given=None):\n         printed = printed_place(...)\n         if not given or not given.get(\"place\"): return printed | {\"record\": \"none\"}\n         counties = given.get(\"counties\") or []\n         if not counties: (printed → \"has no county to check\"; else geocode record place → \"found what the printed place didn't, unchecked\")\n         agrees = gazetteer.same_county(printed, counties) if printed[\"place_id\"] else False\n         if agrees is None: return printed | {\"record\": \"can't be checked against GeoNames' counties\"}\n         if agrees: return printed | {\"record\": \"agrees\"}\n         how = \"replaced the printed place\" if printed resolved else \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n         for county in counties: catalogued = geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], county); if resolved and same_county → return catalogued | {\"record\": how}\n         ghost = gazetteer.ghost(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], counties) → {\"record\": \"a ghost town in the record's county\"}\n         else base() | {\"reason\": \"outside the record's county, whose own place isn't in GeoNames\", \"record\": \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\"}\n     ```\n\n   - **`tests/test_papers.py`** (new). Built on a fake gazetteer with Bloomington (Story County, IA), Muscatine, Saginaw, Clifton (historical) class S in Sully County, Charles Town and Lardo in Nebraska. Six tests:\n     - agrees\n     - replaced (Bloomington to Muscatine)\n     - merged (East Saginaw to Saginaw)\n     - ghost town\n     - no point when nothing agrees\n     - no record or no county\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/geonames.py`** (rewritten class, uncommitted):\n     - Constants: `DUMP`; `TERRITORIES = {\"PR\": \"Puerto Rico\", \"VI\": \"Virgin Islands\"}`; `FILES` includes PR.zip and VI.zip; `OUTSIDE = {\"Guam\", \"American Samoa\"}`.\n     - `COUNTY_AFTER = {\"co\",\"county\",\"parish\",\"borough\",\"census\",\"area\",\"municipality\",\"city\",\"and\",\"independent\"}`; `COUNTY_BEFORE = {\"city\",\"and\",\"county\",\"borough\",\"of\",\"town\",\"municipality\"}`; `HISTORICAL = \" (historical)\"`.\n     - `Gazetteer(places=None, admin1=None, admin2=None, source=None)`:\n       - takes a list of dumps (default US, PR and VI)\n       - reads states by lowercase name, with `u.s. virgin islands` added\n       - a territory's admin1 serves as its county\n       - methods: `add(f)`, `base()`, `result(place)`, `geocode(town, state, county=None)`, `same_county(found, counties)` (None for no counties or a planning region), `ghost(town, state, counties)`\n     - `fetch()` extracts US.txt, PR.txt and VI.txt and writes the manifest.\n     - Real data checks:\n       - Ponce 4566880 and San Juan 4568127 resolve.\n       - Charlotte Amalie 4795467 (Saint Thomas Island) and Christiansted 4795479 resolve.\n       - Clifton resolves as a ghost: 5763634, Sully County, historical True. The gazetteer holds 107,543 ghost entries.\n\n   - **`tests/test_geonames.py`** (updated, 21 pass):\n     - The ROWS fixture is compact, with `# fmt: skip`.\n     - A Charleston row lists \"Charlestown\" as an alternate; DeKalb is included.\n     - The territories fixture covers Onida, the Clifton ghost, Litchfield in a planning region, Charles Town and Ponce in PR.txt.\n     - Tests: state case-insensitivity, Puerto Rico, ghost town, the same_county cases.\n     - The PR unresolved case was changed to `(\"Hagatna\", \"Guam\", \"outside GeoNames' dumps here\")`.\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/loc.py`** (committed in 6c0dbd7, then edited):\n     - `title_record(lccn, cache=CACHE)`.\n     - `recorded(record)` now returns:\n       ```python\n       counties = [c.title() for c in item.get(\"location_county\") or []]\n       return {\"place\": place or None, \"state\": state or None, \"county\": counties[0] if counties else None, \"counties\": counties, \"title\": item.get(\"title\"), \"years\": item.get(\"dates_of_publication\")}\n       ```\n       This last change is uncommitted.\n     - The 5xx handling, which is committed:\n       ```python\n       except httpx.HTTPStatusError as exc:\n           status, body = exc.response.status_code, None\n           if status != 404 and status < 500: raise\n       ...\n       if status >= 500:  # still failing after get's retries: not cached, so asked again later\n           COUNTS[\"errors\"] += 1\n           return record\n       ```\n\n   - **`tests/test_loc_titles.py`**: tests for caching, the 404, the 520 (not cached, counted), and `recorded`. The last now expects counties `[\"Santa Cruz\", \"Pima\"]` for \"Tucson, Arizona\". All pass.\n\n   - **`paperghosts/ingest/crops.py`**: `BUSY = {429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 520, 521, 522, 523, 524}  # 52x: Cloudflare's` (committed).\n\n   - **`paperghosts/schemas/__init__.py`**: the `Title` model (committed). It has `_id` (the LCCN), title, place, county, state, supplied_place, supplied_state, modern_state, then_and_now, years, first_year/last_year (`Annotated[int, Field(ge=1690, le=2100)] | None`), `source: Literal[\"title string\",\"loc.gov title record\",\"none\"]`, record_url and retrieved. Planned: add catalogued_place, catalogued_county and catalogued_state.\n\n   - **`scripts/titles.py`** (committed, plus an \"errors_not_cached\" edit):\n     - `year_titles` and `most_complete(papers)`.\n     - `entry(lccn, papers, states)`: fetches a record only when no string names a place; it puts the record's place and state into supplied_place and supplied_state, sets source \"loc.gov title record\", record_url and retrieved.\n     - `main()` writes `data/titles/titles.json` and `eval/results/phase2b/titles.json`. The summary holds lccns, places_from, candidates_by_source, printed_state_differs_from_modern, no_modern_state, and `loc_gov` {requests, cache_hits, errors_not_cached, records_cached, not_found}.\n     - It is currently running as task bkye7nzvk.\n\n   - **`scripts/geocode_check.py`** (uncommitted):\n     - `query(title)`, `geocode(gazetteer, towns, state, county)`, `order(lccns)` (seeded shuffle), `draw()` (walks to 200 distinct pairs), `rate`, `review`.\n     - `main()` compares results with `geocoder_expected.json`. A pair is right when `found[\"place_id\"] == want[\"place_id\"] and want[\"failure\"] is None`.\n     - The stories section is text-only, with `town_only_with_the_papers_state` counts.\n     - It writes `eval/results/phase2b/geocoder.json`. `--review` prints candidates.\n\n   - **`eval/results/phase2b/geocoder_expected.json`** (uncommitted):\n     - `checked`, `papers` (200 entries keyed \"town|state\", each with place_id, failure and note), and `stories` (24 entries with report, date, given, town, state, county, place_id, true_place_id, paper_state, note).\n     - Failures and their right answers:\n       - Ponce|Puerto Rico: None, \"outside GeoNames' US dump\". This needs updating to 4566880 now that PR is loaded.\n       - Bloomington|Iowa: 4868404 (Muscatine)\n       - East Saginaw|Michigan: 5007989\n       - Lardo|Idaho: 5600363\n       - New Oregon|Iowa: 4853059\n       - Norfolk and Portsmouth|Virginia: 4776222\n       - Clifton|South Dakota: 5763634\n     - Story true IDs: Chicago 4887398, Silver Lake IN 4926434, Trenton 5105496, Plymouth MA 4947597, Bordentown 5095847, Middlebury 5238499, Waterloo OR 5554953, Chittenden 5234855, Adamsville OH 5145410.\n\n   - **Scratch helpers in $S:**\n     - `titles.json`, the collected title strings; its \"null\" keys stand for None\n     - `review.py` and `dryrun.py` (Lazy-lookup harnesses)\n     - `geocoder-sample.json`, `geocoder-review.txt`, `stories-expected.json`, `prefetch.py`, `members.py`, `budget_math.py`\n     - logs: `titles-run.log`, `budget-probe.log`\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/titles.py`** (committed): the parser. The Hot Springs fix (`after_state` and the all-state bracket) is in `f309dea`.\n   - **`tests/test_titles.py`** and **`tests/fixtures/titles.json`**: 200 titles, checked by hand.\n   - **`scripts/budget_stories.py`** and **`eval/results/phase2b/budget_stories.json`** (committed): 0.6076 stories per printing for v1 and 0.802 for the candidates v2 added. The top of the range is 61,960 stories, and the point is 32,761.\n   - **Docs** (uncommitted edits):\n     - DECISIONS: decision 34 appended, with placeholders `TITLE_RECORD_COUNTS` and `TITLE_LOOKUP_COUNTS`.\n     - BUILD-PLAN: a 2C reader state note; 2D geocoder follow-ups; title lookup lever.\n     - ARCHITECTURE section 3.3: title lookup bullet.\n     - RESUME: next step, with 2B.1 passed and 2B.2 built.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **False \"pushed\" claim on `1254820`.** The EM: \"Push it, and check claims like 'pushed' with `ls-remote` before you report them.\" Pushed, then verified with ls-remote.\n   - **Budget probe crashes**, all fixed:\n     - KeyError on `service`: used `p.get`.\n     - Missing LCCN: taken from MEMBER.\n     - Edition and page were \"na\": made optional, then the EM's member-location fix.\n   - **Index sizes polluted by retained pages.** The probe now measures on a fresh scratch collection.\n   - **A scan named `SENTRY_PROJECT`.** False positive: the shared prefix \"paperghosts-\". I had committed before reading the scan; since then the scan is read first.\n   - **Title parser:**\n     - \"H.T.\" was added to Hawaii's forms.\n     - The \"null\" title placeholder came from the collect script; title-less LCCNs were replaced via a second seed.\n     - The Hot Springs bracket fix first broke `[New York, N.Y.]` and `[i.e. Washington, D.C.]`; it was narrowed to brackets that follow the state.\n   - **Geocoder:**\n     - Charlestown resolved to Charleston: fixed with space-free names.\n     - Dalles didn't match \"The Dalles\": names are now also indexed without \"the\".\n     - Ponce was counted right because None == None: a recorded failure is now never right.\n   - **The titles.py run crashed on a 520.** Fixed by adding 52x to BUSY and not caching 5xx.\n   - **The background build was slow** (about 13 s per request, loc.gov's latency). It was stopped, the draw's records were fetched first, and it was restarted. The EM said never to run requests in parallel.\n   - **Lint:**\n     - SIM117 nested `with`: combined.\n     - A stray `head -c 0` broke an `&&` chain: rerun.\n     - The formatter expanded fixture rows: `# fmt: skip` and double quotes.\n   - **2B.1 db-init crashed on the Search cap.** Now reports the index it couldn't create and exits 1.\n   - **Not tuning on the test set:** I declined to tune the geocoder on its failures; the EM later asked for specific changes.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Accuracy before the record check: 193/200 (96.5%, 93.0–98.3%); 193/194 when resolved. The record check is meant to fix Bloomington, the 3 merged towns and Clifton; the PR dump fixes Ponce.\n   - Norfolk and Portsmouth may remain a failure.\n   - The results need measuring with and without the check once the 200 pairs' records are fetched.\n   - Story places: never fall back on the paper's state (EM rule).\n\n6. All user messages (cross-session EM messages):\n   - **The 2A.8 approval:** lexicon v2 with conditions (keep v1, record rules, re-read, measure on the 200, in-sample recall, regenerate charts, close out).\n   - **The 2A.8 addendum:** give each new rule's marginal yield with Wilson intervals; \"Don't tune or narrow any rule on the 200.\"\n   - **The 2B.1 brief:**\n     - keep all five rules\n     - Atlas notes into decision 32 and section 4\n     - Pydantic models and validators\n     - the BinData check\n     - db-init (never touch `sample_mflix` or any other database)\n     - the budget from real sizes\n     - the gate\n   - **The 2B.1 review:**\n     - push\n     - printing location from the member name: edition, reel, issue and frame required; LCCN from the member; page from the scan only\n     - frame hazard: the batch path wins\n     - the verified-quote rule\n     - year, month_day and decade checks\n     - \"Search slot: leave sample_mflix alone ... list it as pending at the gate\"\n     - remeasure the budget\n     - int8 note for 2D\n   - **The second 2B.1 review:**\n     - \"1254820 isn't on origin ... check claims like 'pushed' with ls-remote before you report them\"\n     - BUDGET.md levers measured offline (bbox int16 and int32, title lookup, display_headline, int8)\n     - what Atlas counts, with sources\n     - the 25 judged reports\n     - \"Text and search stay whole.\"\n   - **The 2B.2 brief:**\n     - step 0: a third budget scenario\n     - the title parser, on at least 200 titles checked by hand\n     - LoC title records only for LCCNs with no place or state (3.5 s apart, cached, counted)\n     - then and now, with a test of 1835 Charlestown\n     - the GeoNames US dump, with a manifest\n     - accuracy: 200 pairs and the 24 reports, with Wilson intervals and failure kinds\n     - the gate; report before 2B.3\n   - **The latest EM message (verbatim):**\n     \"EM review of 2B.2 so far. The parser fixtures and the 200 geocoded pairs I sampled are right, and so is the third budget scenario. The only wrong answer is the dangerous kind, a pin in the wrong town (Bloomington, Iowa), and the LoC record can catch those. Changes:\n     1. **Paper places come from the title record first.** loc.gov's item gives `location_city`, `location_county` and `location_state` as catalogued today. Where a record exists:\n        - Geocode its city, county and state. Keep the printed place for display (\"Bloomington, Iowa, now Muscatine\").\n        - Check that GeoNames' county (admin2) is the record's county. On a mismatch, take the record's place, and count those.\n        - This also settles the merged towns, which are catalogued under their successors: East Saginaw, Lardo, New Oregon.\n     2. **Measure it now:** fetch the records for the 200 pairs' LCCNs and rerun the accuracy with and without the check. Queue that after the current fetch, never alongside it: one request every 3.5 s in all.\n     3. **The rest of the LCCNs:** fetch them in 2D, only for those among stored printings (decision 29's pattern), cached. Add that to the plan.\n     4. **Inputs:** add GeoNames' PR.zip, and any other territory the titles name, to the inputs and the manifest.\n     5. **Ghost towns:** accept \"(historical)\" class S features only when the record's county matches, flagged as historical. Clifton, S.D. is the case.\n     6. **For 2C and 2D (plan note):** a story place that is only a town resolves from a county or state the text gives, or from its dateline. Otherwise it stays unresolved. Never fall back on the paper's state, which was right for only 1 of the 3 here. Measure this on the 2C gold set.\n     Then the gate as briefed, with the accuracy with and without the record check.\"\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Wait for the background `scripts/titles.py` (task bkye7nzvk) to finish the remaining LoC records; 178 of 424 were cached at last check. Then read its counts into decision 34's placeholders. Stop the Temporal processes? Not running.\n   - Add `catalogued_place`, `catalogued_county` and `catalogued_state` to the Title model. Fill them in `scripts/titles.py` from any cached record, and use them in the display: `then_and_now` gives \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\" when the catalogued city differs, compared space-free.\n   - **After the current fetch completes,** fetch the records for the 200 sampled pairs' LCCNs, sequentially, cached, about 176 new requests. Never alongside the current fetch.\n   - Update `geocode_check.py` to report the accuracy without the record check (`printed_place`) and with it (`paper_place` using `loc.recorded(title_record(lccn))`). Count the record outcomes (agrees, replaced, found, ghost town, disagrees).\n   - Update the expected answers: Ponce becomes 4566880 with no failure, now that PR is loaded.\n   - Rerun the check; write `eval/results/phase2b/geocoder.json`.\n   - Plan notes in BUILD-PLAN:\n     - 2D fetches records only for stored printings' LCCNs, cached (decision 29's pattern)\n     - the 2C/2D story-place rule: from a county or state in the text, or a dateline; otherwise unresolved; never the paper's state; measured on the 2C gold set\n   - Update decision 34:\n     - the record check results, with and without\n     - PR/VI inputs and the manifest\n     - ghost towns\n     - the counts\n     - the story-place rule\n     - remove the earlier \"paper's state\" suggestions; fix the 2D follow-up list\n   - Update section 4/3.3 if needed, and the RESUME next step.\n   - Write the hand-over note `step-2B.2.md`.\n   - Run lint and the full tests (`make test`, including atlas).\n   - Commit in small parts, scanning each first: geonames territories/ghosts and manifest; papers.py, loc counties and Title catalogued fields; geocode_check, expected answers and results; titles.json summary; docs.\n   - Push `phase-2b-data-layer`, verify with `git ls-remote`, and report \"2B.2: DONE\" (150 words max) with the accuracy with and without the record check.\n\n8. Current Work:\n\n   Implementing the EM's latest 2B.2 review. Done so far, all uncommitted:\n   - `geonames.py` rewritten: PR/VI dumps fetched (the manifest now lists US.zip, PR.zip, VI.zip, the admin files and the extracted US/PR/VI .txt); case-insensitive states; `same_county`; `ghost`; wider `county_key`.\n   - `papers.py` with `paper_place`, its tests (6 pass).\n   - `recorded()` now returns `counties`, its tests (10 pass together).\n\n   The last command ran `uv run pytest tests/test_loc_titles.py tests/test_papers.py -q`, giving \"10 passed\". The background `scripts/titles.py` is still fetching records (task bkye7nzvk; 178 of 424 cached at last check).\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n\n   Continue the EM's latest instructions. Add the Title model's catalogued fields and the \"now Muscatine\" display, test first. Then update `scripts/geocode_check.py` to measure with and without `paper_place`. Once the current fetch finishes (and not before), fetch the 200 pairs' records, as the EM said: \"**Measure it now:** fetch the records for the 200 pairs' LCCNs and rerun the accuracy with and without the check. Queue that after the current fetch, never alongside it: one request every 3.5 s in all.\" Then finish the plan notes (items 3 and 6), decision 34, commits, a verified push, and the gate report \"with the accuracy with and without the record check.\"\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/hotragn/.claude/projects/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"87cf482a-673d-4e7e-a3ff-7826fe929bf4","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.696Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":0,"turnIndex":37},"turnOrigin":"peer","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"87cf482a-673d-4e7e-a3ff-7826fe929bf4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_titles.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_titles.py","content":"\"\"\"The LoC title parser: the title, the place and the state as printed, what the cataloguer\nsupplied in brackets, and the years. Its hard cases are one test each. tests/fixtures/titles.json\nholds 200 titles drawn at random from the candidates' 3,336 LCCNs, each with its expected output\nchecked by hand.\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import parse_title, state_name, then_and_now\n\nFIXTURE = Path(__file__).parent / \"fixtures\" / \"titles.json\"\n\n\ndef parsed(paper, **fields):\n    found = parse_title(paper)\n    return {k: found[k] for k in fields}\n\n\ndef test_a_title_gives_its_place_state_and_years():\n    found = parse_title(\"Eureka daily sentinel. [volume] (Eureka, Nev.) 1871-1887\")\n\n    assert found == {\n        \"title\": \"Eureka daily sentinel.\",\n        \"place\": \"Eureka\",\n        \"county\": None,\n        \"state\": \"Nev.\",\n        \"supplied_place\": None,\n        \"supplied_state\": None,\n        \"years\": \"1871-1887\",\n        \"first_year\": 1871,\n        \"last_year\": 1887,\n    }\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"paper\", \"fields\"),\n    [\n        # a marker after the title, of either kind\n        (\"Daily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975\",\n         {\"title\": \"Daily Kennebec journal.\", \"place\": \"Augusta\", \"state\": \"Me.\"}),\n        # the state only in brackets: supplied by the cataloguer, not printed\n        (\"New Orleans daily crescent. [volume] (New Orleans [La.]) 1851-1866\",\n         {\"place\": \"New Orleans\", \"state\": None, \"supplied_state\": \"La.\"}),\n        (\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\",\n         {\"place\": \"Washington\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n        (\"National Republican. (Washington City (D.C.)) 1872-1888\",\n         {\"place\": \"Washington City\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n        # no place at all: a short form, or a title record's job\n        (\"The New York herald.\",\n         {\"title\": \"The New York herald.\", \"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"years\": None}),\n        # a county between the place and the state, and a territory with its state supplied\n        (\"Devils Lake inter-ocean. (Devils Lake, Ramsey Co., Dakota [N.D.]) 1884-1905\",\n         {\"place\": \"Devils Lake\", \"county\": \"Ramsey Co.\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_state\": \"N.D.\"}),\n        # corrections: printed first, the correction supplied\n        (\"Juliet signal. [volume] (Juliet [i.e. Joliet], Ill.) 1844-1???\",\n         {\"place\": \"Juliet\", \"supplied_place\": \"Joliet\", \"state\": \"Ill.\", \"first_year\": 1844, \"last_year\": None}),\n        (\"Grant County herald. [volume] (Lancaster, W.T. [i.e. Wis.]) 1843-1844\",\n         {\"state\": \"W.T.\", \"supplied_state\": \"Wis.\"}),\n        (\"Marble Hill press. (Marbel [sic] Hill, Mo.) 1881-1923\",\n         {\"place\": \"Marbel Hill\", \"supplied_place\": None}),\n        # the whole place supplied\n        (\"Nashville union. [volume] ([Nashville, Tenn.]) 1851-1853\",\n         {\"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"supplied_place\": \"Nashville\", \"supplied_state\": \"Tenn.\"}),\n        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\",\n         {\"place\": \"Bannock City\", \"state\": \"I.T.\", \"supplied_place\": \"Idaho City\", \"supplied_state\": \"Idaho\"}),\n        # a bracket after the state that starts with a state supplies only a state, with i.e. inside\n        (\"The Hot Springs star. (Hot Spings [Hot Springs], Dakota [Dakota Territory, i.e. S.D.]) 1886-189?\",\n         {\"place\": \"Hot Spings\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_place\": \"Hot Springs\", \"supplied_state\": \"S.D.\"}),\n        # no comma before the state, a semicolon after it, an ampersand between two\n        (\"Cheraw gazette and Pee Dee farmer. [volume] (Cheraw S.C.) 1838-1839\",\n         {\"place\": \"Cheraw\", \"state\": \"S.C.\"}),\n        (\"Backbone. [volume] (St. Paul, Minn. ;) 1897-1906\",\n         {\"place\": \"St. Paul\", \"state\": \"Minn.\"}),\n        (\"Bristol news. [volume] (Bristol, Va. &amp; Tenn.) 1867-189?\",\n         {\"place\": \"Bristol\", \"state\": \"Va. & Tenn.\", \"last_year\": None}),\n        # HTML entities, and UTF-8 that was read as Windows-1252\n        (\"Coeur d&#39;Alene evening press. (Coeur d&#39;Alene, Idaho) 1907-1929\",\n         {\"title\": \"Coeur d'Alene evening press.\", \"place\": \"Coeur d'Alene\"}),\n        (\"Deutscher herold. [volume] (Sioux Falls, SuÌˆd-Dakota) 1907-1918\",\n         {\"state\": \"Süd-Dakota\"}),\n        (\"The Maryland independent. [volume] (Port Tobacco, Md.) 1874-current\",\n         {\"first_year\": 1874, \"last_year\": None, \"years\": \"1874-current\"}),\n    ],\n)  # fmt: skip\ndef test_the_hard_cases(paper, fields):\n    assert parsed(paper, **fields) == fields\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"printed\", \"modern\"),\n    [\n        (\"Va.\", \"Virginia\"),\n        (\"W. Va.\", \"West Virginia\"),\n        (\"Süd-Dakota\", \"South Dakota\"),\n        (\"Nuevo México\", \"New Mexico\"),\n        (\"A.T.\", \"Arizona\"),\n        (\"Minn. Territory\", \"Minnesota\"),\n        (\"Va. & Tenn.\", \"Virginia\"),\n        # a territory's initials can stand for two: the record or the brackets decide\n        (\"W.T.\", None),\n        (\"N.T.\", None),\n        (\"I.T.\", None),\n        (\"Dakota\", None),\n    ],\n)\ndef test_a_printed_state_names_its_modern_state_unless_it_could_be_two(printed, modern):\n    assert state_name(printed) == modern\n\n\ndef test_then_and_now_says_when_a_place_has_changed_state():\n    # 1835: the Virginia free press, of Charlestown, Va., now West Virginia (sn84026784)\n    title = parse_title(\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\")\n\n    assert then_and_now(title, \"West Virginia\") == \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"\n    assert (\n        then_and_now(\n            parse_title(\"Alexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 1834-1974\"),\n            \"Virginia\",\n        )\n        == \"Alexandria, D.C. (now Va.)\"\n    )\n    assert (\n        then_and_now(\n            parse_title(\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\"),\n            \"District of Columbia\",\n        )\n        == \"Washington, D.C.\"\n    )\n    assert (\n        then_and_now(\n            parse_title(\n                \"Aberdeen herald. [volume] (Aberdeen, Chehalis County, W.T.) 1886-1917\"\n            ),\n            \"Washington\",\n        )\n        == \"Aberdeen, W.T. (now Wash.)\"\n    )\n    assert then_and_now(parse_title(\"The New York herald.\"), \"New York\") is None\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"paper\", \"modern\", \"catalogued\", \"shown\"),\n    [\n        # the record's city, as catalogued today, where the town has a new name\n        (\"Muscatine journal. [volume] (Bloomington, Iowa) 1849-185?\", \"Iowa\", \"Muscatine\",\n         \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\"),\n        (\"East Saginaw courier. (East Saginaw, Mich.) 1859-18??\", \"Michigan\", \"Saginaw\",\n         \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\"),\n        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\", \"Idaho\", \"Idaho City\",\n         \"Bannock City, I.T. (now Idaho City, Idaho)\"),\n        # the same town, spelled apart, isn't a new name\n        (\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\", \"West Virginia\", \"Charles Town\",\n         \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"),\n        # a title that prints no place shows the record's\n        (\"The Phoenix Jewish news.\", \"Arizona\", \"Phoenix\", \"Phoenix, Ariz.\"),\n    ],\n)  # fmt: skip\ndef test_then_and_now_gives_the_records_name_for_a_town_renamed(paper, modern, catalogued, shown):\n    assert then_and_now(parse_title(paper), modern, catalogued) == shown\n\n\ndef test_two_hundred_titles_checked_by_hand():\n    cases = json.loads(FIXTURE.read_text())\n\n    assert len({case[\"paper\"] for case in cases}) >= 200\n    for case in cases:\n        assert parse_title(case[\"paper\"]) == case[\"expected\"], case[\"paper\"]\n","numLines":170,"startLine":1,"totalLines":170}},"displayPath":"tests/test_titles.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1b5d7596-b5ea-42f9-8867-8180e24ef004","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.835Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_titles.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"The LoC title parser: the title, the place and the state as printed, what the cataloguer\n2\tsupplied in brackets, and the years. Its hard cases are one test each. tests/fixtures/titles.json\n3\tholds 200 titles drawn at random from the candidates' 3,336 LCCNs, each with its expected output\n4\tchecked by hand.\"\"\"\n5\t\n6\timport json\n7\tfrom pathlib import Path\n8\t\n9\timport pytest\n10\t\n11\tfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import parse_title, state_name, then_and_now\n12\t\n13\tFIXTURE = Path(__file__).parent / \"fixtures\" / \"titles.json\"\n14\t\n15\t\n16\tdef parsed(paper, **fields):\n17\t    found = parse_title(paper)\n18\t    return {k: found[k] for k in fields}\n19\t\n20\t\n21\tdef test_a_title_gives_its_place_state_and_years():\n22\t    found = parse_title(\"Eureka daily sentinel. [volume] (Eureka, Nev.) 1871-1887\")\n23\t\n24\t    assert found == {\n25\t        \"title\": \"Eureka daily sentinel.\",\n26\t        \"place\": \"Eureka\",\n27\t        \"county\": None,\n28\t        \"state\": \"Nev.\",\n29\t        \"supplied_place\": None,\n30\t        \"supplied_state\": None,\n31\t        \"years\": \"1871-1887\",\n32\t        \"first_year\": 1871,\n33\t        \"last_year\": 1887,\n34\t    }\n35\t\n36\t\n37\t@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n38\t    (\"paper\", \"fields\"),\n39\t    [\n40\t        # a marker after the title, of either kind\n41\t        (\"Daily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975\",\n42\t         {\"title\": \"Daily Kennebec journal.\", \"place\": \"Augusta\", \"state\": \"Me.\"}),\n43\t        # the state only in brackets: supplied by the cataloguer, not printed\n44\t        (\"New Orleans daily crescent. [volume] (New Orleans [La.]) 1851-1866\",\n45\t         {\"place\": \"New Orleans\", \"state\": None, \"supplied_state\": \"La.\"}),\n46\t        (\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\",\n47\t         {\"place\": \"Washington\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n48\t        (\"National Republican. (Washington City (D.C.)) 1872-1888\",\n49\t         {\"place\": \"Washington City\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n50\t        # no place at all: a short form, or a title record's job\n51\t        (\"The New York herald.\",\n52\t         {\"title\": \"The New York herald.\", \"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"years\": None}),\n53\t        # a county between the place and the state, and a territory with its state supplied\n54\t        (\"Devils Lake inter-ocean. (Devils Lake, Ramsey Co., Dakota [N.D.]) 1884-1905\",\n55\t         {\"place\": \"Devils Lake\", \"county\": \"Ramsey Co.\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_state\": \"N.D.\"}),\n56\t        # corrections: printed first, the correction supplied\n57\t        (\"Juliet signal. [volume] (Juliet [i.e. Joliet], Ill.) 1844-1???\",\n58\t         {\"place\": \"Juliet\", \"supplied_place\": \"Joliet\", \"state\": \"Ill.\", \"first_year\": 1844, \"last_year\": None}),\n59\t        (\"Grant County herald. [volume] (Lancaster, W.T. [i.e. Wis.]) 1843-1844\",\n60\t         {\"state\": \"W.T.\", \"supplied_state\": \"Wis.\"}),\n61\t        (\"Marble Hill press. (Marbel [sic] Hill, Mo.) 1881-1923\",\n62\t         {\"place\": \"Marbel Hill\", \"supplied_place\": None}),\n63\t        # the whole place supplied\n64\t        (\"Nashville union. [volume] ([Nashville, Tenn.]) 1851-1853\",\n65\t         {\"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"supplied_place\": \"Nashville\", \"supplied_state\": \"Tenn.\"}),\n66\t        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\",\n67\t         {\"place\": \"Bannock City\", \"state\": \"I.T.\", \"supplied_place\": \"Idaho City\", \"supplied_state\": \"Idaho\"}),\n68\t        # a bracket after the state that starts with a state supplies only a state, with i.e. inside\n69\t        (\"The Hot Springs star. (Hot Spings [Hot Springs], Dakota [Dakota Territory, i.e. S.D.]) 1886-189?\",\n70\t         {\"place\": \"Hot Spings\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_place\": \"Hot Springs\", \"supplied_state\": \"S.D.\"}),\n71\t        # no comma before the state, a semicolon after it, an ampersand between two\n72\t        (\"Cheraw gazette and Pee Dee farmer. [volume] (Cheraw S.C.) 1838-1839\",\n73\t         {\"place\": \"Cheraw\", \"state\": \"S.C.\"}),\n74\t        (\"Backbone. [volume] (St. Paul, Minn. ;) 1897-1906\",\n75\t         {\"place\": \"St. Paul\", \"state\": \"Minn.\"}),\n76\t        (\"Bristol news. [volume] (Bristol, Va. &amp; Tenn.) 1867-189?\",\n77\t         {\"place\": \"Bristol\", \"state\": \"Va. & Tenn.\", \"last_year\": None}),\n78\t        # HTML entities, and UTF-8 that was read as Windows-1252\n79\t        (\"Coeur d&#39;Alene evening press. (Coeur d&#39;Alene, Idaho) 1907-1929\",\n80\t         {\"title\": \"Coeur d'Alene evening press.\", \"place\": \"Coeur d'Alene\"}),\n81\t        (\"Deutscher herold. [volume] (Sioux Falls, SuÌˆd-Dakota) 1907-1918\",\n82\t         {\"state\": \"Süd-Dakota\"}),\n83\t        (\"The Maryland independent. [volume] (Port Tobacco, Md.) 1874-current\",\n84\t         {\"first_year\": 1874, \"last_year\": None, \"years\": \"1874-current\"}),\n85\t    ],\n86\t)  # fmt: skip\n87\tdef test_the_hard_cases(paper, fields):\n88\t    assert parsed(paper, **fields) == fields\n89\t\n90\t\n91\t@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n92\t    (\"printed\", \"modern\"),\n93\t    [\n94\t        (\"Va.\", \"Virginia\"),\n95\t        (\"W. Va.\", \"West Virginia\"),\n96\t        (\"Süd-Dakota\", \"South Dakota\"),\n97\t        (\"Nuevo México\", \"New Mexico\"),\n98\t        (\"A.T.\", \"Arizona\"),\n99\t        (\"Minn. Territory\", \"Minnesota\"),\n100\t        (\"Va. & Tenn.\", \"Virginia\"),\n101\t        # a territory's initials can stand for two: the record or the brackets decide\n102\t        (\"W.T.\", None),\n103\t        (\"N.T.\", None),\n104\t        (\"I.T.\", None),\n105\t        (\"Dakota\", None),\n106\t    ],\n107\t)\n108\tdef test_a_printed_state_names_its_modern_state_unless_it_could_be_two(printed, modern):\n109\t    assert state_name(printed) == modern\n110\t\n111\t\n112\tdef test_then_and_now_says_when_a_place_has_changed_state():\n113\t    # 1835: the Virginia free press, of Charlestown, Va., now West Virginia (sn84026784)\n114\t    title = parse_title(\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\")\n115\t\n116\t    assert then_and_now(title, \"West Virginia\") == \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"\n117\t    assert (\n118\t        then_and_now(\n119\t            parse_title(\"Alexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 1834-1974\"),\n120\t            \"Virginia\",\n121\t        )\n122\t        == \"Alexandria, D.C. (now Va.)\"\n123\t    )\n124\t    assert (\n125\t        then_and_now(\n126\t            parse_title(\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\"),\n127\t            \"District of Columbia\",\n128\t        )\n129\t        == \"Washington, D.C.\"\n130\t    )\n131\t    assert (\n132\t        then_and_now(\n133\t            parse_title(\n134\t                \"Aberdeen herald. [volume] (Aberdeen, Chehalis County, W.T.) 1886-1917\"\n135\t            ),\n136\t            \"Washington\",\n137\t        )\n138\t        == \"Aberdeen, W.T. (now Wash.)\"\n139\t    )\n140\t    assert then_and_now(parse_title(\"The New York herald.\"), \"New York\") is None\n141\t\n142\t\n143\t@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n144\t    (\"paper\", \"modern\", \"catalogued\", \"shown\"),\n145\t    [\n146\t        # the record's city, as catalogued today, where the town has a new name\n147\t        (\"Muscatine journal. [volume] (Bloomington, Iowa) 1849-185?\", \"Iowa\", \"Muscatine\",\n148\t         \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\"),\n149\t        (\"East Saginaw courier. (East Saginaw, Mich.) 1859-18??\", \"Michigan\", \"Saginaw\",\n150\t         \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\"),\n151\t        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\", \"Idaho\", \"Idaho City\",\n152\t         \"Bannock City, I.T. (now Idaho City, Idaho)\"),\n153\t        # the same town, spelled apart, isn't a new name\n154\t        (\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\", \"West Virginia\", \"Charles Town\",\n155\t         \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"),\n156\t        # a title that prints no place shows the record's\n157\t        (\"The Phoenix Jewish news.\", \"Arizona\", \"Phoenix\", \"Phoenix, Ariz.\"),\n158\t    ],\n159\t)  # fmt: skip\n160\tdef test_then_and_now_gives_the_records_name_for_a_town_renamed(paper, modern, catalogued, shown):\n161\t    assert then_and_now(parse_title(paper), modern, catalogued) == shown\n162\t\n163\t\n164\tdef test_two_hundred_titles_checked_by_hand():\n165\t    cases = json.loads(FIXTURE.read_text())\n166\t\n167\t    assert len({case[\"paper\"] for case in cases}) >= 200\n168\t    for case in cases:\n169\t        assert parse_title(case[\"paper\"]) == case[\"expected\"], case[\"paper\"]\n170\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"1b5d7596-b5ea-42f9-8867-8180e24ef004","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_loc_titles.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_loc_titles.py","content":"\"\"\"loc.gov title records: one request each, cached with its URL and retrieval date, and a 404\ncached too; and the place, state and title a record gives.\"\"\"\n\nimport httpx\n\nfrom paperghosts.geo import loc\n\n\nclass Answer:\n    def __init__(self, status, body=None):\n        self.status_code, self.body = status, body\n\n    def json(self):\n        return self.body\n\n    def raise_for_status(self):\n        if self.status_code >= 400:\n            request = httpx.Request(\"GET\", \"https://www.loc.gov/\")\n            raise httpx.HTTPStatusError(\n                \"\", request=request, response=httpx.Response(self.status_code)\n            )\n\n\ndef test_a_record_is_asked_for_once_and_cached_with_its_url_and_date(\n    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n):\n    asked = []\n\n    def get(url, **params):\n        asked.append(url)\n        answer = Answer(200, {\"item\": {\"location_str\": \"Phoenix, Arizona\"}})\n        answer.raise_for_status()\n        return answer\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n\n    first = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n    again = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n\n    assert asked == [\"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/\"]\n    assert (\n        first == again\n        and first[\"url\"] == \"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/?fo=json\"\n    )\n    assert first[\"status\"] == 200 and first[\"retrieved\"].startswith(\"20\")\n    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_a_missing_record_is_cached_as_a_404(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    def get(url, **params):\n        Answer(404).raise_for_status()\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n\n    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"status\"] == 404\n    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"body\"] is None\n    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_a_server_error_is_counted_and_not_cached_so_a_later_run_asks_again(\n    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n):\n    def get(url, **params):\n        Answer(520).raise_for_status()\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n\n    found = loc.title_record(\"sn83030273\", tmp_path)\n\n    assert found[\"status\"] == 520 and found[\"body\"] is None\n    assert not list(tmp_path.iterdir())\n    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"errors\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_a_record_gives_its_place_state_county_title_and_years():\n    record = {\n        \"status\": 200,\n        \"body\": {\n            \"item\": {\n                \"location_str\": \"Tucson, Arizona\",\n                \"location_county\": [\"santa cruz\", \"pima\"],\n                \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n                \"dates_of_publication\": \"1859-1871\",\n            }\n        },\n    }\n\n    # a paper that moved has both its counties, to check either printed place against\n    assert loc.recorded(record) == {\n        \"place\": \"Tucson\",\n        \"state\": \"Arizona\",\n        \"county\": \"Santa Cruz\",\n        \"counties\": [\"Santa Cruz\", \"Pima\"],\n        \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n        \"years\": \"1859-1871\",\n    }\n    assert loc.recorded({\"status\": 404, \"body\": None}) is None\n","numLines":101,"startLine":1,"totalLines":101}},"displayPath":"tests/test_loc_titles.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8f1fdc54-72b7-47ad-90e1-7229c8ebce2a","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.835Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_loc_titles.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"loc.gov title records: one request each, cached with its URL and retrieval date, and a 404\n2\tcached too; and the place, state and title a record gives.\"\"\"\n3\t\n4\timport httpx\n5\t\n6\tfrom paperghosts.geo import loc\n7\t\n8\t\n9\tclass Answer:\n10\t    def __init__(self, status, body=None):\n11\t        self.status_code, self.body = status, body\n12\t\n13\t    def json(self):\n14\t        return self.body\n15\t\n16\t    def raise_for_status(self):\n17\t        if self.status_code >= 400:\n18\t            request = httpx.Request(\"GET\", \"https://www.loc.gov/\")\n19\t            raise httpx.HTTPStatusError(\n20\t                \"\", request=request, response=httpx.Response(self.status_code)\n21\t            )\n22\t\n23\t\n24\tdef test_a_record_is_asked_for_once_and_cached_with_its_url_and_date(\n25\t    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n26\t):\n27\t    asked = []\n28\t\n29\t    def get(url, **params):\n30\t        asked.append(url)\n31\t        answer = Answer(200, {\"item\": {\"location_str\": \"Phoenix, Arizona\"}})\n32\t        answer.raise_for_status()\n33\t        return answer\n34\t\n35\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n36\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n37\t\n38\t    first = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n39\t    again = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n40\t\n41\t    assert asked == [\"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/\"]\n42\t    assert (\n43\t        first == again\n44\t        and first[\"url\"] == \"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/?fo=json\"\n45\t    )\n46\t    assert first[\"status\"] == 200 and first[\"retrieved\"].startswith(\"20\")\n47\t    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n48\t\n49\t\n50\tdef test_a_missing_record_is_cached_as_a_404(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n51\t    def get(url, **params):\n52\t        Answer(404).raise_for_status()\n53\t\n54\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n55\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n56\t\n57\t    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"status\"] == 404\n58\t    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"body\"] is None\n59\t    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n60\t\n61\t\n62\tdef test_a_server_error_is_counted_and_not_cached_so_a_later_run_asks_again(\n63\t    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n64\t):\n65\t    def get(url, **params):\n66\t        Answer(520).raise_for_status()\n67\t\n68\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n69\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n70\t\n71\t    found = loc.title_record(\"sn83030273\", tmp_path)\n72\t\n73\t    assert found[\"status\"] == 520 and found[\"body\"] is None\n74\t    assert not list(tmp_path.iterdir())\n75\t    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"errors\": 1}\n76\t\n77\t\n78\tdef test_a_record_gives_its_place_state_county_title_and_years():\n79\t    record = {\n80\t        \"status\": 200,\n81\t        \"body\": {\n82\t            \"item\": {\n83\t                \"location_str\": \"Tucson, Arizona\",\n84\t                \"location_county\": [\"santa cruz\", \"pima\"],\n85\t                \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n86\t                \"dates_of_publication\": \"1859-1871\",\n87\t            }\n88\t        },\n89\t    }\n90\t\n91\t    # a paper that moved has both its counties, to check either printed place against\n92\t    assert loc.recorded(record) == {\n93\t        \"place\": \"Tucson\",\n94\t        \"state\": \"Arizona\",\n95\t        \"county\": \"Santa Cruz\",\n96\t        \"counties\": [\"Santa Cruz\", \"Pima\"],\n97\t        \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n98\t        \"years\": \"1859-1871\",\n99\t    }\n100\t    assert loc.recorded({\"status\": 404, \"body\": None}) is None\n101\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"8f1fdc54-72b7-47ad-90e1-7229c8ebce2a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/paperghosts/geo/papers.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/paperghosts/geo/papers.py","content":"\"\"\"Where each paper was printed (decision 34): its printed place geocoded, then checked against\nits loc.gov title record where it has one.\n\n1. The printed place is geocoded in its modern state: the cataloguer's bracketed place first,\n   then the printed one.\n2. With a record that names counties, a result inside one of them stands.\n3. A result outside them, or no result at all, gives way to the record's place as catalogued\n   today, geocoded in the record's county.\n4. Failing that, a ghost town of the record's name in the record's county, flagged historical.\n5. Failing that, no point: a pin that disagrees with the record is the dangerous kind of answer.\nWithout a record, the printed place stands unchecked. That's so for most papers until 2D asks\nfor the records of the papers it stores (BUILD-PLAN.md).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import state_name\n\n\ndef towns(title):\n    found = [t for t in (title.get(\"supplied_place\"), title.get(\"place\")) if t]\n    return list(dict.fromkeys(found))\n\n\ndef modern_state(title):\n    return title.get(\"modern_state\") or state_name(\n        title.get(\"supplied_state\") or title.get(\"state\")\n    )\n\n\ndef printed_place(gazetteer, title):\n    state, county = modern_state(title), title.get(\"county\")\n    found = gazetteer.geocode(None, state)\n    for town in towns(title):\n        found = gazetteer.geocode(town, state, county)\n        if found[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n            break\n    return found\n\n\ndef paper_place(gazetteer, title, given=None):\n    \"\"\"The GeoNames place a paper was printed in, and what its record (`loc.recorded`) made of\n    it, under \"record\".\"\"\"\n    printed = printed_place(gazetteer, title)\n    if not given or not given.get(\"place\"):\n        return printed | {\"record\": \"none\"}\n    counties = given.get(\"counties\") or []\n    if not counties:\n        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n            return printed | {\"record\": \"has no county to check\"}\n        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"])\n        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n            return catalogued | {\"record\": \"found what the printed place didn't, unchecked\"}\n        return printed | {\"record\": \"has no county to check\"}\n    agrees = gazetteer.same_county(printed, counties) if printed[\"place_id\"] else False\n    if agrees is None:\n        return printed | {\"record\": \"can't be checked against GeoNames' counties\"}\n    if agrees:\n        return printed | {\"record\": \"agrees\"}\n    how = (\n        \"replaced the printed place\"\n        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None\n        else \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n    )\n    for county in counties:\n        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], county)\n        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None and gazetteer.same_county(\n            catalogued, counties\n        ):\n            return catalogued | {\"record\": how}\n    ghost = gazetteer.ghost(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], counties)\n    if ghost:\n        return ghost | {\"record\": \"a ghost town in the record's county\"}\n    return gazetteer.base() | {\n        \"reason\": \"outside the record's county, whose own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n        \"record\": \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n    }\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}},"displayPath":"paperghosts/geo/papers.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1bfa3d68-1304-41d5-936b-a14cb0522c7d","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.835Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/paperghosts/geo/papers.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"Where each paper was printed (decision 34): its printed place geocoded, then checked against\n2\tits loc.gov title record where it has one.\n3\t\n4\t1. The printed place is geocoded in its modern state: the cataloguer's bracketed place first,\n5\t   then the printed one.\n6\t2. With a record that names counties, a result inside one of them stands.\n7\t3. A result outside them, or no result at all, gives way to the record's place as catalogued\n8\t   today, geocoded in the record's county.\n9\t4. Failing that, a ghost town of the record's name in the record's county, flagged historical.\n10\t5. Failing that, no point: a pin that disagrees with the record is the dangerous kind of answer.\n11\tWithout a record, the printed place stands unchecked. That's so for most papers until 2D asks\n12\tfor the records of the papers it stores (BUILD-PLAN.md).\n13\t\"\"\"\n14\t\n15\tfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import state_name\n16\t\n17\t\n18\tdef towns(title):\n19\t    found = [t for t in (title.get(\"supplied_place\"), title.get(\"place\")) if t]\n20\t    return list(dict.fromkeys(found))\n21\t\n22\t\n23\tdef modern_state(title):\n24\t    return title.get(\"modern_state\") or state_name(\n25\t        title.get(\"supplied_state\") or title.get(\"state\")\n26\t    )\n27\t\n28\t\n29\tdef printed_place(gazetteer, title):\n30\t    state, county = modern_state(title), title.get(\"county\")\n31\t    found = gazetteer.geocode(None, state)\n32\t    for town in towns(title):\n33\t        found = gazetteer.geocode(town, state, county)\n34\t        if found[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n35\t            break\n36\t    return found\n37\t\n38\t\n39\tdef paper_place(gazetteer, title, given=None):\n40\t    \"\"\"The GeoNames place a paper was printed in, and what its record (`loc.recorded`) made of\n41\t    it, under \"record\".\"\"\"\n42\t    printed = printed_place(gazetteer, title)\n43\t    if not given or not given.get(\"place\"):\n44\t        return printed | {\"record\": \"none\"}\n45\t    counties = given.get(\"counties\") or []\n46\t    if not counties:\n47\t        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n48\t            return printed | {\"record\": \"has no county to check\"}\n49\t        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"])\n50\t        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n51\t            return catalogued | {\"record\": \"found what the printed place didn't, unchecked\"}\n52\t        return printed | {\"record\": \"has no county to check\"}\n53\t    agrees = gazetteer.same_county(printed, counties) if printed[\"place_id\"] else False\n54\t    if agrees is None:\n55\t        return printed | {\"record\": \"can't be checked against GeoNames' counties\"}\n56\t    if agrees:\n57\t        return printed | {\"record\": \"agrees\"}\n58\t    how = (\n59\t        \"replaced the printed place\"\n60\t        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None\n61\t        else \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n62\t    )\n63\t    for county in counties:\n64\t        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], county)\n65\t        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None and gazetteer.same_county(\n66\t            catalogued, counties\n67\t        ):\n68\t            return catalogued | {\"record\": how}\n69\t    ghost = gazetteer.ghost(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], counties)\n70\t    if ghost:\n71\t        return ghost | {\"record\": \"a ghost town in the record's county\"}\n72\t    return gazetteer.base() | {\n73\t        \"reason\": \"outside the record's county, whose own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n74\t        \"record\": \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n75\t    }\n76\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"1bfa3d68-1304-41d5-936b-a14cb0522c7d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_papers.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_papers.py","content":"\"\"\"Where a paper was printed: its printed place, checked against its loc.gov title record. A\nprinted place in the record's county stands. One elsewhere, or one that resolves to nothing,\ngives way to the record's place. A ghost town is accepted only in the record's county, and\nwhere nothing agrees with the record there is no point at all.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom paperghosts.geo.geonames import Gazetteer\nfrom paperghosts.geo.papers import paper_place\n\nROWS = [\n    (4848880, \"Bloomington\", \"Bloomington\", \"\", 42.0, -93.4, \"P\", \"PPL\", \"US\", \"\", \"IA\", \"169\", \"\", \"\", 0),\n    (4868404, \"Muscatine\", \"Muscatine\", \"\", 41.424, -91.043, \"P\", \"PPLA2\", \"US\", \"\", \"IA\", \"139\", \"\", \"\", 23000),\n    (5007989, \"Saginaw\", \"Saginaw\", \"\", 43.419, -83.951, \"P\", \"PPLA2\", \"US\", \"\", \"MI\", \"145\", \"\", \"\", 48000),\n    (5763634, \"Clifton (historical)\", \"Clifton (historical)\", \"\", 44.6, -100.0, \"S\", \"\", \"US\", \"\", \"SD\", \"119\", \"\", \"\", 0),\n    (4801850, \"Charles Town\", \"Charles Town\", \"\", 39.289, -77.86, \"P\", \"PPLA2\", \"US\", \"\", \"WV\", \"037\", \"\", \"\", 5899),\n    (5500000, \"Lardo\", \"Lardo\", \"\", 40.0, -100.0, \"P\", \"PPL\", \"US\", \"\", \"NE\", \"001\", \"\", \"\", 10),\n]  # fmt: skip\nADMIN1 = \"US.IA\\tIowa\\nUS.MI\\tMichigan\\nUS.SD\\tSouth Dakota\\nUS.WV\\tWest Virginia\\nUS.ID\\tIdaho\\nUS.NE\\tNebraska\\n\"\nADMIN2 = \"US.IA.169\\tStory County\\nUS.IA.139\\tMuscatine County\\nUS.MI.145\\tSaginaw County\\nUS.SD.119\\tSully County\\nUS.WV.037\\tJefferson County\\nUS.NE.001\\tAdams County\\n\"\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef gazetteer(tmp_path):\n    places = tmp_path / \"US.txt\"\n    places.write_text(\n        \"\".join(\"\\t\".join(map(str, (*r, \"\", \"\", \"America/Chicago\", \"2026-10-30\"))) + \"\\n\" for r in ROWS)\n    )\n    admin1 = tmp_path / \"admin1CodesASCII.txt\"\n    admin1.write_text(\"\".join(f\"{line}\\tx\\t1\\n\" for line in ADMIN1.splitlines()))\n    admin2 = tmp_path / \"admin2Codes.txt\"\n    admin2.write_text(\"\".join(f\"{line}\\tx\\t1\\n\" for line in ADMIN2.splitlines()))\n    return Gazetteer([places], admin1, admin2, source=\"geonames dumps of 2026-10-30\")\n\n\ndef title(place, state, modern):\n    return {\"place\": place, \"state\": state, \"modern_state\": modern}\n\n\ndef record(place, county, state):\n    return {\"place\": place, \"state\": state, \"counties\": [county] if county else []}\n\n\ndef test_a_printed_place_in_its_records_county_stands(gazetteer):\n    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Charlestown\", \"Va.\", \"West Virginia\"), record(\"Charles Town\", \"jefferson\", \"West Virginia\"))\n\n    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 4801850 and found[\"record\"] == \"agrees\"\n\n\ndef test_a_printed_place_in_another_county_gives_way_to_the_records(gazetteer):\n    # Muscatine journal (Bloomington, Iowa): Bloomington was Muscatine's name until 1849\n    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Bloomington\", \"Iowa\", \"Iowa\"), record(\"Muscatine\", \"muscatine\", \"Iowa\"))\n\n    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 4868404 and found[\"record\"] == \"replaced the printed place\"\n\n\ndef test_a_merged_town_is_found_under_its_successor(gazetteer):\n    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"East Saginaw\", \"Mich.\", \"Michigan\"), record(\"Saginaw\", \"saginaw\", \"Michigan\"))\n\n    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 5007989 and found[\"record\"] == \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n\n\ndef test_a_ghost_town_is_taken_only_in_its_records_county(gazetteer):\n    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Clifton\", \"Dakota\", \"South Dakota\"), record(\"Clifton\", \"sully\", \"South Dakota\"))\n\n    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 5763634 and found[\"historical\"] is True\n    assert found[\"record\"] == \"a ghost town in the record's county\"\n\n\ndef test_with_nothing_that_agrees_with_the_record_there_is_no_point(gazetteer):\n    # a same-named place elsewhere is the dangerous kind of answer\n    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Lardo\", \"Idaho\", \"Nebraska\"), record(\"McCall\", \"valley\", \"Idaho\"))\n\n    assert found[\"place_id\"] is None\n    assert found[\"record\"] == \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\"\n\n\ndef test_without_a_record_or_its_county_the_printed_place_stands(gazetteer):\n    printed = title(\"Bloomington\", \"Iowa\", \"Iowa\")\n\n    assert paper_place(gazetteer, printed, None)[\"record\"] == \"none\"\n    unchecked = paper_place(gazetteer, printed, record(\"Bloomington\", None, \"Iowa\"))\n    assert unchecked[\"place_id\"] == 4848880 and unchecked[\"record\"] == \"has no county to check\"\n","numLines":84,"startLine":1,"totalLines":84}},"displayPath":"tests/test_papers.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"eda1acba-ac82-4d46-a1fd-f7cf5a7e00c0","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.835Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_papers.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"Where a paper was printed: its printed place, checked against its loc.gov title record. A\n2\tprinted place in the record's county stands. One elsewhere, or one that resolves to nothing,\n3\tgives way to the record's place. A ghost town is accepted only in the record's county, and\n4\twhere nothing agrees with the record there is no point at all.\"\"\"\n5\t\n6\timport pytest\n7\t\n8\tfrom paperghosts.geo.geonames import Gazetteer\n9\tfrom paperghosts.geo.papers import paper_place\n10\t\n11\tROWS = [\n12\t    (4848880, \"Bloomington\", \"Bloomington\", \"\", 42.0, -93.4, \"P\", \"PPL\", \"US\", \"\", \"IA\", \"169\", \"\", \"\", 0),\n13\t    (4868404, \"Muscatine\", \"Muscatine\", \"\", 41.424, -91.043, \"P\", \"PPLA2\", \"US\", \"\", \"IA\", \"139\", \"\", \"\", 23000),\n14\t    (5007989, \"Saginaw\", \"Saginaw\", \"\", 43.419, -83.951, \"P\", \"PPLA2\", \"US\", \"\", \"MI\", \"145\", \"\", \"\", 48000),\n15\t    (5763634, \"Clifton (historical)\", \"Clifton (historical)\", \"\", 44.6, -100.0, \"S\", \"\", \"US\", \"\", \"SD\", \"119\", \"\", \"\", 0),\n16\t    (4801850, \"Charles Town\", \"Charles Town\", \"\", 39.289, -77.86, \"P\", \"PPLA2\", \"US\", \"\", \"WV\", \"037\", \"\", \"\", 5899),\n17\t    (5500000, \"Lardo\", \"Lardo\", \"\", 40.0, -100.0, \"P\", \"PPL\", \"US\", \"\", \"NE\", \"001\", \"\", \"\", 10),\n18\t]  # fmt: skip\n19\tADMIN1 = \"US.IA\\tIowa\\nUS.MI\\tMichigan\\nUS.SD\\tSouth Dakota\\nUS.WV\\tWest Virginia\\nUS.ID\\tIdaho\\nUS.NE\\tNebraska\\n\"\n20\tADMIN2 = \"US.IA.169\\tStory County\\nUS.IA.139\\tMuscatine County\\nUS.MI.145\\tSaginaw County\\nUS.SD.119\\tSully County\\nUS.WV.037\\tJefferson County\\nUS.NE.001\\tAdams County\\n\"\n21\t\n22\t\n23\t@pytest.fixture\n24\tdef gazetteer(tmp_path):\n25\t    places = tmp_path / \"US.txt\"\n26\t    places.write_text(\n27\t        \"\".join(\"\\t\".join(map(str, (*r, \"\", \"\", \"America/Chicago\", \"2026-10-30\"))) + \"\\n\" for r in ROWS)\n28\t    )\n29\t    admin1 = tmp_path / \"admin1CodesASCII.txt\"\n30\t    admin1.write_text(\"\".join(f\"{line}\\tx\\t1\\n\" for line in ADMIN1.splitlines()))\n31\t    admin2 = tmp_path / \"admin2Codes.txt\"\n32\t    admin2.write_text(\"\".join(f\"{line}\\tx\\t1\\n\" for line in ADMIN2.splitlines()))\n33\t    return Gazetteer([places], admin1, admin2, source=\"geonames dumps of 2026-10-30\")\n34\t\n35\t\n36\tdef title(place, state, modern):\n37\t    return {\"place\": place, \"state\": state, \"modern_state\": modern}\n38\t\n39\t\n40\tdef record(place, county, state):\n41\t    return {\"place\": place, \"state\": state, \"counties\": [county] if county else []}\n42\t\n43\t\n44\tdef test_a_printed_place_in_its_records_county_stands(gazetteer):\n45\t    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Charlestown\", \"Va.\", \"West Virginia\"), record(\"Charles Town\", \"jefferson\", \"West Virginia\"))\n46\t\n47\t    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 4801850 and found[\"record\"] == \"agrees\"\n48\t\n49\t\n50\tdef test_a_printed_place_in_another_county_gives_way_to_the_records(gazetteer):\n51\t    # Muscatine journal (Bloomington, Iowa): Bloomington was Muscatine's name until 1849\n52\t    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Bloomington\", \"Iowa\", \"Iowa\"), record(\"Muscatine\", \"muscatine\", \"Iowa\"))\n53\t\n54\t    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 4868404 and found[\"record\"] == \"replaced the printed place\"\n55\t\n56\t\n57\tdef test_a_merged_town_is_found_under_its_successor(gazetteer):\n58\t    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"East Saginaw\", \"Mich.\", \"Michigan\"), record(\"Saginaw\", \"saginaw\", \"Michigan\"))\n59\t\n60\t    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 5007989 and found[\"record\"] == \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n61\t\n62\t\n63\tdef test_a_ghost_town_is_taken_only_in_its_records_county(gazetteer):\n64\t    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Clifton\", \"Dakota\", \"South Dakota\"), record(\"Clifton\", \"sully\", \"South Dakota\"))\n65\t\n66\t    assert found[\"place_id\"] == 5763634 and found[\"historical\"] is True\n67\t    assert found[\"record\"] == \"a ghost town in the record's county\"\n68\t\n69\t\n70\tdef test_with_nothing_that_agrees_with_the_record_there_is_no_point(gazetteer):\n71\t    # a same-named place elsewhere is the dangerous kind of answer\n72\t    found = paper_place(gazetteer, title(\"Lardo\", \"Idaho\", \"Nebraska\"), record(\"McCall\", \"valley\", \"Idaho\"))\n73\t\n74\t    assert found[\"place_id\"] is None\n75\t    assert found[\"record\"] == \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\"\n76\t\n77\t\n78\tdef test_without_a_record_or_its_county_the_printed_place_stands(gazetteer):\n79\t    printed = title(\"Bloomington\", \"Iowa\", \"Iowa\")\n80\t\n81\t    assert paper_place(gazetteer, printed, None)[\"record\"] == \"none\"\n82\t    unchecked = paper_place(gazetteer, printed, record(\"Bloomington\", None, \"Iowa\"))\n83\t    assert unchecked[\"place_id\"] == 4848880 and unchecked[\"record\"] == \"has no county to check\"\n84\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"eda1acba-ac82-4d46-a1fd-f7cf5a7e00c0","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md","content":"# Resume\n\nWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n\nHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n\n## Next step\n\n**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), on branch `phase-2b-data-layer`.\n- **Step 2B.1 passed the EM's review** (decisions 32 and 33): the models and validators, the BinData check, `make db-init` and `BUDGET.md`.\n  - **Pending:** `places_search`. `sample_mflix` holds M0's third Search slot, and freeing it is the owner's call.\n  - **The owner's call:** what to do if the reader's real count needs more storage than M0 has (`BUDGET.md`). The EM takes both to the owner.\n- **Step 2B.2 is built**, and waits on the EM's review (decision 34): the title parser, the loc.gov title records, the title lookup and the GeoNames geocoder.\n- **Step 2B.3,** the reel-to-batch map and the page check, follows.\n- Lexicon v2 keeps all five rules (decision 31).\n\nNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n- the Render deploy\n- domain DNS\n\n## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2 (2026-10-30)\n\nBranches `phase-2a-archive` (PR #2, merged) and `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` (PR #3, step 2A.8).\n\nBuilt:\n- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n- Lexicon v2, which adds five spiritualist rules and records in each candidate's `terms` the rules that matched it (decision 31).\n- Two full reads of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6`:\n  - v1's (decision 25), now archived in `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`\n  - v2's (decision 31)\n- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n- Eval A (`make eval`, 93 s, 4.7 GB at peak):\n  - It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n  - It then compares v2 with v1, and measures a judged sample of v2's added candidates (decision 31).\n  - `make eval-v1` reproduces v1's record from the archive.\n\nMeasured:\n- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages. Lexicon v2 gives 1,236,122 candidates (3,338 per million) and 6,695,792 near-miss articles. v1 gave 1,050,459 (2,837 per million).\n- Each read takes about two hours for 340.4 GB, with four years at a time: 118.6 min for v1 and 120.6 min for v2.\n- Ghost reports: 3.5% of v1's candidates (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800, and 7.0% of the 185,663 that v2 adds (4.2–11.4%), about 13,000 more.\n- Reprints put 30.3% of v2's candidates in 91,301 clusters, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n- Recall:\n  - Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27% (decision 30).\n  - Lexicon v2 finds about 84%, and at least 48%. **v2's figures are in-sample:** its rules were chosen from that screen's misses.\n  - 2C's eval B gives the independent figure (decision 31).\n- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n- 32.0% of v2's candidates have no batch path, and 93.0% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decisions 29 and 31).\n\nGate:\n- All 180 years are done in both reads, and none failed.\n  - In v1's read, six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n  - In v2's read, every year finished at the first attempt, all at code version `2d6ff0faaf81`.\n- v1 ⊆ v2: all 1,050,459 v1 candidates are still candidates, and none of the 185,663 added holds a v1 term.\n- Disk: the v1 archive and v2's outputs take 4.1 GB, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n  - `data/archive/lexicon-v1`: 1.8 GiB\n  - v2's `data/candidates`: 1.5 GiB\n  - v2's `data/samples`: 563 MiB\n- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850 under both lexicons. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the manifest.\n- `make eval-v1` reproduces eval A's v1 record byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`.\n- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n- The EM's spot check of v1, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n  - every candidate's term is in its text\n  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- **The ouija rule stays.** None of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). The EM kept it, and 2C's term strata measure it (decision 31).\n- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31).\n- v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports.\n- v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline.\n- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle these cases:\n  - delusions and hallucinations\n  - spirit control and a felt presence of the dead\n  - decision 31's two borderline kinds\n- 27,817 of v2's candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n- The reader's job is 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's. The Tinker credit check weighs that before the full pass.\n\n## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n\nBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n\nBuilt:\n- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n\nMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n\nUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n\nRisks and open questions:\n- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n\n## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n\n- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n","numLines":123,"startLine":1,"totalLines":123}},"displayPath":"Docs/RESUME.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"35281226-519f-426c-b61e-07c4bc4848b6","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.835Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/Docs/RESUME.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Resume\n2\t\n3\tWhere the build stands, for whoever picks it up next. The spec is `BRIEF.md`, the build order is `BUILD-PLAN.md`, and the reasons behind each choice are in `DECISIONS.md`. Each milestone ends with a STATUS block below, newest first.\n4\t\n5\tHard date: the site must be live on Oct 31.\n6\t\n7\t## Next step\n8\t\n9\t**Phase 2B: schemas and data layer** (`BUILD-PLAN.md` section 4), on branch `phase-2b-data-layer`.\n10\t- **Step 2B.1 passed the EM's review** (decisions 32 and 33): the models and validators, the BinData check, `make db-init` and `BUDGET.md`.\n11\t  - **Pending:** `places_search`. `sample_mflix` holds M0's third Search slot, and freeing it is the owner's call.\n12\t  - **The owner's call:** what to do if the reader's real count needs more storage than M0 has (`BUDGET.md`). The EM takes both to the owner.\n13\t- **Step 2B.2 is built**, and waits on the EM's review (decision 34): the title parser, the loc.gov title records, the title lookup and the GeoNames geocoder.\n14\t- **Step 2B.3,** the reel-to-batch map and the page check, follows.\n15\t- Lexicon v2 keeps all five rules (decision 31).\n16\t\n17\tNeeds the owner's yes when we get there:\n18\t- the Render deploy\n19\t- domain DNS\n20\t\n21\t## STATUS: Phase 2A, archive read, EDA and lexicon v2 (2026-10-30)\n22\t\n23\tBranches `phase-2a-archive` (PR #2, merged) and `phase-2a-lexicon-v2` (PR #3, step 2A.8).\n24\t\n25\tBuilt:\n26\t- Lexicon v1, with OCR variants, and the near-miss lexicon (decisions 19–21). `IngestArchiveWorkflow` and `make ingest`, which skip a year only while every output's hash matches (decisions 22–24).\n27\t- Lexicon v2, which adds five spiritualist rules and records in each candidate's `terms` the rules that matched it (decision 31).\n28\t- Two full reads of all 180 AmericanStories year files at revision `77e27fa6`:\n29\t  - v1's (decision 25), now archived in `data/archive/lexicon-v1/`\n30\t  - v2's (decision 31)\n31\t- Crops, one per box, fetched once and cached, and the display headline without its masthead lines (decisions 26 and 28).\n32\t- Eval A (`make eval`, 93 s, 4.7 GB at peak):\n33\t  - It produces the EDA, the candidates' composition, the lexicon's recall and eight charts (decisions 27, 29 and 30).\n34\t  - It then compares v2 with v1, and measures a judged sample of v2's added candidates (decision 31).\n35\t  - `make eval-v1` reproduces v1's record from the archive.\n36\t\n37\tMeasured:\n38\t- 370,313,913 articles in 16,887,460 pages. Lexicon v2 gives 1,236,122 candidates (3,338 per million) and 6,695,792 near-miss articles. v1 gave 1,050,459 (2,837 per million).\n39\t- Each read takes about two hours for 340.4 GB, with four years at a time: 118.6 min for v1 and 120.6 min for v2.\n40\t- Ghost reports: 3.5% of v1's candidates (1.7–7.0%), about 36,800, and 7.0% of the 185,663 that v2 adds (4.2–11.4%), about 13,000 more.\n41\t- Reprints put 30.3% of v2's candidates in 91,301 clusters, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n42\t- Recall:\n43\t  - Lexicon v1 finds about 49% of the ghost reports, and at least 27% (decision 30).\n44\t  - Lexicon v2 finds about 84%, and at least 48%. **v2's figures are in-sample:** its rules were chosen from that screen's misses.\n45\t  - 2C's eval B gives the independent figure (decision 31).\n46\t- The crop check passes 48 of 50 (86.5–98.9%). The display headline drops 7,509 lines at 158 of 158 precision on the judged sample (97.6–100%).\n47\t- 32.0% of v2's candidates have no batch path, and 93.0% of those are on reels whose batch is known (decisions 29 and 31).\n48\t\n49\tGate:\n50\t- All 180 years are done in both reads, and none failed.\n51\t  - In v1's read, six ran twice: 1872 and 1889 lost their streams, and kill test 1 cost 1881–1884 their first attempts (decision 25).\n52\t  - In v2's read, every year finished at the first attempt, all at code version `2d6ff0faaf81`.\n53\t- v1 ⊆ v2: all 1,050,459 v1 candidates are still candidates, and none of the 185,663 added holds a v1 term.\n54\t- Disk: the v1 archive and v2's outputs take 4.1 GB, within the 10 GB budget, and 36 GiB are free.\n55\t  - `data/archive/lexicon-v1`: 1.8 GiB\n56\t  - v2's `data/candidates`: 1.5 GiB\n57\t  - v2's `data/samples`: 563 MiB\n58\t- `make ingest YEARS=1850` reproduces 1850 under both lexicons. With its candidates moved aside, a rerun wrote all three outputs byte-identical, with the sha256 in the manifest.\n59\t- `make eval-v1` reproduces eval A's v1 record byte for byte, apart from `generated_at`.\n60\t- Kill-test evidence: `eval/results/phase2a/kill-test-1.md`, with Temporal UI screenshots from before and after (`kill-test-1-*.png`).\n61\t- Both crop-check failures are explained: #21 is AmericanStories' stray box, recorded as a known limitation (decision 29), and #50 is a 1964 page with no loc.gov item.\n62\t- The EM's spot check of v1, seed \"EM spot-check 2A\", read 20 random candidates and 10 random non-candidates against their stored text:\n63\t  - every candidate's term is in its text\n64\t  - none of the 10 non-candidates is a report\n65\t  - 1 of the 20 candidates is a report, in line with 3.5%\n66\t\n67\tRisks and open questions:\n68\t- **The ouija rule stays.** None of its 6 sampled candidates is a report (0–39%). The EM kept it, and 2C's term strata measure it (decision 31).\n69\t- Each new rule's marginal yield rests on 0 to 4 reports, so its interval is wide (decision 31).\n70\t- v2's 84% recall is in-sample. The screen behind both lexicons' recall rests on 4 reports, 2 of them borderline. Even with their lexicon terms masked, it would still catch only 5 of eval A's 7 reports.\n71\t- v2's gain rests on 14 reports in 200, 2 of them borderline.\n72\t- `POLICY.md` (2C) has to settle these cases:\n73\t  - delusions and hallucinations\n74\t  - spirit control and a felt presence of the dead\n75\t  - decision 31's two borderline kinds\n76\t- 27,817 of v2's candidates need loc.gov to name their page's IIIF service, and its JSON API allows 20 requests a minute. 2B looks them up only for reports (decision 29).\n77\t- The reader's job is 1,236,122 candidates, 17.7% more than v1's. The Tinker credit check weighs that before the full pass.\n78\t\n79\t## STATUS: Phase 1, scaffolding and environment (2026-10-29)\n80\t\n81\tBranch `phase-1-scaffold`, PR #1. The gate checklist is on the PR.\n82\t\n83\tBuilt:\n84\t- The ARCHITECTURE section 5 layout, the `uv` project on Python 3.12, the pnpm web shell, and a Makefile whose later-phase targets name their phase.\n85\t- `make keys`: a shape check and one cheap authenticated call per key; all 16 keys are OK, and no value is ever printed (decision 13).\n86\t- The Sentry helper, which collects nothing about people (decision 15), and the Temporal dev server, worker and smoke workflow.\n87\t- `eval/results/phase1_env.json`, written by `scripts/phase1_env.py` at `9f53354`.\n88\t- The walking skeleton: the Lewistown gazette's 1850-07-26 \"Mysterious Knockings\" goes from Atlas through FastAPI to a page with its IIIF crop, and the crop matches its text (`eval/results/phase1/skeleton.png`).\n89\t- CI: ruff, offline pytest, the web lint, typecheck and build, and gitleaks over the full history.\n90\t\n91\tMeasured, all from `eval/results/phase1_env.json`:\n92\t- Machine: Apple M4, 10 cores, 24.0 GiB of RAM, 26.2 GiB of free disk.\n93\t- `faro_1850`: 989,144,629 bytes downloaded in 12.0 s, at 82.4 MB/s. It holds 24,664 pages and 733,880 articles.\n94\t- Parsing on one core: 3,835 pages/s and 114,100 articles/s (153.8 MB/s compressed), so 0.5 cores keep up with one download. Ingest is limited by the network, not the CPU.\n95\t- 20 articles of 1850 mention Stratford and knocking; 2 name Phelps.\n96\t- Ollama `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`: 31.0 generated tokens/s, after a 10.5 s load.\n97\t- Atlas 8.0.34: 239,243,264 bytes used, 239,185,920 of them by `sample_mflix`. A Search index and a Vector Search index each became queryable in 23.5 s, then were dropped.\n98\t- SerpApi: 210 searches left.\n99\t- Ultramarine `ultramarine-1-flash`: one tiny completion used 27 tokens and took 2.04 s.\n100\t- Tinker: 35 base models, among them `Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B` and `Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B`, both trainable and sampleable.\n101\t- Sentry: the test event could be read back from `paperghosts-api` after 10.6 s.\n102\t\n103\tUnknown, so null in the JSON with its reason:\n104\t- The Atlas tier and storage limit. The owner can tell us, or an Atlas API key would.\n105\t- Tinker credits. Only the console shows the balance.\n106\t- The Ultramarine cost. The API reports tokens only, so INV-9's \"actual cost\" needs Paper Instruments' price sheet.\n107\t- Whether trained LoRA weights download. The SDK documents it; Phase 2C verifies it.\n108\t\n109\tRisks and open questions:\n110\t- Atlas: if the cluster is the free tier, `sample_mflix` already fills most of it. Dropping it is the owner's call.\n111\t- Crops: 9 of the 20 matching 1850 pages have no `raw_data_loc`, so their batch comes from loc.gov (decision 18). One bounding crop is right only for single-column articles, and AmericanStories headlines can include the page masthead.\n112\t- Speeds varied between the two runs of the script, the parse rate and Gemma's above all, so Phase 2A re-measures at scale before it sets the concurrency.\n113\t- Disk: free space dropped between the two runs because macOS swap grew while Gemma loaded. Don't run Gemma during the ingest.\n114\t- Before any session is published, redact another local account's process list from this phase's Entire logs (step 1.7's note).\n115\t\n116\t## Setup before Phase 1 (2026-10-29)\n117\t\n118\t- The private GitHub repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts` is cloned here.\n119\t- `.gitignore` covers `.env` and keeps `Docs/IDEAS.md` private.\n120\t- `.env` holds the keys, never displayed. The SerpApi and Ultramarine keys are set too; the Ultramarine one is named `PAPERINSTRUMENTS_API_KEY` (decision 12).\n121\t- Entire session capture is enabled for Claude Code in this repo only. Checkpoints sync to `origin` on push.\n122\t- Sentry: projects `paperghosts-api` (Python/FastAPI) and `paperghosts-web` (React) exist in org `hotragn`. Their DSNs are `SENTRY_DSN` and `SENTRY_DSN_WEB`, and an auth token with read access is in `.env`. The GitHub repo is linked in Sentry.\n123\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"35281226-519f-426c-b61e-07c4bc4848b6","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"task_status","taskId":"bkye7nzvk","taskType":"local_bash","description":"Lint, then rebuild the title lookup","status":"running","deltaSummary":null,"outputFilePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/tasks/bkye7nzvk.output","shell":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && uv run ruff check -q . && uv run ruff format --check -q . && echo \"ruff clean\" && S=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad && (time uv run python scripts/titles.py) > $S/titles-run.log 2>&1; echo \"exit $?\" >> $S/titles-run.log","kind":"bash","toolUseId":"toolu_0187f3ixML3TPF4vggNRKqnz"}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"67da2528-9296-41d3-92aa-d96aaa385455","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.836Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nBackground shell bkye7nzvk (\"Lint, then rebuild the title lookup\") is still running (command, shown on one line: `cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && uv run ruff check -q . && uv run ruff format --check -q . && echo \"ruff clean\" && S=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad && (time uv run python scripts/titles.py) > $S/titles-run.log 2>&1; echo \"exit $?\" >> $S/titles-run.log`). Do not start it again; to restart it, stop it with TaskStop first. You can read its output at /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/tasks/bkye7nzvk.output.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"67da2528-9296-41d3-92aa-d96aaa385455","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","path":"plugin:superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents\n\n# Dispatching Parallel Agents\n\n## Overview\n\nYou delegate tasks to specialized agents with isolated context. By precisely crafting their instructions and context, you ensure they stay focused and succeed at their task. They should never inherit your session's context or history — you construct exactly what they need. This also preserves your own context for coordination work.\n\nWhen you have multiple unrelated failures (different test files, different subsystems, different bugs), investigating them sequentially wastes time. Each investigation is independent and can happen in parallel.\n\n**Core principle:** Dispatch one agent per independent problem domain. Let them work concurrently.\n\n## When to Use\n\n```dot\ndigraph when_to_use {\n    \"Multiple failures?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Are they independent?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Single agent investigates all\" [shape=box];\n    \"One agent per problem domain\" [shape=box];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Sequential agents\" [shape=box];\n    \"Parallel dispatch\" [shape=box];\n\n    \"Multiple failures?\" -> \"Are they independent?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Single agent investigates all\" [label=\"no - related\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Can they work in parallel?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Parallel dispatch\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Sequential agents\" [label=\"no - shared state\"];\n}\n```\n\n**Use when:**\n- 3+ test files failing with different root causes\n- Multiple subsystems broken independently\n- Each problem can be understood without context from others\n- No shared state between investigations\n\n**Don't use when:**\n- Failures are related (fix one might fix others)\n- Need to understand full system state\n- Agents would interfere with each other\n\n## The Pattern\n\n### 1. Identify Independent Domains\n\nGroup failures by what's broken:\n- File A tests: Tool approval flow\n- File B tests: Batch completion behavior\n- File C tests: Abort functionality\n\nEach domain is independent - fixing tool approval doesn't affect abort tests.\n\n### 2. Create Focused Agent Tasks\n\nEach agent gets:\n- **Specific scope:** One test file or subsystem\n- **Clear goal:** Make these tests pass\n- **Constraints:** Don't change other code\n- **Expected output:** Summary of what you found and fixed\n\n### 3. Dispatch in Parallel\n\nIssue all three subagent dispatches in the same response — they run in parallel:\n\n```text\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts failures\"\n# All three run concurrently.\n```\n\nMultiple dispatch calls in one response = parallel execution. One per response = sequential.\n\n### 4. Review and Integrate\n\nWhen agents return:\n- Read each summary\n- Verify fixes don't conflict\n- Run full test suite\n- Integrate all changes\n\n## Agent Prompt Structure\n\nGood agent prompts are:\n1. **Focused** - One clear problem domain\n2. **Self-contained** - All context needed to understand the problem\n3. **Specific about output** - What should the agent return?\n\n```markdown\nFix the 3 failing tests in src/agents/agent-tool-abort.test.ts:\n\n1. \"should abort tool with partial output capture\" - expects 'interrupted at' in message\n2. \"should handle mixed completed and aborted tools\" - fast tool aborted instead of completed\n3. \"should properly track pendingToolCount\" - expects 3 results but gets 0\n\nThese are timing/race condition issues. Your task:\n\n1. Read the test file and understand what each test verifies\n2. Identify root cause - timing issues or actual bugs?\n3. Fix by:\n   - Replacing arbitrary timeouts with event-based waiting\n   - Fixing bugs in abort implementation if found\n   - Adjusting test expectations if testing changed behavior\n\nDo NOT just increase timeouts - find the real issue.\n\nReturn: Summary of what you found and what you fixed.\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n**❌ Too broad:** \"Fix all the tests\" - agent gets lost\n**✅ Specific:** \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\" - focused scope\n\n**❌ No context:** \"Fix the race condition\" - agent doesn't know where\n**✅ Context:** Paste the error messages and test names\n\n**❌ No constraints:** Agent might refactor everything\n**✅ Constraints:** \"Do NOT change production code\" or \"Fix tests only\"\n\n**❌ Vague output:** \"Fix it\" - you don't know what changed\n**✅ Specific:** \"Return summary of root cause and changes\"\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n**Related failures:** Fixing one might fix others - investigate together first\n**Need full context:** Understanding requires seeing entire system\n**Exploratory debugging:** You don't know what's broken yet\n**Shared state:** Agents would interfere (editing same files, using same resources)\n\n## Real Example from Session\n\n**Scenario:** 6 test failures across 3 files after major refactoring\n\n**Failures:**\n- agent-tool-abort.test.ts: 3 failures (timing issues)\n- batch-completion-behavior.test.ts: 2 failures (tools not executing)\n- tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts: 1 failure (execution count = 0)\n\n**Decision:** Independent domains - abort logic separate from batch completion separate from race conditions\n\n**Dispatch:**\n```\nAgent 1 → Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\nAgent 2 → Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts\nAgent 3 → Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts\n```\n\n**Results:**\n- Agent 1: Replaced timeouts with event-based waiting\n- Agent 2: Fixed event structure bug (threadId in wrong place)\n- Agent 3: Added wait for async tool execution to complete\n\n**Integration:** All fixes independent, no conflicts, full suite green\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter agents return:\n1. **Review each summary** - Understand what changed\n2. **Check for conflicts** - Did agents edit same code?\n3. **Run full suite** - Verify all fixes work together\n4. **Spot check** - Agents can make systematic errors\n"},{"name":"dataviz","path":"bundled:dataviz","content":"Base directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-502/bundled-skills/2.1.309/b686a53081079aed22896e5adfa24953/dataviz\n\n# Data Visualization\n\nA chart is **read by people and executed by you**. This skill turns \"make it look\ngood\" into a procedure with checks, so the result is right by construction rather\nthan by taste.\n\n**The method here is design-system-agnostic.** Nothing in the procedure, the form\nheuristic, the six checks, or the mark specs is specific to one product. A design\nsystem supplies a small set of *parameters* (its ramps, a categorical order, a\ndiverging pair, a status palette, a texture, its surfaces, its filter components);\nthe method consumes them unchanged. A **validated default palette** is the\nreference instance, fully specified in `references/palette.md`. To target your\nbrand, read that file's structure and substitute its values - touch nothing else.\n\n> The single most important habit: **the color part is computable, so compute it.**\n> Never eyeball whether a palette is colorblind-safe - run `scripts/validate_palette.js`.\n\n## The procedure - do these in order\n\nColor comes LAST. Most bad charts pick colors first.\n\n1. **Pick the form.** What is the data's job - magnitude, identity, polarity, a\n   single headline, change-over-time? The job picks the chart type, and sometimes\n   the answer is *not a chart* (a stat tile or hero number). -> `references/choosing-a-form.md`\n2. **Assign color by the job it does.** Categorical (identity), sequential\n   (magnitude), diverging (polarity), or status (state) - each has one rule.\n   Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled. -> `references/color-formula.md`\n3. **VALIDATE the palette - run the script, don't reason about Delta E.**\n   `node scripts/validate_palette.js \"<hex,hex,...>\" --mode light` (relative to\n   this skill's base directory - or load it as `<script type=\"module\">` in the\n   chart's own page, where it reads\n   `data-palette` off `<body>` and logs a `console.table` report). It returns\n   pass/fail on the lightness band, chroma floor, adjacent-pair CVD separation,\n   the normal-vision floor, and contrast. Fix anything that FAILs before continuing. Re-run for\n   `--mode dark` with that mode's surface.\n4. **Apply mark specs & spacers.** Thin marks, 4px rounded data-ends anchored to\n   the baseline, 2px lines, >=8px markers, a 2px surface gap between fills (stacked\n   segments and adjacent bars alike) and a 2px surface ring on overlapping marks,\n   selective direct labels. -> `references/marks-and-anatomy.md`\n5. **Add the hover layer - by default.** An HTML/SVG chart *is* interactive; ship\n   a crosshair+tooltip on line/area and a per-mark hover tooltip on bar/dot/cell.\n   The only form that skips it is a bare stat tile with no plot. Hit targets bigger\n   than the mark; filters in one row above the charts. -> `references/interaction.md`\n6. **Final accessibility pass.** For >= 2 series a legend is always present and <= 4\n   are also direct-labeled (a single series needs no legend box - the title names\n   it), so identity is never color-alone; a table view exists; dark mode is **selected** - its own\n   steps from the same ramps, validated against the dark surface, not an automatic\n   flip; texture is available for the CVD/print/forced-colors case.\n7. **Render it and look at it.** The validator checks color, not layout - open or\n   screenshot the output and eyeball it for label collisions, geometry, and overflow\n   before calling it done.\n\nThen check the result against **`references/anti-patterns.md`** - it is the catalog\nof what goes wrong. If your chart matches an entry, it's wrong.\n\n## Non-negotiables (true in every design system)\n\n- **Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled.** A 9th series is never a\n  generated hue - it folds into \"Other,\" small multiples, or composite encoding.\n- **One axis.** Never a dual-axis chart (two y-scales). Two measures of different\n  scale -> two charts, small multiples, or indexed to a common base. *(This is the\n  #1 chart mistake - see anti-patterns.)*\n- **Color follows the entity, never its rank.** A filter that changes the series\n  count must not repaint the survivors.\n- **Sequential = one hue, light->dark. Diverging = two hues + a neutral gray\n  midpoint.** Never a rainbow; never a hue at the diverging midpoint.\n- **Run the validator before shipping any categorical palette.** CVD Delta E >= 8 is the\n  target (OKLab ×100); 6-8 is a floor that is legal ONLY with secondary encoding. A\n  normal-vision floor below 15 is a hard FAIL - full-color readers can't tell the\n  pair apart; re-step it on the adjacent pairlist (secondary encoding does not excuse\n  this one); under `--pairs all` cut series or facet instead - see check 4. A contrast WARN\n  obligates visible labels or a table view - it is not dismissable.\n- **Thin marks; a legend always present for >= 2 series (none for one), with\n  selective direct labels (never a number on every point); recessive grid/axes.**\n- **Text wears text tokens, never the series color** - values, labels, and legends\n  stay in primary/secondary/muted ink; a colored mark beside them carries identity.\n- **Status colors are reserved** (good/warning/serious/critical) and never reused\n  for \"series 4\"; they ship with an icon + label, never color alone.\n\n## Plugging in a design system\n\nThe method is invariant; only these parameters change per system. The reference\ninstance - every value filled in - is `references/palette.md`.\n\n| Parameter | What the system provides |\n|---|---|\n| **Ramps** | the hue scales (named steps) the palette draws from |\n| **Categorical theme** | the fixed hue order (a named theme); default + alternates |\n| **Sequential hue** | the default single hue for magnitude |\n| **Diverging pair** | two warm/cool poles + a neutral midpoint |\n| **Status palette** | good / warning / serious / critical - steps distinct from categorical |\n| **Texture fill** | one directional hand-drawn fill, used at 45° / 135° |\n| **Surfaces** | light & dark chart-surface colors (the validator needs these) |\n| **Filter controls** | date-range & dimension controls (behavioral spec in `interaction.md`) |\n\nTo onboard a new system: fill those rows, feed its ramps to the validator, and let\nit snap each slot to the nearest passing step. Structure and rules stay as written.\n\n## Reference files\n\n| File | What it answers |\n|------|-----------------|\n| `references/choosing-a-form.md` | Which chart type / is it even a chart? |\n| `references/color-formula.md` | The four jobs, the six checks, snap-to-passing |\n| `references/marks-and-anatomy.md` | Mark specs, spacers, labels, figures, hero number |\n| `references/interaction.md` | Tooltips & hover, filters & time ranges |\n| `references/components.md` | The pieces a chart is made of - build each in plain HTML |\n| `references/anti-patterns.md` | **What goes wrong - check every chart against this** |\n| `references/palette.md` | **The reference palette instance** - every parameter, filled in; swap for your brand's |\n| `scripts/validate_palette.js` | Runnable six-checks validator (run it; don't eyeball) |\n"},{"name":"superpowers:test-driven-development","path":"plugin:superpowers:test-driven-development","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/test-driven-development\n\n# Test-Driven Development (TDD)\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.\n\n**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.\n\n**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**\n\n## When to Use\n\n**Always:**\n- New features\n- Bug fixes\n- Refactoring\n- Behavior changes\n\n**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**\n- Throwaway prototypes\n- Generated code\n- Configuration files\n\nThinking \"skip TDD just this once\"? Stop. That's rationalization.\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST\n```\n\nWrite code before the test? Delete it. Start over.\n\n**No exceptions:**\n- Don't keep it as \"reference\"\n- Don't \"adapt\" it while writing tests\n- Don't look at it\n- Delete means delete\n\nImplement fresh from tests. Period.\n\n## Red-Green-Refactor\n\n```dot\ndigraph tdd_cycle {\n    rankdir=LR;\n    red [label=\"RED\\nWrite failing test\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ffcccc\"];\n    verify_red [label=\"Verify fails\\ncorrectly\", shape=diamond];\n    green [label=\"GREEN\\nMinimal code\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccffcc\"];\n    verify_green [label=\"Verify passes\\nAll green\", shape=diamond];\n    refactor [label=\"REFACTOR\\nClean up\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccccff\"];\n    next [label=\"Next\", shape=ellipse];\n\n    red -> verify_red;\n    verify_red -> green [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_red -> red [label=\"wrong\\nfailure\"];\n    green -> verify_green;\n    verify_green -> refactor [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_green -> green [label=\"no\"];\n    refactor -> verify_green [label=\"stay\\ngreen\"];\n    verify_green -> next;\n    next -> red;\n}\n```\n\n### RED - Write Failing Test\n\nWrite one minimal test showing what should happen.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\ntest('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {\n  let attempts = 0;\n  const operation = () => {\n    attempts++;\n    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');\n    return 'success';\n  };\n\n  const result = await retryOperation(operation);\n\n  expect(result).toBe('success');\n  expect(attempts).toBe(3);\n});\n```\nClear name, tests real behavior, one thing\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\ntest('retry works', async () => {\n  const mock = jest.fn()\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');\n  await retryOperation(mock);\n  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\n});\n```\nVague name, tests mock not code\n</Bad>\n\n**Requirements:**\n- One behavior\n- Clear name\n- Real code (no mocks unless unavoidable)\n\n### Verify RED - Watch It Fail\n\n**MANDATORY. Never skip.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test fails (not errors)\n- Failure message is expected\n- Fails because feature missing (not typos)\n\n**Test passes?** You're testing existing behavior. Fix test.\n\n**Test errors?** Fix error, re-run until it fails correctly.\n\n### GREEN - Minimal Code\n\nWrite simplest code to pass the test.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn();\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (i === 2) throw e;\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('unreachable');\n}\n```\nJust enough to pass\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  options?: {\n    maxRetries?: number;\n    backoff?: 'linear' | 'exponential';\n    onRetry?: (attempt: number) => void;\n  }\n): Promise<T> {\n  // YAGNI\n}\n```\nOver-engineered\n</Bad>\n\nDon't add features, refactor other code, or \"improve\" beyond the test.\n\n### Verify GREEN - Watch It Pass\n\n**MANDATORY.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test passes\n- Other tests still pass\n- Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n\n**Test fails?** Fix code, not test.\n\n**Other tests fail?** Fix now.\n\n**\"Other tests\" means the project's suite, not just your file.** A\ngreen run of the test you wrote is not a green suite. Before you call\nthe change done, run the project's test command (bare `pytest`,\n`npm test`, `cargo test` — whatever the repo uses) even when your task\nnamed only one test file. A scope statement in your task bounds the\ndeliverable, not your verification. Any failure that run shows —\nincluding one you didn't cause — goes in your report by name; a red\ntest you watched scroll past and didn't mention is a report falsified\nby omission.\n\n### REFACTOR - Clean Up\n\nAfter green only:\n- Remove duplication\n- Improve names\n- Extract helpers\n\nKeep tests green. Don't add behavior.\n\n### Repeat\n\nNext failing test for next feature.\n\n## Good Tests\n\n| Quality | Good | Bad |\n|---------|------|-----|\n| **Minimal** | One thing. \"and\" in name? Split it. | `test('validates email and domain and whitespace')` |\n| **Clear** | Name describes behavior | `test('test1')` |\n| **Shows intent** | Demonstrates desired API | Obscures what code should do |\n\nWhen writing or changing any test, read [writing-good-tests.md](writing-good-tests.md) for the rules that keep tests honest:\n- Name the production change that would make the test fail — before writing it\n- Assert on real behavior, never on mock behavior\n- Keep test-only code in test utilities, out of production classes\n- Understand a dependency's side effects before mocking it\n\n## Common Rationalizations\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Too simple to test\" | Simple code breaks. Test takes 30 seconds. |\n| \"I'll test after\" | Tests written after pass immediately — which proves nothing. They may test the wrong thing, test the implementation instead of the behavior, or miss the edge case you forgot. You never watched it fail, so you never proved it can catch the bug. Test-first forces that failure. |\n| \"Tests after achieve same goals (spirit not ritual)\" | Tests-after answer \"what does this do?\"; tests-first answer \"what should this do?\" Tests written after are biased by the code you already wrote — you verify the cases you remembered, not the ones you'd have discovered. Coverage without proof the tests work. |\n| \"Already manually tested\" | Manual testing is ad-hoc: no record of what you covered, no way to re-run it when the code changes, easy to forget cases under pressure. \"Worked when I tried it\" ≠ comprehensive. Automated tests run the same way every time. |\n| \"Deleting X hours is wasteful\" | Sunk cost fallacy — that time is already spent either way. The real choice: rewrite with TDD (high confidence) vs. keep it and bolt tests on after (low confidence, likely bugs). Keeping code you can't trust is the waste. |\n| \"Keep as reference, write tests first\" | You'll adapt it. That's testing after. Delete means delete. |\n| \"Need to explore first\" | Fine. Throw away exploration, start with TDD. |\n| \"Test hard = design unclear\" | Listen to test. Hard to test = hard to use. |\n| \"TDD will slow me down\" | TDD IS the pragmatic path: catches bugs before commit, prevents regressions, lets you refactor without fear. \"Pragmatic\" shortcuts mean debugging in production — slower, not faster. |\n| \"Manual test faster\" | Manual doesn't prove edge cases. You'll re-test every change. |\n| \"Existing code has no tests\" | You're improving it. Add tests for existing code. |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP and Start Over\n\n- Code before test\n- Test after implementation\n- Test passes immediately\n- Can't explain why test failed\n- Tests added \"later\"\n- Rationalizing \"just this once\"\n- \"I already manually tested it\"\n- \"Tests after achieve the same purpose\"\n- \"It's about spirit not ritual\"\n- \"Keep as reference\" or \"adapt existing code\"\n- \"Already spent X hours, deleting is wasteful\"\n- \"TDD is dogmatic, I'm being pragmatic\"\n- \"This is different because...\"\n\n**All of these mean: Delete code. Start over with TDD.**\n\n## Example: Bug Fix\n\n**Bug:** Empty email accepted\n\n**RED**\n```typescript\ntest('rejects empty email', async () => {\n  const result = await submitForm({ email: '' });\n  expect(result.error).toBe('Email required');\n});\n```\n\n**Verify RED**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nFAIL: expected 'Email required', got undefined\n```\n\n**GREEN**\n```typescript\nfunction submitForm(data: FormData) {\n  if (!data.email?.trim()) {\n    return { error: 'Email required' };\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n**Verify GREEN**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nPASS\n```\n\n**REFACTOR**\nExtract validation for multiple fields if needed.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\nBefore marking work complete:\n\n- [ ] Every new function/method has a test\n- [ ] Watched each test fail before implementing\n- [ ] Each test failed for expected reason (feature missing, not typo)\n- [ ] Wrote minimal code to pass each test\n- [ ] All tests pass\n- [ ] Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n- [ ] Tests use real code (mocks only if unavoidable)\n- [ ] Edge cases and errors covered\n\nCan't check all boxes? You skipped TDD. Start over.\n\n## When Stuck\n\n| Problem | Solution |\n|---------|----------|\n| Don't know how to test | Write wished-for API. Write assertion first. Ask your human partner. |\n| Test too complicated | Design too complicated. Simplify interface. |\n| Must mock everything | Code too coupled. Use dependency injection. |\n| Test setup huge | Extract helpers. Still complex? Simplify design. |\n\n## Debugging Integration\n\nBug found? Write failing test reproducing it. Follow TDD cycle. Test proves fix and prevents regression.\n\nNever fix bugs without a test.\n\n## Final Rule\n\n```\nProduction code → test exists and failed first\nOtherwise → not TDD\n```\n\nNo exceptions without your human partner's permission.\n"},{"name":"superpowers:verification-before-completion","path":"plugin:superpowers:verification-before-completion","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/verification-before-completion\n\n# Verification Before Completion\n\n## Overview\n\n**Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n\n**Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n```\n\nIf you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n\n## The Gate Function\n\n```\nBEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n\n1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n   - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n   - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n\nSkip any step = lying, not verifying\n```\n\n## Common Failures\n\n| Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n|-------|----------|----------------|\n| Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n| Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n| Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n| Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n| Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n| Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n| Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP\n\n- Using \"should\", \"probably\", \"seems to\"\n- Expressing satisfaction before verification (\"Great!\", \"Perfect!\", \"Done!\", etc.)\n- About to commit/push/PR without verification\n- Trusting agent success reports\n- Relying on partial verification\n- Thinking \"just this once\"\n- Tired and wanting work over\n- **ANY wording implying success without having run verification**\n\n## Rationalization Prevention\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Should work now\" | RUN the verification |\n| \"I'm confident\" | Confidence ≠ evidence |\n| \"Just this once\" | No exceptions |\n| \"Linter passed\" | Linter ≠ compiler |\n| \"Agent said success\" | Verify independently |\n| \"I'm tired\" | Exhaustion ≠ excuse |\n| \"Partial check is enough\" | Partial proves nothing |\n| \"Different words so rule doesn't apply\" | Spirit over letter |\n\n## Key Patterns\n\n**Tests:**\n```\n✅ [Run test command] [See: 34/34 pass] \"All tests pass\"\n❌ \"Should pass now\" / \"Looks correct\"\n```\n\n**Regression tests (TDD Red-Green):**\n```\n✅ Write → Run (pass) → Revert fix → Run (MUST FAIL) → Restore → Run (pass)\n❌ \"I've written a regression test\" (without red-green verification)\n```\n\n**Build:**\n```\n✅ [Run build] [See: exit 0] \"Build passes\"\n❌ \"Linter passed\" (linter doesn't check compilation)\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n```\n✅ Re-read plan → Create checklist → Verify each → Report gaps or completion\n❌ \"Tests pass, phase complete\"\n```\n\n**Agent delegation:**\n```\n✅ Agent reports success → Check VCS diff → Verify changes → Report actual state\n❌ Trust agent report\n```\n\n## When To Apply\n\n**ALWAYS before:**\n- ANY variation of success/completion claims\n- ANY expression of satisfaction\n- ANY positive statement about work state\n- Committing, PR creation, task completion\n- Moving to next task\n- Delegating to agents\n\n**Rule applies to:**\n- Exact phrases\n- Paraphrases and synonyms\n- Implications of success\n- ANY communication suggesting completion/correctness\n"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"94cdf2ab-fca6-4c6e-a348-17e025342adb","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:12:01.837Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\nPath: plugin:superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents\n\n# Dispatching Parallel Agents\n\n## Overview\n\nYou delegate tasks to specialized agents with isolated context. By precisely crafting their instructions and context, you ensure they stay focused and succeed at their task. They should never inherit your session's context or history — you construct exactly what they need. This also preserves your own context for coordination work.\n\nWhen you have multiple unrelated failures (different test files, different subsystems, different bugs), investigating them sequentially wastes time. Each investigation is independent and can happen in parallel.\n\n**Core principle:** Dispatch one agent per independent problem domain. Let them work concurrently.\n\n## When to Use\n\n```dot\ndigraph when_to_use {\n    \"Multiple failures?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Are they independent?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Single agent investigates all\" [shape=box];\n    \"One agent per problem domain\" [shape=box];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Sequential agents\" [shape=box];\n    \"Parallel dispatch\" [shape=box];\n\n    \"Multiple failures?\" -> \"Are they independent?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Single agent investigates all\" [label=\"no - related\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Can they work in parallel?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Parallel dispatch\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Sequential agents\" [label=\"no - shared state\"];\n}\n```\n\n**Use when:**\n- 3+ test files failing with different root causes\n- Multiple subsystems broken independently\n- Each problem can be understood without context from others\n- No shared state between investigations\n\n**Don't use when:**\n- Failures are related (fix one might fix others)\n- Need to understand full system state\n- Agents would interfere with each other\n\n## The Pattern\n\n### 1. Identify Independent Domains\n\nGroup failures by what's broken:\n- File A tests: Tool approval flow\n- File B tests: Batch completion behavior\n- File C tests: Abort functionality\n\nEach domain is independent - fixing tool approval doesn't affect abort tests.\n\n### 2. Create Focused Agent Tasks\n\nEach agent gets:\n- **Specific scope:** One test file or subsystem\n- **Clear goal:** Make these tests pass\n- **Constraints:** Don't change other code\n- **Expected output:** Summary of what you found and fixed\n\n### 3. Dispatch in Parallel\n\nIssue all three subagent dispatches in the same response — they run in parallel:\n\n```text\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts failures\"\n# All three run concurrently.\n```\n\nMultiple dispatch calls in one response = parallel execution. One per response = sequential.\n\n### 4. Review and Integrate\n\nWhen agents return:\n- Read each summary\n- Verify fixes don't conflict\n- Run full test suite\n- Integrate all changes\n\n## Agent Prompt Structure\n\nGood agent prompts are:\n1. **Focused** - One clear problem domain\n2. **Self-contained** - All context needed to understand the problem\n3. **Specific about output** - What should the agent return?\n\n```markdown\nFix the 3 failing tests in src/agents/agent-tool-abort.test.ts:\n\n1. \"should abort tool with partial output capture\" - expects 'interrupted at' in message\n2. \"should handle mixed completed and aborted tools\" - fast tool aborted instead of completed\n3. \"should properly track pendingToolCount\" - expects 3 results but gets 0\n\nThese are timing/race condition issues. Your task:\n\n1. Read the test file and understand what each test verifies\n2. Identify root cause - timing issues or actual bugs?\n3. Fix by:\n   - Replacing arbitrary timeouts with event-based waiting\n   - Fixing bugs in abort implementation if found\n   - Adjusting test expectations if testing changed behavior\n\nDo NOT just increase timeouts - find the real issue.\n\nReturn: Summary of what you found and what you fixed.\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n**❌ Too broad:** \"Fix all the tests\" - agent gets lost\n**✅ Specific:** \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\" - focused scope\n\n**❌ No context:** \"Fix the race condition\" - agent doesn't know where\n**✅ Context:** Paste the error messages and test names\n\n**❌ No constraints:** Agent might refactor everything\n**✅ Constraints:** \"Do NOT change production code\" or \"Fix tests only\"\n\n**❌ Vague output:** \"Fix it\" - you don't know what changed\n**✅ Specific:** \"Return summary of root cause and changes\"\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n**Related failures:** Fixing one might fix others - investigate together first\n**Need full context:** Understanding requires seeing entire system\n**Exploratory debugging:** You don't know what's broken yet\n**Shared state:** Agents would interfere (editing same files, using same resources)\n\n## Real Example from Session\n\n**Scenario:** 6 test failures across 3 files after major refactoring\n\n**Failures:**\n- agent-tool-abort.test.ts: 3 failures (timing issues)\n- batch-completion-behavior.test.ts: 2 failures (tools not executing)\n- tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts: 1 failure (execution count = 0)\n\n**Decision:** Independent domains - abort logic separate from batch completion separate from race conditions\n\n**Dispatch:**\n```\nAgent 1 → Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\nAgent 2 → Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts\nAgent 3 → Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts\n```\n\n**Results:**\n- Agent 1: Replaced timeouts with event-based waiting\n- Agent 2: Fixed event structure bug (threadId in wrong place)\n- Agent 3: Added wait for async tool execution to complete\n\n**Integration:** All fixes independent, no conflicts, full suite green\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter agents return:\n1. **Review each summary** - Understand what changed\n2. **Check for conflicts** - Did agents edit same code?\n3. **Run full suite** - Verify all fixes work together\n4. **Spot check** - Agents can make systematic errors\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: dataviz\nPath: bundled:dataviz\n\nBase directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-502/bundled-skills/2.1.309/b686a53081079aed22896e5adfa24953/dataviz\n\n# Data Visualization\n\nA chart is **read by people and executed by you**. This skill turns \"make it look\ngood\" into a procedure with checks, so the result is right by construction rather\nthan by taste.\n\n**The method here is design-system-agnostic.** Nothing in the procedure, the form\nheuristic, the six checks, or the mark specs is specific to one product. A design\nsystem supplies a small set of *parameters* (its ramps, a categorical order, a\ndiverging pair, a status palette, a texture, its surfaces, its filter components);\nthe method consumes them unchanged. A **validated default palette** is the\nreference instance, fully specified in `references/palette.md`. To target your\nbrand, read that file's structure and substitute its values - touch nothing else.\n\n> The single most important habit: **the color part is computable, so compute it.**\n> Never eyeball whether a palette is colorblind-safe - run `scripts/validate_palette.js`.\n\n## The procedure - do these in order\n\nColor comes LAST. Most bad charts pick colors first.\n\n1. **Pick the form.** What is the data's job - magnitude, identity, polarity, a\n   single headline, change-over-time? The job picks the chart type, and sometimes\n   the answer is *not a chart* (a stat tile or hero number). -> `references/choosing-a-form.md`\n2. **Assign color by the job it does.** Categorical (identity), sequential\n   (magnitude), diverging (polarity), or status (state) - each has one rule.\n   Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled. -> `references/color-formula.md`\n3. **VALIDATE the palette - run the script, don't reason about Delta E.**\n   `node scripts/validate_palette.js \"<hex,hex,...>\" --mode light` (relative to\n   this skill's base directory - or load it as `<script type=\"module\">` in the\n   chart's own page, where it reads\n   `data-palette` off `<body>` and logs a `console.table` report). It returns\n   pass/fail on the lightness band, chroma floor, adjacent-pair CVD separation,\n   the normal-vision floor, and contrast. Fix anything that FAILs before continuing. Re-run for\n   `--mode dark` with that mode's surface.\n4. **Apply mark specs & spacers.** Thin marks, 4px rounded data-ends anchored to\n   the baseline, 2px lines, >=8px markers, a 2px surface gap between fills (stacked\n   segments and adjacent bars alike) and a 2px surface ring on overlapping marks,\n   selective direct labels. -> `references/marks-and-anatomy.md`\n5. **Add the hover layer - by default.** An HTML/SVG chart *is* interactive; ship\n   a crosshair+tooltip on line/area and a per-mark hover tooltip on bar/dot/cell.\n   The only form that skips it is a bare stat tile with no plot. Hit targets bigger\n   than the mark; filters in one row above the charts. -> `references/interaction.md`\n6. **Final accessibility pass.** For >= 2 series a legend is always present and <= 4\n   are also direct-labeled (a single series needs no legend box - the title names\n   it), so identity is never color-alone; a table view exists; dark mode is **selected** - its own\n   steps from the same ramps, validated against the dark surface, not an automatic\n   flip; texture is available for the CVD/print/forced-colors case.\n7. **Render it and look at it.** The validator checks color, not layout - open or\n   screenshot the output and eyeball it for label collisions, geometry, and overflow\n   before calling it done.\n\nThen check the result against **`references/anti-patterns.md`** - it is the catalog\nof what goes wrong. If your chart matches an entry, it's wrong.\n\n## Non-negotiables (true in every design system)\n\n- **Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled.** A 9th series is never a\n  generated hue - it folds into \"Other,\" small multiples, or composite encoding.\n- **One axis.** Never a dual-axis chart (two y-scales). Two measures of different\n  scale -> two charts, small multiples, or indexed to a common base. *(This is the\n  #1 chart mistake - see anti-patterns.)*\n- **Color follows the entity, never its rank.** A filter that changes the series\n  count must not repaint the survivors.\n- **Sequential = one hue, light->dark. Diverging = two hues + a neutral gray\n  midpoint.** Never a rainbow; never a hue at the diverging midpoint.\n- **Run the validator before shipping any categorical palette.** CVD Delta E >= 8 is the\n  target (OKLab ×100); 6-8 is a floor that is legal ONLY with secondary encoding. A\n  normal-vision floor below 15 is a hard FAIL - full-color readers can't tell the\n  pair apart; re-step it on the adjacent pairlist (secondary encoding does not excuse\n  this one); under `--pairs all` cut series or facet instead - see check 4. A contrast WARN\n  obligates visible labels or a table view - it is not dismissable.\n- **Thin marks; a legend always present for >= 2 series (none for one), with\n  selective direct labels (never a number on every point); recessive grid/axes.**\n- **Text wears text tokens, never the series color** - values, labels, and legends\n  stay in primary/secondary/muted ink; a colored mark beside them carries identity.\n- **Status colors are reserved** (good/warning/serious/critical) and never reused\n  for \"series 4\"; they ship with an icon + label, never color alone.\n\n## Plugging in a design system\n\nThe method is invariant; only these parameters change per system. The reference\ninstance - every value filled in - is `references/palette.md`.\n\n| Parameter | What the system provides |\n|---|---|\n| **Ramps** | the hue scales (named steps) the palette draws from |\n| **Categorical theme** | the fixed hue order (a named theme); default + alternates |\n| **Sequential hue** | the default single hue for magnitude |\n| **Diverging pair** | two warm/cool poles + a neutral midpoint |\n| **Status palette** | good / warning / serious / critical - steps distinct from categorical |\n| **Texture fill** | one directional hand-drawn fill, used at 45° / 135° |\n| **Surfaces** | light & dark chart-surface colors (the validator needs these) |\n| **Filter controls** | date-range & dimension controls (behavioral spec in `interaction.md`) |\n\nTo onboard a new system: fill those rows, feed its ramps to the validator, and let\nit snap each slot to the nearest passing step. Structure and rules stay as written.\n\n## Reference files\n\n| File | What it answers |\n|------|-----------------|\n| `references/choosing-a-form.md` | Which chart type / is it even a chart? |\n| `references/color-formula.md` | The four jobs, the six checks, snap-to-passing |\n| `references/marks-and-anatomy.md` | Mark specs, spacers, labels, figures, hero number |\n| `references/interaction.md` | Tooltips & hover, filters & time ranges |\n| `references/components.md` | The pieces a chart is made of - build each in plain HTML |\n| `references/anti-patterns.md` | **What goes wrong - check every chart against this** |\n| `references/palette.md` | **The reference palette instance** - every parameter, filled in; swap for your brand's |\n| `scripts/validate_palette.js` | Runnable six-checks validator (run it; don't eyeball) |\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: superpowers:test-driven-development\nPath: plugin:superpowers:test-driven-development\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/test-driven-development\n\n# Test-Driven Development (TDD)\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.\n\n**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.\n\n**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**\n\n## When to Use\n\n**Always:**\n- New features\n- Bug fixes\n- Refactoring\n- Behavior changes\n\n**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**\n- Throwaway prototypes\n- Generated code\n- Configuration files\n\nThinking \"skip TDD just this once\"? Stop. That's rationalization.\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST\n```\n\nWrite code before the test? Delete it. Start over.\n\n**No exceptions:**\n- Don't keep it as \"reference\"\n- Don't \"adapt\" it while writing tests\n- Don't look at it\n- Delete means delete\n\nImplement fresh from tests. Period.\n\n## Red-Green-Refactor\n\n```dot\ndigraph tdd_cycle {\n    rankdir=LR;\n    red [label=\"RED\\nWrite failing test\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ffcccc\"];\n    verify_red [label=\"Verify fails\\ncorrectly\", shape=diamond];\n    green [label=\"GREEN\\nMinimal code\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccffcc\"];\n    verify_green [label=\"Verify passes\\nAll green\", shape=diamond];\n    refactor [label=\"REFACTOR\\nClean up\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccccff\"];\n    next [label=\"Next\", shape=ellipse];\n\n    red -> verify_red;\n    verify_red -> green [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_red -> red [label=\"wrong\\nfailure\"];\n    green -> verify_green;\n    verify_green -> refactor [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_green -> green [label=\"no\"];\n    refactor -> verify_green [label=\"stay\\ngreen\"];\n    verify_green -> next;\n    next -> red;\n}\n```\n\n### RED - Write Failing Test\n\nWrite one minimal test showing what should happen.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\ntest('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {\n  let attempts = 0;\n  const operation = () => {\n    attempts++;\n    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');\n    return 'success';\n  };\n\n  const result = await retryOperation(operation);\n\n  expect(result).toBe('success');\n  expect(attempts).toBe(3);\n});\n```\nClear name, tests real behavior, one thing\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\ntest('retry works', async () => {\n  const mock = jest.fn()\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');\n  await retryOperation(mock);\n  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\n});\n```\nVague name, tests mock not code\n</Bad>\n\n**Requirements:**\n- One behavior\n- Clear name\n- Real code (no mocks unless unavoidable)\n\n### Verify RED - Watch It Fail\n\n**MANDATORY. Never skip.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test fails (not errors)\n- Failure message is expected\n- Fails because feature missing (not typos)\n\n**Test passes?** You're testing existing behavior. Fix test.\n\n**Test errors?** Fix error, re-run until it fails correctly.\n\n### GREEN - Minimal Code\n\nWrite simplest code to pass the test.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn();\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (i === 2) throw e;\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('unreachable');\n}\n```\nJust enough to pass\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  options?: {\n    maxRetries?: number;\n    backoff?: 'linear' | 'exponential';\n    onRetry?: (attempt: number) => void;\n  }\n): Promise<T> {\n  // YAGNI\n}\n```\nOver-engineered\n</Bad>\n\nDon't add features, refactor other code, or \"improve\" beyond the test.\n\n### Verify GREEN - Watch It Pass\n\n**MANDATORY.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test passes\n- Other tests still pass\n- Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n\n**Test fails?** Fix code, not test.\n\n**Other tests fail?** Fix now.\n\n**\"Other tests\" means the project's suite, not just your file.** A\ngreen run of the test you wrote is not a green suite. Before you call\nthe change done, run the project's test command (bare `pytest`,\n`npm test`, `cargo test` — whatever the repo uses) even when your task\nnamed only one test file. A scope statement in your task bounds the\ndeliverable, not your verification. Any failure that run shows —\nincluding one you didn't cause — goes in your report by name; a red\ntest you watched scroll past and didn't mention is a report falsified\nby omission.\n\n### REFACTOR - Clean Up\n\nAfter green only:\n- Remove duplication\n- Improve names\n- Extract helpers\n\nKeep tests green. Don't add behavior.\n\n### Repeat\n\nNext failing test for next feature.\n\n## Good Tests\n\n| Quality | Good | Bad |\n|---------|------|-----|\n| **Minimal** | One thing. \"and\" in name? Split it. | `test('validates email and domain and whitespace')` |\n| **Clear** | Name describes behavior | `test('test1')` |\n| **Shows intent** | Demonstrates desired API | Obscures what code should do |\n\nWhen writing or changing any test, read [writing-good-tests.md](writing-good-tests.md) for the rules that keep tests honest:\n- Name the production change that would make the test fail — before writing it\n- Assert on real behavior, never on mock behavior\n- Keep test-only code in test utilities, out of production classes\n- Understand a dependency's side effects before mocking it\n\n## Common Rationalizations\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Too simple to test\" | Simple code breaks. Test takes 30 seconds. |\n| \"I'll test after\" | Tests written after pass immediately — which proves nothing. They may test the wrong thing, test the implementation instead of the behavior, or miss the edge case you forgot. You never watched it fail, so you never proved it can catch the bug. Test-first forces that failure. |\n| \"Tests after achieve same goals (spirit not ritual)\" | Tests-after answer \"what does this do?\"; tests-first answer \"what should this do?\" Tests written after are biased by the code you already wrote — you verify the cases you remembered, not the ones you'd have discovered. Coverage without proof the tests work. |\n| \"Already manually tested\" | Manual testing is ad-hoc: no record of what you covered, no way to re-run it when the code changes, easy to forget cases under pressure. \"Worked when I tried it\" ≠ comprehensive. Automated tests run the same way every time. |\n| \"Deleting X hours is wasteful\" | Sunk cost fallacy — that time is already spent either way. The real choice: rewrite with TDD (high confidence) vs. keep it and bolt tests on after (low confidence, likely bugs). Keeping code you can't trust is the waste. |\n| \"Keep as reference, write tests first\" | You'll adapt it. That's testing after. Delete means delete. |\n| \"Need to explore first\" | Fine. Throw away exploration, start with TDD. |\n| \"Test hard = design unclear\" | Listen to test. Hard to test = hard to use. |\n| \"TDD will slow me down\" | TDD IS the pragmatic path: catches bugs before commit, prevents regressions, lets you refactor without fear. \"Pragmatic\" shortcuts mean debugging in production — slower, not faster. |\n| \"Manual test faster\" | Manual doesn't prove edge cases. You'll re-test every change. |\n| \"Existing code has no tests\" | You're improving it. Add tests for existing code. |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP and Start Over\n\n- Code before test\n- Test after implementation\n- Test passes immediately\n- Can't explain why test failed\n- Tests added \"later\"\n- Rationalizing \"just this once\"\n- \"I already manually tested it\"\n- \"Tests after achieve the same purpose\"\n- \"It's about spirit not ritual\"\n- \"Keep as reference\" or \"adapt existing code\"\n- \"Already spent X hours, deleting is wasteful\"\n- \"TDD is dogmatic, I'm being pragmatic\"\n- \"This is different because...\"\n\n**All of these mean: Delete code. Start over with TDD.**\n\n## Example: Bug Fix\n\n**Bug:** Empty email accepted\n\n**RED**\n```typescript\ntest('rejects empty email', async () => {\n  const result = await submitForm({ email: '' });\n  expect(result.error).toBe('Email required');\n});\n```\n\n**Verify RED**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nFAIL: expected 'Email required', got undefined\n```\n\n**GREEN**\n```typescript\nfunction submitForm(data: FormData) {\n  if (!data.email?.trim()) {\n    return { error: 'Email required' };\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n**Verify GREEN**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nPASS\n```\n\n**REFACTOR**\nExtract validation for multiple fields if needed.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\nBefore marking work complete:\n\n- [ ] Every new function/method has a test\n- [ ] Watched each test fail before implementing\n- [ ] Each test failed for expected reason (feature missing, not typo)\n- [ ] Wrote minimal code to pass each test\n- [ ] All tests pass\n- [ ] Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n- [ ] Tests use real code (mocks only if unavoidable)\n- [ ] Edge cases and errors covered\n\nCan't check all boxes? You skipped TDD. Start over.\n\n## When Stuck\n\n| Problem | Solution |\n|---------|----------|\n| Don't know how to test | Write wished-for API. Write assertion first. Ask your human partner. |\n| Test too complicated | Design too complicated. Simplify interface. |\n| Must mock everything | Code too coupled. Use dependency injection. |\n| Test setup huge | Extract helpers. Still complex? Simplify design. |\n\n## Debugging Integration\n\nBug found? Write failing test reproducing it. Follow TDD cycle. Test proves fix and prevents regression.\n\nNever fix bugs without a test.\n\n## Final Rule\n\n```\nProduction code → test exists and failed first\nOtherwise → not TDD\n```\n\nNo exceptions without your human partner's permission.\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: superpowers:verification-before-completion\nPath: plugin:superpowers:verification-before-completion\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/verification-before-completion\n\n# Verification Before Completion\n\n## Overview\n\n**Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n\n**Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n```\n\nIf you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n\n## The Gate Function\n\n```\nBEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n\n1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n   - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n   - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n\nSkip any step = lying, not verifying\n```\n\n## Common Failures\n\n| Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n|-------|----------|----------------|\n| Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n| Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n| Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n| Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n| Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n| Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n| Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP\n\n- Using \"should\", \"probably\", \"seems to\"\n- Expressing satisfaction before verification (\"Great!\", \"Perfect!\", \"Done!\", etc.)\n- About to commit/push/PR without verification\n- Trusting agent success reports\n- Relying on partial verification\n- Thinking \"just this once\"\n- Tired and wanting work over\n- **ANY wording implying success without having run verification**\n\n## Rationalization Prevention\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Should work now\" | RUN the verification |\n| \"I'm confident\" | Confidence ≠ evidence |\n| \"Just this once\" | No exceptions |\n| \"Linter passed\" | Linter ≠ compiler |\n| \"Agent said success\" | Verify independently |\n| \"I'm tired\" | Exhaustion ≠ excuse |\n| \"Partial check is enough\" | Partial proves nothing |\n| \"Different words so rule doesn't apply\" | Spirit over letter |\n\n## Key Patterns\n\n**Tests:**\n```\n✅ [Run test command] [See: 34/34 pass] \"All tests pass\"\n❌ \"Should pass now\" / \"Looks correct\"\n```\n\n**Regression tests (TDD Red-Green):**\n```\n✅ Write → Run (pass) → Revert fix → Run (MUST FAIL) → Restore → Run (pass)\n❌ \"I've written a regression test\" (without red-green verification)\n```\n\n**Build:**\n```\n✅ [Run build] [See: exit 0] \"Build passes\"\n❌ \"Linter passed\" (linter doesn't check compilation)\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n```\n✅ Re-read plan → Create checklist → Verify each → Report gaps or completion\n❌ \"Tests pass, phase complete\"\n```\n\n**Agent delegation:**\n```\n✅ Agent reports success → Check VCS diff → Verify changes → Report actual state\n❌ Trust agent report\n```\n\n## When To Apply\n\n**ALWAYS before:**\n- ANY variation of success/completion claims\n- ANY expression of satisfaction\n- ANY positive statement about work state\n- Committing, PR creation, task completion\n- Moving to next task\n- Delegating to agents\n\n**Rule applies to:**\n- Exact phrases\n- Paraphrases and synonyms\n- Implications of success\n- ANY communication suggesting completion/correctness\n\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
