{"parentUuid":"2a0159b8-642b-4a4d-94c3-e661c8333aa4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZ2DEHdCxAPmVo7bJth1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JaYw15BnzV1ACS7kX4kDyu","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && awk '/^\\*\\*34\\./,0' Docs/DECISIONS.md | grep -n \"\" | sed -n '28,110p'","description":"Read decision 34's draft from the then-and-now bullet on"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2454,"cache_read_input_tokens":292018,"output_tokens":239,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":41},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2454,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":239,"cache_read_input_tokens":292018,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2454,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2454},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01JaYw15BnzV1ACS7kX4kDyu":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && awk '/^\\*\\*34\\./,0' Docs/DECISIONS.md | grep -n \"\" | sed -n '28,110p'","description":"Read decision 34's draft from the then-and-now bullet on"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"935c9fa4-5a10-4c59-be6a-381f7b2b94c1","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgZ2DDXEq3rmHoC6j1jCz","requestRef":"6e3350fe-7b18-460c-8e2d-293e6f68c8c4","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"6fb61e01-bc18-4f1e-b948-1a92d7f292c7","timestamp":"2026-10-30T20:04:50.031Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"6fb61e01-bc18-4f1e-b948-1a92d7f292c7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b997187a-e537-4e1f-b3c7-20b1dfc34455","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JaYw15BnzV1ACS7kX4kDyu","type":"tool_result","content":"28:  - The final build made 327 requests in 39 minutes, 7.2 s each on average. It found the other 97 already cached: the draw's papers, fetched first, and those from a first build that a 520 stopped.\n29:  - The records of the accuracy draw's 200 papers came next, queued after that fetch and never beside it: {{PAIR_RECORD_COUNTS}}\n30:  - The other LCCNs' records come in 2D, and only for the papers among stored printings, as decision 29 does for pages (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).\n31:- **The title lookup** (`scripts/titles.py`, `data/titles/titles.json`, the `Title` model) has one entry per LCCN: the title, the place and state as printed, what was supplied, the state today, and the two together. It also keeps the place, county and state that a fetched record catalogues today (`catalogued_place`, `catalogued_county` and `catalogued_state`). {{TITLE_LOOKUP_COUNTS}}\n32:- **GeoNames** (`paperghosts/geo/geonames.py`):\n33:  - The data: GeoNames' US, Puerto Rico and Virgin Islands dumps of 2026-10-30, with their state and county codes.\n34:    - `US.zip`: 71.1 MB, 2,241,410 rows\n35:    - `PR.zip`: 0.3 MB, 8,277 rows\n36:    - `VI.zip`: 0.1 MB, 1,872 rows\n37:  - A territory's municipios or islands serve as its counties. No title names any other territory.\n38:  - `data/geonames/manifest.json` records each file's URL, retrieval date, size and sha256, and the CC BY 4.0 licence.\n39:  - Only populated places count. GeoNames' 107,543 \"(historical)\" places in class S are kept apart as ghost towns, and only a title record's county can accept one.\n40:  - Names compare without accents, case, punctuation or spaces, so \"Charlestown\" is \"Charles Town\", and \"St.\" reads as Saint.\n41:  - Ties break in this order:\n42:    1. the printed county\n43:    2. a living place before a section, and before a historical, abandoned or destroyed one\n44:    3. a match by name before one by alternate name\n45:    4. a capital or county seat\n46:    5. the larger population\n47:    6. the lower ID\n48:  - The space-free names came from this check's first look at Charlestown. GeoNames lists Charlestown among the capital Charleston's alternate names, and Charleston's population won.\n49:- **The record check** (`paperghosts/geo/papers.py`, from the EM's review). loc.gov's title record gives each paper's city, county and state as catalogued today.\n50:  1. The printed place is geocoded in its modern state, the cataloguer's bracketed place first.\n51:  2. It is checked against the record's county: GeoNames' county (admin2) has to be one the record names, in the record's state. A paper that moved has all its counties.\n52:  3. A place outside the record's county, or no place at all, gives way to the record's place, geocoded in its county. The printed place stays for display: \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\". Merged towns are catalogued under their successors, so this settles them too.\n53:  4. Failing that, a ghost town of the record's name in the record's county counts, flagged historical. Clifton, S.D. is the case.\n54:  5. Failing that, there is no point. A pin that disagrees with the record is the dangerous kind of answer.\n55:  - A record that names no county GeoNames has in its state can't check a place. The printed place stands, or, where that resolves to nothing, the record's place, unchecked.\n56:    - Alaska is one such case: loc.gov files Seward under \"Seward\", and GeoNames files it under the Kenai Peninsula Borough.\n57:    - Connecticut is another: GeoNames files its places under planning regions now.\n58:  - **I made the Alaska rule on these 200.** The first run of the check, with 23 records cached, turned Seward from a right answer into no point.\n59:- **Accuracy on the papers** (`scripts/geocode_check.py`, `eval/results/phase2b/geocoder.json`, answers in `geocoder_expected.json`):\n60:  - The draw: 200 distinct (place, state) pairs, by walking the papers in seeded order (seed \"2B geocoder\"). I checked each by hand.\n61:  - Each pair is geocoded twice: by its printed place alone, and with the record check against its paper's record.\n62:  - **Without the record check, {{N_WITHOUT}} are right: {{P_WITHOUT}}.**\n63:    - Ponce, P.R. is right now, because the Puerto Rico dump is an input.\n64:    - Before the dump, 193 were right (96.5%). Before the parser fix the draw prompted, 192 were (96.0%).\n65:  - **With the record check, {{N_WITH}} are right: {{P_WITH}}.** {{WRONG_PINS}}\n66:  - What the records made of the 200: {{RECORD_OUTCOMES}}\n67:  - Some are right under an old name or a merger: Allegheny is Pittsburgh, and Daytona is Daytona Beach. Fort Wrangel, Sumterville, North Yakima and Langston City are old names their towns list.\n68:  - **The failures, by kind:**\n69:\n70:    | Kind | Without the check | With it | Cases |\n71:    |---|---|---|---|\n72:    | merged into a town that doesn't list its name | 3 | {{MERGED}} | East Saginaw into Saginaw; Lardo into McCall; New Oregon into Cresco |\n73:    | renamed, with its old name now another town's | 1 | {{RENAMED}} | Bloomington, Iowa, Muscatine's name until 1849. Without the check, it's a wrong pin, on a living Bloomington elsewhere |\n74:    | two towns named as one place | 1 | {{TWO}} | \"Norfolk and Portsmouth, Va.\" |\n75:    | a ghost town filed outside populated places | 1 | {{GHOST}} | Clifton, S.D., which GeoNames has as \"Clifton (historical)\" in class S |\n76:    | outside GeoNames' US dump | 0 | 0 | Ponce, P.R., right since the Puerto Rico dump became an input |\n77:\n78:  - **These 200 are in-sample for the check.** Its fixes come from their failures, through the EM's review, and so does the Alaska rule. 2D measures any further fix on a fresh draw (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).\n79:- **The 24 judged reports' story places,** as their text gives them: an early look at what the reader will extract.\n80:  - The text gives a town and a state for only 3. The geocoder puts all 3 right (43.8–100%).\n81:  - 9 name a town but no state. Datelines like \"CHICAGO, Jan. 1\" are the usual case.\n82:  - 8 name no town at all, or only a venue such as \"the National Theater\".\n83:  - 4 are abroad: Jersey, Peking, Blickling and France.\n84:  - **For the 9 with a town only, the paper's own state is a poor guess:**\n85:    - right for 3, the local stories at Middlebury, Waterloo and Chittenden\n86:    - a wrong place for 2: Adamsville lands in Pennsylvania, and Easton in Indiana matches West Newton, which lists Easton as an alternate name\n87:    - nothing for 4: Chicago in Connecticut, Kansas and Utah papers, and Bordentown in a Michigan one\n88:  - **So a story place that is only a town resolves from a county or state the text gives, or from its dateline. Otherwise it stays unresolved, and it never falls back on the paper's state** (the EM's review). 2C measures the rule on its gold set, and 2D applies it (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"05008e50-6809-4e5c-9e06-e80c6fcbec41","timestamp":"2026-10-30T20:04:50.703Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"28:  - The final build made 327 requests in 39 minutes, 7.2 s each on average. It found the other 97 already cached: the draw's papers, fetched first, and those from a first build that a 520 stopped.\n29:  - The records of the accuracy draw's 200 papers came next, queued after that fetch and never beside it: {{PAIR_RECORD_COUNTS}}\n30:  - The other LCCNs' records come in 2D, and only for the papers among stored printings, as decision 29 does for pages (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).\n31:- **The title lookup** (`scripts/titles.py`, `data/titles/titles.json`, the `Title` model) has one entry per LCCN: the title, the place and state as printed, what was supplied, the state today, and the two together. It also keeps the place, county and state th