ts county. The printed place stays for display: \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\". Merged towns are catalogued under their successors, so this settles them too.\n53:  4. Failing that, a ghost town of the record's name in the record's county counts, flagged historical. Clifton, S.D. is the case.\n54:  5. Failing that, there is no point. A pin that disagrees with the record is the dangerous kind of answer.\n55:  - A record that names no county GeoNames has in its state can't check a place. The printed place stands, or, where that resolves to nothing, the record's place, unchecked.\n56:    - Alaska is one such case: loc.gov files Seward under \"Seward\", and GeoNames files it under the Kenai Peninsula Borough.\n57:    - Connecticut is another: GeoNames files its places under planning regions now.\n58:  - **I made the Alaska rule on these 200.** The first run of the check, with 23 records cached, turned Seward from a right answer into no point.\n59:- **Accuracy on the papers** (`scripts/geocode_check.py`, `eval/results/phase2b/geocoder.json`, answers in `geocoder_expected.json`):\n60:  - The draw: 200 distinct (place, state) pairs, by walking the papers in seeded order (seed \"2B geocoder\"). I checked each by hand.\n61:  - Each pair is geocoded twice: by its printed place alone, and with the record check against its paper's record.\n62:  - **Without the record check, {{N_WITHOUT}} are right: {{P_WITHOUT}}.**\n63:    - Ponce, P.R. is right now, because the Puerto Rico dump is an input.\n64:    - Before the dump, 193 were right (96.5%). Before the parser fix the draw prompted, 192 were (96.0%).\n65:  - **With the record check, {{N_WITH}} are right: {{P_WITH}}.** {{WRONG_PINS}}\n66:  - What the records made of the 200: {{RECORD_OUTCOMES}}\n67:  - Some are right under an old name or a merger: Allegheny is Pittsburgh, and Daytona is Daytona Beach. Fort Wrangel, Sumterville, North Yakima and Langston City are old names their towns list.\n68:  - **The failures, by kind:**\n69:\n70:    | Kind | Without the check | With it | Cases |\n71:    |---|---|---|---|\n72:    | merged into a town that doesn't list its name | 3 | {{MERGED}} | East Saginaw into Saginaw; Lardo into McCall; New Oregon into Cresco |\n73:    | renamed, with its old name now another town's | 1 | {{RENAMED}} | Bloomington, Iowa, Muscatine's name until 1849. Without the check, it's a wrong pin, on a living Bloomington elsewhere |\n74:    | two towns named as one place | 1 | {{TWO}} | \"Norfolk and Portsmouth, Va.\" |\n75:    | a ghost town filed outside populated places | 1 | {{GHOST}} | Clifton, S.D., which GeoNames has as \"Clifton (historical)\" in class S |\n76:    | outside GeoNames' US dump | 0 | 0 | Ponce, P.R., right since the Puerto Rico dump became an input |\n77:\n78:  - **These 200 are in-sample for the check.** Its fixes come from their failures, through the EM's review, and so does the Alaska rule. 2D measures any further fix on a fresh draw (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).\n79:- **The 24 judged reports' story places,** as their text gives them: an early look at what the reader will extract.\n80:  - The text gives a town and a state for only 3. The geocoder puts all 3 right (43.8–100%).\n81:  - 9 name a town but no state. Datelines like \"CHICAGO, Jan. 1\" are the usual case.\n82:  - 8 name no town at all, or only a venue such as \"the National Theater\".\n83:  - 4 are abroad: Jersey, Peking, Blickling and France.\n84:  - **For the 9 with a town only, the paper's own state is a poor guess:**\n85:    - right for 3, the local stories at Middlebury, Waterloo and Chittenden\n86:    - a wrong place for 2: Adamsville lands in Pennsylvania, and Easton in Indiana matches West Newton, which lists Easton as an alternate name\n87:    - nothing for 4: Chicago in Connecticut, Kansas and Utah papers, and Bordentown in a Michigan one\n88:  - **So a story place that is only a town resolves from a county or state the text gives, or from its dateline. Otherwise it stays unresolved, and it never falls back on the paper's state** (the EM's review). 2C measures the rule on its gold set, and 2D applies it (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"935c9fa4-5a10-4c59-be6a-381f7b2b94c1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2b-data-layer","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":12,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":4},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6fb61e01-bc18-4f1e-b948-1a92d7f292c7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","id":"a4a88b73-8491-4583-a2e3-622421c4f954","timestamp":"2026-10-30T20:04:50.728Z","version":"2.1.309","querySource":"sdk","shapeHash":"7b35af0d1ba22d1b1b7d4c91038d50b583ea5d544dd1607adac5368903cb8a35","params":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","dangerous-tool-use-2026-09-03","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","thinking-display-updates-2026-08-18","afk-mode-2026-01-31","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07","message-threads-2026-08-12"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"updates"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"max"}},"base":"6e3350fe-7b18-460c-8e2d-293e6f68c8c4","keep":211,"tail":["547ed2aeb0030648a712b52c26194d5bcff0064e4556f13a942a544c639d8f98","939f3858d2a0d2fcb28bd49100df6335a676432b08a48ef7d7077b2e9cead923"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",212,"content",0],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"27465289075ddded5d2c986c9956a0f4f752ed4cc41a5006b46091c9dd8ea63d"}
{"parentUuid":"05008e50-6809-4e5c-9e06-e80c6fcbec41","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZ2DZZkFCNRgb5J2zFke","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SAncZjaP8Fh9VGo8VqbwLX","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && uv run python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"Docs/DECISIONS.md\")\ns = p.read_text()\n\ndef swap(old, new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old) == 1, old[:90]\n    s = s.replace(old, new)\n\nswap(\"which puts {{SHARE_WITH}} of papers' places right.**\", \"which puts 98.0% of papers' places right.**\")\n\nswap(\"\"\"  - Where the paper's title record has been fetched, it also names a town that the record catalogues under another name today: \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\". Two spellings of one town, such as Charlestown and Charles Town, aren't a new name.\n\"\"\", \"\"\"  - Where the record check puts a paper's point at its record's place, the display names the record's town too: \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\". Two spellings of one town, such as Charlestown and Charles Town, aren't a new name.\n  - A paper that moved keeps its printed town: Zajedničar stays \"Allegheny, Pa.\", not \"now Youngstown\". A first build compared the names alone, and 8 of the 14 towns it showed as \"now\" were moves.\n\"\"\")\n\nswap(\"queued after that fetch and never beside it: {{PAIR_RECORD_COUNTS}}\", \"queued after that fetch and never beside it. 23 were cached already, and 177 requests took 20 minutes. One got a 503 that outlasted the retries, so it wasn't cached, and the next run fetched it. All 200 have a record.\")\n\nswap(\"(`catalogued_place`, `catalogued_county` and `catalogued_state`). {{TITLE_LOOKUP_COUNTS}}\", \"\"\"(`catalogued_place`, `catalogued_county` and `catalogued_state`).\n  - Of the 3,336 LCCNs, 2,912 take their place from a title string and 423 from a title record. One has neither: The Western star.\n  - 115 show a state that has changed.\n  - 599 have a record. 15 of those records name a town other than the printed one, because the town was renamed or merged, or the paper moved. Only East Saginaw, whose point the check takes from its record, is shown with the record's town.\"\"\")\n\nswap(\"\"\"  2. It is checked against the record's county: GeoNames' county (admin2) has to be one the record names, in the record's state. A paper that moved has all its counties.\n  3. A place outside the record's county, or no place at all, gives way to the record's place, geocoded in its county. The printed place stays for display: \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\". Merged towns are catalogued under their successors, so this settles them too.\n\"\"\", \"\"\"  2. It is checked against the record's county: GeoNames' county (admin2) has to be one the record names, in one of the record's states. A paper that moved has all its counties and states.\n  3. A place outside the record's county, or no place at all, gives way to the record's place, geocoded in its county. The printed place stays for display, with the record's town: \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\".\n\"\"\")\n\nswap(\"\"\"  - **I made the Alaska rule on these 200.** The first run of the check, with 23 records cached, turned Seward from a right answer into no point.\n\"\"\", \"\"\"  - **I made two of the check's rules on these 200:**\n    - Alaska's rule. The first run, with 23 records cached, turned Seward from a right answer into no point.\n    - Reading the record's `location_state` list, as your review said, instead of the one state in its `location_str`. Zajedničar (Allegheny, Pa.) moved to Youngstown, Ohio, and with Ohio alone, the check replaced a right answer with Youngstown.\n\"\"\")\n\nswap(\"**Without the record check, {{N_WITHOUT}} are right: {{P_WITHOUT}}.**\", \"**Without the record check, 194 are right: 97.0% (93.6–98.6%).** 195 resolve, and 194 of those are right: 99.5% (97.2–99.9%). The one wrong pin is Bloomington.\")\n\nswap(\"**With the record check, {{N_WITH}} are right: {{P_WITH}}.** {{WRONG_PINS}}\", \"\"\"**With the record check, 196 are right: 98.0% (95.0–99.2%).** 198 resolve, and 196 of those are right: 99.0% (96.4–99.7%).\n    - The check settles East Saginaw, under Saginaw, and Clifton, as a ghost town.\n    - **It leaves 2 wrong pins, one more than without it:**\n      - Bloomington still. loc.gov's own record puts Bloomington in Story County: it names Story and Muscatine, so the check agrees with the wrong Bloomington. No record can catch this one.\n      - Norfolk and Portsmouth, which the check adds. The record names no county and gives Portsmouth as its place, so the unchecked rule pins Portsmouth, about 2 km from Norfolk. Its notes say the paper was published in both. I kept my expected answer, Norfolk, from the title.\n    - No printed place that resolved was replaced.\"\"\")\n\nswap(\"  - What the records made of the 200: {{RECORD_OUTCOMES}}\", \"\"\"  - What the records made of the 200:\n\n    | Outcome | Papers |\n    |---|---|\n    | agrees | 177 |\n    | has no county to check: independent cities, D.C., Alaska and Ponce | 14 |\n    | can't be checked: Connecticut's planning regions, and Seward | 4 |\n    | found what the printed place didn't: East Saginaw | 1 |\n    | a ghost town in the record's county: Clifton | 1 |\n    | found what the printed place didn't, unchecked: Norfolk and Portsmouth | 1 |\n    | disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames: Lardo and New Oregon | 2 |\n\n  - Only one of the three merged towns is catalogued under its successor: East Saginaw, under Saginaw.\n    - The Long Valley advocate's record names Lardo and Roseberry, in Idaho County, its county then. GeoNames has Roseberry and McCall in Valley County.\n    - New Oregon's record names New Oregon itself, which GeoNames doesn't have.\n    - Both stay as no point, which is the safe failure.\"\"\")\n\nswap(\"| 3 | {{MERGED}} | East Saginaw into Saginaw; Lardo into McCall; New Oregon into Cresco |\", \"| 3 | 2 | East Saginaw into Saginaw, which the check settles; Lardo into McCall; New Oregon into Cresco |\")\nswap(\"| 1 | {{RENAMED}} | Bloomington, Iowa, Muscatine's name until 1849. Without the check, it's a wrong pin, on a living Bloomington elsewhere |\", \"| 1 | 1 | Bloomington, Iowa, Muscatine's name until 1849: a wrong pin either way, on a living Bloomington in Story County |\")\nswap(\"| 1 | {{TWO}} | \\\"Norfolk and Portsmouth, Va.\\\" |\", \"| 1 | 1 | \\\"Norfolk and Portsmouth, Va.\\\": no point without the check, and a pin on Portsmouth with it |\")\nswap(\"| 1 | {{GHOST}} | Clifton, S.D., which GeoNames has as \\\"Clifton (historical)\\\" in class S |\", \"| 1 | 0 | Clifton, S.D., which GeoNames has as \\\"Clifton (historical)\\\" in class S |\")\n\nswap(\"\"\"  - **These 200 are in-sample for the check.** Its fixes come from their failures, through the EM's review, and so does the Alaska rule. 2D measures any further fix on a fresh draw (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).\"\"\",\n     \"\"\"  - **These 200 are in-sample for the check.** Its fixes come from their failures, through the EM's review, and so do two of its rules. 2D measures it, and any further fix, on a fresh draw (`BUILD-PLAN.md`).\"\"\")\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ngrep -c \"{{\" Docs/DECISIONS.md; grep -n \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\" Docs/*.md","description":"Fill decision 34 with the measured numbers"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2914,"cache_read_input_tokens":294472,"output_tokens":2847,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2914,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2847,"cache_read_