{"parentUuid":"28b253b2-c1d4-4176-b4ad-ce2f542f741f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"eb4299df-98e8-4a21-a6f8-2c287186b7f2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<tools-used-before-summary>Agent, Bash, Edit, Monitor, Read, SendMessage, Skill, TaskStop, ToolSearch, WebSearch, Write, mcp__Claude_Browser__browser_batch, mcp__Claude_Browser__computer, mcp__Claude_Browser__find, mcp__Claude_Browser__get_page_text, mcp__Claude_Browser__javascript_tool, mcp__Claude_Browser__navigate, mcp__ccd_pr__get_status, mcp__ccd_session__mark_chapter, mcp__ccd_turn__update_plan, mcp__ccd_turn__wrap_up</tools-used-before-summary>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n\n   **Setup.** This is a child session of the EM session `local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e`, on the Paper Ghosts project (repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts`, at `/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts`). Hand-over notes go to `/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-X.md`.\n\n   **Standing rules (verbatim where possible):**\n   - \"never print, log or echo secrets from `.env`, and check keys by shape only\"\n   - \"No global installs and no Homebrew\"\n   - \"Stop and ask before anything public (repo visibility, deploys, DNS, posting) or any spending beyond free tiers and existing credits\"\n   - \"Make small commits on phase branches and open pull requests into `main`; I merge them\"\n   - \"Record numbered decisions in `Docs/DECISIONS.md`, and end each milestone with a STATUS block in `Docs/RESUME.md`\"\n   - \"Entire capture is enabled through `.claude/settings.json`; don't touch it\"\n   - \"Don't run brainstorming Q&A with the owner; put questions in your reply to me\"\n   - \"Start every reply to me with `<step>: DONE` or `<step>: BLOCKED — reason`, then 150 words at most\"\n   - Scope pgrep/ps to my uid and never print the hostname. Filter push output with `grep -v -i -F \"$(hostname -s)\"`.\n   - Before every commit, scan the staged diff with `uv run python $S/scan_staged.py .env <diff>` (names only), count machine-name matches, and read the scan output before committing.\n   - loc.gov JSON API: at most one request every 3.5 s in all, never in parallel.\n   - \"Never touch sample_mflix or any other database\"\n   - \"check claims like 'pushed' with `ls-remote` before you report them\"\n   - Commits end with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. PR bodies end with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`.\n   - Scratch directory: `S=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad`\n\n   **Current task:** implement the EM's review of 2B.2 (quoted in section 6), then the gate with the accuracy with and without the record check, and report \"2B.2: DONE\". This is now complete except for the final closing message.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Tooling: uv with Python 3.12, ruff (`make lint`), pytest (`make test`; the `atlas` marker skips without MONGODB_URI; pythonpath includes `scripts`).\n   - Wilson intervals: `paperghosts.stats.wilson`.\n   - loc.gov item JSON fields used:\n     - `location_str` (the main place)\n     - `location_city`, `location_county`, `location_state` (lists, aligned by publication order)\n     - `notes`\n   - GeoNames gazetteer:\n     - inputs: the US, PR and VI dumps\n     - states are keyed lowercase\n     - `county_key`, with COUNTY_AFTER and COUNTY_BEFORE word stripping\n     - ghost towns are \"(historical)\" class S places\n   - The record check outcomes (strings), produced by `paper_place`:\n     - \"none\"\n     - \"has no county to check\"\n     - \"can't be checked against GeoNames' counties\"\n     - \"agrees\"\n     - \"replaced the printed place\"\n     - \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n     - \"found what the printed place didn't, unchecked\"\n     - \"a ghost town in the record's county\"\n     - \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\"\n   - Display rule: the record's town is shown only where the check put the point there (FROM_THE_RECORD).\n   - Waiting on background work: background Bash plus Monitor (max 30 min, re-arm); every turn ends with wrap_up, and each turn end relays a note to the EM.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/papers.py`** (committed in 7ac574b and e24e2e3). This is the record check.\n     ```python\n     def paper_place(gazetteer, title, given=None):\n         printed = printed_place(gazetteer, title)\n         if not given or not given.get(\"place\"):\n             return printed | {\"record\": \"none\"}\n         counties = given.get(\"counties\") or []\n         states = given.get(\"states\") or [given[\"state\"]]\n         agrees = gazetteer.same_county(printed, counties, states)\n         if agrees is None:\n             why = \"can't be checked against GeoNames' counties\" if counties else \"has no county to check\"\n             if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n                 return printed | {\"record\": why}\n             catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"])\n             if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n                 return catalogued | {\"record\": \"found what the printed place didn't, unchecked\"}\n             return printed | {\"record\": why}\n         if agrees:\n             return printed | {\"record\": \"agrees\"}\n         how = \"replaced the printed place\" if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None else \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n         for county in counties:\n             catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], county)\n             if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None and gazetteer.same_county(catalogued, counties, states):\n                 return catalogued | {\"record\": how}\n         ghost = gazetteer.ghost(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], counties)\n         if ghost:\n             return ghost | {\"record\": \"a ghost town in the record's county\"}\n         return gazetteer.base() | {\"reason\": \"outside the record's county, whose own place isn't in GeoNames\", \"record\": \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\"}\n     ```\n     The module docstring describes steps 1–5 and the uncheckable-county case (Alaska, Connecticut).\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/geonames.py`** (committed in 2e2fc32 and e24e2e3).\n     ```python\n     def same_county(self, found, counties, states):\n         codes = {self.states.get(s.lower()) for s in states or []} - {None}\n         wanted = {county_key(c) for c in counties or []}\n         if not any((code, county) in self.counties for code in codes for county in wanted):\n             return None\n         return found.get(\"state\") in codes and county_key(found.get(\"county\") or \"\") in wanted\n     ```\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/loc.py`**: `recorded()` adds `\"states\": states or ([state] if state else [])`, from `location_state`, title-cased. The docstrings were updated: records are asked for only where needed (no-place LCCNs, the accuracy draw, 2D's stored printings), and a moved paper has all its counties and states, as with Zajedničar.\n\n   - **`paperghosts/geo/titles.py`**: `then_and_now(title, modern, catalogued=None)` builds a `now` list, holding catalogued if `flat(name_key(catalogued)) != flat(name_key(place))` and the modern state abbreviation if the state differs. It returns `f\"{place}, {state}\" + (f\" (now {', '.join(now)})\" if now else \"\")`. It imports `flat` and `name_key` from geonames. The docstring example is \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\".\n\n   - **`paperghosts/schemas/__init__.py`**: the Title model gains `catalogued_place`, `catalogued_county` and `catalogued_state` (all `str | None`).\n\n   - **`scripts/titles.py`** (committed in 4e1db3f):\n     - FROM_THE_RECORD constant.\n     - `entry(lccn, papers, states, gazetteer)`: a record is used if the title has no place or the record is cached; `catalogued_*`, `record_url` and `retrieved` come from the record; the no-place fill-in is as before.\n     - Then:\n       ```python\n       found[\"modern_state\"] = modern\n       catalogued = None\n       if given and paper_place(gazetteer, found, given)[\"record\"] in FROM_THE_RECORD:\n           catalogued = given[\"place\"]\n       found[\"then_and_now\"] = then_and_now(found, modern, catalogued)\n       ```\n     - `shown(title, modern=None, catalogued=None)` helper.\n     - `main()` loads `Gazetteer()`.\n     - Summary keys:\n       - printed_state_differs_from_modern\n       - catalogued_under_another_town\n       - shown_with_the_records_town\n       - records_used\n       - loc_gov: requests, cache_hits, errors_not_cached, records_cached, not_found\n\n   - **`scripts/geocode_check.py`** (committed in ae066ed):\n     - `draw()` uses `towns` and `modern_state` and keeps \"title\" in the sample.\n     - `judged(found, want)`: right if `found == want[\"place_id\"] and (found is not None or want[\"failure\"] is None)`; the failure is want's kind or \"not foreseen: to check by hand\".\n     - `summary(results)`: right, resolved, right_when_resolved, wrong_pins, failures.\n     - `main()`: per pair, calls `loc.title_record` and `loc.recorded`, then `printed_place` and `paper_place`. The report has `without_the_record_check` and `with_the_record_check` (plus record outcomes), and title_records counts.\n     - The review mode was fixed.\n\n   - **Tests:**\n     - `tests/test_papers.py` (8 tests, including the moved-across-state-line test and the lookup display test, using the fake gazetteer fixture with Seward and Valley County).\n     - `tests/test_geonames.py` (same_county parametrized with states lists, Seward/AK, Berkeley).\n     - `tests/test_loc_titles.py` (states in `recorded`; the lookup test moved out).\n     - `tests/test_titles.py` (5 then_and_now catalogued cases).\n     - The full suite gives 311 passed.\n\n   - **`eval/results/phase2b/`** (committed): `geocoder.json`, `geocoder_expected.json` (Ponce 4566880; the Norfolk note corrected, answer kept as 4776222) and `titles.json`.\n\n   - **Docs** (committed in 5b606c7):\n     - Decision 34 is fully filled (title \"...checked against each paper's loc.gov title record, which puts 98.0% of papers' places right.\").\n     - BUILD-PLAN: the 2C story-place rule; 2D record fetch for stored printings only; the story rule; measure the check on a fresh draw.\n     - ARCHITECTURE 3.3.\n     - RESUME next step.\n\n   - **Hand-over note:** `step-2B.2.md`, written with the commits table, the gate, the six changes and the \"For you\" items.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **Seward became no point.** loc.gov's Alaskan county names aren't GeoNames counties. Fixed by returning None (\"can't be checked\") when none of the record's counties is known in its states. Disclosed as an in-sample rule.\n   - **Allegheny was replaced with Youngstown, Ohio.** I had read the state from `location_str` instead of `location_state`, which the EM's spec named. Fixed by reading `location_state` as a list. Disclosed as in-sample.\n   - **The display showed \"(now X)\" for papers that moved** (8 of 14, e.g. \"Manchester, Vt. (now Bennington)\"). Fixed by showing the record's town only when the check put the point there.\n   - **A file collision while preparing the split commits.** Two `titles.py` files overwrote each other in `$S/final/`, and the parser text was copied over `scripts/titles.py`. Recovered from `$S/final/titles.py` (parser) and `$S/titles.keep2` plus the re-applied summary edit. Verified identical outputs: eval report, lookup and geocoder report all True.\n   - **Lint formatting** (`test_papers.py`, `test_titles.py`, and a trailing newline in the intermediate test file): fixed with `ruff format`.\n   - **A 503 from loc.gov** during the pairs' fetch: not cached; the next run fetched it.\n   - **Overclaim \"No record can catch this one\"** in decision 34: changed to \"Its record can't catch it.\"\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n\n   Final accuracy on the 200 pairs:\n\n   | | Right | Resolved, and right | Wrong pins |\n   |---|---|---|---|\n   | without the check | 194: 97.0% (93.6–98.6%) | 194 of 195: 99.5% (97.2–99.9%) | 1 (Bloomington) |\n   | with the check | 196: 98.0% (95.0–99.2%) | 196 of 198: 99.0% (96.4–99.7%) | 2 (Bloomington; Norfolk and Portsmouth → Portsmouth) |\n\n   - The check settles East Saginaw (under Saginaw) and Clifton (ghost town 5763634, flagged historical).\n   - It can't catch Bloomington: loc.gov's own record lists Story County.\n   - Only East Saginaw of the three merged towns is catalogued under its successor.\n   - Record outcomes:\n     - agrees: 177\n     - no county: 14\n     - can't be checked: 4\n     - found: 1\n     - ghost: 1\n     - unchecked: 1\n     - disagrees: 2\n     - replaced: 0\n   - Lookup: 3,336 LCCNs (2,912 placed from a title string, 423 from a title record, 1 with neither); 115 states changed; 599 records used; 15 name another town; 1 shown with the record's town.\n   - Story places: the paper's state was right for 3 of the 9 town-only stories, wrong for 2, nothing for 4. The EM said \"1 of 3\"; this was flagged in the note.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - The only messages in this section were automated task and monitor notifications, plus the final summary instruction. No new genuine user or EM messages arrived.\n   - The governing EM message, verbatim from before: \"EM review of 2B.2 so far. The parser fixtures and the 200 geocoded pairs I sampled are right, and so is the third budget scenario. The only wrong answer is the dangerous kind, a pin in the wrong town (Bloomington, Iowa), and the LoC record can catch those. Changes: 1. **Paper places come from the title record first.** loc.gov's item gives `location_city`, `location_county` and `location_state` as catalogued today. Where a record exists: - Geocode its city, county and state. Keep the printed place for display (\"Bloomington, Iowa, now Muscatine\"). - Check that GeoNames' county (admin2) is the record's county. On a mismatch, take the record's place, and count those. - This also settles the merged towns, which are catalogued under their successors: East Saginaw, Lardo, New Oregon. 2. **Measure it now:** fetch the records for the 200 pairs' LCCNs and rerun the accuracy with and without the check. Queue that after the current fetch, never alongside it: one request every 3.5 s in all. 3. **The rest of the LCCNs:** fetch them in 2D, only for those among stored printings (decision 29's pattern), cached. Add that to the plan. 4. **Inputs:** add GeoNames' PR.zip, and any other territory the titles name, to the inputs and the manifest. 5. **Ghost towns:** accept \"(historical)\" class S features only when the record's county matches, flagged as historical. Clifton, S.D. is the case. 6. **For 2C and 2D (plan note):** a story place that is only a town resolves from a county or state the text gives, or from its dateline. Otherwise it stays unresolved. Never fall back on the paper's state, which was right for only 1 of the 3 here. Measure this on the 2C gold set. Then the gate as briefed, with the accuracy with and without the record check.\"\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Only the final closing message to the EM remains. It must start with \"2B.2: DONE\" and stay within 150 words.\n   - Everything else is done:\n     - commits through 5b606c7, pushed and verified with ls-remote\n     - hand-over note written\n     - lint 0, 311 tests pass\n     - `wrap_up` already called\n   - Possible follow-ups, for the EM to decide:\n     - whether to keep the unchecked record-place rule (Norfolk and Portsmouth)\n     - Phase 2B's PR at the end of 2B\n     - step 2B.3 next, after the EM's review\n\n8. Current Work:\n   All 2B.2 work is finished and pushed. `phase-2b-data-layer` is at `5b606c7` on both origin and local, verified with `git ls-remote`.\n\n   Commits in this stretch:\n   - `2e2fc32`: PR and VI dumps, ghost towns, county check\n   - `c8e01f7`: the lookup keeps each paper's catalogued place\n   - `7ac574b`: the record check\n   - `e24e2e3`: the check reads all of a record's states (`location_state`)\n   - `4e1db3f`: the display names the record's town only where the check put the point there\n   - `ae066ed`: the accuracy with and without the check, and the lookup's report\n   - `5b606c7`: decision 34, the plan notes, ARCHITECTURE 3.3, the resume\n\n   The final verification gave make lint 0, 311 tests passed and a clean tree. `wrap_up` was called with the recap: \"2B.2: DONE. Pushed phase-2b-data-layer at 5b606c7 (ls-remote checked). On the 200 pairs: without the check 194 right, 97.0% (93.6–98.6%), 1 wrong pin; with it 196, 98.0% (95.0–99.2%), 2 wrong pins. 311 tests and lint pass.\" The tool result said: \"Your closing message is your answer to the user: write it now, as the last thing you say this turn.\"\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write the closing message to the EM. It must start with \"2B.2: DONE\", stay within 150 words, and use no tools. It should cover:\n   - pushed at `5b606c7`, verified with ls-remote; hand-over in `step-2B.2.md`\n   - the accuracy without the check (194, 97.0%, 93.6–98.6%, 1 wrong pin) and with it (196, 98.0%, 95.0–99.2%, 2 wrong pins)\n   - East Saginaw and Clifton fixed\n   - Bloomington not catchable, because loc.gov's own record lists Story County\n   - Norfolk and Portsmouth → Portsmouth as the EM's call\n   - only East Saginaw of the merged towns catalogued under its successor\n   - two in-sample rules: Alaska, and `location_state`\n   - the display fix\n   - the PR and VI inputs and the plan notes added\n\n   No further work should start without the EM's review.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/hotragn/.claude/projects/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"33d53044-365c-4138-b78c-e6f0d6709f89","timestamp":"2026-10-30T21:05:17.222Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":0,"turnIndex":40},"turnOrigin":"peer","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"33d53044-365c-4138-b78c-e6f0d6709f89","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.2.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.2.md","content":"# Step 2B.2: DONE\n\nOn `phase-2b-data-layer`. I checked origin with `git ls-remote`: it shows `5b606c7`, the same as local.\n\n| Commit | What |\n|---|---|\n| `ff9f93e` | step 0: BUDGET.md's third scenario, stories per printing from the judged reports |\n| `324dd30` | the title parser, tested on 200 titles checked by hand |\n| `3384621` | the GeoNames geocoder over the US dump, with its manifest |\n| `f309dea` | a parser fix the geocoder's draw found |\n| `6c0dbd7` | loc.gov title records, and the title lookup by LCCN |\n| `2e2fc32` | your review: the PR and VI dumps, ghost towns, and the county check |\n| `c8e01f7` | your review: the lookup keeps each paper's place as its record catalogues it |\n| `7ac574b` | your review: the record check |\n| `e24e2e3` | the check reads all of a record's states, from `location_state` |\n| `4e1db3f` | the display names the record's town only where the check put the point there |\n| `ae066ed` | the accuracy with and without the check, and the lookup's report |\n| `5b606c7` | decision 34, the plan's notes for 2C and 2D, ARCHITECTURE 3.3 and the resume |\n\nNo PR yet. Phase 2B's PR comes at the end of 2B, unless you want one now.\n\n## Gate\n\n- **Tests and lint:** `make test` gives 311 passed, the live `atlas` test included, and `make lint` exits 0.\n- **Parser:** the 200 hand-checked titles in `tests/fixtures/titles.json` all pass, as does the 1835 Charlestown test (\"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\").\n- **Title records:** asked for only for the 424 LCCNs whose title strings name no place or state. Requests are at least 3.5 s apart, never in parallel, and each record is cached with its URL, date and status.\n  - 423 were found, and 1 got a 404: sn88084224, The Western star (Texas). None failed.\n  - The final build made 327 requests in 39 minutes. The other 97 were cached already.\n- **The 200 pairs' records:** these ran after that fetch, never beside it. 177 requests took 20 minutes. One got a 503, wasn't cached, and was fetched on the next run. All 200 have a record.\n- **GeoNames:** the US, PR and VI dumps of 2026-10-30. `data/geonames/manifest.json` gives each file's URL, size and sha256, and the CC BY 4.0 licence. No title names any other territory.\n- **Accuracy on the 200 pairs**, answers checked by hand (`geocoder_expected.json`, `geocoder.json`):\n\n  | | Right | Resolved, and right | Wrong pins |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | without the record check | 194: 97.0% (93.6–98.6%) | 194 of 195: 99.5% (97.2–99.9%) | 1: Bloomington |\n  | with it | 196: 98.0% (95.0–99.2%) | 196 of 198: 99.0% (96.4–99.7%) | 2: Bloomington, and Norfolk and Portsmouth |\n\n  - Failure kinds without the check, then with it:\n    - merged: 3, then 2\n    - renamed: 1, then 1\n    - two towns named as one place: 1, then 1\n    - ghost town: 1, then 0\n  - What the records made of the 200:\n    - agrees: 177\n    - no county to check: 14\n    - can't be checked: 4\n    - found what the printed place didn't: 1 (East Saginaw)\n    - ghost town: 1 (Clifton)\n    - unchecked record place: 1 (Norfolk and Portsmouth)\n    - disagrees, with no point: 2 (Lardo and New Oregon)\n    - replaced: 0\n- **The 24 reports' story places:**\n  - 3 give a town and a state, and all 3 are right (43.8–100%).\n  - 9 give a town only, 8 no town, and 4 are abroad.\n\n## Your review's changes\n\n1. **Title record first.** `paper_place` in `paperghosts/geo/papers.py` checks the printed place against the record's county. It fixes East Saginaw and Clifton. It doesn't catch Bloomington: loc.gov's own record lists Story and Muscatine counties, so Bloomington in Story County agrees.\n   - The display keeps the printed place. It adds the record's town only where the check put the point there: \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\". A first version compared names alone, and 8 of its 14 \"now\" towns were papers that moved (\"Manchester, Vt. (now Bennington)\"). The fix is `4e1db3f`.\n   - **Merged towns:** only East Saginaw is catalogued under its successor. Lardo's record names Lardo and Roseberry in Idaho County, its county then, and GeoNames files both in Valley County. New Oregon's record names New Oregon. Both stay as no point.\n2. **Measured now:** see the table above.\n3. **2D's record fetch** is in the plan: records only for the papers among stored printings, cached.\n4. **Inputs:** PR.zip and VI.zip are in the inputs and the manifest.\n5. **Ghost towns:** Clifton, S.D. becomes 5763634, flagged historical, in the record's Sully County.\n6. **The story-place rule** is in the plan for 2C and 2D. My count for the paper's state: right for 3 of the 9 town-only stories, all of them local; wrong for 2; nothing for 4. Your note said 1 of 3. The rule stands either way.\n\n## For you\n\n- **Two rules came from these 200, so they're in-sample**, as decision 34 says:\n  - A county GeoNames doesn't have leaves the place unchecked. Seward, Alaska is the case.\n  - The check reads `location_state`, as you specified. I had taken the state from `location_str`, and Zajedničar, which moved from Pennsylvania to Ohio, broke.\n- **Norfolk and Portsmouth: your call.** The record names no county and gives Portsmouth, so the check pins Portsmouth, about 2 km from my expected Norfolk. Its notes say the paper was published in both.\n  - I'd keep the rule: the pin is loc.gov's catalogued place.\n  - The alternative is no point wherever a record has no county and the printed place doesn't resolve.\n- **The title lookup build now loads the gazetteer**, because the display needs the check. It takes 8 s from the cache.\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}},"displayPath":"../../../../../Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.2.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6ce9325d-c8cf-4332-a5e9-e56433d3f550","timestamp":"2026-10-31T00:10:51.478Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.2.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Step 2B.2: DONE\n2\t\n3\tOn `phase-2b-data-layer`. I checked origin with `git ls-remote`: it shows `5b606c7`, the same as local.\n4\t\n5\t| Commit | What |\n6\t|---|---|\n7\t| `ff9f93e` | step 0: BUDGET.md's third scenario, stories per printing from the judged reports |\n8\t| `324dd30` | the title parser, tested on 200 titles checked by hand |\n9\t| `3384621` | the GeoNames geocoder over the US dump, with its manifest |\n10\t| `f309dea` | a parser fix the geocoder's draw found |\n11\t| `6c0dbd7` | loc.gov title records, and the title lookup by LCCN |\n12\t| `2e2fc32` | your review: the PR and VI dumps, ghost towns, and the county check |\n13\t| `c8e01f7` | your review: the lookup keeps each paper's place as its record catalogues it |\n14\t| `7ac574b` | your review: the record check |\n15\t| `e24e2e3` | the check reads all of a record's states, from `location_state` |\n16\t| `4e1db3f` | the display names the record's town only where the check put the point there |\n17\t| `ae066ed` | the accuracy with and without the check, and the lookup's report |\n18\t| `5b606c7` | decision 34, the plan's notes for 2C and 2D, ARCHITECTURE 3.3 and the resume |\n19\t\n20\tNo PR yet. Phase 2B's PR comes at the end of 2B, unless you want one now.\n21\t\n22\t## Gate\n23\t\n24\t- **Tests and lint:** `make test` gives 311 passed, the live `atlas` test included, and `make lint` exits 0.\n25\t- **Parser:** the 200 hand-checked titles in `tests/fixtures/titles.json` all pass, as does the 1835 Charlestown test (\"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\").\n26\t- **Title records:** asked for only for the 424 LCCNs whose title strings name no place or state. Requests are at least 3.5 s apart, never in parallel, and each record is cached with its URL, date and status.\n27\t  - 423 were found, and 1 got a 404: sn88084224, The Western star (Texas). None failed.\n28\t  - The final build made 327 requests in 39 minutes. The other 97 were cached already.\n29\t- **The 200 pairs' records:** these ran after that fetch, never beside it. 177 requests took 20 minutes. One got a 503, wasn't cached, and was fetched on the next run. All 200 have a record.\n30\t- **GeoNames:** the US, PR and VI dumps of 2026-10-30. `data/geonames/manifest.json` gives each file's URL, size and sha256, and the CC BY 4.0 licence. No title names any other territory.\n31\t- **Accuracy on the 200 pairs**, answers checked by hand (`geocoder_expected.json`, `geocoder.json`):\n32\t\n33\t  | | Right | Resolved, and right | Wrong pins |\n34\t  |---|---|---|---|\n35\t  | without the record check | 194: 97.0% (93.6–98.6%) | 194 of 195: 99.5% (97.2–99.9%) | 1: Bloomington |\n36\t  | with it | 196: 98.0% (95.0–99.2%) | 196 of 198: 99.0% (96.4–99.7%) | 2: Bloomington, and Norfolk and Portsmouth |\n37\t\n38\t  - Failure kinds without the check, then with it:\n39\t    - merged: 3, then 2\n40\t    - renamed: 1, then 1\n41\t    - two towns named as one place: 1, then 1\n42\t    - ghost town: 1, then 0\n43\t  - What the records made of the 200:\n44\t    - agrees: 177\n45\t    - no county to check: 14\n46\t    - can't be checked: 4\n47\t    - found what the printed place didn't: 1 (East Saginaw)\n48\t    - ghost town: 1 (Clifton)\n49\t    - unchecked record place: 1 (Norfolk and Portsmouth)\n50\t    - disagrees, with no point: 2 (Lardo and New Oregon)\n51\t    - replaced: 0\n52\t- **The 24 reports' story places:**\n53\t  - 3 give a town and a state, and all 3 are right (43.8–100%).\n54\t  - 9 give a town only, 8 no town, and 4 are abroad.\n55\t\n56\t## Your review's changes\n57\t\n58\t1. **Title record first.** `paper_place` in `paperghosts/geo/papers.py` checks the printed place against the record's county. It fixes East Saginaw and Clifton. It doesn't catch Bloomington: loc.gov's own record lists Story and Muscatine counties, so Bloomington in Story County agrees.\n59\t   - The display keeps the printed place. It adds the record's town only where the check put the point there: \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\". A first version compared names alone, and 8 of its 14 \"now\" towns were papers that moved (\"Manchester, Vt. (now Bennington)\"). The fix is `4e1db3f`.\n60\t   - **Merged towns:** only East Saginaw is catalogued under its successor. Lardo's record names Lardo and Roseberry in Idaho County, its county then, and GeoNames files both in Valley County. New Oregon's record names New Oregon. Both stay as no point.\n61\t2. **Measured now:** see the table above.\n62\t3. **2D's record fetch** is in the plan: records only for the papers among stored printings, cached.\n63\t4. **Inputs:** PR.zip and VI.zip are in the inputs and the manifest.\n64\t5. **Ghost towns:** Clifton, S.D. becomes 5763634, flagged historical, in the record's Sully County.\n65\t6. **The story-place rule** is in the plan for 2C and 2D. My count for the paper's state: right for 3 of the 9 town-only stories, all of them local; wrong for 2; nothing for 4. Your note said 1 of 3. The rule stands either way.\n66\t\n67\t## For you\n68\t\n69\t- **Two rules came from these 200, so they're in-sample**, as decision 34 says:\n70\t  - A county GeoNames doesn't have leaves the place unchecked. Seward, Alaska is the case.\n71\t  - The check reads `location_state`, as you specified. I had taken the state from `location_str`, and Zajedničar, which moved from Pennsylvania to Ohio, broke.\n72\t- **Norfolk and Portsmouth: your call.** The record names no county and gives Portsmouth, so the check pins Portsmouth, about 2 km from my expected Norfolk. Its notes say the paper was published in both.\n73\t  - I'd keep the rule: the pin is loc.gov's catalogued place.\n74\t  - The alternative is no point wherever a record has no county and the printed place doesn't resolve.\n75\t- **The title lookup build now loads the gazetteer**, because the display needs the check. It takes 8 s from the cache.\n76\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"6ce9325d-c8cf-4332-a5e9-e56433d3f550","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/paperghosts/geo/papers.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/paperghosts/geo/papers.py","content":"\"\"\"Where each paper was printed (decision 34): its printed place geocoded, then checked against\nits loc.gov title record where it has one.\n\n1. The printed place is geocoded in its modern state: the cataloguer's bracketed place first,\n   then the printed one.\n2. With a record that names counties, a result inside one of them, in one of its states,\n   stands.\n3. A result outside them, or no result at all, gives way to the record's place as catalogued\n   today, geocoded in the record's county.\n4. Failing that, a ghost town of the record's name in the record's county, flagged historical.\n5. Failing that, no point: a pin that disagrees with the record is the dangerous kind of answer.\nA record that names no county GeoNames has in its states, as for Alaska's and Connecticut's\nplaces, can't check one: the printed place stands, or where that resolves to nothing, the\nrecord's own place, unchecked. Without a record, the printed place stands unchecked too. That's\nso for most papers until 2D asks for the records of the papers it stores (BUILD-PLAN.md).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import state_name\n\n\ndef towns(title):\n    found = [t for t in (title.get(\"supplied_place\"), title.get(\"place\")) if t]\n    return list(dict.fromkeys(found))\n\n\ndef modern_state(title):\n    return title.get(\"modern_state\") or state_name(\n        title.get(\"supplied_state\") or title.get(\"state\")\n    )\n\n\ndef printed_place(gazetteer, title):\n    state, county = modern_state(title), title.get(\"county\")\n    found = gazetteer.geocode(None, state)\n    for town in towns(title):\n        found = gazetteer.geocode(town, state, county)\n        if found[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n            break\n    return found\n\n\ndef paper_place(gazetteer, title, given=None):\n    \"\"\"The GeoNames place a paper was printed in, and what its record (`loc.recorded`) made of\n    it, under \"record\".\"\"\"\n    printed = printed_place(gazetteer, title)\n    if not given or not given.get(\"place\"):\n        return printed | {\"record\": \"none\"}\n    counties = given.get(\"counties\") or []\n    states = given.get(\"states\") or [given[\"state\"]]\n    agrees = gazetteer.same_county(printed, counties, states)\n    if agrees is None:\n        why = (\n            \"can't be checked against GeoNames' counties\"\n            if counties\n            else \"has no county to check\"\n        )\n        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n            return printed | {\"record\": why}\n        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"])\n        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n            return catalogued | {\n                \"record\": \"found what the printed place didn't, unchecked\"\n            }\n        return printed | {\"record\": why}\n    if agrees:\n        return printed | {\"record\": \"agrees\"}\n    how = (\n        \"replaced the printed place\"\n        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None\n        else \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n    )\n    for county in counties:\n        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], county)\n        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None and gazetteer.same_county(\n            catalogued, counties, states\n        ):\n            return catalogued | {\"record\": how}\n    ghost = gazetteer.ghost(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], counties)\n    if ghost:\n        return ghost | {\"record\": \"a ghost town in the record's county\"}\n    return gazetteer.base() | {\n        \"reason\": \"outside the record's county, whose own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n        \"record\": \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n    }\n","numLines":85,"startLine":1,"totalLines":85}},"displayPath":"paperghosts/geo/papers.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c1ece5d0-35ac-4204-ae62-1aa9f94f6516","timestamp":"2026-10-31T00:10:51.478Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/paperghosts/geo/papers.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"Where each paper was printed (decision 34): its printed place geocoded, then checked against\n2\tits loc.gov title record where it has one.\n3\t\n4\t1. The printed place is geocoded in its modern state: the cataloguer's bracketed place first,\n5\t   then the printed one.\n6\t2. With a record that names counties, a result inside one of them, in one of its states,\n7\t   stands.\n8\t3. A result outside them, or no result at all, gives way to the record's place as catalogued\n9\t   today, geocoded in the record's county.\n10\t4. Failing that, a ghost town of the record's name in the record's county, flagged historical.\n11\t5. Failing that, no point: a pin that disagrees with the record is the dangerous kind of answer.\n12\tA record that names no county GeoNames has in its states, as for Alaska's and Connecticut's\n13\tplaces, can't check one: the printed place stands, or where that resolves to nothing, the\n14\trecord's own place, unchecked. Without a record, the printed place stands unchecked too. That's\n15\tso for most papers until 2D asks for the records of the papers it stores (BUILD-PLAN.md).\n16\t\"\"\"\n17\t\n18\tfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import state_name\n19\t\n20\t\n21\tdef towns(title):\n22\t    found = [t for t in (title.get(\"supplied_place\"), title.get(\"place\")) if t]\n23\t    return list(dict.fromkeys(found))\n24\t\n25\t\n26\tdef modern_state(title):\n27\t    return title.get(\"modern_state\") or state_name(\n28\t        title.get(\"supplied_state\") or title.get(\"state\")\n29\t    )\n30\t\n31\t\n32\tdef printed_place(gazetteer, title):\n33\t    state, county = modern_state(title), title.get(\"county\")\n34\t    found = gazetteer.geocode(None, state)\n35\t    for town in towns(title):\n36\t        found = gazetteer.geocode(town, state, county)\n37\t        if found[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n38\t            break\n39\t    return found\n40\t\n41\t\n42\tdef paper_place(gazetteer, title, given=None):\n43\t    \"\"\"The GeoNames place a paper was printed in, and what its record (`loc.recorded`) made of\n44\t    it, under \"record\".\"\"\"\n45\t    printed = printed_place(gazetteer, title)\n46\t    if not given or not given.get(\"place\"):\n47\t        return printed | {\"record\": \"none\"}\n48\t    counties = given.get(\"counties\") or []\n49\t    states = given.get(\"states\") or [given[\"state\"]]\n50\t    agrees = gazetteer.same_county(printed, counties, states)\n51\t    if agrees is None:\n52\t        why = (\n53\t            \"can't be checked against GeoNames' counties\"\n54\t            if counties\n55\t            else \"has no county to check\"\n56\t        )\n57\t        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n58\t            return printed | {\"record\": why}\n59\t        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"])\n60\t        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None:\n61\t            return catalogued | {\n62\t                \"record\": \"found what the printed place didn't, unchecked\"\n63\t            }\n64\t        return printed | {\"record\": why}\n65\t    if agrees:\n66\t        return printed | {\"record\": \"agrees\"}\n67\t    how = (\n68\t        \"replaced the printed place\"\n69\t        if printed[\"place_id\"] is not None\n70\t        else \"found what the printed place didn't\"\n71\t    )\n72\t    for county in counties:\n73\t        catalogued = gazetteer.geocode(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], county)\n74\t        if catalogued[\"place_id\"] is not None and gazetteer.same_county(\n75\t            catalogued, counties, states\n76\t        ):\n77\t            return catalogued | {\"record\": how}\n78\t    ghost = gazetteer.ghost(given[\"place\"], given[\"state\"], counties)\n79\t    if ghost:\n80\t        return ghost | {\"record\": \"a ghost town in the record's county\"}\n81\t    return gazetteer.base() | {\n82\t        \"reason\": \"outside the record's county, whose own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n83\t        \"record\": \"disagrees, and its own place isn't in GeoNames\",\n84\t    }\n85\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"c1ece5d0-35ac-4204-ae62-1aa9f94f6516","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/numbers.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/numbers.py","content":"\"\"\"The numbers decision 34 and the hand-over need, from the geocoder's and the lookup's results.\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nroot = Path(sys.argv[1])\ngeo = json.loads((root / \"eval/results/phase2b/geocoder.json\").read_text())[\"papers\"]\ntitles = json.loads((root / \"eval/results/phase2b/titles.json\").read_text())\n\n\ndef pct(r):\n    return f\"{r['right']}/{r['n']}, {100 * r['share']:.1f}% ({100 * r['low']:.1f}–{100 * r['high']:.1f}%)\"\n\n\nfor variant in (\"without_the_record_check\", \"with_the_record_check\"):\n    v = geo[variant]\n    print(variant)\n    print(\"  right\", pct(v[\"right\"]))\n    print(\"  resolved\", v[\"resolved\"], \"right when resolved\", pct(v[\"right_when_resolved\"]))\n    print(\"  wrong pins\", v[\"wrong_pins\"])\n    print(\"  failures\", v[\"failures\"])\n    if \"record\" in v:\n        print(\"  record\", v[\"record\"])\nprint(\"title records\", geo[\"title_records\"])\nprint()\nprint(\"changed by the check:\")\nfor p in geo[\"results\"]:\n    a, b = p[\"without_the_record_check\"], p[\"with_the_record_check\"]\n    if a[\"found\"] != b[\"found\"] or a[\"right\"] != b[\"right\"]:\n        print(\n            f\"  {p['town']}, {p['state']} ({p['lccn']}): {a['found']} -> {b['found']}\"\n            f\" [{b['record']}] right {a['right']} -> {b['right']}; expected {p['expected']};\"\n            f\" catalogued {p['catalogued']}\"\n        )\nprint()\nprint(\"wrong with the check:\")\nfor p in geo[\"results\"]:\n    b = p[\"with_the_record_check\"]\n    if not b[\"right\"]:\n        print(f\"  {p['town']}, {p['state']}: {b['found']} [{b['record']}] {b['failure']}; catalogued {p['catalogued']}\")\nprint()\nprint(\"titles\", {k: v for k, v in titles.items() if k != \"generated_at\"})\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}},"displayPath":"../../../../../../../private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/numbers.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2ee625d6-ea0d-4e3a-815f-fd2822bc0070","timestamp":"2026-10-31T00:10:51.478Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/numbers.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"The numbers decision 34 and the hand-over need, from the geocoder's and the lookup's results.\"\"\"\n2\t\n3\timport json\n4\timport sys\n5\tfrom pathlib import Path\n6\t\n7\troot = Path(sys.argv[1])\n8\tgeo = json.loads((root / \"eval/results/phase2b/geocoder.json\").read_text())[\"papers\"]\n9\ttitles = json.loads((root / \"eval/results/phase2b/titles.json\").read_text())\n10\t\n11\t\n12\tdef pct(r):\n13\t    return f\"{r['right']}/{r['n']}, {100 * r['share']:.1f}% ({100 * r['low']:.1f}–{100 * r['high']:.1f}%)\"\n14\t\n15\t\n16\tfor variant in (\"without_the_record_check\", \"with_the_record_check\"):\n17\t    v = geo[variant]\n18\t    print(variant)\n19\t    print(\"  right\", pct(v[\"right\"]))\n20\t    print(\"  resolved\", v[\"resolved\"], \"right when resolved\", pct(v[\"right_when_resolved\"]))\n21\t    print(\"  wrong pins\", v[\"wrong_pins\"])\n22\t    print(\"  failures\", v[\"failures\"])\n23\t    if \"record\" in v:\n24\t        print(\"  record\", v[\"record\"])\n25\tprint(\"title records\", geo[\"title_records\"])\n26\tprint()\n27\tprint(\"changed by the check:\")\n28\tfor p in geo[\"results\"]:\n29\t    a, b = p[\"without_the_record_check\"], p[\"with_the_record_check\"]\n30\t    if a[\"found\"] != b[\"found\"] or a[\"right\"] != b[\"right\"]:\n31\t        print(\n32\t            f\"  {p['town']}, {p['state']} ({p['lccn']}): {a['found']} -> {b['found']}\"\n33\t            f\" [{b['record']}] right {a['right']} -> {b['right']}; expected {p['expected']};\"\n34\t            f\" catalogued {p['catalogued']}\"\n35\t        )\n36\tprint()\n37\tprint(\"wrong with the check:\")\n38\tfor p in geo[\"results\"]:\n39\t    b = p[\"with_the_record_check\"]\n40\t    if not b[\"right\"]:\n41\t        print(f\"  {p['town']}, {p['state']}: {b['found']} [{b['record']}] {b['failure']}; catalogued {p['catalogued']}\")\n42\tprint()\n43\tprint(\"titles\", {k: v for k, v in titles.items() if k != \"generated_at\"})\n44\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"2ee625d6-ea0d-4e3a-815f-fd2822bc0070","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_titles.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_titles.py","content":"\"\"\"The LoC title parser: the title, the place and the state as printed, what the cataloguer\nsupplied in brackets, and the years. Its hard cases are one test each. tests/fixtures/titles.json\nholds 200 titles drawn at random from the candidates' 3,336 LCCNs, each with its expected output\nchecked by hand.\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import parse_title, state_name, then_and_now\n\nFIXTURE = Path(__file__).parent / \"fixtures\" / \"titles.json\"\n\n\ndef parsed(paper, **fields):\n    found = parse_title(paper)\n    return {k: found[k] for k in fields}\n\n\ndef test_a_title_gives_its_place_state_and_years():\n    found = parse_title(\"Eureka daily sentinel. [volume] (Eureka, Nev.) 1871-1887\")\n\n    assert found == {\n        \"title\": \"Eureka daily sentinel.\",\n        \"place\": \"Eureka\",\n        \"county\": None,\n        \"state\": \"Nev.\",\n        \"supplied_place\": None,\n        \"supplied_state\": None,\n        \"years\": \"1871-1887\",\n        \"first_year\": 1871,\n        \"last_year\": 1887,\n    }\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"paper\", \"fields\"),\n    [\n        # a marker after the title, of either kind\n        (\"Daily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975\",\n         {\"title\": \"Daily Kennebec journal.\", \"place\": \"Augusta\", \"state\": \"Me.\"}),\n        # the state only in brackets: supplied by the cataloguer, not printed\n        (\"New Orleans daily crescent. [volume] (New Orleans [La.]) 1851-1866\",\n         {\"place\": \"New Orleans\", \"state\": None, \"supplied_state\": \"La.\"}),\n        (\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\",\n         {\"place\": \"Washington\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n        (\"National Republican. (Washington City (D.C.)) 1872-1888\",\n         {\"place\": \"Washington City\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n        # no place at all: a short form, or a title record's job\n        (\"The New York herald.\",\n         {\"title\": \"The New York herald.\", \"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"years\": None}),\n        # a county between the place and the state, and a territory with its state supplied\n        (\"Devils Lake inter-ocean. (Devils Lake, Ramsey Co., Dakota [N.D.]) 1884-1905\",\n         {\"place\": \"Devils Lake\", \"county\": \"Ramsey Co.\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_state\": \"N.D.\"}),\n        # corrections: printed first, the correction supplied\n        (\"Juliet signal. [volume] (Juliet [i.e. Joliet], Ill.) 1844-1???\",\n         {\"place\": \"Juliet\", \"supplied_place\": \"Joliet\", \"state\": \"Ill.\", \"first_year\": 1844, \"last_year\": None}),\n        (\"Grant County herald. [volume] (Lancaster, W.T. [i.e. Wis.]) 1843-1844\",\n         {\"state\": \"W.T.\", \"supplied_state\": \"Wis.\"}),\n        (\"Marble Hill press. (Marbel [sic] Hill, Mo.) 1881-1923\",\n         {\"place\": \"Marbel Hill\", \"supplied_place\": None}),\n        # the whole place supplied\n        (\"Nashville union. [volume] ([Nashville, Tenn.]) 1851-1853\",\n         {\"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"supplied_place\": \"Nashville\", \"supplied_state\": \"Tenn.\"}),\n        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\",\n         {\"place\": \"Bannock City\", \"state\": \"I.T.\", \"supplied_place\": \"Idaho City\", \"supplied_state\": \"Idaho\"}),\n        # a bracket after the state that starts with a state supplies only a state, with i.e. inside\n        (\"The Hot Springs star. (Hot Spings [Hot Springs], Dakota [Dakota Territory, i.e. S.D.]) 1886-189?\",\n         {\"place\": \"Hot Spings\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_place\": \"Hot Springs\", \"supplied_state\": \"S.D.\"}),\n        # no comma before the state, a semicolon after it, an ampersand between two\n        (\"Cheraw gazette and Pee Dee farmer. [volume] (Cheraw S.C.) 1838-1839\",\n         {\"place\": \"Cheraw\", \"state\": \"S.C.\"}),\n        (\"Backbone. [volume] (St. Paul, Minn. ;) 1897-1906\",\n         {\"place\": \"St. Paul\", \"state\": \"Minn.\"}),\n        (\"Bristol news. [volume] (Bristol, Va. &amp; Tenn.) 1867-189?\",\n         {\"place\": \"Bristol\", \"state\": \"Va. & Tenn.\", \"last_year\": None}),\n        # HTML entities, and UTF-8 that was read as Windows-1252\n        (\"Coeur d&#39;Alene evening press. (Coeur d&#39;Alene, Idaho) 1907-1929\",\n         {\"title\": \"Coeur d'Alene evening press.\", \"place\": \"Coeur d'Alene\"}),\n        (\"Deutscher herold. [volume] (Sioux Falls, SuÌˆd-Dakota) 1907-1918\",\n         {\"state\": \"Süd-Dakota\"}),\n        (\"The Maryland independent. [volume] (Port Tobacco, Md.) 1874-current\",\n         {\"first_year\": 1874, \"last_year\": None, \"years\": \"1874-current\"}),\n    ],\n)  # fmt: skip\ndef test_the_hard_cases(paper, fields):\n    assert parsed(paper, **fields) == fields\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"printed\", \"modern\"),\n    [\n        (\"Va.\", \"Virginia\"),\n        (\"W. Va.\", \"West Virginia\"),\n        (\"Süd-Dakota\", \"South Dakota\"),\n        (\"Nuevo México\", \"New Mexico\"),\n        (\"A.T.\", \"Arizona\"),\n        (\"Minn. Territory\", \"Minnesota\"),\n        (\"Va. & Tenn.\", \"Virginia\"),\n        # a territory's initials can stand for two: the record or the brackets decide\n        (\"W.T.\", None),\n        (\"N.T.\", None),\n        (\"I.T.\", None),\n        (\"Dakota\", None),\n    ],\n)\ndef test_a_printed_state_names_its_modern_state_unless_it_could_be_two(printed, modern):\n    assert state_name(printed) == modern\n\n\ndef test_then_and_now_says_when_a_place_has_changed_state():\n    # 1835: the Virginia free press, of Charlestown, Va., now West Virginia (sn84026784)\n    title = parse_title(\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\")\n\n    assert then_and_now(title, \"West Virginia\") == \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"\n    assert (\n        then_and_now(\n            parse_title(\"Alexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 1834-1974\"),\n            \"Virginia\",\n        )\n        == \"Alexandria, D.C. (now Va.)\"\n    )\n    assert (\n        then_and_now(\n            parse_title(\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\"),\n            \"District of Columbia\",\n        )\n        == \"Washington, D.C.\"\n    )\n    assert (\n        then_and_now(\n            parse_title(\n                \"Aberdeen herald. [volume] (Aberdeen, Chehalis County, W.T.) 1886-1917\"\n            ),\n            \"Washington\",\n        )\n        == \"Aberdeen, W.T. (now Wash.)\"\n    )\n    assert then_and_now(parse_title(\"The New York herald.\"), \"New York\") is None\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n    (\"paper\", \"modern\", \"catalogued\", \"shown\"),\n    [\n        # the record's city, as catalogued today, where the town has a new name\n        (\"Muscatine journal. [volume] (Bloomington, Iowa) 1849-185?\", \"Iowa\", \"Muscatine\",\n         \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\"),\n        (\"East Saginaw courier. (East Saginaw, Mich.) 1859-18??\", \"Michigan\", \"Saginaw\",\n         \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\"),\n        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\", \"Idaho\", \"Idaho City\",\n         \"Bannock City, I.T. (now Idaho City, Idaho)\"),\n        # the same town, spelled apart, isn't a new name\n        (\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\", \"West Virginia\", \"Charles Town\",\n         \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"),\n        (\"Backbone. [volume] (St. Paul, Minn. ;) 1897-1906\", \"Minnesota\", \"Saint Paul\", \"St. Paul, Minn.\"),\n    ],\n)  # fmt: skip\ndef test_then_and_now_gives_the_records_name_for_a_town_renamed(\n    paper, modern, catalogued, shown\n):\n    assert then_and_now(parse_title(paper), modern, catalogued) == shown\n\n\ndef test_two_hundred_titles_checked_by_hand():\n    cases = json.loads(FIXTURE.read_text())\n\n    assert len({case[\"paper\"] for case in cases}) >= 200\n    for case in cases:\n        assert parse_title(case[\"paper\"]) == case[\"expected\"], case[\"paper\"]\n","numLines":171,"startLine":1,"totalLines":171}},"displayPath":"tests/test_titles.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2fc421c5-1743-4ce2-b1b2-739ee40e44bd","timestamp":"2026-10-31T00:10:51.478Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_titles.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"The LoC title parser: the title, the place and the state as printed, what the cataloguer\n2\tsupplied in brackets, and the years. Its hard cases are one test each. tests/fixtures/titles.json\n3\tholds 200 titles drawn at random from the candidates' 3,336 LCCNs, each with its expected output\n4\tchecked by hand.\"\"\"\n5\t\n6\timport json\n7\tfrom pathlib import Path\n8\t\n9\timport pytest\n10\t\n11\tfrom paperghosts.geo.titles import parse_title, state_name, then_and_now\n12\t\n13\tFIXTURE = Path(__file__).parent / \"fixtures\" / \"titles.json\"\n14\t\n15\t\n16\tdef parsed(paper, **fields):\n17\t    found = parse_title(paper)\n18\t    return {k: found[k] for k in fields}\n19\t\n20\t\n21\tdef test_a_title_gives_its_place_state_and_years():\n22\t    found = parse_title(\"Eureka daily sentinel. [volume] (Eureka, Nev.) 1871-1887\")\n23\t\n24\t    assert found == {\n25\t        \"title\": \"Eureka daily sentinel.\",\n26\t        \"place\": \"Eureka\",\n27\t        \"county\": None,\n28\t        \"state\": \"Nev.\",\n29\t        \"supplied_place\": None,\n30\t        \"supplied_state\": None,\n31\t        \"years\": \"1871-1887\",\n32\t        \"first_year\": 1871,\n33\t        \"last_year\": 1887,\n34\t    }\n35\t\n36\t\n37\t@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n38\t    (\"paper\", \"fields\"),\n39\t    [\n40\t        # a marker after the title, of either kind\n41\t        (\"Daily Kennebec journal. [microfilm reel] (Augusta, Me.) 1870-1975\",\n42\t         {\"title\": \"Daily Kennebec journal.\", \"place\": \"Augusta\", \"state\": \"Me.\"}),\n43\t        # the state only in brackets: supplied by the cataloguer, not printed\n44\t        (\"New Orleans daily crescent. [volume] (New Orleans [La.]) 1851-1866\",\n45\t         {\"place\": \"New Orleans\", \"state\": None, \"supplied_state\": \"La.\"}),\n46\t        (\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\",\n47\t         {\"place\": \"Washington\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n48\t        (\"National Republican. (Washington City (D.C.)) 1872-1888\",\n49\t         {\"place\": \"Washington City\", \"state\": \"D.C.\"}),\n50\t        # no place at all: a short form, or a title record's job\n51\t        (\"The New York herald.\",\n52\t         {\"title\": \"The New York herald.\", \"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"years\": None}),\n53\t        # a county between the place and the state, and a territory with its state supplied\n54\t        (\"Devils Lake inter-ocean. (Devils Lake, Ramsey Co., Dakota [N.D.]) 1884-1905\",\n55\t         {\"place\": \"Devils Lake\", \"county\": \"Ramsey Co.\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_state\": \"N.D.\"}),\n56\t        # corrections: printed first, the correction supplied\n57\t        (\"Juliet signal. [volume] (Juliet [i.e. Joliet], Ill.) 1844-1???\",\n58\t         {\"place\": \"Juliet\", \"supplied_place\": \"Joliet\", \"state\": \"Ill.\", \"first_year\": 1844, \"last_year\": None}),\n59\t        (\"Grant County herald. [volume] (Lancaster, W.T. [i.e. Wis.]) 1843-1844\",\n60\t         {\"state\": \"W.T.\", \"supplied_state\": \"Wis.\"}),\n61\t        (\"Marble Hill press. (Marbel [sic] Hill, Mo.) 1881-1923\",\n62\t         {\"place\": \"Marbel Hill\", \"supplied_place\": None}),\n63\t        # the whole place supplied\n64\t        (\"Nashville union. [volume] ([Nashville, Tenn.]) 1851-1853\",\n65\t         {\"place\": None, \"state\": None, \"supplied_place\": \"Nashville\", \"supplied_state\": \"Tenn.\"}),\n66\t        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\",\n67\t         {\"place\": \"Bannock City\", \"state\": \"I.T.\", \"supplied_place\": \"Idaho City\", \"supplied_state\": \"Idaho\"}),\n68\t        # a bracket after the state that starts with a state supplies only a state, with i.e. inside\n69\t        (\"The Hot Springs star. (Hot Spings [Hot Springs], Dakota [Dakota Territory, i.e. S.D.]) 1886-189?\",\n70\t         {\"place\": \"Hot Spings\", \"state\": \"Dakota\", \"supplied_place\": \"Hot Springs\", \"supplied_state\": \"S.D.\"}),\n71\t        # no comma before the state, a semicolon after it, an ampersand between two\n72\t        (\"Cheraw gazette and Pee Dee farmer. [volume] (Cheraw S.C.) 1838-1839\",\n73\t         {\"place\": \"Cheraw\", \"state\": \"S.C.\"}),\n74\t        (\"Backbone. [volume] (St. Paul, Minn. ;) 1897-1906\",\n75\t         {\"place\": \"St. Paul\", \"state\": \"Minn.\"}),\n76\t        (\"Bristol news. [volume] (Bristol, Va. &amp; Tenn.) 1867-189?\",\n77\t         {\"place\": \"Bristol\", \"state\": \"Va. & Tenn.\", \"last_year\": None}),\n78\t        # HTML entities, and UTF-8 that was read as Windows-1252\n79\t        (\"Coeur d&#39;Alene evening press. (Coeur d&#39;Alene, Idaho) 1907-1929\",\n80\t         {\"title\": \"Coeur d'Alene evening press.\", \"place\": \"Coeur d'Alene\"}),\n81\t        (\"Deutscher herold. [volume] (Sioux Falls, SuÌˆd-Dakota) 1907-1918\",\n82\t         {\"state\": \"Süd-Dakota\"}),\n83\t        (\"The Maryland independent. [volume] (Port Tobacco, Md.) 1874-current\",\n84\t         {\"first_year\": 1874, \"last_year\": None, \"years\": \"1874-current\"}),\n85\t    ],\n86\t)  # fmt: skip\n87\tdef test_the_hard_cases(paper, fields):\n88\t    assert parsed(paper, **fields) == fields\n89\t\n90\t\n91\t@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n92\t    (\"printed\", \"modern\"),\n93\t    [\n94\t        (\"Va.\", \"Virginia\"),\n95\t        (\"W. Va.\", \"West Virginia\"),\n96\t        (\"Süd-Dakota\", \"South Dakota\"),\n97\t        (\"Nuevo México\", \"New Mexico\"),\n98\t        (\"A.T.\", \"Arizona\"),\n99\t        (\"Minn. Territory\", \"Minnesota\"),\n100\t        (\"Va. & Tenn.\", \"Virginia\"),\n101\t        # a territory's initials can stand for two: the record or the brackets decide\n102\t        (\"W.T.\", None),\n103\t        (\"N.T.\", None),\n104\t        (\"I.T.\", None),\n105\t        (\"Dakota\", None),\n106\t    ],\n107\t)\n108\tdef test_a_printed_state_names_its_modern_state_unless_it_could_be_two(printed, modern):\n109\t    assert state_name(printed) == modern\n110\t\n111\t\n112\tdef test_then_and_now_says_when_a_place_has_changed_state():\n113\t    # 1835: the Virginia free press, of Charlestown, Va., now West Virginia (sn84026784)\n114\t    title = parse_title(\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\")\n115\t\n116\t    assert then_and_now(title, \"West Virginia\") == \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"\n117\t    assert (\n118\t        then_and_now(\n119\t            parse_title(\"Alexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 1834-1974\"),\n120\t            \"Virginia\",\n121\t        )\n122\t        == \"Alexandria, D.C. (now Va.)\"\n123\t    )\n124\t    assert (\n125\t        then_and_now(\n126\t            parse_title(\"Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-1972\"),\n127\t            \"District of Columbia\",\n128\t        )\n129\t        == \"Washington, D.C.\"\n130\t    )\n131\t    assert (\n132\t        then_and_now(\n133\t            parse_title(\n134\t                \"Aberdeen herald. [volume] (Aberdeen, Chehalis County, W.T.) 1886-1917\"\n135\t            ),\n136\t            \"Washington\",\n137\t        )\n138\t        == \"Aberdeen, W.T. (now Wash.)\"\n139\t    )\n140\t    assert then_and_now(parse_title(\"The New York herald.\"), \"New York\") is None\n141\t\n142\t\n143\t@pytest.mark.parametrize(\n144\t    (\"paper\", \"modern\", \"catalogued\", \"shown\"),\n145\t    [\n146\t        # the record's city, as catalogued today, where the town has a new name\n147\t        (\"Muscatine journal. [volume] (Bloomington, Iowa) 1849-185?\", \"Iowa\", \"Muscatine\",\n148\t         \"Bloomington, Iowa (now Muscatine)\"),\n149\t        (\"East Saginaw courier. (East Saginaw, Mich.) 1859-18??\", \"Michigan\", \"Saginaw\",\n150\t         \"East Saginaw, Mich. (now Saginaw)\"),\n151\t        (\"Boise news. (Bannock City, I.T. [Idaho City, Idaho]) 1863-1864\", \"Idaho\", \"Idaho City\",\n152\t         \"Bannock City, I.T. (now Idaho City, Idaho)\"),\n153\t        # the same town, spelled apart, isn't a new name\n154\t        (\"Virginia free press. (Charlestown, Va. [W. Va.]) 1832-1916\", \"West Virginia\", \"Charles Town\",\n155\t         \"Charlestown, Va. (now W.Va.)\"),\n156\t        (\"Backbone. [volume] (St. Paul, Minn. ;) 1897-1906\", \"Minnesota\", \"Saint Paul\", \"St. Paul, Minn.\"),\n157\t    ],\n158\t)  # fmt: skip\n159\tdef test_then_and_now_gives_the_records_name_for_a_town_renamed(\n160\t    paper, modern, catalogued, shown\n161\t):\n162\t    assert then_and_now(parse_title(paper), modern, catalogued) == shown\n163\t\n164\t\n165\tdef test_two_hundred_titles_checked_by_hand():\n166\t    cases = json.loads(FIXTURE.read_text())\n167\t\n168\t    assert len({case[\"paper\"] for case in cases}) >= 200\n169\t    for case in cases:\n170\t        assert parse_title(case[\"paper\"]) == case[\"expected\"], case[\"paper\"]\n171\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"2fc421c5-1743-4ce2-b1b2-739ee40e44bd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_loc_titles.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_loc_titles.py","content":"\"\"\"loc.gov title records: one request each, cached with its URL and retrieval date, and a 404\ncached too; and the place, state and title a record gives.\"\"\"\n\nimport httpx\n\nfrom paperghosts.geo import loc\n\n\nclass Answer:\n    def __init__(self, status, body=None):\n        self.status_code, self.body = status, body\n\n    def json(self):\n        return self.body\n\n    def raise_for_status(self):\n        if self.status_code >= 400:\n            request = httpx.Request(\"GET\", \"https://www.loc.gov/\")\n            raise httpx.HTTPStatusError(\n                \"\", request=request, response=httpx.Response(self.status_code)\n            )\n\n\ndef test_a_record_is_asked_for_once_and_cached_with_its_url_and_date(\n    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n):\n    asked = []\n\n    def get(url, **params):\n        asked.append(url)\n        answer = Answer(200, {\"item\": {\"location_str\": \"Phoenix, Arizona\"}})\n        answer.raise_for_status()\n        return answer\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n\n    first = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n    again = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n\n    assert asked == [\"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/\"]\n    assert (\n        first == again\n        and first[\"url\"] == \"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/?fo=json\"\n    )\n    assert first[\"status\"] == 200 and first[\"retrieved\"].startswith(\"20\")\n    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_a_missing_record_is_cached_as_a_404(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    def get(url, **params):\n        Answer(404).raise_for_status()\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n\n    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"status\"] == 404\n    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"body\"] is None\n    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_a_server_error_is_counted_and_not_cached_so_a_later_run_asks_again(\n    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n):\n    def get(url, **params):\n        Answer(520).raise_for_status()\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n\n    found = loc.title_record(\"sn83030273\", tmp_path)\n\n    assert found[\"status\"] == 520 and found[\"body\"] is None\n    assert not list(tmp_path.iterdir())\n    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"errors\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_a_record_gives_its_place_state_county_title_and_years():\n    record = {\n        \"status\": 200,\n        \"body\": {\n            \"item\": {\n                \"location_str\": \"Tucson, Arizona\",\n                \"location_county\": [\"santa cruz\", \"pima\"],\n                \"location_state\": [\"arizona\"],\n                \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n                \"dates_of_publication\": \"1859-1871\",\n            }\n        },\n    }\n\n    # a paper that moved has both its counties, to check either printed place against\n    assert loc.recorded(record) == {\n        \"place\": \"Tucson\",\n        \"state\": \"Arizona\",\n        \"county\": \"Santa Cruz\",\n        \"counties\": [\"Santa Cruz\", \"Pima\"],\n        \"states\": [\"Arizona\"],\n        \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n        \"years\": \"1859-1871\",\n    }\n    assert loc.recorded({\"status\": 404, \"body\": None}) is None\n","numLines":103,"startLine":1,"totalLines":103}},"displayPath":"tests/test_loc_titles.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"50afe369-3410-4fff-86b3-8fbe42a9818c","timestamp":"2026-10-31T00:10:51.478Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_loc_titles.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"loc.gov title records: one request each, cached with its URL and retrieval date, and a 404\n2\tcached too; and the place, state and title a record gives.\"\"\"\n3\t\n4\timport httpx\n5\t\n6\tfrom paperghosts.geo import loc\n7\t\n8\t\n9\tclass Answer:\n10\t    def __init__(self, status, body=None):\n11\t        self.status_code, self.body = status, body\n12\t\n13\t    def json(self):\n14\t        return self.body\n15\t\n16\t    def raise_for_status(self):\n17\t        if self.status_code >= 400:\n18\t            request = httpx.Request(\"GET\", \"https://www.loc.gov/\")\n19\t            raise httpx.HTTPStatusError(\n20\t                \"\", request=request, response=httpx.Response(self.status_code)\n21\t            )\n22\t\n23\t\n24\tdef test_a_record_is_asked_for_once_and_cached_with_its_url_and_date(\n25\t    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n26\t):\n27\t    asked = []\n28\t\n29\t    def get(url, **params):\n30\t        asked.append(url)\n31\t        answer = Answer(200, {\"item\": {\"location_str\": \"Phoenix, Arizona\"}})\n32\t        answer.raise_for_status()\n33\t        return answer\n34\t\n35\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n36\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n37\t\n38\t    first = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n39\t    again = loc.title_record(\"sn78001213\", tmp_path)\n40\t\n41\t    assert asked == [\"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/\"]\n42\t    assert (\n43\t        first == again\n44\t        and first[\"url\"] == \"https://www.loc.gov/item/sn78001213/?fo=json\"\n45\t    )\n46\t    assert first[\"status\"] == 200 and first[\"retrieved\"].startswith(\"20\")\n47\t    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n48\t\n49\t\n50\tdef test_a_missing_record_is_cached_as_a_404(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n51\t    def get(url, **params):\n52\t        Answer(404).raise_for_status()\n53\t\n54\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n55\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n56\t\n57\t    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"status\"] == 404\n58\t    assert loc.title_record(\"sn00000000\", tmp_path)[\"body\"] is None\n59\t    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"cache_hits\": 1}\n60\t\n61\t\n62\tdef test_a_server_error_is_counted_and_not_cached_so_a_later_run_asks_again(\n63\t    tmp_path, monkeypatch\n64\t):\n65\t    def get(url, **params):\n66\t        Answer(520).raise_for_status()\n67\t\n68\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"get\", get)\n69\t    monkeypatch.setattr(loc, \"COUNTS\", loc.Counter())\n70\t\n71\t    found = loc.title_record(\"sn83030273\", tmp_path)\n72\t\n73\t    assert found[\"status\"] == 520 and found[\"body\"] is None\n74\t    assert not list(tmp_path.iterdir())\n75\t    assert loc.COUNTS == {\"requests\": 1, \"errors\": 1}\n76\t\n77\t\n78\tdef test_a_record_gives_its_place_state_county_title_and_years():\n79\t    record = {\n80\t        \"status\": 200,\n81\t        \"body\": {\n82\t            \"item\": {\n83\t                \"location_str\": \"Tucson, Arizona\",\n84\t                \"location_county\": [\"santa cruz\", \"pima\"],\n85\t                \"location_state\": [\"arizona\"],\n86\t                \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n87\t                \"dates_of_publication\": \"1859-1871\",\n88\t            }\n89\t        },\n90\t    }\n91\t\n92\t    # a paper that moved has both its counties, to check either printed place against\n93\t    assert loc.recorded(record) == {\n94\t        \"place\": \"Tucson\",\n95\t        \"state\": \"Arizona\",\n96\t        \"county\": \"Santa Cruz\",\n97\t        \"counties\": [\"Santa Cruz\", \"Pima\"],\n98\t        \"states\": [\"Arizona\"],\n99\t        \"title\": \"Weekly Arizonian (Tubac, Ariz.) 1859-1871\",\n100\t        \"years\": \"1859-1871\",\n101\t    }\n102\t    assert loc.recorded({\"status\": 404, \"body\": None}) is None\n103\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"50afe369-3410-4fff-86b3-8fbe42a9818c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","path":"plugin:superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents\n\n# Dispatching Parallel Agents\n\n## Overview\n\nYou delegate tasks to specialized agents with isolated context. By precisely crafting their instructions and context, you ensure they stay focused and succeed at their task. They should never inherit your session's context or history — you construct exactly what they need. This also preserves your own context for coordination work.\n\nWhen you have multiple unrelated failures (different test files, different subsystems, different bugs), investigating them sequentially wastes time. Each investigation is independent and can happen in parallel.\n\n**Core principle:** Dispatch one agent per independent problem domain. Let them work concurrently.\n\n## When to Use\n\n```dot\ndigraph when_to_use {\n    \"Multiple failures?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Are they independent?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Single agent investigates all\" [shape=box];\n    \"One agent per problem domain\" [shape=box];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Sequential agents\" [shape=box];\n    \"Parallel dispatch\" [shape=box];\n\n    \"Multiple failures?\" -> \"Are they independent?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Single agent investigates all\" [label=\"no - related\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Can they work in parallel?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Parallel dispatch\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Sequential agents\" [label=\"no - shared state\"];\n}\n```\n\n**Use when:**\n- 3+ test files failing with different root causes\n- Multiple subsystems broken independently\n- Each problem can be understood without context from others\n- No shared state between investigations\n\n**Don't use when:**\n- Failures are related (fix one might fix others)\n- Need to understand full system state\n- Agents would interfere with each other\n\n## The Pattern\n\n### 1. Identify Independent Domains\n\nGroup failures by what's broken:\n- File A tests: Tool approval flow\n- File B tests: Batch completion behavior\n- File C tests: Abort functionality\n\nEach domain is independent - fixing tool approval doesn't affect abort tests.\n\n### 2. Create Focused Agent Tasks\n\nEach agent gets:\n- **Specific scope:** One test file or subsystem\n- **Clear goal:** Make these tests pass\n- **Constraints:** Don't change other code\n- **Expected output:** Summary of what you found and fixed\n\n### 3. Dispatch in Parallel\n\nIssue all three subagent dispatches in the same response — they run in parallel:\n\n```text\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts failures\"\n# All three run concurrently.\n```\n\nMultiple dispatch calls in one response = parallel execution. One per response = sequential.\n\n### 4. Review and Integrate\n\nWhen agents return:\n- Read each summary\n- Verify fixes don't conflict\n- Run full test suite\n- Integrate all changes\n\n## Agent Prompt Structure\n\nGood agent prompts are:\n1. **Focused** - One clear problem domain\n2. **Self-contained** - All context needed to understand the problem\n3. **Specific about output** - What should the agent return?\n\n```markdown\nFix the 3 failing tests in src/agents/agent-tool-abort.test.ts:\n\n1. \"should abort tool with partial output capture\" - expects 'interrupted at' in message\n2. \"should handle mixed completed and aborted tools\" - fast tool aborted instead of completed\n3. \"should properly track pendingToolCount\" - expects 3 results but gets 0\n\nThese are timing/race condition issues. Your task:\n\n1. Read the test file and understand what each test verifies\n2. Identify root cause - timing issues or actual bugs?\n3. Fix by:\n   - Replacing arbitrary timeouts with event-based waiting\n   - Fixing bugs in abort implementation if found\n   - Adjusting test expectations if testing changed behavior\n\nDo NOT just increase timeouts - find the real issue.\n\nReturn: Summary of what you found and what you fixed.\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n**❌ Too broad:** \"Fix all the tests\" - agent gets lost\n**✅ Specific:** \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\" - focused scope\n\n**❌ No context:** \"Fix the race condition\" - agent doesn't know where\n**✅ Context:** Paste the error messages and test names\n\n**❌ No constraints:** Agent might refactor everything\n**✅ Constraints:** \"Do NOT change production code\" or \"Fix tests only\"\n\n**❌ Vague output:** \"Fix it\" - you don't know what changed\n**✅ Specific:** \"Return summary of root cause and changes\"\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n**Related failures:** Fixing one might fix others - investigate together first\n**Need full context:** Understanding requires seeing entire system\n**Exploratory debugging:** You don't know what's broken yet\n**Shared state:** Agents would interfere (editing same files, using same resources)\n\n## Real Example from Session\n\n**Scenario:** 6 test failures across 3 files after major refactoring\n\n**Failures:**\n- agent-tool-abort.test.ts: 3 failures (timing issues)\n- batch-completion-behavior.test.ts: 2 failures (tools not executing)\n- tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts: 1 failure (execution count = 0)\n\n**Decision:** Independent domains - abort logic separate from batch completion separate from race conditions\n\n**Dispatch:**\n```\nAgent 1 → Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\nAgent 2 → Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts\nAgent 3 → Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts\n```\n\n**Results:**\n- Agent 1: Replaced timeouts with event-based waiting\n- Agent 2: Fixed event structure bug (threadId in wrong place)\n- Agent 3: Added wait for async tool execution to complete\n\n**Integration:** All fixes independent, no conflicts, full suite green\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter agents return:\n1. **Review each summary** - Understand what changed\n2. **Check for conflicts** - Did agents edit same code?\n3. **Run full suite** - Verify all fixes work together\n4. **Spot check** - Agents can make systematic errors\n"},{"name":"dataviz","path":"bundled:dataviz","content":"Base directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-502/bundled-skills/2.1.309/b686a53081079aed22896e5adfa24953/dataviz\n\n# Data Visualization\n\nA chart is **read by people and executed by you**. This skill turns \"make it look\ngood\" into a procedure with checks, so the result is right by construction rather\nthan by taste.\n\n**The method here is design-system-agnostic.** Nothing in the procedure, the form\nheuristic, the six checks, or the mark specs is specific to one product. A design\nsystem supplies a small set of *parameters* (its ramps, a categorical order, a\ndiverging pair, a status palette, a texture, its surfaces, its filter components);\nthe method consumes them unchanged. A **validated default palette** is the\nreference instance, fully specified in `references/palette.md`. To target your\nbrand, read that file's structure and substitute its values - touch nothing else.\n\n> The single most important habit: **the color part is computable, so compute it.**\n> Never eyeball whether a palette is colorblind-safe - run `scripts/validate_palette.js`.\n\n## The procedure - do these in order\n\nColor comes LAST. Most bad charts pick colors first.\n\n1. **Pick the form.** What is the data's job - magnitude, identity, polarity, a\n   single headline, change-over-time? The job picks the chart type, and sometimes\n   the answer is *not a chart* (a stat tile or hero number). -> `references/choosing-a-form.md`\n2. **Assign color by the job it does.** Categorical (identity), sequential\n   (magnitude), diverging (polarity), or status (state) - each has one rule.\n   Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled. -> `references/color-formula.md`\n3. **VALIDATE the palette - run the script, don't reason about Delta E.**\n   `node scripts/validate_palette.js \"<hex,hex,...>\" --mode light` (relative to\n   this skill's base directory - or load it as `<script type=\"module\">` in the\n   chart's own page, where it reads\n   `data-palette` off `<body>` and logs a `console.table` report). It returns\n   pass/fail on the lightness band, chroma floor, adjacent-pair CVD separation,\n   the normal-vision floor, and contrast. Fix anything that FAILs before continuing. Re-run for\n   `--mode dark` with that mode's surface.\n4. **Apply mark specs & spacers.** Thin marks, 4px rounded data-ends anchored to\n   the baseline, 2px lines, >=8px markers, a 2px surface gap between fills (stacked\n   segments and adjacent bars alike) and a 2px surface ring on overlapping marks,\n   selective direct labels. -> `references/marks-and-anatomy.md`\n5. **Add the hover layer - by default.** An HTML/SVG chart *is* interactive; ship\n   a crosshair+tooltip on line/area and a per-mark hover tooltip on bar/dot/cell.\n   The only form that skips it is a bare stat tile with no plot. Hit targets bigger\n   than the mark; filters in one row above the charts. -> `references/interaction.md`\n6. **Final accessibility pass.** For >= 2 series a legend is always present and <= 4\n   are also direct-labeled (a single series needs no legend box - the title names\n   it), so identity is never color-alone; a table view exists; dark mode is **selected** - its own\n   steps from the same ramps, validated against the dark surface, not an automatic\n   flip; texture is available for the CVD/print/forced-colors case.\n7. **Render it and look at it.** The validator checks color, not layout - open or\n   screenshot the output and eyeball it for label collisions, geometry, and overflow\n   before calling it done.\n\nThen check the result against **`references/anti-patterns.md`** - it is the catalog\nof what goes wrong. If your chart matches an entry, it's wrong.\n\n## Non-negotiables (true in every design system)\n\n- **Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled.** A 9th series is never a\n  generated hue - it folds into \"Other,\" small multiples, or composite encoding.\n- **One axis.** Never a dual-axis chart (two y-scales). Two measures of different\n  scale -> two charts, small multiples, or indexed to a common base. *(This is the\n  #1 chart mistake - see anti-patterns.)*\n- **Color follows the entity, never its rank.** A filter that changes the series\n  count must not repaint the survivors.\n- **Sequential = one hue, light->dark. Diverging = two hues + a neutral gray\n  midpoint.** Never a rainbow; never a hue at the diverging midpoint.\n- **Run the validator before shipping any categorical palette.** CVD Delta E >= 8 is the\n  target (OKLab ×100); 6-8 is a floor that is legal ONLY with secondary encoding. A\n  normal-vision floor below 15 is a hard FAIL - full-color readers can't tell the\n  pair apart; re-step it on the adjacent pairlist (secondary encoding does not excuse\n  this one); under `--pairs all` cut series or facet instead - see check 4. A contrast WARN\n  obligates visible labels or a table view - it is not dismissable.\n- **Thin marks; a legend always present for >= 2 series (none for one), with\n  selective direct labels (never a number on every point); recessive grid/axes.**\n- **Text wears text tokens, never the series color** - values, labels, and legends\n  stay in primary/secondary/muted ink; a colored mark beside them carries identity.\n- **Status colors are reserved** (good/warning/serious/critical) and never reused\n  for \"series 4\"; they ship with an icon + label, never color alone.\n\n## Plugging in a design system\n\nThe method is invariant; only these parameters change per system. The reference\ninstance - every value filled in - is `references/palette.md`.\n\n| Parameter | What the system provides |\n|---|---|\n| **Ramps** | the hue scales (named steps) the palette draws from |\n| **Categorical theme** | the fixed hue order (a named theme); default + alternates |\n| **Sequential hue** | the default single hue for magnitude |\n| **Diverging pair** | two warm/cool poles + a neutral midpoint |\n| **Status palette** | good / warning / serious / critical - steps distinct from categorical |\n| **Texture fill** | one directional hand-drawn fill, used at 45° / 135° |\n| **Surfaces** | light & dark chart-surface colors (the validator needs these) |\n| **Filter controls** | date-range & dimension controls (behavioral spec in `interaction.md`) |\n\nTo onboard a new system: fill those rows, feed its ramps to the validator, and let\nit snap each slot to the nearest passing step. Structure and rules stay as written.\n\n## Reference files\n\n| File | What it answers |\n|------|-----------------|\n| `references/choosing-a-form.md` | Which chart type / is it even a chart? |\n| `references/color-formula.md` | The four jobs, the six checks, snap-to-passing |\n| `references/marks-and-anatomy.md` | Mark specs, spacers, labels, figures, hero number |\n| `references/interaction.md` | Tooltips & hover, filters & time ranges |\n| `references/components.md` | The pieces a chart is made of - build each in plain HTML |\n| `references/anti-patterns.md` | **What goes wrong - check every chart against this** |\n| `references/palette.md` | **The reference palette instance** - every parameter, filled in; swap for your brand's |\n| `scripts/validate_palette.js` | Runnable six-checks validator (run it; don't eyeball) |\n"},{"name":"superpowers:test-driven-development","path":"plugin:superpowers:test-driven-development","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/test-driven-development\n\n# Test-Driven Development (TDD)\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.\n\n**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.\n\n**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**\n\n## When to Use\n\n**Always:**\n- New features\n- Bug fixes\n- Refactoring\n- Behavior changes\n\n**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**\n- Throwaway prototypes\n- Generated code\n- Configuration files\n\nThinking \"skip TDD just this once\"? Stop. That's rationalization.\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST\n```\n\nWrite code before the test? Delete it. Start over.\n\n**No exceptions:**\n- Don't keep it as \"reference\"\n- Don't \"adapt\" it while writing tests\n- Don't look at it\n- Delete means delete\n\nImplement fresh from tests. Period.\n\n## Red-Green-Refactor\n\n```dot\ndigraph tdd_cycle {\n    rankdir=LR;\n    red [label=\"RED\\nWrite failing test\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ffcccc\"];\n    verify_red [label=\"Verify fails\\ncorrectly\", shape=diamond];\n    green [label=\"GREEN\\nMinimal code\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccffcc\"];\n    verify_green [label=\"Verify passes\\nAll green\", shape=diamond];\n    refactor [label=\"REFACTOR\\nClean up\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccccff\"];\n    next [label=\"Next\", shape=ellipse];\n\n    red -> verify_red;\n    verify_red -> green [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_red -> red [label=\"wrong\\nfailure\"];\n    green -> verify_green;\n    verify_green -> refactor [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_green -> green [label=\"no\"];\n    refactor -> verify_green [label=\"stay\\ngreen\"];\n    verify_green -> next;\n    next -> red;\n}\n```\n\n### RED - Write Failing Test\n\nWrite one minimal test showing what should happen.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\ntest('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {\n  let attempts = 0;\n  const operation = () => {\n    attempts++;\n    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');\n    return 'success';\n  };\n\n  const result = await retryOperation(operation);\n\n  expect(result).toBe('success');\n  expect(attempts).toBe(3);\n});\n```\nClear name, tests real behavior, one thing\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\ntest('retry works', async () => {\n  const mock = jest.fn()\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');\n  await retryOperation(mock);\n  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\n});\n```\nVague name, tests mock not code\n</Bad>\n\n**Requirements:**\n- One behavior\n- Clear name\n- Real code (no mocks unless unavoidable)\n\n### Verify RED - Watch It Fail\n\n**MANDATORY. Never skip.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test fails (not errors)\n- Failure message is expected\n- Fails because feature missing (not typos)\n\n**Test passes?** You're testing existing behavior. Fix test.\n\n**Test errors?** Fix error, re-run until it fails correctly.\n\n### GREEN - Minimal Code\n\nWrite simplest code to pass the test.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn();\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (i === 2) throw e;\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('unreachable');\n}\n```\nJust enough to pass\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  options?: {\n    maxRetries?: number;\n    backoff?: 'linear' | 'exponential';\n    onRetry?: (attempt: number) => void;\n  }\n): Promise<T> {\n  // YAGNI\n}\n```\nOver-engineered\n</Bad>\n\nDon't add features, refactor other code, or \"improve\" beyond the test.\n\n### Verify GREEN - Watch It Pass\n\n**MANDATORY.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test passes\n- Other tests still pass\n- Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n\n**Test fails?** Fix code, not test.\n\n**Other tests fail?** Fix now.\n\n**\"Other tests\" means the project's suite, not just your file.** A\ngreen run of the test you wrote is not a green suite. Before you call\nthe change done, run the project's test command (bare `pytest`,\n`npm test`, `cargo test` — whatever the repo uses) even when your task\nnamed only one test file. A scope statement in your task bounds the\ndeliverable, not your verification. Any failure that run shows —\nincluding one you didn't cause — goes in your report by name; a red\ntest you watched scroll past and didn't mention is a report falsified\nby omission.\n\n### REFACTOR - Clean Up\n\nAfter green only:\n- Remove duplication\n- Improve names\n- Extract helpers\n\nKeep tests green. Don't add behavior.\n\n### Repeat\n\nNext failing test for next feature.\n\n## Good Tests\n\n| Quality | Good | Bad |\n|---------|------|-----|\n| **Minimal** | One thing. \"and\" in name? Split it. | `test('validates email and domain and whitespace')` |\n| **Clear** | Name describes behavior | `test('test1')` |\n| **Shows intent** | Demonstrates desired API | Obscures what code should do |\n\nWhen writing or changing any test, read [writing-good-tests.md](writing-good-tests.md) for the rules that keep tests honest:\n- Name the production change that would make the test fail — before writing it\n- Assert on real behavior, never on mock behavior\n- Keep test-only code in test utilities, out of production classes\n- Understand a dependency's side effects before mocking it\n\n## Common Rationalizations\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Too simple to test\" | Simple code breaks. Test takes 30 seconds. |\n| \"I'll test after\" | Tests written after pass immediately — which proves nothing. They may test the wrong thing, test the implementation instead of the behavior, or miss the edge case you forgot. You never watched it fail, so you never proved it can catch the bug. Test-first forces that failure. |\n| \"Tests after achieve same goals (spirit not ritual)\" | Tests-after answer \"what does this do?\"; tests-first answer \"what should this do?\" Tests written after are biased by the code you already wrote — you verify the cases you remembered, not the ones you'd have discovered. Coverage without proof the tests work. |\n| \"Already manually tested\" | Manual testing is ad-hoc: no record of what you covered, no way to re-run it when the code changes, easy to forget cases under pressure. \"Worked when I tried it\" ≠ comprehensive. Automated tests run the same way every time. |\n| \"Deleting X hours is wasteful\" | Sunk cost fallacy — that time is already spent either way. The real choice: rewrite with TDD (high confidence) vs. keep it and bolt tests on after (low confidence, likely bugs). Keeping code you can't trust is the waste. |\n| \"Keep as reference, write tests first\" | You'll adapt it. That's testing after. Delete means delete. |\n| \"Need to explore first\" | Fine. Throw away exploration, start with TDD. |\n| \"Test hard = design unclear\" | Listen to test. Hard to test = hard to use. |\n| \"TDD will slow me down\" | TDD IS the pragmatic path: catches bugs before commit, prevents regressions, lets you refactor without fear. \"Pragmatic\" shortcuts mean debugging in production — slower, not faster. |\n| \"Manual test faster\" | Manual doesn't prove edge cases. You'll re-test every change. |\n| \"Existing code has no tests\" | You're improving it. Add tests for existing code. |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP and Start Over\n\n- Code before test\n- Test after implementation\n- Test passes immediately\n- Can't explain why test failed\n- Tests added \"later\"\n- Rationalizing \"just this once\"\n- \"I already manually tested it\"\n- \"Tests after achieve the same purpose\"\n- \"It's about spirit not ritual\"\n- \"Keep as reference\" or \"adapt existing code\"\n- \"Already spent X hours, deleting is wasteful\"\n- \"TDD is dogmatic, I'm being pragmatic\"\n- \"This is different because...\"\n\n**All of these mean: Delete code. Start over with TDD.**\n\n## Example: Bug Fix\n\n**Bug:** Empty email accepted\n\n**RED**\n```typescript\ntest('rejects empty email', async () => {\n  const result = await submitForm({ email: '' });\n  expect(result.error).toBe('Email required');\n});\n```\n\n**Verify RED**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nFAIL: expected 'Email required', got undefined\n```\n\n**GREEN**\n```typescript\nfunction submitForm(data: FormData) {\n  if (!data.email?.trim()) {\n    return { error: 'Email required' };\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n**Verify GREEN**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nPASS\n```\n\n**REFACTOR**\nExtract validation for multiple fields if needed.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\nBefore marking work complete:\n\n- [ ] Every new function/method has a test\n- [ ] Watched each test fail before implementing\n- [ ] Each test failed for expected reason (feature missing, not typo)\n- [ ] Wrote minimal code to pass each test\n- [ ] All tests pass\n- [ ] Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n- [ ] Tests use real code (mocks only if unavoidable)\n- [ ] Edge cases and errors covered\n\nCan't check all boxes? You skipped TDD. Start over.\n\n## When Stuck\n\n| Problem | Solution |\n|---------|----------|\n| Don't know how to test | Write wished-for API. Write assertion first. Ask your human partner. |\n| Test too complicated | Design too complicated. Simplify interface. |\n| Must mock everything | Code too coupled. Use dependency injection. |\n| Test setup huge | Extract helpers. Still complex? Simplify design. |\n\n## Debugging Integration\n\nBug found? Write failing test reproducing it. Follow TDD cycle. Test proves fix and prevents regression.\n\nNever fix bugs without a test.\n\n## Final Rule\n\n```\nProduction code → test exists and failed first\nOtherwise → not TDD\n```\n\nNo exceptions without your human partner's permission.\n"},{"name":"superpowers:verification-before-completion","path":"plugin:superpowers:verification-before-completion","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/verification-before-completion\n\n# Verification Before Completion\n\n## Overview\n\n**Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n\n**Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n```\n\nIf you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n\n## The Gate Function\n\n```\nBEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n\n1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n   - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n   - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n\nSkip any step = lying, not verifying\n```\n\n## Common Failures\n\n| Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n|-------|----------|----------------|\n| Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n| Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n| Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n| Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n| Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n| Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n| Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP\n\n- Using \"should\", \"probably\", \"seems to\"\n- Expressing satisfaction before verification (\"Great!\", \"Perfect!\", \"Done!\", etc.)\n- About to commit/push/PR without verification\n- Trusting agent success reports\n- Relying on partial verification\n- Thinking \"just this once\"\n- Tired and wanting work over\n- **ANY wording implying success without having run verification**\n\n## Rationalization Prevention\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Should work now\" | RUN the verification |\n| \"I'm confident\" | Confidence ≠ evidence |\n| \"Just this once\" | No exceptions |\n| \"Linter passed\" | Linter ≠ compiler |\n| \"Agent said success\" | Verify independently |\n| \"I'm tired\" | Exhaustion ≠ excuse |\n| \"Partial check is enough\" | Partial proves nothing |\n| \"Different words so rule doesn't apply\" | Spirit over letter |\n\n## Key Patterns\n\n**Tests:**\n```\n✅ [Run test command] [See: 34/34 pass] \"All tests pass\"\n❌ \"Should pass now\" / \"Looks correct\"\n```\n\n**Regression tests (TDD Red-Green):**\n```\n✅ Write → Run (pass) → Revert fix → Run (MUST FAIL) → Restore → Run (pass)\n❌ \"I've written a regression test\" (without red-green verification)\n```\n\n**Build:**\n```\n✅ [Run build] [See: exit 0] \"Build passes\"\n❌ \"Linter passed\" (linter doesn't check compilation)\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n```\n✅ Re-read plan → Create checklist → Verify each → Report gaps or completion\n❌ \"Tests pass, phase complete\"\n```\n\n**Agent delegation:**\n```\n✅ Agent reports success → Check VCS diff → Verify changes → Report actual state\n❌ Trust agent report\n```\n\n## When To Apply\n\n**ALWAYS before:**\n- ANY variation of success/completion claims\n- ANY expression of satisfaction\n- ANY positive statement about work state\n- Committing, PR creation, task completion\n- Moving to next task\n- Delegating to agents\n\n**Rule applies to:**\n- Exact phrases\n- Paraphrases and synonyms\n- Implications of success\n- ANY communication suggesting completion/correctness\n"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"19e11593-9835-4f0a-bd3f-01cb6d3aee3d","timestamp":"2026-10-31T00:10:51.480Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\nPath: plugin:superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents\n\n# Dispatching Parallel Agents\n\n## Overview\n\nYou delegate tasks to specialized agents with isolated context. By precisely crafting their instructions and context, you ensure they stay focused and succeed at their task. They should never inherit your session's context or history — you construct exactly what they need. This also preserves your own context for coordination work.\n\nWhen you have multiple unrelated failures (different test files, different subsystems, different bugs), investigating them sequentially wastes time. Each investigation is independent and can happen in parallel.\n\n**Core principle:** Dispatch one agent per independent problem domain. Let them work concurrently.\n\n## When to Use\n\n```dot\ndigraph when_to_use {\n    \"Multiple failures?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Are they independent?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Single agent investigates all\" [shape=box];\n    \"One agent per problem domain\" [shape=box];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Sequential agents\" [shape=box];\n    \"Parallel dispatch\" [shape=box];\n\n    \"Multiple failures?\" -> \"Are they independent?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Single agent investigates all\" [label=\"no - related\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Can they work in parallel?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Parallel dispatch\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Sequential agents\" [label=\"no - shared state\"];\n}\n```\n\n**Use when:**\n- 3+ test files failing with different root causes\n- Multiple subsystems broken independently\n- Each problem can be understood without context from others\n- No shared state between investigations\n\n**Don't use when:**\n- Failures are related (fix one might fix others)\n- Need to understand full system state\n- Agents would interfere with each other\n\n## The Pattern\n\n### 1. Identify Independent Domains\n\nGroup failures by what's broken:\n- File A tests: Tool approval flow\n- File B tests: Batch completion behavior\n- File C tests: Abort functionality\n\nEach domain is independent - fixing tool approval doesn't affect abort tests.\n\n### 2. Create Focused Agent Tasks\n\nEach agent gets:\n- **Specific scope:** One test file or subsystem\n- **Clear goal:** Make these tests pass\n- **Constraints:** Don't change other code\n- **Expected output:** Summary of what you found and fixed\n\n### 3. Dispatch in Parallel\n\nIssue all three subagent dispatches in the same response — they run in parallel:\n\n```text\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts failures\"\n# All three run concurrently.\n```\n\nMultiple dispatch calls in one response = parallel execution. One per response = sequential.\n\n### 4. Review and Integrate\n\nWhen agents return:\n- Read each summary\n- Verify fixes don't conflict\n- Run full test suite\n- Integrate all changes\n\n## Agent Prompt Structure\n\nGood agent prompts are:\n1. **Focused** - One clear problem domain\n2. **Self-contained** - All context needed to understand the problem\n3. **Specific about output** - What should the agent return?\n\n```markdown\nFix the 3 failing tests in src/agents/agent-tool-abort.test.ts:\n\n1. \"should abort tool with partial output capture\" - expects 'interrupted at' in message\n2. \"should handle mixed completed and aborted tools\" - fast tool aborted instead of completed\n3. \"should properly track pendingToolCount\" - expects 3 results but gets 0\n\nThese are timing/race condition issues. Your task:\n\n1. Read the test file and understand what each test verifies\n2. Identify root cause - timing issues or actual bugs?\n3. Fix by:\n   - Replacing arbitrary timeouts with event-based waiting\n   - Fixing bugs in abort implementation if found\n   - Adjusting test expectations if testing changed behavior\n\nDo NOT just increase timeouts - find the real issue.\n\nReturn: Summary of what you found and what you fixed.\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n**❌ Too broad:** \"Fix all the tests\" - agent gets lost\n**✅ Specific:** \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\" - focused scope\n\n**❌ No context:** \"Fix the race condition\" - agent doesn't know where\n**✅ Context:** Paste the error messages and test names\n\n**❌ No constraints:** Agent might refactor everything\n**✅ Constraints:** \"Do NOT change production code\" or \"Fix tests only\"\n\n**❌ Vague output:** \"Fix it\" - you don't know what changed\n**✅ Specific:** \"Return summary of root cause and changes\"\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n**Related failures:** Fixing one might fix others - investigate together first\n**Need full context:** Understanding requires seeing entire system\n**Exploratory debugging:** You don't know what's broken yet\n**Shared state:** Agents would interfere (editing same files, using same resources)\n\n## Real Example from Session\n\n**Scenario:** 6 test failures across 3 files after major refactoring\n\n**Failures:**\n- agent-tool-abort.test.ts: 3 failures (timing issues)\n- batch-completion-behavior.test.ts: 2 failures (tools not executing)\n- tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts: 1 failure (execution count = 0)\n\n**Decision:** Independent domains - abort logic separate from batch completion separate from race conditions\n\n**Dispatch:**\n```\nAgent 1 → Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\nAgent 2 → Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts\nAgent 3 → Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts\n```\n\n**Results:**\n- Agent 1: Replaced timeouts with event-based waiting\n- Agent 2: Fixed event structure bug (threadId in wrong place)\n- Agent 3: Added wait for async tool execution to complete\n\n**Integration:** All fixes independent, no conflicts, full suite green\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter agents return:\n1. **Review each summary** - Understand what changed\n2. **Check for conflicts** - Did agents edit same code?\n3. **Run full suite** - Verify all fixes work together\n4. **Spot check** - Agents can make systematic errors\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: dataviz\nPath: bundled:dataviz\n\nBase directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-502/bundled-skills/2.1.309/b686a53081079aed22896e5adfa24953/dataviz\n\n# Data Visualization\n\nA chart is **read by people and executed by you**. This skill turns \"make it look\ngood\" into a procedure with checks, so the result is right by construction rather\nthan by taste.\n\n**The method here is design-system-agnostic.** Nothing in the procedure, the form\nheuristic, the six checks, or the mark specs is specific to one product. A design\nsystem supplies a small set of *parameters* (its ramps, a categorical order, a\ndiverging pair, a status palette, a texture, its surfaces, its filter components);\nthe method consumes them unchanged. A **validated default palette** is the\nreference instance, fully specified in `references/palette.md`. To target your\nbrand, read that file's structure and substitute its values - touch nothing else.\n\n> The single most important habit: **the color part is computable, so compute it.**\n> Never eyeball whether a palette is colorblind-safe - run `scripts/validate_palette.js`.\n\n## The procedure - do these in order\n\nColor comes LAST. Most bad charts pick colors first.\n\n1. **Pick the form.** What is the data's job - magnitude, identity, polarity, a\n   single headline, change-over-time? The job picks the chart type, and sometimes\n   the answer is *not a chart* (a stat tile or hero number). -> `references/choosing-a-form.md`\n2. **Assign color by the job it does.** Categorical (identity), sequential\n   (magnitude), diverging (polarity), or status (state) - each has one rule.\n   Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled. -> `references/color-formula.md`\n3. **VALIDATE the palette - run the script, don't reason about Delta E.**\n   `node scripts/validate_palette.js \"<hex,hex,...>\" --mode light` (relative to\n   this skill's base directory - or load it as `<script type=\"module\">` in the\n   chart's own page, where it reads\n   `data-palette` off `<body>` and logs a `console.table` report). It returns\n   pass/fail on the lightness band, chroma floor, adjacent-pair CVD separation,\n   the normal-vision floor, and contrast. Fix anything that FAILs before continuing. Re-run for\n   `--mode dark` with that mode's surface.\n4. **Apply mark specs & spacers.** Thin marks, 4px rounded data-ends anchored to\n   the baseline, 2px lines, >=8px markers, a 2px surface gap between fills (stacked\n   segments and adjacent bars alike) and a 2px surface ring on overlapping marks,\n   selective direct labels. -> `references/marks-and-anatomy.md`\n5. **Add the hover layer - by default.** An HTML/SVG chart *is* interactive; ship\n   a crosshair+tooltip on line/area and a per-mark hover tooltip on bar/dot/cell.\n   The only form that skips it is a bare stat tile with no plot. Hit targets bigger\n   than the mark; filters in one row above the charts. -> `references/interaction.md`\n6. **Final accessibility pass.** For >= 2 series a legend is always present and <= 4\n   are also direct-labeled (a single series needs no legend box - the title names\n   it), so identity is never color-alone; a table view exists; dark mode is **selected** - its own\n   steps from the same ramps, validated against the dark surface, not an automatic\n   flip; texture is available for the CVD/print/forced-colors case.\n7. **Render it and look at it.** The validator checks color, not layout - open or\n   screenshot the output and eyeball it for label collisions, geometry, and overflow\n   before calling it done.\n\nThen check the result against **`references/anti-patterns.md`** - it is the catalog\nof what goes wrong. If your chart matches an entry, it's wrong.\n\n## Non-negotiables (true in every design system)\n\n- **Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled.** A 9th series is never a\n  generated hue - it folds into \"Other,\" small multiples, or composite encoding.\n- **One axis.** Never a dual-axis chart (two y-scales). Two measures of different\n  scale -> two charts, small multiples, or indexed to a common base. *(This is the\n  #1 chart mistake - see anti-patterns.)*\n- **Color follows the entity, never its rank.** A filter that changes the series\n  count must not repaint the survivors.\n- **Sequential = one hue, light->dark. Diverging = two hues + a neutral gray\n  midpoint.** Never a rainbow; never a hue at the diverging midpoint.\n- **Run the validator before shipping any categorical palette.** CVD Delta E >= 8 is the\n  target (OKLab ×100); 6-8 is a floor that is legal ONLY with secondary encoding. A\n  normal-vision floor below 15 is a hard FAIL - full-color readers can't tell the\n  pair apart; re-step it on the adjacent pairlist (secondary encoding does not excuse\n  this one); under `--pairs all` cut series or facet instead - see check 4. A contrast WARN\n  obligates visible labels or a table view - it is not dismissable.\n- **Thin marks; a legend always present for >= 2 series (none for one), with\n  selective direct labels (never a number on every point); recessive grid/axes.**\n- **Text wears text tokens, never the series color** - values, labels, and legends\n  stay in primary/secondary/muted ink; a colored mark beside them carries identity.\n- **Status colors are reserved** (good/warning/serious/critical) and never reused\n  for \"series 4\"; they ship with an icon + label, never color alone.\n\n## Plugging in a design system\n\nThe method is invariant; only these parameters change per system. The reference\ninstance - every value filled in - is `references/palette.md`.\n\n| Parameter | What the system provides |\n|---|---|\n| **Ramps** | the hue scales (named steps) the palette draws from |\n| **Categorical theme** | the fixed hue order (a named theme); default + alternates |\n| **Sequential hue** | the default single hue for magnitude |\n| **Diverging pair** | two warm/cool poles + a neutral midpoint |\n| **Status palette** | good / warning / serious / critical - steps distinct from categorical |\n| **Texture fill** | one directional hand-drawn fill, used at 45° / 135° |\n| **Surfaces** | light & dark chart-surface colors (the validator needs these) |\n| **Filter controls** | date-range & dimension controls (behavioral spec in `interaction.md`) |\n\nTo onboard a new system: fill those rows, feed its ramps to the validator, and let\nit snap each slot to the nearest passing step. Structure and rules stay as written.\n\n## Reference files\n\n| File | What it answers |\n|------|-----------------|\n| `references/choosing-a-form.md` | Which chart type / is it even a chart? |\n| `references/color-formula.md` | The four jobs, the six checks, snap-to-passing |\n| `references/marks-and-anatomy.md` | Mark specs, spacers, labels, figures, hero number |\n| `references/interaction.md` | Tooltips & hover, filters & time ranges |\n| `references/components.md` | The pieces a chart is made of - build each in plain HTML |\n| `references/anti-patterns.md` | **What goes wrong - check every chart against this** |\n| `references/palette.md` | **The reference palette instance** - every parameter, filled in; swap for your brand's |\n| `scripts/validate_palette.js` | Runnable six-checks validator (run it; don't eyeball) |\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: superpowers:test-driven-development\nPath: plugin:superpowers:test-driven-development\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/test-driven-development\n\n# Test-Driven Development (TDD)\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.\n\n**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.\n\n**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**\n\n## When to Use\n\n**Always:**\n- New features\n- Bug fixes\n- Refactoring\n- Behavior changes\n\n**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**\n- Throwaway prototypes\n- Generated code\n- Configuration files\n\nThinking \"skip TDD just this once\"? Stop. That's rationalization.\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST\n```\n\nWrite code before the test? Delete it. Start over.\n\n**No exceptions:**\n- Don't keep it as \"reference\"\n- Don't \"adapt\" it while writing tests\n- Don't look at it\n- Delete means delete\n\nImplement fresh from tests. Period.\n\n## Red-Green-Refactor\n\n```dot\ndigraph tdd_cycle {\n    rankdir=LR;\n    red [label=\"RED\\nWrite failing test\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ffcccc\"];\n    verify_red [label=\"Verify fails\\ncorrectly\", shape=diamond];\n    green [label=\"GREEN\\nMinimal code\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccffcc\"];\n    verify_green [label=\"Verify passes\\nAll green\", shape=diamond];\n    refactor [label=\"REFACTOR\\nClean up\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccccff\"];\n    next [label=\"Next\", shape=ellipse];\n\n    red -> verify_red;\n    verify_red -> green [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_red -> red [label=\"wrong\\nfailure\"];\n    green -> verify_green;\n    verify_green -> refactor [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_green -> green [label=\"no\"];\n    refactor -> verify_green [label=\"stay\\ngreen\"];\n    verify_green -> next;\n    next -> red;\n}\n```\n\n### RED - Write Failing Test\n\nWrite one minimal test showing what should happen.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\ntest('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {\n  let attempts = 0;\n  const operation = () => {\n    attempts++;\n    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');\n    return 'success';\n  };\n\n  const result = await retryOperation(operation);\n\n  expect(result).toBe('success');\n  expect(attempts).toBe(3);\n});\n```\nClear name, tests real behavior, one thing\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\ntest('retry works', async () => {\n  const mock = jest.fn()\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');\n  await retryOperation(mock);\n  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\n});\n```\nVague name, tests mock not code\n</Bad>\n\n**Requirements:**\n- One behavior\n- Clear name\n- Real code (no mocks unless unavoidable)\n\n### Verify RED - Watch It Fail\n\n**MANDATORY. Never skip.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test fails (not errors)\n- Failure message is expected\n- Fails because feature missing (not typos)\n\n**Test passes?** You're testing existing behavior. Fix test.\n\n**Test errors?** Fix error, re-run until it fails correctly.\n\n### GREEN - Minimal Code\n\nWrite simplest code to pass the test.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn();\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (i === 2) throw e;\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('unreachable');\n}\n```\nJust enough to pass\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  options?: {\n    maxRetries?: number;\n    backoff?: 'linear' | 'exponential';\n    onRetry?: (attempt: number) => void;\n  }\n): Promise<T> {\n  // YAGNI\n}\n```\nOver-engineered\n</Bad>\n\nDon't add features, refactor other code, or \"improve\" beyond the test.\n\n### Verify GREEN - Watch It Pass\n\n**MANDATORY.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test passes\n- Other tests still pass\n- Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n\n**Test fails?** Fix code, not test.\n\n**Other tests fail?** Fix now.\n\n**\"Other tests\" means the project's suite, not just your file.** A\ngreen run of the test you wrote is not a green suite. Before you call\nthe change done, run the project's test command (bare `pytest`,\n`npm test`, `cargo test` — whatever the repo uses) even when your task\nnamed only one test file. A scope statement in your task bounds the\ndeliverable, not your verification. Any failure that run shows —\nincluding one you didn't cause — goes in your report by name; a red\ntest you watched scroll past and didn't mention is a report falsified\nby omission.\n\n### REFACTOR - Clean Up\n\nAfter green only:\n- Remove duplication\n- Improve names\n- Extract helpers\n\nKeep tests green. Don't add behavior.\n\n### Repeat\n\nNext failing test for next feature.\n\n## Good Tests\n\n| Quality | Good | Bad |\n|---------|------|-----|\n| **Minimal** | One thing. \"and\" in name? Split it. | `test('validates email and domain and whitespace')` |\n| **Clear** | Name describes behavior | `test('test1')` |\n| **Shows intent** | Demonstrates desired API | Obscures what code should do |\n\nWhen writing or changing any test, read [writing-good-tests.md](writing-good-tests.md) for the rules that keep tests honest:\n- Name the production change that would make the test fail — before writing it\n- Assert on real behavior, never on mock behavior\n- Keep test-only code in test utilities, out of production classes\n- Understand a dependency's side effects before mocking it\n\n## Common Rationalizations\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Too simple to test\" | Simple code breaks. Test takes 30 seconds. |\n| \"I'll test after\" | Tests written after pass immediately — which proves nothing. They may test the wrong thing, test the implementation instead of the behavior, or miss the edge case you forgot. You never watched it fail, so you never proved it can catch the bug. Test-first forces that failure. |\n| \"Tests after achieve same goals (spirit not ritual)\" | Tests-after answer \"what does this do?\"; tests-first answer \"what should this do?\" Tests written after are biased by the code you already wrote — you verify the cases you remembered, not the ones you'd have discovered. Coverage without proof the tests work. |\n| \"Already manually tested\" | Manual testing is ad-hoc: no record of what you covered, no way to re-run it when the code changes, easy to forget cases under pressure. \"Worked when I tried it\" ≠ comprehensive. Automated tests run the same way every time. |\n| \"Deleting X hours is wasteful\" | Sunk cost fallacy — that time is already spent either way. The real choice: rewrite with TDD (high confidence) vs. keep it and bolt tests on after (low confidence, likely bugs). Keeping code you can't trust is the waste. |\n| \"Keep as reference, write tests first\" | You'll adapt it. That's testing after. Delete means delete. |\n| \"Need to explore first\" | Fine. Throw away exploration, start with TDD. |\n| \"Test hard = design unclear\" | Listen to test. Hard to test = hard to use. |\n| \"TDD will slow me down\" | TDD IS the pragmatic path: catches bugs before commit, prevents regressions, lets you refactor without fear. \"Pragmatic\" shortcuts mean debugging in production — slower, not faster. |\n| \"Manual test faster\" | Manual doesn't prove edge cases. You'll re-test every change. |\n| \"Existing code has no tests\" | You're improving it. Add tests for existing code. |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP and Start Over\n\n- Code before test\n- Test after implementation\n- Test passes immediately\n- Can't explain why test failed\n- Tests added \"later\"\n- Rationalizing \"just this once\"\n- \"I already manually tested it\"\n- \"Tests after achieve the same purpose\"\n- \"It's about spirit not ritual\"\n- \"Keep as reference\" or \"adapt existing code\"\n- \"Already spent X hours, deleting is wasteful\"\n- \"TDD is dogmatic, I'm being pragmatic\"\n- \"This is different because...\"\n\n**All of these mean: Delete code. Start over with TDD.**\n\n## Example: Bug Fix\n\n**Bug:** Empty email accepted\n\n**RED**\n```typescript\ntest('rejects empty email', async () => {\n  const result = await submitForm({ email: '' });\n  expect(result.error).toBe('Email required');\n});\n```\n\n**Verify RED**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nFAIL: expected 'Email required', got undefined\n```\n\n**GREEN**\n```typescript\nfunction submitForm(data: FormData) {\n  if (!data.email?.trim()) {\n    return { error: 'Email required' };\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n**Verify GREEN**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nPASS\n```\n\n**REFACTOR**\nExtract validation for multiple fields if needed.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\nBefore marking work complete:\n\n- [ ] Every new function/method has a test\n- [ ] Watched each test fail before implementing\n- [ ] Each test failed for expected reason (feature missing, not typo)\n- [ ] Wrote minimal code to pass each test\n- [ ] All tests pass\n- [ ] Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n- [ ] Tests use real code (mocks only if unavoidable)\n- [ ] Edge cases and errors covered\n\nCan't check all boxes? You skipped TDD. Start over.\n\n## When Stuck\n\n| Problem | Solution |\n|---------|----------|\n| Don't know how to test | Write wished-for API. Write assertion first. Ask your human partner. |\n| Test too complicated | Design too complicated. Simplify interface. |\n| Must mock everything | Code too coupled. Use dependency injection. |\n| Test setup huge | Extract helpers. Still complex? Simplify design. |\n\n## Debugging Integration\n\nBug found? Write failing test reproducing it. Follow TDD cycle. Test proves fix and prevents regression.\n\nNever fix bugs without a test.\n\n## Final Rule\n\n```\nProduction code → test exists and failed first\nOtherwise → not TDD\n```\n\nNo exceptions without your human partner's permission.\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: superpowers:verification-before-completion\nPath: plugin:superpowers:verification-before-completion\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/verification-before-completion\n\n# Verification Before Completion\n\n## Overview\n\n**Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n\n**Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n```\n\nIf you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n\n## The Gate Function\n\n```\nBEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n\n1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n   - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n   - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n\nSkip any step = lying, not verifying\n```\n\n## Common Failures\n\n| Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n|-------|----------|----------------|\n| Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n| Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n| Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n| Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n| Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n| Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n| Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP\n\n- Using \"should\", \"probably\", \"seems to\"\n- Expressing satisfaction before verification (\"Great!\", \"Perfect!\", \"Done!\", etc.)\n- About to commit/push/PR without verification\n- Trusting agent success reports\n- Relying on partial verification\n- Thinking \"just this once\"\n- Tired and wanting work over\n- **ANY wording implying success without having run verification**\n\n## Rationalization Prevention\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Should work now\" | RUN the verification |\n| \"I'm confident\" | Confidence ≠ evidence |\n| \"Just this once\" | No exceptions |\n| \"Linter passed\" | Linter ≠ compiler |\n| \"Agent said success\" | Verify independently |\n| \"I'm tired\" | Exhaustion ≠ excuse |\n| \"Partial check is enough\" | Partial proves nothing |\n| \"Different words so rule doesn't apply\" | Spirit over letter |\n\n## Key Patterns\n\n**Tests:**\n```\n✅ [Run test command] [See: 34/34 pass] \"All tests pass\"\n❌ \"Should pass now\" / \"Looks correct\"\n```\n\n**Regression tests (TDD Red-Green):**\n```\n✅ Write → Run (pass) → Revert fix → Run (MUST FAIL) → Restore → Run (pass)\n❌ \"I've written a regression test\" (without red-green verification)\n```\n\n**Build:**\n```\n✅ [Run build] [See: exit 0] \"Build passes\"\n❌ \"Linter passed\" (linter doesn't check compilation)\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n```\n✅ Re-read plan → Create checklist → Verify each → Report gaps or completion\n❌ \"Tests pass, phase complete\"\n```\n\n**Agent delegation:**\n```\n✅ Agent reports success → Check VCS diff → Verify changes → Report actual state\n❌ Trust agent report\n```\n\n## When To Apply\n\n**ALWAYS before:**\n- ANY variation of success/completion claims\n- ANY expression of satisfaction\n- ANY positive statement about work state\n- Committing, PR creation, task completion\n- Moving to next task\n- Delegating to agents\n\n**Rule applies to:**\n- Exact phrases\n- Paraphrases and synonyms\n- Implications of success\n- ANY communication suggesting completion/correctness\n\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
