{"parentUuid":"59e72530-1ec0-41e2-b41b-7a4abb32690d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e9ec6031-6d70-425a-a94b-94b6c6cc5b96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VskKtn19VN4SXZwP9zzKM7","type":"tool_result","content":"160:  - 80,770 clusters hold 31.4% of candidates, which leaves 801,071 distinct stories. 71,191 of the clusters span more than one paper.\n181:  - The reader's job is 1,050,459 candidates, or 801,071 if it reads one printing per cluster.\n182:  - Atlas would hold about 22,300 ghost-report stories, counting each candidate as 1/k of a story when its cluster holds k printings. Three of the 7 reports were reprinted (7, 13 and 30 printings), so this is a rough estimate.\n293:  - 91,301 reprint clusters hold 30.3% of candidates, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n390:  9. To weigh in 2B and 2C: cluster reprints before the reader, read one printing per cluster, and split gold, dev and train by cluster. That means less work for the reader and no leakage.\n420:  - The test set: 2,000 vectors of 384 dimensions, in 40 clusters, each stored as float32 and as int8, under one Vector Search index.\n430:  - M0's cap of three counts every database in the cluster, and `sample_mflix.movies` holds a Search index named `default`. So `places_search` isn't created.\n434:  - The cluster also holds a database `cubby`, with one collection, which isn't this project's. Nothing here touches it.\n436:  - **What counts:** Atlas counts data size plus B-tree index size, from `dbStats`, against the cap. Search indexes aren't in `dbStats`, and Free clusters have no Search size metrics, so by the docs they don't count. No page says so outright.\n452:  - Each judged report counts as 1/k of a story when its v2 cluster holds k printings.\n7:   /usr/share/dict/words knows; reprint clusters among the candidates; and the candidates\n34:from paperghosts.cluster.reprints import clusters, keys\n110:def ocr_quality(text, words, least=20):\n112:    once line-break hyphens are joined and common endings come off; None under `least`.\"\"\"\n114:    if len(found) < least:\n192:    \"\"\"One year's candidates: per-article length, OCR quality for a fixed sample, reprint keys,\n211:                    tuple(keys(text)),\n392:    groups = clusters([doc[2] for doc in docs], least=2)\n395:    rng = random.Random(\"eval A reprint check\")\n447:            out / \"reprint-check.json\",\n543:        \"reprints\": {\n556:        report[\"reprints\"][\"check\"] = summarise_checks(checks)\n630:        \"stories\": \"each candidate counts as 1/k of a story when its reprint cluster holds k printings\",\n723:        out[\"recall_at_least\"] = {\n736:    r = report[\"reprints\"]\n740:    yield f\"reprints: {r['clusters']:,} clusters hold {r['clustered_candidates']:,} candidates ({r['share_clustered']:.1%}); {r['distinct_stories']:,} distinct stories; {r['cross_paper_clusters']:,} clusters span papers\"\n746:        yield f\"  non-candidates: {s['random']['count']} reports in {s['random']['n']:,}, at most {s['random']['implied_high']:,}; recall {s['recall']:.1%}, at least {s['recall_at_least']['point']:.1%}\"","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"a3ffa8a6-f3c8-4421-821d-668563357572","timestamp":"2026-10-31T01:41:26.133Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"160:  - 80,770 clusters hold 31.4% of candidates, which leaves 801,071 distinct stories. 71,191 of the clusters span more than one paper.\n181:  - The reader's job is 1,050,459 candidates, or 801,071 if it reads one printing per cluster.\n182:  - Atlas would hold about 22,300 ghost-report stories, counting each candidate as 1/k of a story when its cluster holds k printings. Three of the 7 reports were reprinted (7, 13 and 30 printings), so this is a rough estimate.\n293:  - 91,301 reprint clusters hold 30.3% of candidates, which leaves 952,591 distinct stories.\n390:  9. To weigh in 2B and 2C: cluster reprints before the reader, read one printing per cluster, and split gold, dev and train by cluster. That