{"parentUuid":"2082d16d-99e2-4b55-84ca-0715d995aa73","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e9ec6031-6d70-425a-a94b-94b6c6cc5b96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H6PA32ntysfMzSznTEETL2","type":"tool_result","content":"=== eval-0003\n  first: The commonwealth. (Greenwood, Miss.) 1896-1923 1897-09-09 | PERSONAL AND LITERARY.\n    Prof. Marsh, who holds the chair o. .paleontology at Yale, has drawn no sal AIRY for his services since 1866. MIR. Ruskin's health has improved, and he is interesting himself in the pub LEGATION of the addresses delivered by him at oxford on landscape painting. Miss Mary Rachel Dobson, the cid est daughter Of Austin Dobson, the English poet, has joined a missionary settlement for college women in Bonn bay. She is a graduate of London uni yersity. Miss Nellie Patterson, a young woman of Mount Carmel. Conn, has completed a f~our-years' apprenticeship to the ~machinist trade and proposes to undertake toolmaking as her specia! line of work. Assistant Secretary\n  second: The Van Buren press. 1897-09-11 | PERSONAL AND LITERARY.\n    Prof. Marsh, who holds the chair Of paleontology at Yale, has drawn no sal airy for his services since 1866. Mr.. Ruskin's health has improved and he is interesting himself in the pub lieation of the addresses delivered by him al Oxford on landscape painting. Miss Mary Rachel Dobson, the eld est daughter of Austin Dobson, the English poet, has Joined missionary settlement for college women in Bonn bay. She is graduate of London uni versity, Miss Nellie Patterson, a young woman of Mount Carmel, Conn, has completed a fonr-years' pprenticeship to the machinist trade and proposes to undertake toolmaking as her special line of work Assistant Secretary of State\n=== eval-0082\n  first: Fort Worth gazette. [volume] (Fort Worth, Tex.) 18 1891-09-13 | \n    that than by pressing your suit unduiy- forgive the Sord-at this early day. 1eel quite safe in agreeing with you there Blarnaby reached out his hand and TOOK hers. which was simultaneously raised To- wards his for a friendly grip. With spas moGic effort that evolved good deal OF agility he bent over and kissed my lady's riding glove with more than merely hnlghtly courtesy. He had hardly recovered his position when the excited apparition of Sir John flashed upon him from l sharp turn IN the road ahead. Blarnaby fairly shook with dread. But his feat of gallanrry had not been Sin John had galloped back at utmost speed\n  second: Fort Worth gazette. [volume] (Fort Worth, Tex.) 18 1891-09-17 | \n    |, that than by pressing your suit unduly- forgive the word-at this early day. / feel quite safe in agreeing with you there Blarnaby reached out his hand and took hers. which was simultaneously raised to- wards his for a friendly grip. With spas moGic effort that evolved a good deal of agility he bent over and kissed my lady s ~idin9-glOve with more than merely knightly courtesy. He had hardly recovered his position when the excited apparition of Sir John flashed upon him from sharp turn in the road ahead Blarnaby fairly shook with dread. But mis- feat Of gallantry had not been spied Sir John had galloped back\n=== eval-0117\n  first: The Ashland union. [volume] (Ashland, Ashland Coun 1859-01-19 | p0ttrg.\n\nOVER THE RIVER.\n    Over the river they beckon to me, Loved ones who've crossed to the further side, The CLEAN of their snowy robes l see, But their voices are lost in the dashing tide. There's one with ringlets of sunny gold, And eyes the reflection of heavens own blue, He crossed in the twilight gray and cold, And the pale mist hid him from mortal view; We saw not the angels who met him there, The gates of the city we could not see, Over the river, over the river, My brother stands waiting to welcome me. Over the river the boatman pale Carried another, the household pet; Her brown curls waved\n  second: Juniata sentinel. [volume] (Mifflintown, Pa.) 1846 1870-12-14 | 4cti's Gorner.\n\nOVER THE RIVER.\nBY MRS. A. C. WAKEFIELD.\n    Over the river they beckon to me, Loved ones who've crossed to the further side; The gleam of their snowy robes l see, But their voices are lost in the dashing tide. There's one with ringlets Of snowy gold, And eyes the reflection Of Heavens own blue; lie crossed in the twilight gray aud cold, And the pale mist hid him from mortal view. We saw not the angels who met him there, The gates Of the city we could not see. Over the river-over the river 4-'-+\" Ny brother stands waiting to welcome me. Over the river the boatman pale Carried another, the household pet: Her brown curls waved\n=== eval-0165\n  first: Laurel outlook. 1948-02-25 | PLANETARY:\nMarian Life\n    After having given rise to much idle speculation and phantasmago- rial nonsense among recent genera tons, one of the minor riddles of the universe-whether life exists on the planet Mars-fnally got a tentative answer. With scientific reticence, DR.. Ger ard P. Kuiper, director of McDon- aid observatory at Mt. Locke, Tex, offered a timorous \"Yes-maybe.\" He made his disclosure after TAX ing a peek at the red planet, aided by a new-fangled infra-red spectre graph. But if there is life it exists in the most primitive of forms. DR.. Kuiper, aided by astronomers from the Universities of Chicago and Texas, came to the conclusion that probably lichen, the hard, moss-like\n  second: Dickenson County herald. [volume] (Clintwood, Va.) 1948-03-04 | PLANETARY:\nMarian Life\n    After having given rise to much idle speculation and phantasmago- rial nonsense among recent genera tions, one of the minor riddles of the universe-whether life exists on the planet Mars--fnally got a tentative answer. With scientific reticence, DR.. Ger ard P. Kuiper, director of Melon aid observatory at Mt. Locke, Tex, offered a tinorous reply: eyes maybe\" He made his disclosure, after TAX ing a peek at the red planet, aided by new-fangled infra-red spectre graph, as it whirled to within So million miles of earth, closest it will come in two years. But il there is life 1t exists in the most primitive of forms. DR.. Kuiper, aided by\n=== eval-0135\n  first: The Ely miner. [volume] (Ely, Minn.) 1895-1986 1906-09-28 | In Memoriam\n\n\nRIGBY\n\"a lovely apparition, sent\nTo be a moments ornamen\n    And is this by Rigby's orders, too?' asked the boss, with grin. Hardly\" said Brown. Brown said the boss, you have a prophetic soul. Tack up your own lit tie contribution to this galaxy of asininity. It'll be highly appropriate within the next five minutes. Tack it up so it'll stick, Beown.\" Then he made for the stairs. From that day to this, not one of us ever laid eyes on Rigby; and some of the boys still say that the old man, in the frightful wrath tlat possessed him, killed and ate the boy, blood. bones and all, right there in Otis Office.\n  second: Warren sheaf. [volume] (Warren, Marshall County, M 1906-11-08 | ..,- 'rita-.. O.. $3.50.\nIn Memoriam\nRIGBY +.\n\n\"A lovely apparition, s\n    4 l. 3 - '.'...''.......' ............ ! \"And is this by Rigby's orders, too?\" asked the boss, with grin. Hardly\" said Brown. Brown\" said the boss, you have a prophetic soul. Tack up your own lit. tie contribution to this galaxy OF asininity. It'll be highly appropriate within the next five minutes. Tack it up so it'll stick, Brown = # Then he made for tho stairs. F,rou.. that day to this, not one CT us e,e;. laid eyes on Rigby; and some u s Y. 4 boys still say that the old ma i, ,5@ frightful wrath that pnsses:se killed and ate the bot n; vs., NJ and all.\n=== eval-0045\n  first: Iowa County democrat. [volume] (Mineral Point, Wis 1900-02-08 | THE NORTH STAR.\n    Doubtless you all know where the north star is, says a. writer in St Nicholas. If not you can easily get some one to tell you, or, better still perhaps YOU can find it Yourself Look at the northern Sky any clear evening and YOU can easily find the seven bright stars of the Big Dipper in the group constellation Ursa Major As is we'll known, the two in the end of the bowl of the dpper arc in line with, or point to, the north star This star has recently attracted much at tention from the fact that in Septom- Der, 1899 Prof, w, W. Campbell of the Lick\n  second: Northern Wisconsin advertiser. [volume] (Wabeno, W 1900-02-08 | THE NORTH STAR.\n    Doubtless you all know where the north star is, says a writer in st. Nicholas. II not, you can easily get some one to tell you, or. better still, perhaps you can find it yourself. Look at the northern sky any clear evening. and you can easily find the seven bright stars of the Big Dipper in the group constellation Ursa Major. AS is we'll known, the two in the end of the bowl of the dipper are in line with, or point to, the north star This star has recently attracted much at tention from the fact that in Septem- per 1899, Prof w. w Campbell of the LICK\n=== eval-0067\n  first: Brownsville herald. [volume] (Brownsville, Tex.) 1 1918-12-27 | \n    BlucfeId. WI. Vn.--Drclnring the house was haunted nnd thht he had seen heaslleSs npraritiuns marching around the place. Babe Taylor. n car penter, has given away his farm of 13 acres with n house and barns 10 a neichbor. Taylor threw In hne COW ns nm indncemcnt. There III Il bad railroad crossing IN the property and many fatal accidents have occurred there.\n  second: Martinsburg W Va evening journal. [volume] (Martin 1918-10-03 | GIVES AWAY \"HAuNTED\" HOUSE\n\nnv International News Service.)\n    Bluefield, w. Va, Oct 3-Declal ing the house was haunted and that he had seen headless apparitions marching around the place, Babe Taylor, carpenter has given away his farm of thirteen acres with hose and barns to neighbor. Taylor threw in a fine COW as an inducement There is bad railroad crossing on the property and many fatal acct dents have occurred there.\n=== eval-0159\n  first: The Breckenridge news. 1906-12-26 | TALK OE WAR\n\nDeprecated by the Japanese Ambas'\n\n\nsador to United State\n    New York. Dec 1S.-After review ing the am'cable relations long exist ing between the United States and Japan, Viscount Aoki the Japanee ambassador, declared that his people were fully alive to the magnitude of Japan's obligation to this country, the memory of which would be a lasting monument of the cordial friendship that cements the two nations. East ern Asia, he said was large enough for the commerce of all nations, and the dream of the pessimist who saw the phantom of a struggle between the United States and Japan for the supremacy of the Pacific was too ridic- ulOus to be made the subject of seri- Gus thought Ambassador\n  second: Tazewell Republican. [volume] (Tazewell, Va.) 1892 1906-12-20 | U. S. AND Japan.\n\nVlscount 0akl Talks Of Relations Be-\n\n\ntween the Two\n    New York, Dec. II. After viewing the amicable relations, long existing be- tween the United States and Japan, Viscount Aoki, the Japanese Ambassa- dor, tonight declared that his people were fully alive to the magnitude OF Japan's obligation to this country, the memory of which would be a living monument of the cordial friendship that cements the two nations. Eastern Asia he said, was large enough for the COM merce Of all nations and the dream OF the pessimist who saw the phantom OF a struggle between the United States and Japan for the supremacy of the Pacafic was too ridiculous to be made the subject of serious thought. Ambassador\n=== eval-0119\n  first: Brownsville herald. [volume] (Brownsville, Tex.) 1 1931-10-16 | DENTISTS? NEVER!\n    Observation-Disclosures, Indians, rattlesnakes and ghosts hold less terror for a certain 14-year-old Houston boy than the dentists chair. His mother told him she was going to take him 10 the dentist one day. The next morning she entered his room TO find he had packed all his clothes and had run away. She enlisted aid Of police in finding him.\n  second: Brownsville herald. [volume] (Brownsville, Tex.) 1 1931-10-16 | DENTISTS? NEVER,\n    Observation-Disclosures, Indians, r~attlesnakes and ghosts hold less terror for a certain 14-year-old Houston boy than the dentists chair. His mother told him she was going to take him TO the dentist one day. The next morning she entered his room TO find he had packed all his clothes and had run away. She enlisted aid Of police In finding him.\n=== eval-0111\n  first: The Lambertville press. 1860-04-18 | \n    glimpse of his face, started back in CONSERVATION. 1t was the very face he had seen, three or four hours before, looking up at him from the captains desk. At first he tried to persuade himself it might be fancy; but the more be examined the man the more sure he became that. he was right. Not only the face, but the person and the dress exactly corresponded. As soon as the exhausted crew and famished passengers were cared for and the barque on her course again, the mate called the captain aside. \"It seems that was not ghost I saw to- day, sir; the mans alive\" What do you\n  second: Ashtabula weekly telegraph. [volume] (Ashtabula, O 1860-02-25 | \n    CONSERVATION. It was the very face he had seen three or four hours before, look ing up at him from the captains desk. AT 6rst he tried to persuade himself il might be fancy but the more he examined the man the more sure he became that he was right. Not only the face, but the person, and the dress, exactly correspon. ded. As soon as the exhausted crew and fam ished passengers we're cared for, and the bark on her course again, the mate called the captain aside. 1t seems that was not a ghost saw to-day, sir, the mans alive. What do you mean Who's alive p why,\n=== eval-0106\n  first: Albuquerque morning journal. [volume] (Albuquerque 1910-06-09 | Prevalence OF Superstition.\n    Superstition Is yet Il big factor in the affairs OF men. We laugh Mt the idea, but there is q latent influence ftnl cNertinR itself In superstitione minds. The appearance OF the comet proved this, and there are still those who are confident II they pick their teeth with n splinter cut from n troe which has been struck by lightning that they viii nevor have the tooth ache. There are some 5000 different supcrstitions which still hold sway in the world. II Is n day Of enlighten- mcnt. II is a day when we feel the influence of higher education. and yet the most educated. the best read and\n  second: The Tupelo journal. 1910-05-27 | Superstition q Big Factor.\n    Superstition is yet a big factor in the affairs of men. We laugh at the idea, but there is a latent influence still asserting itself in superstitious minds. The ap- pearance Of the comet proved this, and there are still those who are confident if they pick their teeth with a splinter cut from a tree which has been struck by lightning, that they will never have the toothache. There are some 5000 different superstitions which still hold sway in the world. It is a day of enlightenment. 1t is a day when we feel the influence of higher education, and yet the most educated, the best read and the\n=== eval-0069\n  first: The gold leaf. [volume] (Henderson, N.C.) 1881-191 1894-01-04 | \n    Have You ever s;. heard the wind go IT'S pitiful sound to hear! 1t seems to chill you through and through With strange and speechless fear. It's the voice of the night that broods out side When folks should be ak2ep, And many and manys the time I've cried To the darkness that brooded far and wide Over the land and deep: Whom do you want, o lonely night, That you wall the long hours through ?\" And the night would say in its ghostly way \" OOOOOOOO. IOOOOOOOO 1OOOOOOOO ~ My mother told me long ago (When | was a little lad) That when the night was wailing so,\n  second: The daily Gate City. [volume] (Keokuk, Iowa) 1855- 1914-02-12 | keokvs. Iowa .....- February fa, 1914\n\nTHE sIeaT WIND.\n    Did sou ever hear The wind 20 Yoooooo77 It's . pitffal sound TO heart : It seems to thrill you through and through With strange and speechless fear. IT'S the voice OF the night that broods outside When folks should be