ritish empire wilt be chara in few sentences, while for the other glowing pages and fervid chapters must be set apart. In these two events medievalish and modernism, the order that is to be and the order that is ultimately to pass away, meet face tc face How petty the GROWING of a king by one nation when contrasted with the unselfsh creation Of new nationa, full equipped for the battle, by another onei In the one we have hollow display\n  second: The Appeal. [volume] (Saint Paul, Minn. ;) 1889-19 1902-06-07 | MINNEAPOLIS. MINI. sATURDAYJUNE q 100\n    scends in Importance all the coronations hat have taken place in hundreds OF years. In the perspective of history the gorgeous ceremonial in the greatest church edtfice In the British empire will be characterized in few sentences, while for the other glowing pages and fervi0 chapters must be set apart. In these two events medievalish and modernism, the order that is to be and the order that is ultimately to pass away, meet face to face How petty the GROWING of a king by one nation when contrasted with the unselfsh creation Of a new nationa, full equipped for the battle, by another onei In the one we have hollow\n=== eval-0196\n  first: The Tacoma times. [volume] (Tacoma, Wash.) 1903-19 1911-12-16 | MISCELLANEOUS\n    Sprague Memorial Presbyterian church. cor. 5Sth and Warner, So. Tacoma. Sunday school, 9:45 a. M; p. M; Adults Bible class, ESQ p. m. ; service, 1880 p. m. Rev. DIR. Dodds. pastor. The Independent Spiritualist church will hold one meeting Sum day, Dec. Sd. at s p. m, in Odd Fellows hall. 712 Pac. av., 711h Commerce. The Friends church. No Sth and State sts.. Esther B. X. Terrell, pas tor. Bible school. 10 a. m; meeting for worship. IL a. m\n  second: The Tacoma times. [volume] (Tacoma, Wash.) 1903-19 1912-03-02 | \n    The Independent Spiritualist church will hold one meeting Sum day, Dec. Sd. at s D. m, in Odd Fellows hall, 712 Pac. av., 711h Commerce.\n=== eval-0155\n  first: Semi-weekly standard. [volume] (Raleigh, N.C.) 185 1862-08-09 | For the Standard.\n    Mr. EDrToR:-I have at last obtained my own consent to trouble you with article Of army news, knowing that before this reaches you, the State cleo tons will be over, and the heavy pressure on your fearless and patriotic columns will have ceased Permit me to say, that nobly have you done your duty to your country, in the late campaign fear lcssly and with unsurpassed ability, have you ex- posed corruption, selishness, and mean countrywide stroying partyism in thigh places\" You have placed mark as hideous and as lasting as that Of Cain, upon the brow of more than one dcmagogue who las heretofore deceived honest men to their\n  second: Weekly standard. [volume] (Raleigh, N.C.) 1858-186 1862-08-13 | For the Standard.\n    bravely MANIFESTED. .-f.., wa r-. 49, For the Standard. Nix, EprroR:-I have at last obtained my own consent to trouble you with article OF army news knowing that before this reaches you the State cleo tons will be over, and the heavy pressure on your fearless and patriotic columns will have ceased Permit me to say, that nobly have you done your duty to your country, in the late campaign; pear' lessiy and with unsurpassed ability, have you ex- posed corruption, sclfishness and mean countryae' stroyng partyism in \"high places\" You nave nlaced mark as hideous, and as lasting as that OF Cain. upon the brow Of more than one\n=== eval-0068\n  first: Dodgeville chronicle. [volume] (Dodgeville, Wis.)  1873-04-25 | Recognizing Relatives Among the\n\n\nSaved of the Atlantic.\n    NO SOONER was breakfast over imam the inquiries for friends were begun. There is department especially devoted to these inquiries at Castle Garden, where persons who expect friends or relatives are required to apply for information. The room is in low, long shed, just outside of the Castle, and is fitted up with rallied in desk and numerous benches. This room was crowded with men and women, the latter being in the proportion of about two to one, all Of whom were restless and uneasy. The post-office rule-frst come first served-Was in full force, and men and women were admitted to the desk one at time to inquire for expected\n  second: Perrysburg journal. (Perrysburg, Wood Co., O. [Ohi 1873-04-25 | Recognizing Relatives Among tn\n\n\nSaved OF the Atlantic.\n    No SOONER was breakfast over than the | inquiries for friends were begun. There | is department especially devoted TO | these inquiries at Castle Garden, where persons who expect friends or relatives are required to apply for information The room is in low, long shed, just outside of the Castle, and is fitted up with rallied | In desk and numerous benches. This room was crowded with men and women, the latter being in the proportion Of about two TO one, all of whom were restless and uneasy. The post-office rule--first come first served-was in full force, and men and women were admitted to the desk one at u\n=== eval-0020\n  first: The Wellington enterprise. [volume] (Wellington, O 1881-03-10 | THE PILOrs sTORI.\n    We had grown mp together, as 1t were, Mollie and ], our parents being near neighbors, and-which does not always tollow-firm friends as we'll. They were poor, and suspect that fact had much 70 do with. their friendship. for opportunities were always turning up for helping one another; and have often noticed that when near ncighbors arc well Of and have no need for mutual help, there is very seldom any friend ship between them; there is more apt 70 be Jealousy and competition. Our parents being such good friends; followed their example. We went to school together, read together. played together, and. somehow, when Mollie wss eighteen and ]\n  second: Staunton vindicator. [volume] (Staunton, Va.) 1860 1891-08-21 | A RACE OF FIRE.\n    Dell Dowler Ringeling in Cosmopolitan. A RACE OF FIRE. We had grown up together, as 1t were, Mollie and /, our parents being near neigh bors, and-which does not always follow firm friends as we'll They were poor, and | suspect that fact had much to do with their friendship, for opportunities were always turning up for helping one another; and have often noticed that when near neighbors are well off and have no need for mutual help, there is very seldom any friendship between them. There is more apt to be jealousy and competition. Our parents being such good friends, it naturally resulted that Mollie and l fol- lowed\n=== eval-0184\n  first: Burlington weekly free press. [volume] (Burlington 1912-12-26 | \n\\ y. : .ROQlT%r GPLe@\n    forever. and may hold it's corner in the combination room with pride. Not so tho new-fangled lightwood dresser or dressing table. The girl with good taste is very careful in selecting furnishings for her combination room. She doesn't pick up a curtain or a rug here and there without an eye as to color schemes and harmony. She realizes that harmony is the keynote of good taste. and she never outrages the ar tistic eye. The general tone Of the room should be restful and warm. but never loud and gaudy. Brilliant colors should be avoided. or course, the wood finlshings Of the room must in no way conflict with\n  second: The Birmingham age-herald. 1912-12-01 | A. Combination\n\n x s. q ROQII1 1S1 GLRLeg\n    forever, and may hold it's corner in the combination room with pride. Not so the new-fangled light-wood dresser or dressing table. The girl with good taste Is very careful in selecting furnishings for her combination room. She doesn't pick up a curtain or a rug here and there without an eye as to color schemes and harmony. She realizes that harmony is the keynote of good taste, and she never outrages the ar tistic eye. The general tone of the room should be restful and warm, but never loud and gaudy. Brilliant colors should be avoided. Of course, the wood finishings of the room must in no way conflict with\n=== eval-0130\n  first: Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-197 1888-12-06 | A Portrait of The Gar Nicholas\n    From the Duke of Coburg's Recollections The Gar Nicholas was, in fact. the last real autocrat in Europe. I count it among nv most instructive experiences and recollections to have obtained a living picture of the most remarkable man of my time, which was able to supplement and complete from time to time by means OF numerous connections with rela- tives common to us both. In the person of the Emperor Nicholas all vague distractions of state, church and nationality vanished One looked at him and perceived that all the force expressed in these terms was concentrated in his person. Apart from him nothing existed or seemed capable of existing.\n  second: Thomas County cat. [volume] (Colby, Kan.) 1885-189 1889-01-31 | THE CAR NICHOLAS.\n\nA vivid Pen Picture Drawn By Ernes\n\n\nDuke Of C0burg\n    A vivid Pen Picture Drawn By Ernest, Duke Of Coburg. The Car Nicholas was, in fact, the last real autocrat in Europe. / count it among my most instructive expert ences and recollections to have OBJ tanned living picture of the most re- markable man of my time. which l was able to supplement and complete from time to time by means of numerous connections with relatives common to us both. In the person of the Emperor Nicho- las all vague distractions of state. church and nationality vanished. One looked at him and perceived that all the force expressed in these terms was concentrated In his person. Apart from him\n=== eval-0268\n  first: Evansville daily journal. (Evansville, Ind.) 1863- 1864-08-09 | The 40 GoYernmenl Loan.\n    Many Of the advantages of this loan are apparent on their face, but there are others that viii be best understood after consideration Among them there are, It's ABSOLUTE SEcuRlTY Nearly all active credits are now based on Govern ment securities. Banks OF issue aud Savings banks hold them in large guam iities-in many cases more than the en- tire amount OF their capitals-and they hold them as the very best and strong. est investment they could possibly make. If it were possible to contemplate the financial failure Of the Government. no bank would be any better or safer. Sa- wings banks that allow their deposi,ors bat five per cent\n  second: The Ashland union. [volume] (Ashland, Ashland Coun 1864-08-24 | INC ESQ Government Loan.\n    are apparent on their face, but there are others that will be best understood after consideration. Among them there are. It's ABSOLUTE SECURITY.-Nearly all active credits are now based on Govern ment securities. Banks of issue and Sa- wings banks hold them in large quaint ties-ir many cases, more than the entire amount of their capitals-and they bold them as the very best and strongest in vestment they could possibly make. 1t it were possible to contemplate the fnan- cial failure of the Government, no bank would be any better or safer. Savings Banks already have a large part of their assets invested in Government securities. As a ;ule they\n=== eval-0237\n  first: Evening star. 1925-05-24 | \n    351 Memories OF Lincoln. Whitman 2aS Irish Fotk Songs and Tales 4o4 in Memorism Tennyson 427 Poems OF John Keats 4ss Great Yiddish Poetry 497 Poems OF VILLA m Morris sol Poems OF Carew, Sucking. I.oveIace and Herbert SIS Haunted House. Niereck 579 Three Sphinxes Niereck 70l Poems OF Robert Herrick SIS The Lady OF the Lake Scott 61s Poems Of VILLA m Wordsworth Sc Negro Songs. Anthology 644645 Poem Of the cid. vols. s77 Poems Of Blake 719 Poetry of IN. Southern states 724 Poems OF Friedrich HoIderlin 1Sa Verses of Terror and Wonder 40 Poems of Rryant 7a1 Poems Of Whittier 742 Poems Of Emerson 743 Great Christian\n  second: Evening star. 1925-05-11 | \n    351 Memories of Lincoln. Whitman 39S Irish Folk Songs and Tales 404 In Memoriam. Tennyson 427 Poems of John Keats 4s8 Great Yiddish Poetry 492 Poems of William Morris sol Poems of Carew, Sucking, Lovelace and Herbert 57S Haunted House. Viereck 579 Three Sphinxes. Viereck 701 Poems of Robert Herrick Sis The Lady of the Lake Scott 618 Poems of William Wordsworth 6e6 Negro Songs. Anthology 644-645 Poem of the Cid. 9 vols. 677 Poems of Blake 719 Poetry of the Southern States 724 Poems of Friedrich HolderIin 739 Verses of Terror and Wonder 74o Poems of Bryant 741 Poems of Whittier 742 Poems of Emerson 743 Great Christian Hymns\n=== eval-0281\n  first: The sun. [volume] (Morris, Stevens County, Minn.)  1886-09-02 | IN A OLOISTER.\n    In A OLOISTER. How Novices Live and How They spend Their Time. From the New York Mail and Express. / had been invited by the master OF novices of well-known Jesuit institu- tion, some distance from New York, to spend a few days in the novitiate as his guest. The invitation was &C. cepted with pleasure, mingled with a slight misgiving at the thought of the uninteresting solitude of the place was going to. The misgiving was speedily allayed ON arriving at my destination, and the new sphere OF life in which l found myself proved interesting beyond measure. I met a hospitable welcome, was politely es- corted to one\n  second: The Warner sun. (Warner, Brown Co., Dakota [S.D.]) 1886-09-03 | IN A CLOISTER.\n\nHow Noyices Live and Row They Spend\n\n\nTheir Time.\n    tiop, some distance from New York to spend a few days in the novitiatC as his guest. The invitation was &C cepted with pleasure, mingled with 8 slight misgiving at the thought Of the uninteresting solitude of the place was going to. The misgiving was speedily allayed on arriving at my destination, and the new sphere Of life in which / found myself proved interesting beyond measure. | met a hospitable welcome, was politely es, corted to one OF the guest chambers and requested to make myself as com, Portable as possible. The apartment to which / had been allotted was marked by the simplicity which char acterizes the establishment.\n=== eval-0211\n  first: The Carlisle independent. 1919-02-06 | REGULAR coNNoIssEUR.\n    Cert tof the gloves)-My beau is an excellent judge of pictures. Nell (of the notions)-Does he haunt the gallerie