r helping one another; and have often noticed that when near ncighbors arc well Of and have no need for mutual help, there is very seldom any friend ship between them; there is more apt 70 be Jealousy and competition. Our parents being such good friends; followed their example. We went to school together, read together. played together, and. somehow, when Mollie wss eighteen and ]\n  second: Staunton vindicator. [volume] (Staunton, Va.) 1860 1891-08-21 | A RACE OF FIRE.\n    Dell Dowler Ringeling in Cosmopolitan. A RACE OF FIRE. We had grown up together, as 1t were, Mollie and /, our parents being near neigh bors, and-which does not always follow firm friends as we'll They were poor, and | suspect that fact had much to do with their friendship, for opportunities were always turning up for helping one another; and have often noticed that when near neighbors are well off and have no need for mutual help, there is very seldom any friendship between them. There is more apt to be jealousy and competition. Our parents being such good friends, it naturally resulted that Mollie and l fol- lowed\n=== eval-0184\n  first: Burlington weekly free press. [volume] (Burlington 1912-12-26 | \n\\ y. : .ROQlT%r GPLe@\n    forever. and may hold it's corner in the combination room with pride. Not so tho new-fangled lightwood dresser or dressing table. The girl with good taste is very careful in selecting furnishings for her combination room. She doesn't pick up a curtain or a rug here and there without an eye as to color schemes and harmony. She realizes that harmony is the keynote of good taste. and she never outrages the ar tistic eye. The general tone Of the room should be restful and warm. but never loud and gaudy. Brilliant colors should be avoided. or course, the wood finlshings Of the room must in no way conflict with\n  second: The Birmingham age-herald. 1912-12-01 | A. Combination\n\n x s. q ROQII1 1S1 GLRLeg\n    forever, and may hold it's corner in the combination room with pride. Not so the new-fangled light-wood dresser or dressing table. The girl with good taste Is very careful in selecting furnishings for her combination room. She doesn't pick up a curtain or a rug here and there without an eye as to color schemes and harmony. She realizes that harmony is the keynote of good taste, and she never outrages the ar tistic eye. The general tone of the room should be restful and warm, but never loud and gaudy. Brilliant colors should be avoided. Of course, the wood finishings of the room must in no way conflict with\n=== eval-0130\n  first: Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-197 1888-12-06 | A Portrait of The Gar Nicholas\n    From the Duke of Coburg's Recollections The Gar Nicholas was, in fact. the last real autocrat in Europe. I count it among nv most instructive experiences and recollections to have obtained a living picture of the most remarkable man of my time, which was able to supplement and complete from time to time by means OF numerous connections with rela- tives common to us both. In the person of the Emperor Nicholas all vague distractions of state, church and nationality vanished One looked at him and perceived that all the force expressed in these terms was concentrated in his person. Apart from him nothing existed or seemed capable of existing.\n  second: Thomas County cat. [volume] (Colby, Kan.) 1885-189 1889-01-31 | THE CAR NICHOLAS.\n\nA vivid Pen Picture Drawn By Ernes\n\n\nDuke Of C0burg\n    A vivid Pen Picture Drawn By Ernest, Duke Of Coburg. The Car Nicholas was, in fact, the last real autocrat in Europe. / count it among my most instructive expert ences and recollections to have OBJ tanned living picture of the most re- markable man of my time. which l was able to supplement and complete from time to time by means of numerous connections with relatives common to us both. In the person of the Emperor Nicho- las all vague distractions of state. church and nationality vanished. One looked at him and perceived that all the force expressed in these terms was concentrated In his person. Apart from him\n=== eval-0268\n  first: Evansville daily journal. (Evansville, Ind.) 1863- 1864-08-09 | The 40 GoYernmenl Loan.\n    Many Of the advantages of this loan are apparent on their face, but there are others that viii be best understood after consideration Among them there are, It's ABSOLUTE SEcuRlTY Nearly all active credits are now based on Govern ment securities. Banks OF issue aud Savings banks hold them in large guam iities-in many cases more than the en- tire amount OF their capitals-and they hold them as the very best and strong. est investment they could possibly make. If it were possible to contemplate the financial failure Of the Government. no bank would be any better or safer. Sa- wings banks that allow their deposi,ors bat five per cent\n  second: The Ashland union. [volume] (Ashland, Ashland Coun 1864-08-24 | INC ESQ Government Loan.\n    are apparent on their face, but there are others that will be best understood after consideration. Among them there are. It's ABSOLUTE SECURITY.-Nearly all active credits are now based on Govern ment securities. Banks of issue and Sa- wings banks hold them in large quaint ties-ir many cases, more than the entire amount of their capitals-and they bold them as the very best and strongest in vestment they could possibly make. 1t it were possible to contemplate the fnan- cial failure of the Government, no bank would be any better or safer. Savings Banks already have a large part of their assets invested in Government securities. As a ;ule they\n=== eval-0237\n  first: Evening star. 1925-05-24 | \n    351 Memories OF Lincoln. Whitman 2aS Irish Fotk Songs and Tales 4o4 in Memorism Tennyson 427 Poems OF John Keats 4ss Great Yiddish Poetry 497 Poems OF VILLA m Morris sol Poems OF Carew, Sucking. I.oveIace and Herbert SIS Haunted House. Niereck 579 Three Sphinxes Niereck 70l Poems OF Robert Herrick SIS The Lady OF the Lake Scott 61s Poe