{"parentUuid":"6fd40dec-751c-4b6a-aa02-d8618fd65870","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e9ec6031-6d70-425a-a94b-94b6c6cc5b96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012rFs5o2nuMS9VjvugQoxYf","type":"tool_result","content":"24\n=== eval-0253\n  first: Evening capital and Maryland gazette. 1918-10-29 | WHITE LADY\n\n\nTHREATENS KAISER\n\nSpectre OF Ancestor In Old Palace\nAZ Be\n    The famous White Lady of the Hohenzollerns is reported TO have been seen recently in the old palace at Berlin. Does the apparition give warning of the Kaiser's death? He is VIGOROUSLY believer in the mystical and may well be frightened. An American governess of his children (when they were young) has quoted the Crown Princess Cecilie as saying TO her that the Emperor and his wife were \"as superstitious as two old peasants\" The White Lady. according TO the story. was an ancestress OF the Kaiser, the Countess Agnes Won Orlamunde, and the appearance OF her ghost from century\n  second: The Bourbon news. [volume] (Paris, Ky.) 1895-19?? 1918-11-05 | THE WHITE LADY\" THREATENS\n\n\n= KAISER BILL.\n    The famous white Lady, of the Hohenzollerns is reported to have been seen recently in the old palace at Berlin. Does the apparition give warning of the KaiSer's death? He is VIGOROUSLY a believer in the mystical and may well be frightened An American governess of his chill dren (when they were young) has quoted the Crown Princess Cecilie as saying to her that the Emperor and his wife were \"as superstitious as two old peasants\" The White Lady, according to the story, was an ancesstress of the Kaiser and the Countess Agnes Won Orlamunde, and the appearance of her\n=== eval-0261\n  first: Alexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 18 1874-03-26 | \n    pending consideration the Senate adjourned | The House OF Delegates, when quorum was present, was engaged ID discussing the lax bill. ! The Currency. Initiation is The order OF the day. Yesterday the House of Representatives passed the bill to make the legal tender circulation four hun drcd million dollars which legalizes the TWEEN ty-six millions already out without authority OF law and adds eighteen millions to the whole amount. Apparently the Senate will concur, and, of course, the President will sign the bill, for he is without financial policy and has no ground of opposition. He is apparently as\n  second: The New York herald. [volume] (New York [N.Y.]) 18 1874-03-24 | Imitation OF the Currcncy-GOOd News\n\n\nfor the Pnper-Makers.\n    Imitation OF the Currcncy-Good News for the Pnpcr-Makcrs. Imitation is the order Of the day. Yesterday the House of Representatives passed the bill to make the legal tender circulation four hun dred million dollars, which legalizes the twenty-six millions already out without authority of law and adds eighteen millions to the whole amount. Apparently the Senate will concur, and, of course, the President will policy and has no ground of opposition. He is apparently as much at a loss for O financial plan as General Mcclellan once was for military plan; and, without policy of his own inconsistent with the\n=== eval-0290\n  first: St. Tammany farmer. [volume] (Covington, La.) 1874 1900-04-21 | \n    This was always Q pleasant time ol the day. and her uncle loved to lingeI and chat before adjourning to the Ii brary to read his voluminous evening mail. go back to visit Nayence, and Co logne, and Coblentz, and their wonder full art treasures and those queer at. tractive German villages? Spend q month at the Spa? Then to Berlin? \"A very pleasing plan. Why not ex' ecute it?\" Aurore and her uncle sat and planned while Mrs. d'Aubert had gone off and left them and was ensconced by the reading lamp with late magazine And Aurore was eager\n  second: Warren sheaf. [volume] (Warren, Marshall County, M 1900-03-22 | \n    at the anticipated pleasure of tour abroad, as her uncle thought, but in ! the hope of pcstponing that fateful fu- | ture which loomed up so immutably. That future, planned by her father, approved by all, urged so persistently by her dear and loving old aunt, and pleaded for by Robert Horton, with the silent, but passionate, entreaty of eyes that never left her, and by thousand acts of ceaseless devotion, from which her heart turned with a horrible in gratitude, Aurore thought miserably. \"Ah, but, little one, your aunt and I are too old for such junketings,'' her\n=== eval-0276\n  first: Fort Worth daily gazette. (Fort Worth, Tex.) 1882- 1889-07-02 | COTTON WORMS\n    A Rams county farmer had $50 acres of sandy land in cotton last year. He put oil lights IN his field at night and made lao bales of cotton. A neighboI with about the same acreage had no lamps and made seven bales. | think every paper in the state should publish this fact and comment on it.. Lamps cost about thirty-five cents and will burn about two cents worth of oil night. The following from the Paris News will be of interest IN this connection: 'The Becknell Prairie agricultural society, or gained to protect our crops against the ravages\n  second: The Bossier banner. [volume] (Bellevue, Bossier Pa 1889-08-08 | Killing cotton-worm MilIers.\n    The Becknell Prairie Agricultural Society, organized to protect our crops, against the ravages of the cotton worm and to discuss all matters per. tanning to horticulture and to the i'm. provement of stock, now boasts OF \\ membership of about fifty Of our best farmers. All who have put out lights report that they are destroying the parsley worm millers by the thou. sands. Those that sting the YOUNG squares are the most easily caught and l feel assured that 1 have caught the most of these on my cotton land. We are catching some boll-worm mil. lers, and peep\n=== eval-0269\n  first: The Ely miner. [volume] (Ely, Minn.) 1895-1986 1907-12-20 | \n    ever's your advice. SAM he con- cloods, gain anxious-eyed at En- right. If it was nothin' worse than a hostile sheriff on my trail, I'd stand my hand