ht goes off on soothin' angle. Peets chippin in encouragin'. They both suggests to Boggs that thar's no call to be preecipitate It'll most likely be weeks before Missis Rucker really de. Glars herself, an sinks them widowed talons into her selected victim Meanwhile, as preparin' for the worst all Boggs has to do. they argues, is to\n=== eval-0238\n  first: Waterbury evening Democrat. 1894-03-13 | CHAPTER XXI.\n    JOHN FREEMAN CONCLUDES HIS STORY. My story is nearly ended. Next day Ned and Mir. Watkins and myself de- scended into the Devils Parlor.\" We took with us a rock drill, blasting pow der, 8 fuse and po1tmanteau. The reader will probably guess our purpose. It was to break open the tomb of old Curry and appropriate his treasures. I may have had some fear of the ghost, but not enough to prevent me from ri- fling his tomb. We bored, charged and blasted into fragments the big stone which debarred entrance to the little nook where his ashes lay,\n  second: The Irish standard. [volume] (Minneapolis, Minn. ; 1915-07-03 | CHAPTER XXI.\n    JOHN FREEMAN CONCLUDES HIS STORY, My story is nearly ended. Next day Ned and Mr.. Watkins and myself de- scended into the DEVILS Parlor.\" We took with US rock drill, blasting pow der, fuse and po1tmanteau. The reader will probably guess our purpose. It was to break open the tomb of old Curry and appropriate his treasures. I may have had some fear of the ghost, but not enough to prevent me from RI fling bis tomb. We bored, charged and blasted into fragments the big stone which debarred entrance to the little nook where his ashes lay, thereby de-\n=== eval-0397\n  first: Cleveland morning leader. [volume] (Cleveland [Ohi 1864-07-06 | Clothing.\n\nTHE POET ON A TRAIN;\n\n\nl OR, ESQ lfatail\nTHE ROElANCE OF TH\n    - CHAPTER IX.-THE Kiss IN The DARE. After waiting six long hours, l- Aud with hunger almost dead, ---.- News came that the track vas mended -t And the train sodtI went ahead - soon we passed place called Johnstovn, Eilled