r hch IL of the compartments OF dyspepsia and biousness, can test ify TO the misery of mind and body which they in' yolye. The motive power of the system l. partially par ally zed the mind is haunted by anxiety aud fear and the sufferer is incapable OF\n  second: Edgefield advertiser. [volume] (Edgefield, S.C.) 1 1872-08-01 | \n    TRUTH AND PoErRY.-Milton puts in E the mouth of Lucifer, in Paradise Lost these memorable words \"To BE WEAK Is MISERABLE\" Never was tTuer sentence written tha:. shis-supposed to be wrung from tho Arch-fiend in his impotence and agony Every victim of nervous debility, or o; that depressing languor which is one y. the accompaniments of dyspepsia an,: biliousness, can testify 10 the misery :. mind and body which they involve. Th, motive power of the system is PARTIAL, paralyzed; the mind Is haunted by ankle \" and fear; and the sufferer Ia as inca pable OF applying himself eaergeticall\n=== eval-0302\n  first: Clinch Valley news. [volume] (Jeffersonville, Va.) 1911-05-19 | Do Ghosts Haunt Swamps\n    No, never. Its foolish TO fear a fan DIED evil, when there are real aud deadly perils to guard against In swamps and marshes, buyous and low lands. These a c the muIarid germs that cause ngue. clills and fever. weakness, aches in the bones and muscles and may induce deadly ty- phoid. But Electric BItters DESTROYS and casts out these vicious germs from the blood. \"ihree bottles dro e I'll the malaria from my system wrote VIM. Fretwell, OF Lucama. N. C. \"and I've had fire health ever SINCE Use this safe, sure remedy only SOC at J. E,\n  second: Pine Bluff daily graphic. (Pine Bluff, Ark.) 1893- 1911-04-14 | u. n. McNew devotes his time to\nfts. 100 Pine st.\n\nDo GHOSTS HAUNT SWA\n    No, Never. It's foolish to fear a fan cied evil, where there are real and and deadly perils to guard against in swamps and marshes, bayous, and loWlands. These are the malaria\n=== eval-0382\n  first: Alexandria gazette. [volume] (Alexandria, D.C.) 18 1899-09-04 | REDUCED RATES TO FBILADE1pH.\n    bEObNED IA Eo ~ / III Ia DELHI - The National Export Exposition opens st Philsdephis on September 1 4 and CONTINUES until November 30. In addition 10 its vain able commercial exhibits it will present n,su. features of popular interest and amuscmeu The United States Marine Band Sousa'> Band. the Banda Rosss, lnncs's Band. Dan = roschs Orchestra and other celebrated banG, will furnish rnuec. aud Midway Plaisauce. equal II not superior to the famous World'> Fair Midway at Chicsgo, and comprising a Chinese Village, Chinese Theatre. acrobats. and customs; an Oriental Village. Loudo. Ghost Show, Hsgenbock's Wild Animal\n  second: The Mount Holly news. [volume] (Mount Holly, Burli 1899-09-05 | NATIONAL EXPORT EXPOSITION,\n\n\nPHILADELPHIA.\n    Reducted Rates via Pennsylvania Railroad. The National Export Exposition, which opens at Philadelphia on September ID and continues until November 30, will be the most interesting and important event occurring in Philadelphia since the Cen tennial Exhibition of 1876 In addition to its valuable commercial exhibits it will present many features of popular interest and amusement. The United States Ma rine Band, Sousa's Band, the Banda Rosa, lines Band, Damrosch's Orchestra, and other celebrated bands will furnish music alternately, and Midway Plaisance equal if not superior to the famous Worlds Fair Midway at Chicago, and comprising a Chinese Village, Chinese\n=== eval-0487\n  first: The daily union. [volume] (Washington [D.C.]) 1845 1848-11-26 | \n    NEW MUsIC.--Just received at the old-es,ablished ~ store, first door of 18th street. the following music, which will be sold onethrd less than the regular rice, making it only cents page - | want beau music by w. J. Lentcr In the lonely grove lnger; by E L White Oh! home OF my boyhood; by s. BrcEer The silver moon ; by b. s. Burditte. Voices from the spirit land by Metcalf. The merry -heared soldier; by Turner Prepare we, nymphs Tor three va,ces. Win h