{"parentUuid":"a07bf59a-0469-4838-b5b4-ccd2cf62e41c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e9ec6031-6d70-425a-a94b-94b6c6cc5b96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HXFeGqmBYYzfUpU7vbH6dm","type":"tool_result","content":"=== eval-0290\n  first: St. Tammany farmer. [volume] (Covington, La.) 1874 1900-04-21 | \n    This was always Q pleasant time ol the day. and her uncle loved to lingeI and chat before adjourning to the Ii brary to read his voluminous evening mail. go back to visit Nayence, and Co logne, and Coblentz, and their wonder full art treasures and those queer at. tractive German villages? Spend q month at the Spa? Then to Berlin? \"A very pleasing plan. Why not ex' ecute it?\" Aurore and her uncle sat and planned while Mrs. d'Aubert had gone off and left them and was ensconced by the reading lamp with late magazine And Aurore was eager and radiant, not at the anticipated pleasure of tour abroad, as her uncle thought, but in the hope of pcstponing that fateful flu ture which loomed up SO immutably That future, planned by her father approved by all, urged SO persistently by her dear and loving old aunt, and pleaded for by Robert Horton, with the silent. but passionate, entreaty of eyes that never left her, and BY a Thousand acts of ceaseless devotion, from which her heart turned with horrible in. gratitude, Aurore thought miserably. \"Ah, but, little one, your aunt and are too old for such junketings,\" her uncle resumed, with a shake of the head. You must listen to good advice and let Robert be your guide and conn panion. We will join you in the fall in London or Paris, and settle down to a pleasant winter of enjoyment-such a winter as your youth and beauty de- serves\" With loving gesture he went to- ward the library in search Of his mail, and Aurore, with a sigh, passed out on the porch. Aurore sat on the marble steps and looked out over the moonlit garden, and determined to have it out once for all with her innermost thoughts. and to come to a distinct understanding of all those chaotic, troublous feelings which had SO insidiously taken possession of her. The faint chimes of the village church bell came on the still, odorous air, ome'fireflies went fitting through the Towering shrubs. and a profound melancholy took possession of Aurore a yearning she could not define, while reason asserted itself with cold insistence. .You do not even know him. You saw him for one short half hour, that memorable night when wind and storm were abroad: thatGyinter night, whose dawn severed the Tail thread Of life so dear\n  second: Warren sheaf. [volume] (Warren, Marshall County, M 1900-03-22 | \n    at the anticipated pleasure of tour abroad, as her uncle thought, but in ! the hope of pcstponing that fateful fu- | ture which loomed up so immutably. That future, planned by her father, approved by all, urged so persistently by her dear and loving old aunt, and pleaded for by Robert Horton, with the silent, but passionate, entreaty of eyes that never left her, and by thousand acts of ceaseless devotion, from which her heart turned with a horrible in gratitude, Aurore thought miserably. \"Ah, but, little one, your aunt and I are too old for such junketings,'' her uncle resumed, with a shake of the head. \"You must listen to good advice and let Robert be your guide and COM panion. We will join you in the fall in London or Paris, and settle down to a pleasant winter of enjoyment such a winter as your youth and beauty de- serves ......-. ......- With a loving gesture he went to- ward the library in search of his mail, and Aurore, with a sigh, passed out on the porch. Aurore sat on the marble steps and looked out over the moonlit garden, and determined to have it out once for | all with her innermost thoughts, and to | come to distinct understanding of all those chaotic, troublous feelings which ! had so insidiously taken possession of her. The faint chimes of the village church bell came on the still, odorous air, some firefies went fitting through the Towering shrubs, and a profound melancholy took possession of Aurore \"a yearning she. could not define. ! while reason asserted itself with cold insistence. 'You do not even know him. You saw him for one short half hour, that memorable night when wind and storm were abroad; that winter night, whose dawn severed the frail thread of life | SO dear to you. \"How do you know\" said reason, ''that he remembers you? That he has given you single thought? \"Because he looked full and keenly and long, your vanity has supposed | that there was interest and admira- ton in a glance only remarkable be- cause it came from eyes of singular and splendid forcefulness. \"You must, you must thrust this silly recollection from heart and brain. You must get rid of it. Bury it under cares, ambitions, anything! And rub out from memory the lines of that strong, fine","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"048b0b54-7038-4297-b5b2-f4b3059235f6","timestamp":"2026-10-31T02:12:55.273Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== eval-0290\n  first: St. Tammany farmer. [volume] (Covington, La.) 1874 1900-04-21 | \n    This was always Q pleasant time ol the day. and her uncle loved to lingeI and chat before adjourning to the Ii brary to read his voluminous evening mail. go back to visit Nayence, and Co logne, and Coblentz, and their wonder full art treasures and those queer at. tractive German villages? Spend q month at the Spa? Then to Berlin? \"A very pleasing plan. Why not ex' ecute it?\" Aurore and her uncle sat and planned while Mrs. d'Aubert had gone off and left them and was ensconced by the reading lamp with late magazine And Aurore was eager and radiant, not at the anticipated pleasure of tour abroad, as her uncle thought, but in the hope of pcstponing that fateful flu ture which loomed up SO immutably That future, planned by her father approved by all, urged SO persistently by her dear and loving old aunt, and pleaded for by Robert Horton, with the silent. but pass