the general practice to 'Glorify Cold Are we then in this twentieth century to revert to the barbar- Gus worship of Mammon? Are Americans to renOunce their Christian heritage? Are they to repudiate the Hebrew law of\n=== eval-0509\n  first: Indiana State sentinel. 1845-11-13 | \n    MARvELLoUs-A French Sdrdnt, at Dion, went one night quite exhausted to bed after long and vain ef forts to make out the sense of passage In Greek poet On falling asleep, he seemed TO himself to be transported in spirit to Stockholm where he was con- Quoted into the palace of Queen Christina usheTed n to the royal library, and placed before compartment in which he distinguished a small volume that bore title new to him. He opened the volume, and found the solution of the grammatical difficulty which bad perpexed him. The Joy which he felt at this Disco\n  second: Kosciusko chronicle. 1846-02-21 | vi M0rvelIOllS.\n    L. night, quite exhausted 10 bed, after lon, and vain efforts 10 make out the sense d a passage in a Greek poet. On fallns asleep, he seemed TO himself to be trans ported in spirit To Stockholm, where he was conducted INTO the palace Of Queer Chris,ana, nshered' inlu the royal libra, Try, and placed before compartment ii which he distinguished small volume ,ha. bore title new 10 him. He opened the volume, and found in il the sulutior nf the grammatical difficulty which had so perplexed him. The joy which he felt AZ this discovery awaking him, he\n=== eval-0539\n  first: The San Francisco call. 1898-02-06 | THE DAYS OF FoRTY-NINE.\n    Brave days of long ago, how memories ebb and flow, Like bubbles on youths wine The days when men were strong in hope and zeal and song. Those days of Forty-Nine. We toiled where nuggest gleamed, where waters foamed and streamed. An, that was life divinei We joined in bout and fray-shook hands, that was the way, In days of FortyNine. But now through yelling tears I scan those misty years, Those years of yours and mine, Which through Times open door have slowly passed before. Those years of Forty-Nine. And from that silent ghost comes rAany . wandering ghost,\n  second: Wadsworth semi-weekly dispatch. [volume] (Wadswort 1900-09-14 | Sam Davis Latest Poer,.\n    Sam Davis has written anotb.r pretty poem; this time in commerno- ration Of The Californian golden jubilee. \" appears in the Carson Appeal, which says: The Appeal desires to throw this shall spray of green over the range. Brave days of long ago; how memories ebb and flow Like bubbles on youths wine N days. when men were strong, and right controlled the wrong The days of Forty-nine. We toiled where nuggets cleaned. where waters foamed and steamed All, that was life divine. We joined in bout and fray-shook hands, that was the way In days of Forty-nine. But now,\n=== eval-0513\n  first: The Penn's Grove record. 1885-09-26 | SCIENTIFIC.\n    The remarkable arrangement for breathing which insects possess Is thus lucidly described II we take any moderately large insect, say p wasp OF a hornet, We can see, even with the naked eye, that a series Of small, spot like marks run along either side of the body. These apparent spots, which are generally eighteen or twenty in number, are, in fact, the apertures through which air ls admitted into the sy tenn, and are generally formed 10 such a manner that no %extraneous mat ter can by any possibility find entrance. Sometimes they are furnished with o pair of\n  second: The Anderson intelligencer. [volume] (Anderson Cou 1885-06-25 | How an Insect Breathes.\n    If we take any moderately large insect, say wasp or hornet, we can see, even with the naked eye, that series of small, j, spot.lik marks run along either side of the body. These apparent spots, which arc generally eighteen or twenty in num- ber, are, in fact, the apcrtures through which air is admitted into the system, and are generally found in such man ner that no extraneous matter can by any possibility find entrance. Sometimes they are furnished with pair of horny lips, which can be opened and closed at the will Of the insect; in other cases","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9e1f546e-8862-4084-b6e9-3c161b2f5b84","timestamp":"2026-10-31T02:13:03.807Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"37\n=== eval-0433\n  first: The Pulaski citizen. [volume] (Pulaski, Tenn.) 186 1885-01-15 | EVERY YEAR\n\nBY ALBERT PIKE.\n\nNUMBER \".\n    Life is o count of losses Every year Every year Lost with sobs replying, Unto weary autumn's sighing, While those We love are dying, Every year. The days have lees of gladness, Every year; The nights more weight of sadness, Every year Fair springs no longer cbarm US; The win DIS aad weather harm us, The threats of death alarm US, Every year. There comes new cares and SORROWS, Every year. Dark days and darker morows, Every year The ghosts OF changed friends taunt us And disappoiutments daunt US, Every year. To the past go more dead faces, And the\n  second: Reporter and farmer. [volume] (Webster, Day County 1911-01-26 | n. =. DENTON, Editor.\n\nTHURSDAY. JANUARY 70, 1911.\n\n\"Every Year,\n    'LNCN5 i1dt The Spring has less of brightness, Every year; And the snow a gbastlier whiteness, Every year; Nor do Summer flowers quicken, Nor Autumn's fruitage thicken, As they once did, for they sicken, Every year. Life is a court Of losses, Every year; For the weak are heavier crosses, Every year; Lost Spring, with sobs replying, Unto weary Autumn's signing, While those we love are dying, Every year. 1t is growing darker, colder, Every year; As the heart and soul grow older, Every year; j care not now for dancing, Or for eyes with passion glancing, Love is less\n=== eval-0485\n  first: The Fremont weekly journal. [volume] (Fremont, San 1870-01-21 | \n    nsYCHOMANCY FASCINATION OR SOUL L CHARAiING.-400 pages; cloth. This wonder full book has full instructions to enable the reader t0 fascinate either sex, or any animal, at will Mesmerism, Spiritualism, and hundreds of other curious experiments. 1t be obtained by sending ad