the President was correct tuntil the contrary appeared) when he said that it had been made on basis honorable and just to both parties. He greatly mistook the temper and sense OF the American people If they would toler- ate any discussion OF the pending treaty\n=== eval-0062  [per candidate; agent: close — Heney lead paragraphs match verbatim, but later paragraphs are reworded and diverge; borderline.; sim [13, 0.2281, 0.1806]]\n  first: The Richmond Virginian. (Richmond, Va.) 1910-1920 1913-09-28 | HENEY FLOPS BACK\n\n\nINTO c. 0.P. RANKS\n\nCause Whya Cause Heney\nWants Jo\n    WASHINGTON. Sept 27.--\"The bot movement I'm going to climb aboard the Republican band wagon\" This--in Effects the remark attrieu- ted 10 Francis ]. Heney, the man whose red-hot speech at Chicago In favor of Roosevelt will be remembered as long as politics live in America Heney. of California. who, In the strenuous days Of the 1912 presidential campaign. worked day and night for the bull moose, whose red bandana Haunted side by side with Roosevelt, is now ready TO run for the United States senate from the State OF California ana. &C cording TO California congressman who were interviewed at the Capitol today. believes he will stand no chance OF elec\n\n... [3419 characters truncated] ...\n\neese).; sim [17, 0.1189, 0.1156]]\n  first: The Daily state journal. [volume] (Alexandria, Va. 1872-09-17 | \n    SEPTENBga lS, l672 APPIES-Creen 81 .5Oa2 90. tf ENGLISH ALE.--Pints 1925; quarts. 1870 BARK.--oak Spanish, Chestnut, and Black SIS uer cord nominal and no sales. Turkey Oak Bark brings bnt the price Of lire wood. Market very dull BUTTER.-Prime 70 choice yellow Virginia, 2j TO q cents eommonto good, l0a20c Receipts more liberal. Prime very scarce CANDLES.-TalfoW J3hal4 cents per pound; Adamantiue, Il ca, l13sal6 cents; l6 o., q COFFEE java sSc., Rio far to good, 22ka 22c.; prime to choice, 22Na23c Laguayra, 23'Se.; CORN MEAL-ci,y Mills, bolted, go cents; CHT;tSF-Northern and Western prime cut.