{"parentUuid":"869b69ba-fcd9-4f8e-bce0-f182c0d56452","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e9ec6031-6d70-425a-a94b-94b6c6cc5b96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bd5pMobzm8FEHHZEcrgH42","type":"tool_result","content":"=== eval-0319  [large cluster, least similar pair; agent: different — Both mention the Seneca freed-bird custom, in different words, inside otherwise unrelated articles.; sim [0, 0.0, 0.0]]\n  first: Wheeling times and advertiser. [volume] (Wheeling, 1845-05-14 | \n    THE FREED BIRD.-Among the super. stitions of the Senecas, IS one which, for its singular beauty, is already well known. When a maiden dies, they imprison a young bird I'M TIL IT first begins to try its powers of song, and then, loading it with kisses and caresses. they loose It's bonds over her grave, in the belief that it\n  second: Evening star. [volume] (Washington, D.C.) 1854-197 1905-07-14 | Omens Of Death.\n    But some of the most interesting super stitions are connected with death. In some country districts of England and Germany there is an idea that If the bees swarm upon a rotten tree there will be a death in the family owning or living on the property within year. In Greenland, when a child dies, the natives bury live dog\n=== eval-0321  [large cluster, least similar pair; agent: different — Share one stanza of 'Over the River'; second is death notices quoting it, name changed.; sim [0, 0.0, 0.0]]\n  first: Fremont journal. [volume] (Fremont, Sandusky Count 1858-12-10 | 0ottrn.\n\nFrom the Western Episcopalian.\nOVER THE RIVER.\n    fro\" the western tp'se\"pa''an' OVER THE RIVER Over tho river they bcckon TO me, Loved ones who've crossed to the further SIDE, The CLEAN orthcir snowy robes Sec But their voices arc los' IN the dash'hg uoe' There's one with ringle's OF sunny goln' And eyes the rejection oi he\"Fen own own o'ue' And rale mist hid him from mortal\n  second: The Kenosha telegraph. [volume] (Kenosha, Wis.) 18 1861-07-18 | E9EEk3.\n