ec73cf","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"17765fc1-0dea-4c40-a4b5-89be8f94968b","timestamp":"2026-10-31T02:15:56.754Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"17765fc1-0dea-4c40-a4b5-89be8f94968b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e9ec6031-6d70-425a-a94b-94b6c6cc5b96","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016CB4ycs5GBs874PnWJwNp7","type":"tool_result","content":"=== eval-0336  [large cluster, least similar pair; agent: close — Both quote Ingersoll's 'march proudly away' sentence; second's shown text stops there, so further overlap unseen.; sim [0, 0.0, 0.0]]\n  first: Mineral Point tribune. (Mineral Point, Wis.) 1869- 1878-06-05 | Eloquent Bob Ing eESoU.\n    A man who can speak of the fallen heroes of the war as Robert IngeI soll does, cannot be wholly bad. What touching pathos in these lines: \"We see them all as they march proudly away under the Haunting flags, keeping time to the wild, grand music of war-Marching down the streets of the great cities-through the towns and across the praries- down to the fields Of glory, to do and to die for the eternal right. These heroes are dead. They died for liberty-they died for US. They are at rest. They sleep in the land they made free, under the flag they rendered stainless, under the solemn pines, and sad hemlocks, the fearful willows, and the embracing vines. They sleep beneath the shadows of the clouds, careless alike of the sun slime or of storm, each in his win dowless palace of rest. Earth may run red with other wars-they are at peace. In the midst of battle, in the roar of conflict, they fonnd tho terenity of death. l have one sen. timent for the soldiers living and dead-cheers for the living aud tears for the dead\" .\n  second: Jackson County journal. (Sylva, N.C.) 19??-19?? 1914-09-18 | INGERSOLLS VISlOY OF WAR.\n    Extracts From Speech Delivered in 1875. The past rises before me like a dream. Again we are in the great struggle for national life. We hear the sounds of preparation; the music Of boisterous drums, the silver voices of heroic bugles. We see thousands of assemblages and hear the appeals of orators. We see pale cheeks of women and flushed faces of men, and in those assemblages we see all the dead whose dust we we have covered with flowers. We lose sight of them no more. We are with them when they enlist in the great army of freedom. We see them part with those they love. Some are walking for their last time in quiet, woody places with the maidens they adore. We hear the whisperings and the sweet VOWS Of eternal love as they lingeringly part for ever. Others are bending over cradles, kissing babies that are asleep. Some are receiving the blessings at old men. Some are parting with mothers who hold them and press them to their hearts again and again and say nothing Kisses and tears, tears and kisses divine mingling of agony and love! And some are talking with wives and endeavoring with brave words, spoken in the old tones, to drive from their hearts the awful fear. We see them part. We see the wife standing in the door with the babe in her arms standing in the sunlight sobbing. At the turn in the road a hand waves she answers by holding high in her arms the child. He is gone, AMI forever! We see them as they march proudly away under the Haunting flags, keeping time to the grand wild music of war-marching down","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"24214a8f-8a0b-4219-b031-e8078ff5d50d","timestamp":"2026-10-31T02:15:57.481Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== eval-0336  [large cluster, least similar pair; agent: close — Both quote Ingersoll's 'march proudly away' sentence; second's shown text stops there, so further overlap unseen.; sim [0, 0.0, 0.0]]\n  first: Mineral Point tribune. (Mineral Point, Wis.) 1869- 1878-06-05 | Eloquent Bob Ing eESoU.\n    A man who can speak of the fallen heroes of the war as Robert IngeI soll does, cannot be wholly bad. What touching pathos in these lines: \"We see them all as they march proudly away under the Haunting flags, keeping time to the wild, grand music of war-Marching down the streets of the great cities-through the towns and across the praries- down to the fields Of glory, to do and to die for the eternal right. These heroes are dead. They died for liberty-they died for US. They are at rest. They sleep in the land they made free, under the flag they rendered stainless, under the solemn pines, and sad hemlocks, the fearful willows, and the embracing vines. They sleep beneath the shadows of the clouds, careless alike of the sun slime or of storm, each in his win dowless palace of rest. Earth may run red with other wars-they are at peace. In the midst of battle, in the roar of conflict, they fonnd tho terenity of death. l have one sen. timent for the soldiers living and dead-cheers for the living aud tears for the dead\" .\n  second: Jackson County journal. (Sylva, N.C.) 19??-19?? 1914-09-18 | INGERSOLLS VISlOY OF WAR.\n    Extracts From Speech Delivered in 1875. The past rises before me like a dream. Again we are in the great struggle for national life. We hear the sounds of preparation; the music Of boisterous drums, the silver voices of heroic bugles. We see thousands of assemblages and hear the appeals of orators. We see pale cheeks of women and flushed faces of men, and in those assemblages we see all the dead whose dust we we have covered with flowers. We lose sight of them no more. We are with them when they enlist in the great army of freedom. We see them part with those they love. Some are walking for their last time in quiet, woody places with the maidens they adore. We hear the whisperings and the sweet VOWS Of eternal love as they lingeringly part for ever. Others are bending over cradles, kissing babies that are asleep. Some are receiving the blessings at old men. Some are parting with mothers who hold them and press them to their hearts again and again and say nothing Kisses and tears, tears and kisses divine mingling of agony and love! And some are talking with wives and endeavoring with brave words, spoken in the old tones, to drive from their hearts the awful fear. We see them part. We see the wife standing in the door with the babe in her arms standing in the sunlight sobbing. At the turn in the road a hand waves she answers by holding high in her arms the child. He is gone, AMI forever! We see them as they march proudly away under the Haunting flags, keeping time to the grand wild music of war-marching down","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"0e742075-ae8c-46cb-8e57-d67d5d747e63","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"phase-2b-data-layer","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":2,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":4},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"17765fc1-0dea-4c40-a4b5-89be8f94968b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2b-data-layer","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
