ccused of stealing a pair of gold-bowed spectacles, and spoke re- vengefully of her accusers. pistol shot, and upon fore\n=== dev-0030  [per candidate; agent: close — Second is a single notice that also sits inside the first's multi-item local column; only that line shared.; sim [3, 1.0, 0.0577]]\n  first: Daily camera. [volume] (Boulder, Colo.) 1891-1928 1899-10-24 | \n    singers now with the Hottest Coon in Dixie\" was for sixteen conse cutive weeks the rage al the New York Casino Roof garden. They were also seen for eight consecutive weeks in Chicago. and have appeared in all the important cities of the country between New York and San Francisco. Remember the Phantom party. Get your fortune told at the Phantom party October 18th. 23s, Try Aurole toilet soap, P. M Nelson & Son. I8u The Hottest Coon in Dixie\" company will appear at the Temple hall next Fri day\n  second: Daily camera. [volume] (Boulder, Colo.) 1891-1928 1899-10-25 | \n    Don't forget sheet and pillow case when you 60 TO the Phantom party DC tober 27th. a,u\n=== dev-0044  [near the threshold; agent: close — Shared Scribner's blurb verbatim for about 150 words, then each column diverges; intro and later items differ.; sim [19, 0.2436, 0.2065]]\n  first: Delaware gazette and state journal. (Wilmington, D 1899-08-31 | \n    Scribner's for September has a num- Der of articles with an outdoor flavor to them. 1t opens with an account by Frederic Irland of what he calls \"the finest canoeing country in the world. - He made 800 mile journey from Mattawa to the headwaters of the Ottawa and Gatineu Rivers, through a region Astounding in fish and moose. Ii has been for centuries and is now the home of the Algonquin Indian. The region through which Mr.. Irland tray eled with Algonquins for guides is the best sportsman's haunt\n  second: The Portland daily press. 1899-09-01 | THE MAGAZINES.\n    Soribner's for September opens with an account by Frederic Irland of what he calls \"the finest canoeing country in the world\" He made five hundred mile journey from Mattawa at the headwaters OF the Ottawa and Gatineau Rivers, through a region Astounding In fish and moose. 1t has been for centuries ana is now the home of the Algonquin Indian. The region through which MIR. Irland travelled with Algonq uins for guides is the best sportsman's haunt left in America. Much of the wilderness is as yet uncharted. The illustrations\n=== dev-0062  [near the threshold; agent: close — Cleveland wedding: benediction and Folsom sentence shared verbatim; otherwise first has vows and dress, second has departure.; sim [31, 0.369, 0.2348]]\n  first: Jamestown weekly alert. [volume] (Jamestown, Stuts 1886-06-03 | NUMBER 81.\n    come now to enter. If any now can show just cause why they may not be united ln marriage let him now speak or else here after hold his peace. To bride and groom\" If you desire to be united in marriage you will JOIN your right hands (groom ard bride join hands) \"Grover,\" said the minister, edo you take this woman whom you hold by the hand to be your lawful welded wife, to live together after Gods ordinance in the holy estate Of wedlock, ds you promise to\n  second: New Ulm weekly review. [volume] (New Ulm, Minn.) 1 1886-06-09 | \n    God the Father. God the Son, and God the Holy Ghost, bless, preserve and keep you; the Lord mercifully fill you with all temporal and spiritual blessings, and grant that you may so live together in this world that in the world to come you may have life everlasting. Amen. At the conclusion of the ceremony Mrs. Folsom, showing traces of deep emotion, was the first to tender her concentrations to the newly married pair She was fol- lowed by Miss Cleveland, the Rev. Mr.. Cleve- land and the other","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4a250ac2-6219-43a9-bd1a-3649057c57c2","timestamp":"2026-10-31T02:20:49.861Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"138 judged; missing from the plan: []\ndev-0018: words 81 and 177; of the first's runs in the second 0.53, of the secon