{"parentUuid":"4fbfc124-9d9a-4c70-8beb-42f2996bd75a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"85c09507-e1dc-4ee2-9bb2-d7c89f179db7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y2oq3M3GFY3HzL8M29D6ME","type":"tool_result","content":"== eval A reports\n  21_1854-11-16_p1_sn83021169_00514152792_1854111601_0398.json 1854 ['ghost'] | a voice in the house taken for Phie's ghost, a trapped man whom searchers on the roof reach with a rope\n  1_1875-10-08_p1_sn84023963_0020029595A_1875100801_0921.json 1875 ['ghost', 'haunt'] | the ghost of an Englishwoman put to death for witchcraft, believed to haunt the spot\n  11_1878-10-12_p1_sn83030272_00206533511_1878101201_0896.json 1878 ['ghost', 'spook'] | spirit communications from Vanderbilt's dead wife through the medium Foster, in will-case testimony\n  6_1904-05-28_p1_sn84020358_00271744432_1904052801_1153.json 1904 ['spectre'] | a defendant in court points at a spectre, which the paper calls imaginary\n  1_1911-07-27_pna_sn92053934_00202198326_1911072701_0413.json 1911 ['apparition', 'ghost'] | Anne Boleyn's headless coach at Blickling, on the anniversary of her execution\n  6_1911-12-15_pna_sn86058226_00271744924_1911121501_0691.json 1911 ['ghost'] | lights moving in a cemetery, followed by a party that set out to investigate\n  14_1916-12-09_p5_sn84024055_00280761072_1916120901_0525.json 1916 ['ghost', 'haunt', 'spook'] | the American legation in Peking haunted by the shade of an officer killed in the Boxer massacres\n== 2A.8 added: reports and close calls\n  17_1857-07-29_pna_sn83025667_00280777808_1857072901_0724.json 1857 True report full | 1857, Middlebury: the paper's reporter attends a ten-cent séance at Union Hall where a spirit named Judd speaks through a trance medium from Massachus\n  1_1857-04-02_p1_sn83025802_00295862026_1857040201_0270.json 1857 True close full | Borderline. 1857: the end of the Picaud ('real Monte Cristo') narrative, printed as true. The dying Allut tells his confessor he has 'seen and spoken \n  11_1858-11-25_p3_sn83030213_00206530601_1858112501_0379.json 1858 False close full | 1858, New York: a long letter sorting mediums' phenomena into tricks and a few genuine marvels, in general, with no particular sitting.\n  1_1859-08-11_p1_sn82014511_no_reel_1859081101_0001.json 1859 True report full | 1859, Plymouth, Mass.: at a spiritualist convention its president says Washington's and Lafayette's spirits went through a Masonic rehearsal in his pr\n  2_1869-10-23_p1_sn84026005_00280776403_1869102301_0548.json 1869 True report full | 1869, Muskingum County, Ohio: a spiritualist family say the spirit world has told them each will inherit $50,000 from a Jersey City estate, and one ha\n  17_1872-01-30_p1_sn84022046_00279555211_1872013001_0104.json 1872 False close full | 1872, from a Vermont paper: an elder's daughter, a writing medium, prays to know spiritualism's parent and writes 'The Devil' in trance. A moral joke \n  2_1873-09-01_pna_sn87062253_00271765186_1873090101_0288.json 1873 True report full | 1873, Wilmington: an account of a spiritualists' excursion whose circle materialized spirits: a Quaker lady's dead brother, a young woman dead of cons\n  5_1873-06-15_p1_sn83016555_00295875549_1873061501_0198.json 1873 False close full | 1873, New Orleans: the paper's reporter has a medium raise the 'shade' of Pelagie Brown about a suit against the Picayune, and she answers in dialect.\n  4_1875-03-29_pna_sn84022473_00280777559_1875032901_0300.json 1875 True report full | 1875, Rutland: a letter describes the Thursday's séance at the Eddys' in Chittenden, where fourteen spirits materialized in a lighted room and some sp\n  7_1880-09-18_p2_sn88064592_00280761862_1880091801_0568.json 1880 False close full | 1880, Arkansas: a fugitive murderer dies before his capture. His wife is a medium, but no spirit message is told.\n  38_1883-02-15_pna_sn85025620_00212479330_1883021501_0023.json 1883 False close full | 1883, Worthington: a letter against spiritualism. The writer has seen many 'spirit manifestations' but describes none.\n  40_1888-12-05_pna_sn90059522_00175033373_1888120501_0403.json 1888 False close full | 1888, St. Paul: Tennyson's letter on a waking trance in which his individuality dissolves, which the paper calls spiritualism. A mystical state, not a\n  7_1890-02-21_pna_sn91068075_00296022883_1890022101_0179.json 1890 False close full | 1890: a news digest item. Spiritualists are arrested in Chicago for obtaining money under false pretences, with no séance or manifestation told.\n  9_1891-08-11_p1_sn82014635_00237286728_1891081101_0546.json 1891 False close full | 1891, Economy, Pa.: a woman claiming to be a spiritualist preaches a hollow-earth doctrine to the Harmony Society. No spirit communication is told.\n  22_1893-03-25_p1_sn83016672_00415668089_1893032501_0541.json 1893 False close full | 1893, Grand Rapids: an unreadable letter disputing a charge of fraud against a medium's séances. No phenomena can be read.\n  27_1893-03-30_p1_sn96076998_00415669252_1893033001_1077.json 1893 False close full | 1893: a squib about a spiritualist periodical claiming exclusive communications from Shakespeare's spirit. A joke, with no communication told.\n  1_1895-05-24_pna_sn97071028_00416152664_1895052401_0955.json 1895 True close full | 1895, Waterloo, Oregon: a letter describes the Sunday's spiritualist meeting at Yew Park, where a man spoke under the control of Sitting Bull's spirit\n  16_1897-01-02_p7_sn84024442_211103073_1897010201_0249.json 1897 False close full | 1897, Alexandria: a woman in a trance under a spiritualist doctor's hypnotism prophesies an eruption and a tidal wave. A trance prophecy, with no spir\n  4_1898-01-12_pna_sn82015104_00280768790_1898011201_0105.json 1898 True report full | 1898, Sacramento: John Slater's test séance of the night before, with its 'manifestations': the reading of sealed ballots and answers to sitters. A me\n  21_1902-11-24_pna_sn84024441_100492052_1902112401_0152.json 1902 False close full | 1902, Old Bridge, N.J.: at a 'second sight seance' a hypnotized woman names a murderer in the audience. Stage clairvoyance under hypnosis, with no spi\n  14_1903-07-09_p1_sn85053117_00295860431_1903070901_0527.json 1903 False close full | 1903, Montana: a jailed man accused of a shooting says he is a spiritualist who can hear spirits, and the paper jokes it was an evil spirit he heard. \n  3_1907-02-23_p1_sn86076224_00332890414_1907022301_0155.json 1907 True no full | Borderline. 1907, Winnemucca: an editorial on psychical research reports that members of the Society for Psychical Research claim the dead Professor H\n  5_1908-10-29_pna_sn86063790_00295863018_1908102901_0063.json 1908 False close full | 1908: the canonization of St. Seraphim and the Tsar's heir. Prayer to a saint, which is religious, with no spirit manifestation.\n  17_1913-06-27_p4_sn85038485_0034058367A_1913062701_1167.json 1913 False close full | 1913, Birmingham: Dickens's quip that the deaf Bulwer-Lytton always claimed to hear Home's raps. A literary jibe about vanity, with no particular sitt\n  44_1917-08-04_pna_sn83045211_0023728768A_1917080401_0238.json 1917 False close full | 1917, Philadelphia: a porch conversation, fiction, about the ouija board and Napoleon's Oraculum. Patience Worth is named in passing.\n  17_1919-06-18_pna_sn84022374_00332895400_1919061801_0622.json 1919 False close full | 1919: Agnes Repplier's quip on Patience Worth's 'This be nuff'. A literary joke.\n  15_1920-07-19_p1_sn85025007_00415664175_1920071901_0261.json 1920 True report full | 1920, Trenton: an officer of the First Spiritualist Church says Abraham Lincoln's spirit sent a message that Cox will be elected. A claimed spirit mes\n  12_1922-04-14_p2_sn85042243_00212478854_1922041401_0708.json 1922 True report full | 1922, New York: Conan Doyle, landing for his tour, says he has many times spoken with his dead son Kingsley at séances. A first-person claim of commun\n  38_1922-05-02_