{"parentUuid":"d8d95c70-6d54-4ae8-98d9-603fee8766b5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"22277250-1783-45bc-9d5b-e32f754ab6c5","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<tools-used-before-summary>Agent, Bash, Edit, Monitor, Read, SendMessage, Skill, TaskStop, ToolSearch, WebSearch, Write, mcp__Claude_Browser__browser_batch, mcp__Claude_Browser__computer, mcp__Claude_Browser__find, mcp__Claude_Browser__get_page_text, mcp__Claude_Browser__javascript_tool, mcp__Claude_Browser__navigate, mcp__ccd_pr__get_status, mcp__ccd_session__mark_chapter, mcp__ccd_turn__update_plan, mcp__ccd_turn__wrap_up</tools-used-before-summary>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n\n**Setup.**\n- This is a child session of the EM session `local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e`, on the Paper Ghosts project: repo `Hotragn/Paperghosts`, at `/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts`.\n- Hand-over notes go to `/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-X.md`.\n- Every turn ends with `mcp__ccd_turn__wrap_up` and a closing message. Use `mcp__ccd_turn__update_plan` for the task list.\n\n**Standing rules (verbatim where possible):**\n- \"never print, log or echo secrets from `.env`, and check keys by shape only\"\n- \"No global installs and no Homebrew\"\n- \"Stop and ask before anything public (repo visibility, deploys, DNS, posting) or any spending beyond free tiers and existing credits\"\n- \"Make small commits on phase branches and open pull requests into `main`; I merge them\"\n- \"Record numbered decisions in `Docs/DECISIONS.md`, and end each milestone with a STATUS block in `Docs/RESUME.md`\"\n- \"Entire capture is enabled through `.claude/settings.json`; don't touch it\"\n- \"Don't run brainstorming Q&A with the owner; put questions in your reply to me\"\n- \"Start every reply to me with `<step>: DONE` or `<step>: BLOCKED — reason`, then 150 words at most\". For 2C.1 the first line is `2C.1: GATE PASSED` or `2C.1: BLOCKED — <reason>`.\n- Scope pgrep/ps to my uid and never print the hostname. Filter push output with `grep -v -i -F \"$(hostname -s)\"`.\n- Before every commit, scan the staged diff with `uv run python $S/scan_staged.py .env <diff>` (names only). Count machine-name matches with `grep -c -i -F \"$(hostname -s)\" <diff>`, and read the scan output before committing.\n- Library of Congress requests:\n  - one at a time, never in parallel\n  - each cached with its URL, date and status\n  - www.loc.gov (the JSON API) at least 3.5 s apart; tile.loc.gov stays at 1 s (the EM's call)\n  - a CAPTCHA (HTML) answer counts as busy and is never cached\n- \"Never touch sample_mflix or any other database\"\n- \"check claims like 'pushed' with `ls-remote` before you report them\"\n- Commits end with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. PR bodies end with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`.\n- Scratch: `S=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad`.\n- The EM said: \"Commit and push at each natural point, so a limit costs nothing.\"\n- The EM said: \"Stop for my review before you label anything for gold.\" No PR until 2C closes.\n\n**Current task: 2C.1, the gold set's design** (persona: ML specialist), on branch `phase-2c-reader`, branched from main at e350721. Its parts:\n- `eval/gold/POLICY.md` before any draw or label (done).\n- Reproduce the EM's 400-word check in a committed script (done).\n- Record the EM's calls: a note in decision 36 (done), and decision 37 (pending).\n- The inclusive grouping (call B) and `scripts/check_leakage.py` (RDR-4), with its threshold justified on decision 36's labelled pairs.\n- The seeded draw of 150–300 gold items:\n  - stratified by decade and by each of lexicon v2's five rules, with near misses\n  - sized for enough reports for field-level intervals, stating the expected count from eval A's rates\n  - each item's inclusion weight recorded\n  - gold, dev and the train pool disjoint by B's grouping\n  - the leakage check passing\n  - dev's size and labeller stated\n  - `split.json` and `HASH` committed, after POLICY.md in git order\n- A local labelling page:\n  - OCR text beside crops via `crops_for`, with POLICY's rules at hand\n  - RDR-2's fields with quote capture\n  - a test that it serves a page and saves a label\n- Plan and STATUS fixes.\n- **Gate:**\n  - tests and lint pass\n  - git order shows POLICY.md before the draw\n  - the hash is committed and the leakage check passes\n  - decision 36's note and decision 37 are written\n  - pushed and checked with `ls-remote`\n\n  Report with `2C.1: GATE PASSED`.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n- **Tooling:** uv with Python 3.12, ruff (`make lint`, which also runs pnpm web lint and tsc), and pytest (`make test`; the pythonpath includes `scripts`; the atlas test skips without `.env`). numpy is a dependency. Candidates are zstd JSONL.\n- **Wilson intervals:** `paperghosts.stats.wilson`.\n- **Eval A's clusters (the blocking):** 7-word runs of `[a-z]{3,}` from the first 120 words, 1 in 8 kept. Docs are linked through pairs among the 6 smallest keys (least=2), with a boilerplate cap of 1000, joined by union-find.\n- **Decision 36's stories:**\n  - Docs are taken earliest first. Each joins the story whose first printing it matches best, if at least 0.25 of the shorter's shingles are the other's; otherwise it starts its own.\n  - Shingles are 5-word runs from the first 600 words, 1 in 4 kept.\n  - A story's id is its first printing's article id.\n- **The inclusive grouping (call B):** connected components over eval A's clusters plus the body links. Body links are pair-keys of the runs from word 120, with 2..1000 docs per key.\n- **Leakage measure:** whole-text, unsampled 5-word runs, as the containment of the shorter.\n- **RDR-2 schema** (`paperghosts/schemas`):\n  - `Fields(place: Stated, people: list[Stated], seen: Stated, outcome: StatedOutcome, specific_place, place_type, precision)`\n  - `Outcome = Literal[\"unexplained\",\"explained\",\"hoax\",\"unknown\"]`\n  - `Precision = Literal[\"town\",\"street\",\"building\"]`\n  - the `PlaceType` literals\n  - `Stated(value, quote, verified, score)`, where a value requires a verified quote\n- **RDR-1:** 150–300 articles, with `eval/gold/split.json` and `HASH`.\n- **RDR-3:** POLICY.md comes before any labelling.\n- **RDR-4:** `check_leakage.py` exits 0.\n- **RDR-11:** every triaged candidate is read.\n- **SRS 7.3:** edge-case slices E1–E14, each needing at least 5 gold items where the archive has them. Some need targeted top-ups:\n  - E8: phantom ships, trains and lights\n  - E12: non-English text\n  - E6: hoax exposures\n- **Lexicon API:**\n  - `lexicon.ghost_terms(text, precheck=True)` returns `{term: forms}`, including the v1 terms (ghost, apparition, spectre, phantom, haunt, spook, wraith, poltergeist, banshee, goblin, knocking, rapping), the v2 rules (spiritualism, seance, spirit_phrase, ouija) and `spirit+medium`.\n  - `lexicon.near_miss_terms(text)` returns the near-miss terms (knock, rap, noise, spirit, spiritualism, seance, supernatural).\n- **Data:**\n  - `data/counts/{year}.json` keys: year, lexicon_version, pages, articles, candidates, near_miss, by_term, only_by_term, by_state.\n  - `data/samples/{year}.jsonl.zst` rows have `sample` set to `'random'` or `'near_miss'`, 2,000 each per year.\n  - Candidate rows have: article_id, bbox_list, date, headline, lccn, member, paper, scan, state, terms, text.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n\n**`eval/gold/POLICY.md`** (committed in 6cd57e2 and pushed, before any draw).\n- Structure:\n  - an intro (written before the draw and labels; examples by article id from judged items; the draw excludes them and their groups)\n  - \"What is labelled\"\n  - a kinds table (report, fiction, idiom, religious, advert, other)\n  - 18 rules\n  - fields for reports only, with a verbatim quote for each value\n  - the label record\n- The rules:\n  1. A ghost explained is still a report (1854, Phie's ghost).\n  2. Delusions and hallucinations, one rule: a claim on an occasion is a report, and where the text says delusion the outcome is `explained`. It covers the 1904 spectre and the 1909 lunacy hearing, overruling eval A's `other`. A delusion with no ghost claimed is `other` (1917, the aeroplane).\n  3. Spirit control and a felt presence count: Sitting Bull 1895, the 1898 wife murderer, the 1918 scout. A trance or hypnotism with no spirit claimed is `other`.\n  4. Séance reports: Middlebury 1857, the Eddys 1875, Slater 1898. Arrests with no phenomenon are `other`, and so is a French *séance* meaning a sitting.\n  5. A first-person claim of communication with a named dead person counts: Conan Doyle, Vale Owen, Curran.\n  6. A claim printed as fact in a doubtful narrative counts (Allut 1857), and so does a particular claim inside an essay (Hodgson 1907). A general essay is `other`.\n  7. Hoaxes are reports with the outcome `hoax`. General exposures are `advert` or `other` (Bishop 1878, Houdini 1924, Seashore 1922). No judged hoax report exists yet.\n  8. Humour is `fiction`.\n  9. Reprinted fiction is `fiction`.\n  10. Idioms are `idiom`.\n  11. Religious matter is `religious`.\n  12. Adverts, including mediums' cards, are `advert`.\n  13. Names, titles, social events and OCR accidents are `other`.\n  14. Phantom ships, trains, lights and traditions are reports.\n  15. Spiritualism with no spirit experience is `other`.\n  16. Merged articles: a report if any item is one.\n  17. Garbled OCR: use the crop; if nothing settles it, `other` with the note \"unreadable\".\n  18. Non-English text follows the same rules, with quotes kept in its own language.\n- Fields: `place`, `place_state` (gold-only, for the story-place rule), `people`, `seen`, `outcome`, `specific_place`, `place_type`, `precision`.\n- Label record: one JSON per article in `eval/gold/labels.jsonl` and `eval/gold/dev_labels.jsonl`, with keys:\n  - `article_id`, `kind`, `kind_quote`\n  - `place`, `place_state`, `seen`, `outcome`, `specific_place`, `place_type`, `precision`, each `{\"value\",\"quote\"}` or null\n  - `people`, a list\n  - `rules`, `crop_used`, `note`, `labeller`, `labelled_at`\n- All 63 cited example ids were verified to exist in the judged files.\n\n**`scripts/clusters.py`** (2B.4 file; the 2C.1 additions are committed in aab6290):\n```python\nWORDS_SHOWN = 400  # of each printing, for the reading agents\n# words past WORDS_SHOWN that both printings need before the unseen check compares their tails\nTAIL = 50\n\ndef runs_of(text: str) -> set:\n    \"\"\"Every five-word run of 3+ letters in the text, none sampled.\"\"\"\n    words = WORD.findall(text.lower())\n    return {\" \".join(words[i : i + SHINGLE]) for i in range(len(words) - SHINGLE + 1)}\n\ndef overlap_of(a: set, b: set):\n    \"\"\"The share of the smaller set of runs the other holds.\"\"\"\n    return round(len(a & b) / min(len(a), len(b)), 4) if a and b else None\n\ndef unseen(found: dict, root: Path = ROOT) -> dict:\n    # joined pairs (stories at THRESHOLD) in the per candidate / per cluster / near the threshold\n    # strata; texts split on whitespace; a pair is counted if a printing is longer than 400 words;\n    # first_400 = overlap_of(runs of the two openings); past_400 = overlap_of(runs of the two tails)\n    # only where min(len) >= 400 + TAIL; ranked ascending by past_400\n    # returns joined_pairs, with_a_printing_past_400_words, with_both_tails_compared, lowest[:10], pairs\n```\n- `main` gained `elif run == \"unseen\":`, which writes `eval/results/phase2b/clusters_unseen.json`.\n- Its imports include `SHINGLE` and `WORD` from reprints.\n- The rest of `clusters.py` (from 2B.4):\n  - `THRESHOLD = 0.25`, tuned on dev; `LOOSE = 1.25`; `GRID` from 0.2 to 0.8; `LARGE = 50`\n  - `features()`, which caches `data/clusters/features.pkl` with ids, dates, lccns, words, keys, body, shingles (clustered docs only) and frame\n  - `stories_at`, `order_of`, `similarity`, `least_similar`, `report`, `body_links`\n  - `draw`, `texts`, `batches`, `together(index, story, pair)`, `rate`, `scored(index, story, labelled)`\n  - `tune`, `score`, `freeze`\n\n**`eval/results/phase2b/clusters_unseen.json`** (committed in aab6290):\n- joined_pairs 318; with_a_printing_past_400_words 184; with_both_tails_compared 127.\n- Lowest: eval-0100 (0.0677), eval-0576, eval-0251, eval-0509, eval-0040, eval-0255, eval-0261, eval-0217, eval-0548, eval-0058.\n- `read_in_full` records:\n  - the EM's five (eval-0040, 0052, 0217, 0261, 0576)\n  - my four (eval-0100, Peary's letter with an item glued on; eval-0251, garbled OCR; eval-0255, OCR noise; eval-0509, a shipwreck item glued on), all one text\n\n**`Docs/DECISIONS.md`**: decision 36 gained \"The EM's calls on this step (2026-10-31)\", committed in 2467e28:\n- the stories stand\n- the reader reads every triaged candidate, superseding the skip rule (233,917 printings skipped, 38,127 past 600 words); a member that reads differently becomes a report of its own; the stop and owner trade-off if credits fall short\n- the split uses the inclusive grouping (decision 37)\n- the 400-word limitation and the check (the 9 pairs read)\n- digits in the shingles go to 2D (eval-0508 at 0.267)\n- my calls stand\n\n**`Docs/BUILD-PLAN.md`** (2467e28):\n- The 2C step-1 line is now the inclusive-grouping split, plus check_leakage and at most one printing per story in the training pool.\n- Step 10 says every triaged candidate is read, with a stop if credits are short.\n- The 2D notes add digits in the shingles, and counting members whose readings differ.\n\n**`Docs/RESUME.md`** (2467e28): the STATUS lines on the reader now say that every candidate, 1,236,122, is read.\n\n**`paperghosts/cluster/reprints.py`** (uncommitted change):\n```python\ndef shingles(text, window=WINDOW, sample=SAMPLE):\n    \"\"\"... The leakage check reads every run of the whole text: window=None, sample=1.\"\"\"\n    text = (text or \"\") if window is None else (text or \"\")[:40000]\n    words = WORD.findall(text.replace(\"-\\n\", \"\").lower())[:window]\n    found = set()\n    for i in range(len(words) - SHINGLE + 1):\n        run = \" \".join(words[i : i + SHINGLE]).encode()\n        low = zlib.crc32(run)\n        if low % sample == 0:\n            found.add(zlib.crc32(run, 0x5BD1E995) << 32 | low)\n    return np.array(sorted(found), dtype=np.uint64)\n```\n- Other functions: `keys(text, start=0)`, `pairs`, `clusters`, `overlap(a, b)` returning (shared, of_shorter, of_longer), and `stories(doc_keys, doc_shingles, order, threshold, boilerplate)` returning (story, match).\n\n**`tests/test_reprints.py`** (uncommitted addition; 11 pass):\n```python\ndef test_the_leakage_check_compares_every_run_of_the_whole_text():\n    # check_leakage (RDR-4) reads all of each text, past the stories' 600 words, unsampled\n    long = \" \".join(\n        f\"word{chr(97 + i % 26)}{chr(97 + i // 26 % 26)}{chr(97 + i // 676)}\"\n        for i in range(700)\n    )\n    every = shingles(long, window=None, sample=1)\n\n    assert len(every) == 700 - 4\n    assert len(shingles(long)) < len(every) / 2\n    assert set(shingles(long)) <= set(every)\n```\n\n**`paperghosts/ingest/crops.py`** (from 2B.3, merged; the labelling page will use it):\n- Constants: `CACHE` (data/loc/requests), `COUNTS`, `SENT`, `BUSY`, `GAPS={\"www.loc.gov\": 3.5}`\n- `class Busy(httpx.HTTPError)`; `captcha(response)`, true for an HTML answer that isn't a 404\n- `service(batch, lccn, reel, issue, seq)`, `service_id`, `place`, `page_at`, `agrees`\n- `batch_path_service`: the member's frame where only the frame differs\n- `get` (retries busy answers; raises `Busy`)\n- `cached(url, keep=lambda response: response.json(), path=None, counts=None, **params)`\n- `services_listed`, `listed(lccn, reel, issue, seq)`, `page_service(member, scan)`, `mapped(member)`\n- `alto_url`, `alto_words`, `words`, `in_boxes`\n- `page_check(service, scan, boxes, text)` returns a reason or None; `SHARE=0.2`, `FEWEST_WORDS=10`, `NO_SUCH_PAGE`\n- `crops_for(member, scan, boxes, service=None, text=None)`:\n  - returns (service, [crop paths])\n  - raises LookupError on a rejected page\n  - shows pages whose member and batch path agree unchecked\n  - checks every other page\n\n**Other merged files from 2B.3 and 2B.4:**\n- `paperghosts/ingest/reels.py`: build, flagged, day, version, ranked, pick, load\n- `scripts/reel_map.py`, with `frame_rule`\n- `scripts/page_check.py`\n- `scripts/budget_stories.py`, now using the stories\n- `eval/clusters/` (LABELLING.md, eval.json, dev.json, frozen.json, tuning.json)\n- the reports `eval/results/phase2b/{reels,page_check,clusters,budget_stories}.json`\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n\n**2C.1:**\n- **The unseen check.**\n  - `F821 SHINGLE` undefined: fixed by importing it from reprints.\n  - The text loop refiltered on every file: now it collects only the wanted ids.\n  - The first measure, the long printing's tail against the other's whole text, ranked cuts at 0. Redefined as the two tails compared with each other, only where both printings run at least 50 words past word 400. Four of the EM's five then land in my lowest eight, and I read the four extra low pairs.\n- **The whole-text shingles test** expected 696 runs and got 676, because its made-up words repeated after 676. Fixed with 3-letter suffixes.\n\n**2B.4 (merged):**\n- A heredoc wrote a literal `\\n` at the end of reprints.py; trimmed.\n- `noqa` E402 imports were moved to the top.\n- The loose rule flagged OCR noise (166,717 members); redefined by length.\n- Lint: an I001 import-order fix and a ruff format.\n- Truncated output was read from the report files instead.\n\n**2B.3 (merged):**\n- `crops.service` was missing, so a run failed at import before sending any request.\n- The `httpx.Response.json` default failed on the test's fake response; changed to a lambda.\n- The reissue fallback was briefly switched to the batch path's frame, then reverted after the member-frame finding.\n- Lint: zip → pairwise; unused loop variables; `lambda: {}` → `dict`; the loop variable `service` renamed to `trial`.\n- zsh doesn't split variables, so `${=VAR}` is needed.\n- The viewer crashed on a None paper; fixed.\n\n**The EM's feedback that changed course:**\n- The frame rule changed.\n- tile.loc.gov stays at 1 s.\n- The reader reads every candidate.\n- The split uses the inclusive grouping, not the story id.\n- Record the 400-word limitation, with a committed check script.\n- My protocol mapping and hard-case calls were right.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n\n**Inclusive grouping, explored.** Union-find over eval A's frame clusters plus consecutive docs in body pair-key lists (2..1000) gives 99,558 groups of 2 or more, holding 417,565 candidates, and 818,557 singletons. The largest groups are 732, 684, 493, 481, 461, 403, 379, 373, 324 and 308, so there's no giant component.\n\n**Leakage threshold, explored on decision 36's labelled pairs** (whole-text unsampled run containment of the shorter):\n- At t=0.10, every representative one-story pair is flagged, 603 of 603:\n\n  | Stratum | One-story pairs flagged |\n  |---|---|\n  | per candidate | 196/196 |\n  | per cluster | 100/100 |\n  | near the threshold | 79/79 |\n  | body probe | 76/76 |\n  | dev, near the threshold | 115/115 |\n  | dev, per candidate | 37/37 |\n\n- The large clusters' one-story pairs are flagged 90/153; these are grouped anyway.\n- Different-story pairs are flagged 13 of 17 in the representative strata, and 8 of 54 in the large clusters. A false flag only keeps a candidate out of training.\n- The minimum containment among the 603 is 0.105. The fewest shared runs among them is 6, so the floor is 5.\n- **The rule:** flag when at least 5 whole-text runs are shared and they make at least 10% of the shorter's runs.\n- The scores are saved in `$S/c1/leak_scores.json`.\n\n**Draw design inputs:**\n- Report rates:\n  - v1 candidates 3.5% (1.7–7.0%)\n  - v2-added 7.0%\n  - spiritualism 9.8%, seance 10.5%, spirit_phrase 10.8%, spirit+medium 4.5%, ouija 0/6\n  - near misses 0/300\n- Headline enrichment:\n  - eval A: a term in the headline, 4 of 14 (29%), against 3 of 186 (1.6%)\n  - 2A.8: 5 of 19 (26%), against 9 of 181 (5.0%)\n  - combined about 27% (15–44%)\n- v2 rule candidate counts: spiritualism 74,516, seance 34,206, spirit_phrase 42,186, spirit+medium 79,374, ouija 6,389.\n\n**The plan:**\n- Strata are the term class by priority (ouija, then seance, spirit_phrase, spiritualism, spirit+medium, then v1), crossed with a term in the headline or not. Within each, systematic sampling over date order spreads the draw across decades, with a minimum top-up for early decades.\n- Near misses come from the `near_miss` samples. Their π is the draw fraction times the reservoir fraction (2,000 over the year's near-miss pool).\n- Edge-case top-ups:\n  - E8: a regex for phantom ships, trains and lights\n  - E12: a non-English stopword test\n  - E6: a hoax or exposure regex\n- Each item's π is n/N per frame, with π = 1 − Π(1 − π_f) where an item falls in several frames.\n- **Gold, about 300:**\n  - about 130 with a term in the headline\n  - about 120 without\n  - about 25 near misses\n  - about 25 top-ups\n  - expected reports about 35–45\n- **Dev, about 150, the same design:** I label it under POLICY.md in 2C.2, so model selection aims at the same truth as gold.\n- Leave out POLICY's 63 example articles and their groups.\n- Quarantine flagged pool candidates and their groups, so `check_leakage` passes.\n- The training pool takes at most one printing per story when drawn (2C.4).\n\n6. All user messages:\n\n- **The EM: 2B.2 accepted, and the 2B.3 brief.**\n  - Its five parts:\n    1. the reel→batch map, with counts reconciled with decision 31\n    2. validation by hiding whole issues, with a Wilson rate overall and for flagged reels, and the reels that can't be tested\n    3. every multi-batch reel flagged with date ranges, `info.json` on each batch for a sample, and the rule; \"The batch path wins wherever it exists.\"\n    4. the page check measured on differing and agreeing samples, with a text check if false accepts exceed a few percent\n    5. loc.gov: \"one request at a time, at least 3.5 s apart, and each response cached with its URL, date and status. `page_service` gets its cache here. Plan the samples to about 600 requests, ask me before going over, and report what they took. No lookups for the 27,817 yet.\"\n  - Its gate: decision 35, pushed and checked with ls-remote.\n- **The EM: 2B.3 accepted, with calls on my six items, and the 2B.4 brief.**\n  - The six calls:\n    1. Change the frame rule: the member's frame where only the frame differs; the batch path's page where the reel differs; check pages whose member and batch path disagree; count by batch; supersede decision 33.\n    2. Accepted.\n    3. The fifth is frozen; 2D records scores and reasons.\n    4. Leave load_skeleton; 3B's crops only through `crops_for`.\n    5. Add the 2D disk notes (187 KB, about 4 GB).\n    6. \"Keep 1 s for tile.loc.gov… Treat a CAPTCHA page (HTML where JSON or XML was expected) as busy: pause, retry, and never cache it. Cite the page and today's date in decision 35.\"\n  - The 2B.4 brief:\n    - design decision 36\n    - chaining\n    - false-merge cost\n    - an evaluation set drawn, labelled and frozen before tuning, with a separate dev draw\n    - per-candidate, per-cluster, the 50+ clusters' least similar pairs, and recall probes\n    - the 2A.7 labelling protocol\n    - precision and recall with Wilson intervals\n    - BUDGET's third scenario\n    - then close 2B with the STATUS block and a PR into main\n- **The EM: 2B accepted, PR #4 merged as e350721.** Start 2C on `phase-2c-reader`. The review, calls on five items, calls A, B and C, and the 2C.1 brief and gate, quoted in full in the analysis. It includes: \"Stop for my review before you label anything for gold.\" It ends: \"Next, in 2C.2, you label gold yourself before any model output, with a blind second pass to measure agreement, then freeze it.\"\n- **The EM:** \"The session limit has reset: resume 2C.1 where you stopped… Finish that check, then the rest of 2C.1 as briefed: decision 36's note and decision 37; the draw, the split and the leakage check; the labelling page; the plan and STATUS fixes. Same gate, and stop before any gold label. Commit and push at each natural point, so a limit costs nothing.\"\n- **Other user-role turns:** \"Tool loaded.\" acknowledgements, and the reading subagents' hand-back reports. These are agent outputs, not user instructions.\n\n7. Pending Tasks (2C.1):\n- Commit and push the `shingles(window, sample)` change and its test.\n- Add `groups(found)` to `scripts/clusters.py`: union-find over eval A's frame and the body links, with the group id as its earliest member's. Test it.\n- Write `scripts/check_leakage.py`:\n  - `LEAK = 0.10`, `FEWEST = 5`, with the justification\n  - a justify mode writing a results JSON, for example `eval/results/phase2c/leakage_threshold.json`\n  - a scan of all candidates outside the exclusions against the gold and dev items' whole-text runs, in parallel by year\n  - a direct check of gold against dev\n  - exit 0 when clean, 1 otherwise\n  - tests\n- Write the draw script, for example `scripts/gold_draw.py`, producing `eval/gold/split.json` and `eval/gold/HASH`. It covers the strata, inclusion weights, the exclusion of POLICY examples and their groups, near misses, edge-case top-ups, dev, and the quarantine. Commit it after POLICY.md, and confirm `check_leakage.py` exits 0.\n- Build the local labelling page, for example a small FastAPI app. It shows the OCR text beside crops through `crops_for`, with POLICY's rules at hand and RDR-2's fields with quote capture, and saves to `eval/gold/labels.jsonl`. Add a test with TestClient and `crops_for` monkeypatched.\n- Write decision 37: the gold design, the grouping numbers, the leakage threshold justification, the draw sizes and expected reports, dev's size and labeller, inclusion weights, the training pool at most one printing per story, and the labelling page. Update RESUME's next step.\n- Run `make lint` and `make test`, commit, push, and check with `git ls-remote`.\n- Write the hand-over note `step-2C.1.md`. Report starting with `2C.1: GATE PASSED`, at most 150 words. No PR, and no gold labels.\n\n8. Current Work:\n- On `phase-2c-reader`, pushed at 2467e28; its commits are 6cd57e2 POLICY.md, aab6290 the unseen check and 2467e28 decision 36's note.\n- **Uncommitted:**\n  - `paperghosts/cluster/reprints.py`: `shingles(text, window=WINDOW, sample=SAMPLE)`\n  - `tests/test_reprints.py`: `test_the_leakage_check_compares_every_run_of_the_whole_text`\n  - 11 of 11 pass in that file; the full suite was last 355 passing before this change.\n- I had measured the inclusive grouping's sizes and justified the leakage rule (at least 5 shared runs, and 10% of the shorter).\n- I had then inspected the data shape for the draw: the lexicon API (`ghost_terms` / `near_miss_terms`), a counts file (1880: candidates 11,389, near_miss 52,574, articles 2,482,134), and the samples' `sample` field (`random` and `near_miss`, 2,000 each).\n- The update_plan state: step 4, \"Grouping and check_leakage.py, threshold justified\", is in progress. Next are step 5, the gold and dev draw (split.json and HASH); step 6, the labelling page and its test; and step 7, decisions 36 and 37, plan notes and push.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\nContinue step 4 of 2C.1, per the EM's latest instruction: \"Finish that check, then the rest of 2C.1 as briefed: decision 36's note and decision 37; the draw, the split and the leakage check; the labelling page; the plan and STATUS fixes. Same gate, and stop before any gold label. Commit and push at each natural point.\"\n- First, scan and commit the uncommitted whole-text `shingles` change and its test, then push.\n- Then add `groups(found)` to `scripts/clusters.py`, with a test.\n- Then write `scripts/check_leakage.py`, with the threshold justified on decision 36's labelled pairs: flag when at least 5 shared whole-text runs make up at least 10% of the shorter. That flags all 603 representative one-story pairs. Include its justify output and tests.\n- Then move to the gold and dev draw.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/hotragn/.claude/projects/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"03f7dfbd-bd79-46e3-9b74-471dc1dc25f6","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:42:38.712Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":0,"turnIndex":44},"turnOrigin":"peer","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"03f7dfbd-bd79-46e3-9b74-471dc1dc25f6","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/eval/gold/POLICY.md","displayPath":"eval/gold/POLICY.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"dfdf1aa9-13eb-4d84-9e0d-7ae85339ae62","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:42:38.920Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/eval/gold/POLICY.md was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"dfdf1aa9-13eb-4d84-9e0d-7ae85339ae62","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.4.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.4.md","content":"# Step 2B.4: DONE, and Phase 2B is closed\n\nOn `phase-2b-data-layer`. I checked origin with `git ls-remote`: it shows `42c96c2`, the same as local. **PR [Hotragn/Paperghosts#4](https://github.com/Hotragn/Paperghosts/pull/4) is open into `main`.** It's mergeable, and its 3 CI checks were pending when I wrote this.\n\n| Commit | What |\n|---|---|\n| `f933ae3` | your calls on 2B.3: the member's frame where only the frame differs, and CAPTCHA pages as busy |\n| `4f3d40a` | decision 35's record of your calls, loc.gov's limits page, and the 2D and 3B plan notes |\n| `1a2f8b7` | the stories: a printing joins a story only by matching its first printing directly |\n| `f86021f` | the evaluation set, labelled and frozen before any tuning, and the dev set |\n| `d7697cd` | the threshold, 0.25, tuned on dev alone, and the one score on the frozen set |\n| `e9fd6e2` | BUDGET.md's third scenario from the stories: 62,402 stories |\n| `42c96c2` | decision 36, the plan's notes for 2C and 2D, and Phase 2B's STATUS block |\n\nEach commit's tests pass on its own in a clean worktree. `make test` gives 355 passed, the live `atlas` test included, and `make lint` exits 0.\n\n## Your six calls on 2B.3\n\n1. **The frame rule** (`crops.batch_path_service`, used by `printings.py`, `page_service` and `crops_for`):\n   - Where only the frame differs, the service is the batch path's batch with the member's frame. Where the reel differs too, the batch path's page stands.\n   - `crops_for` checks every page whose member and batch path don't agree.\n   - It changes 20,247 candidates' stored service, in 187 batches. That's decision 33's 20,643 with another frame, less the 396 with another reel. The counts by batch are in `reels.json`, under `frame_rule`.\n   - Decision 35 records it as your call, superseding decision 33's frame rule.\n2. Done.\n3. The fifth is frozen. The 2D plan notes now say to record each check's score and reason.\n4. `load_skeleton.py` is untouched. The 3B plan says crops come only through `crops_for`.\n5. **Disk, in the 2D plan notes:** I count 197 cached ALTO records, all answered 200, where you counted 199. Their median is 191,680 bytes, which is 187 KiB, so your figure stands.\n   - About 21,000 checks would take about 4.0 GB, or 1.3 GB gzipped.\n6. **Rates:** tile.loc.gov stays at 1 s. I read the limits page myself on 2026-10-30, and decision 35 cites it.\n   - An HTML answer that isn't a 404 counts as busy: `get` pauses and asks again.\n   - A CAPTCHA page still there after the pauses raises `crops.Busy`, which is never cached.\n\n## 2B.4 gate\n\n- **The frozen set:** `eval/clusters/eval.json`, 667 pairs, with its sha256 `094d4070…754c` in `frozen.json`. It was pushed at `f86021f`, before tuning.\n- **Labelling:**\n  - 17 reading agents labelled both sets, blind to set, stratum and score.\n  - I re-judged a seeded 10% blind and agreed on 61 of 61.\n  - I read every rarer label and close call in full, 81 pairs. All 43 \"different\" labels stand, and the 38 close calls went 15 same and 23 different.\n- **Tuning on dev only:** 0.25 gives no false merges and 22 false splits. 0.5 was the provisional value.\n- **At 0.25 on the frozen set:**\n\n  | Stratum | Precision | Recall | Eval A |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | per candidate | 166/166, 100% (97.7–100%) | 166/196, 84.7% (79.0–89.1%) | 196/200 one story |\n  | per cluster | 87/87, 100% (95.8–100%) | 87/100, 87.0% (79.0–92.2%) | 100/100 |\n  | near the threshold | 65/65, 100% (94.4–100%) | 65/79, 82.3% | 79/80 |\n  | large clusters' least similar pairs | 26/29, 89.7% | 26/153, 17.0% | 153/207 |\n  | body probe | none joined | 0/76 | none joined |\n\n- **Chaining:** 54 of the 207 least similar pairs are different stories, and the method splits 51.\n  - Each chained cluster is explained by kind in decision 36. The kinds are standing columns, templates, quoted passages, serials, fragments, unrelated texts and one variant.\n  - The 3 left joined each hold a short first printing whole inside two different texts.\n- **v2's stories:**\n  - 974,399 distinct stories, against eval A's 952,591.\n  - The reader reads each story's first printing and every member at least 25% longer: 1,002,205 printings in all.\n  - Story ids are the first printing's article id, which keeps them stable.\n- **BUDGET.md:** 62,402 stories at the top of the range, needing 461.8 MB as stored. The conclusions stand.\n\n## For you\n\n1. **Stories or eval A's clusters is your call.**\n   - The stories remove eval A's false merges: 4 of 200 per candidate, and 54 of the 207 large clusters' pairs.\n   - In return they split 15% of true reprints, mostly through OCR noise. Reader work rises 5.2%.\n   - At five to one, per candidate, eval A costs 20 and the stories 30. On dev, eval A costs 40 and the stories 22.\n   - I'd use the stories, since a false merge can hide a report. A hybrid that kept eval A's clusters below 20 printings would cost 24 on dev, so I didn't adopt it.\n2. **Eval A's links miss many reprints.** The body probe found 76 of 80 pairs to be one story outside the links.\n   - Its frame is 55,711 links across clusters, touching 77,641 candidates, 42,733 of them in no cluster.\n   - Proposed, not done: add the body's links to the blocking and measure it on a fresh draw. That's in the 2D notes.\n3. **The labelling protocol, as I applied it:** I read in full the rare label in every stratum, which was \"different\", and re-checked 10% of \"same\" blind. Say if you meant every \"same\" read in full: that would be about 600 more pairs.\n4. **My one-story calls on the hard cases:**\n   - A printing held whole in a longer one is the same story.\n   - Disjoint fragments of one long text, a speech or a statement, are different, since neither holds the other.\n   - A shared opening with different continuations of comparable length is different.\n5. **Auto-fix** is off for #4. I can turn it on if you want me to watch the CI.\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}},"displayPath":"../../../../../Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.4.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7dbe0157-b1ca-4220-8f0a-68934562774c","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:42:38.920Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_98906164-c8d6-46c4-b1d0-192cf333434e/step-2B.4.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Step 2B.4: DONE, and Phase 2B is closed\n2\t\n3\tOn `phase-2b-data-layer`. I checked origin with `git ls-remote`: it shows `42c96c2`, the same as local. **PR [Hotragn/Paperghosts#4](https://github.com/Hotragn/Paperghosts/pull/4) is open into `main`.** It's mergeable, and its 3 CI checks were pending when I wrote this.\n4\t\n5\t| Commit | What |\n6\t|---|---|\n7\t| `f933ae3` | your calls on 2B.3: the member's frame where only the frame differs, and CAPTCHA pages as busy |\n8\t| `4f3d40a` | decision 35's record of your calls, loc.gov's limits page, and the 2D and 3B plan notes |\n9\t| `1a2f8b7` | the stories: a printing joins a story only by matching its first printing directly |\n10\t| `f86021f` | the evaluation set, labelled and frozen before any tuning, and the dev set |\n11\t| `d7697cd` | the threshold, 0.25, tuned on dev alone, and the one score on the frozen set |\n12\t| `e9fd6e2` | BUDGET.md's third scenario from the stories: 62,402 stories |\n13\t| `42c96c2` | decision 36, the plan's notes for 2C and 2D, and Phase 2B's STATUS block |\n14\t\n15\tEach commit's tests pass on its own in a clean worktree. `make test` gives 355 passed, the live `atlas` test included, and `make lint` exits 0.\n16\t\n17\t## Your six calls on 2B.3\n18\t\n19\t1. **The frame rule** (`crops.batch_path_service`, used by `printings.py`, `page_service` and `crops_for`):\n20\t   - Where only the frame differs, the service is the batch path's batch with the member's frame. Where the reel differs too, the batch path's page stands.\n21\t   - `crops_for` checks every page whose member and batch path don't agree.\n22\t   - It changes 20,247 candidates' stored service, in 187 batches. That's decision 33's 20,643 with another frame, less the 396 with another reel. The counts by batch are in `reels.json`, under `frame_rule`.\n23\t   - Decision 35 records it as your call, superseding decision 33's frame rule.\n24\t2. Done.\n25\t3. The fifth is frozen. The 2D plan notes now say to record each check's score and reason.\n26\t4. `load_skeleton.py` is untouched. The 3B plan says crops come only through `crops_for`.\n27\t5. **Disk, in the 2D plan notes:** I count 197 cached ALTO records, all answered 200, where you counted 199. Their median is 191,680 bytes, which is 187 KiB, so your figure stands.\n28\t   - About 21,000 checks would take about 4.0 GB, or 1.3 GB gzipped.\n29\t6. **Rates:** tile.loc.gov stays at 1 s. I read the limits page myself on 2026-10-30, and decision 35 cites it.\n30\t   - An HTML answer that isn't a 404 counts as busy: `get` pauses and asks again.\n31\t   - A CAPTCHA page still there after the pauses raises `crops.Busy`, which is never cached.\n32\t\n33\t## 2B.4 gate\n34\t\n35\t- **The frozen set:** `eval/clusters/eval.json`, 667 pairs, with its sha256 `094d4070…754c` in `frozen.json`. It was pushed at `f86021f`, before tuning.\n36\t- **Labelling:**\n37\t  - 17 reading agents labelled both sets, blind to set, stratum and score.\n38\t  - I re-judged a seeded 10% blind and agreed on 61 of 61.\n39\t  - I read every rarer label and close call in full, 81 pairs. All 43 \"different\" labels stand, and the 38 close calls went 15 same and 23 different.\n40\t- **Tuning on dev only:** 0.25 gives no false merges and 22 false splits. 0.5 was the provisional value.\n41\t- **At 0.25 on the frozen set:**\n42\t\n43\t  | Stratum | Precision | Recall | Eval A |\n44\t  |---|---|---|---|\n45\t  | per candidate | 166/166, 100% (97.7–100%) | 166/196, 84.7% (79.0–89.1%) | 196/200 one story |\n46\t  | per cluster | 87/87, 100% (95.8–100%) | 87/100, 87.0% (79.0–92.2%) | 100/100 |\n47\t  | near the threshold | 65/65, 100% (94.4–100%) | 65/79, 82.3% | 79/80 |\n48\t  | large clusters' least similar pairs | 26/29, 89.7% | 26/153, 17.0% | 153/207 |\n49\t  | body probe | none joined | 0/76 | none joined |\n50\t\n51\t- **Chaining:** 54 of the 207 least similar pairs are different stories, and the method splits 51.\n52\t  - Each chained cluster is explained by kind in decision 36. The kinds are standing columns, templates, quoted passages, serials, fragments, unrelated texts and one variant.\n53\t  - The 3 left joined each hold a short first printing whole inside two different texts.\n54\t- **v2's stories:**\n55\t  - 974,399 distinct stories, against eval A's 952,591.\n56\t  - The reader reads each story's first printing and every member at least 25% longer: 1,002,205 printings in all.\n57\t  - Story ids are the first printing's article id, which keeps them stable.\n58\t- **BUDGET.md:** 62,402 stories at the top of the range, needing 461.8 MB as stored. The conclusions stand.\n59\t\n60\t## For you\n61\t\n62\t1. **Stories or eval A's clusters is your call.**\n63\t   - The stories remove eval A's false merges: 4 of 200 per candidate, and 54 of the 207 large clusters' pairs.\n64\t   - In return they split 15% of true reprints, mostly through OCR noise. Reader work rises 5.2%.\n65\t   - At five to one, per candidate, eval A costs 20 and the stories 30. On dev, eval A costs 40 and the stories 22.\n66\t   - I'd use the stories, since a false merge can hide a report. A hybrid that kept eval A's clusters below 20 printings would cost 24 on dev, so I didn't adopt it.\n67\t2. **Eval A's links miss many reprints.** The body probe found 76 of 80 pairs to be one story outside the links.\n68\t   - Its frame is 55,711 links across clusters, touching 77,641 candidates, 42,733 of them in no cluster.\n69\t   - Proposed, not done: add the body's links to the blocking and measure it on a fresh draw. That's in the 2D notes.\n70\t3. **The labelling protocol, as I applied it:** I read in full the rare label in every stratum, which was \"different\", and re-checked 10% of \"same\" blind. Say if you meant every \"same\" read in full: that would be about 600 more pairs.\n71\t4. **My one-story calls on the hard cases:**\n72\t   - A printing held whole in a longer one is the same story.\n73\t   - Disjoint fragments of one long text, a speech or a statement, are different, since neither holds the other.\n74\t   - A shared opening with different continuations of comparable length is different.\n75\t5. **Auto-fix** is off for #4. I can turn it on if you want me to watch the CI.\n76\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"7dbe0157-b1ca-4220-8f0a-68934562774c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/pr-body.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/pr-body.md","content":"Phase 2B: schemas and the data layer, in four steps on `phase-2b-data-layer`. The EM reviewed each step. Decisions 32 to 36 record them, and `Docs/RESUME.md` ends with the phase's STATUS block.\n\n- **2B.1** (decisions 32 and 33):\n  - Pydantic models, with the Atlas validators generated from them\n  - BinData embeddings\n  - `make db-init`, which can be re-run safely\n  - `BUDGET.md`, built from real document sizes\n- **2B.2** (decision 34): the LCCN title parser, the loc.gov title records and the title lookup, the GeoNames geocoder, and the record check.\n  - The parser is tested on 200 titles checked by hand, and the records are cached.\n  - The geocoder covers the US, Puerto Rico and Virgin Islands dumps, with its manifest.\n  - **98.0% (95.0–99.2%) of 200 papers' places are right.**\n- **2B.3** (decision 35):\n  - **The reel-to-batch map** gives 93.9% of candidates with no batch path a batch from local data. It gets 99.78% of hidden issues right.\n  - **The page check runs before a crop is shown.** It accepted 0 of 90 wrong frames and rejected 2 of 85 right ones.\n  - **`page_service` caches** every Library of Congress answer, and a CAPTCHA page counts as busy.\n  - **The EM's frame rule:** where only the frame differs, the member's frame is the page. That changes 20,247 candidates' service.\n- **2B.4** (decision 36): reprint stories, in which a printing joins a story only by matching its first printing directly.\n  - A 667-pair evaluation set was labelled and frozen before tuning, and pushed at `f86021f` with its sha256.\n  - **Precision per candidate is 100% (97.7–100%), and recall is 84.7% (79.0–89.1%).** The chaining in 51 of the 54 chained large clusters is split.\n\n**Checks**\n- `make test` gives 355 passed, the live Atlas test included, and `make lint` exits 0.\n- Each commit's tests pass on its own in a clean worktree.\n- Each commit's staged diff was scanned against `.env`'s values, by name only, before it was made.\n\n**For the owner**\n- `places_search` needs a free Search slot, and `sample_mflix` holds M0's third (decision 33).\n- At the top of the range, the stories fit on M0 only without `sample_mflix` (`BUDGET.md`).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}},"displayPath":"../../../../../../../private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/pr-body.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b46834df-0baf-4ecb-afe2-d4c120c79d9c","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:42:38.920Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/pr-body.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\tPhase 2B: schemas and the data layer, in four steps on `phase-2b-data-layer`. The EM reviewed each step. Decisions 32 to 36 record them, and `Docs/RESUME.md` ends with the phase's STATUS block.\n2\t\n3\t- **2B.1** (decisions 32 and 33):\n4\t  - Pydantic models, with the Atlas validators generated from them\n5\t  - BinData embeddings\n6\t  - `make db-init`, which can be re-run safely\n7\t  - `BUDGET.md`, built from real document sizes\n8\t- **2B.2** (decision 34): the LCCN title parser, the loc.gov title records and the title lookup, the GeoNames geocoder, and the record check.\n9\t  - The parser is tested on 200 titles checked by hand, and the records are cached.\n10\t  - The geocoder covers the US, Puerto Rico and Virgin Islands dumps, with its manifest.\n11\t  - **98.0% (95.0–99.2%) of 200 papers' places are right.**\n12\t- **2B.3** (decision 35):\n13\t  - **The reel-to-batch map** gives 93.9% of candidates with no batch path a batch from local data. It gets 99.78% of hidden issues right.\n14\t  - **The page check runs before a crop is shown.** It accepted 0 of 90 wrong frames and rejected 2 of 85 right ones.\n15\t  - **`page_service` caches** every Library of Congress answer, and a CAPTCHA page counts as busy.\n16\t  - **The EM's frame rule:** where only the frame differs, the member's frame is the page. That changes 20,247 candidates' service.\n17\t- **2B.4** (decision 36): reprint stories, in which a printing joins a story only by matching its first printing directly.\n18\t  - A 667-pair evaluation set was labelled and frozen before tuning, and pushed at `f86021f` with its sha256.\n19\t  - **Precision per candidate is 100% (97.7–100%), and recall is 84.7% (79.0–89.1%).** The chaining in 51 of the 54 chained large clusters is split.\n20\t\n21\t**Checks**\n22\t- `make test` gives 355 passed, the live Atlas test included, and `make lint` exits 0.\n23\t- Each commit's tests pass on its own in a clean worktree.\n24\t- Each commit's staged diff was scanned against `.env`'s values, by name only, before it was made.\n25\t\n26\t**For the owner**\n27\t- `places_search` needs a free Search slot, and `sample_mflix` holds M0's third (decision 33).\n28\t- At the top of the range, the stories fit on M0 only without `sample_mflix` (`BUDGET.md`).\n29\t\n30\t🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n31\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"b46834df-0baf-4ecb-afe2-d4c120c79d9c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_clusters.py","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_clusters.py","content":"\"\"\"The clustering evaluation's scoring: precision over the labelled pairs the stories keep\ntogether, recall over the pairs labelled one story, and a cluster's least similar pair.\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom clusters import least_similar, scored\n\n\ndef runs(a, b):\n    return np.arange(a, b, dtype=np.uint64)\n\n\ndef test_precision_counts_the_pairs_kept_together_and_recall_those_labelled_one_story():\n    index = {\"a\": 0, \"b\": 1, \"c\": 2, \"d\": 3}\n    story = [0, 0, 2, 2]\n    labelled = [\n        {\"pair\": \"kept and one story\", \"a\": \"a\", \"b\": \"b\", \"label\": \"same\"},\n        {\"pair\": \"kept but two\", \"a\": \"c\", \"b\": \"d\", \"label\": \"different\"},\n        {\"pair\": \"split but one\", \"a\": \"a\", \"b\": \"c\", \"label\": \"same\"},\n        {\"pair\": \"split and two\", \"a\": \"b\", \"b\": \"d\", \"label\": \"different\"},\n    ]\n\n    found = scored(index, story, labelled)\n\n    assert (found[\"precision\"][\"hits\"], found[\"precision\"][\"n\"]) == (1, 2)\n    assert (found[\"recall\"][\"hits\"], found[\"recall\"][\"n\"]) == (1, 2)\n    assert found[\"false_merges\"] == [\"kept but two\"]\n    assert found[\"false_splits\"] == [\"split but one\"]\n\n\ndef test_a_clusters_least_similar_pair_shares_least_of_the_shorters_shingles():\n    found = {\"shingles\": {0: runs(0, 10), 1: runs(0, 9), 2: runs(5, 15)}}\n\n    # 0 and 1 share all of 1's, 0 and 2 half of theirs, and 1 and 2 four of 1's nine\n    assert least_similar(found, [0, 1, 2]) == (1, 2)\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1,"totalLines":35}},"displayPath":"tests/test_clusters.py"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b71522c2-88bd-4e75-980b-22e18185c95e","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:42:38.920Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/tests/test_clusters.py\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t\"\"\"The clustering evaluation's scoring: precision over the labelled pairs the stories keep\n2\ttogether, recall over the pairs labelled one story, and a cluster's least similar pair.\"\"\"\n3\t\n4\timport numpy as np\n5\tfrom clusters import least_similar, scored\n6\t\n7\t\n8\tdef runs(a, b):\n9\t    return np.arange(a, b, dtype=np.uint64)\n10\t\n11\t\n12\tdef test_precision_counts_the_pairs_kept_together_and_recall_those_labelled_one_story():\n13\t    index = {\"a\": 0, \"b\": 1, \"c\": 2, \"d\": 3}\n14\t    story = [0, 0, 2, 2]\n15\t    labelled = [\n16\t        {\"pair\": \"kept and one story\", \"a\": \"a\", \"b\": \"b\", \"label\": \"same\"},\n17\t        {\"pair\": \"kept but two\", \"a\": \"c\", \"b\": \"d\", \"label\": \"different\"},\n18\t        {\"pair\": \"split but one\", \"a\": \"a\", \"b\": \"c\", \"label\": \"same\"},\n19\t        {\"pair\": \"split and two\", \"a\": \"b\", \"b\": \"d\", \"label\": \"different\"},\n20\t    ]\n21\t\n22\t    found = scored(index, story, labelled)\n23\t\n24\t    assert (found[\"precision\"][\"hits\"], found[\"precision\"][\"n\"]) == (1, 2)\n25\t    assert (found[\"recall\"][\"hits\"], found[\"recall\"][\"n\"]) == (1, 2)\n26\t    assert found[\"false_merges\"] == [\"kept but two\"]\n27\t    assert found[\"false_splits\"] == [\"split but one\"]\n28\t\n29\t\n30\tdef test_a_clusters_least_similar_pair_shares_least_of_the_shorters_shingles():\n31\t    found = {\"shingles\": {0: runs(0, 10), 1: runs(0, 9), 2: runs(5, 15)}}\n32\t\n33\t    # 0 and 1 share all of 1's, 0 and 2 half of theirs, and 1 and 2 four of 1's nine\n34\t    assert least_similar(found, [0, 1, 2]) == (1, 2)\n35\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"b71522c2-88bd-4e75-980b-22e18185c95e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/decision36-eval.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/decision36-eval.md","content":"- **The evaluation set** (`eval/clusters/eval.json`, 667 pairs): drawn from eval A's clusters before any label or tuning (seed \"2B.4 eval\"), then labelled and frozen, with its sha256 in `frozen.json`. No candidate is in two pairs, except where a large cluster's least similar pair is fixed.\n  - **Per candidate, 200:** a uniform draw of clustered candidates, each paired with a random other member of its cluster. That weights precision the way the reader's job and the story counts are weighted.\n  - **Per cluster, 100:** a uniform draw of clusters with a random pair from each, as eval A drew them.\n  - **Large clusters, 207:** every cluster of 50 printings or more (v1 had 157), with its two least similar members.\n  - **Near the threshold, 80:** per-candidate pairs whose shorter printing has 20% to 60% of its shingles in the other. This is where the threshold was expected to fall before tuning.\n  - **The body probe, 80:** pairs that share a pair of runs among their body's, from word 120, but sit in different clusters of eval A's or in none. These are reprints the links might miss, such as one with a rewritten opening.\n- **The dev set** (`eval/clusters/dev.json`, 160 pairs, seed \"2B.4 dev\"): 40 per-candidate pairs and 120 from 10% to 80%, none sharing a candidate with the evaluation set. The threshold is tuned on it alone.\n- **Labelling** (`eval/clusters/LABELLING.md`): 17 reading agents read 50 pairs each, with both sets shuffled together (seed \"2B.4 batches\"). A batch names no set, stratum or score, and shows each printing's paper, date, headline and first 400 words.\n","numLines":9,"startLine":1,"totalLines":9}},"displayPath":"../../../../../../../private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/decision36-eval.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e0f1b780-5e5b-4456-927f-cc5e54f40a26","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:42:38.920Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/b4/decision36-eval.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t- **The evaluation set** (`eval/clusters/eval.json`, 667 pairs): drawn from eval A's clusters before any label or tuning (seed \"2B.4 eval\"), then labelled and frozen, with its sha256 in `frozen.json`. No candidate is in two pairs, except where a large cluster's least similar pair is fixed.\n2\t  - **Per candidate, 200:** a uniform draw of clustered candidates, each paired with a random other member of its cluster. That weights precision the way the reader's job and the story counts are weighted.\n3\t  - **Per cluster, 100:** a uniform draw of clusters with a random pair from each, as eval A drew them.\n4\t  - **Large clusters, 207:** every cluster of 50 printings or more (v1 had 157), with its two least similar members.\n5\t  - **Near the threshold, 80:** per-candidate pairs whose shorter printing has 20% to 60% of its shingles in the other. This is where the threshold was expected to fall before tuning.\n6\t  - **The body probe, 80:** pairs that share a pair of runs among their body's, from word 120, but sit in different clusters of eval A's or in none. These are reprints the links might miss, such as one with a rewritten opening.\n7\t- **The dev set** (`eval/clusters/dev.json`, 160 pairs, seed \"2B.4 dev\"): 40 per-candidate pairs and 120 from 10% to 80%, none sharing a candidate with the evaluation set. The threshold is tuned on it alone.\n8\t- **Labelling** (`eval/clusters/LABELLING.md`): 17 reading agents read 50 pairs each, with both sets shuffled together (seed \"2B.4 batches\"). A batch names no set, stratum or score, and shows each printing's paper, date, headline and first 400 words.\n9\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"e0f1b780-5e5b-4456-927f-cc5e54f40a26","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","path":"plugin:superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents\n\n# Dispatching Parallel Agents\n\n## Overview\n\nYou delegate tasks to specialized agents with isolated context. By precisely crafting their instructions and context, you ensure they stay focused and succeed at their task. They should never inherit your session's context or history — you construct exactly what they need. This also preserves your own context for coordination work.\n\nWhen you have multiple unrelated failures (different test files, different subsystems, different bugs), investigating them sequentially wastes time. Each investigation is independent and can happen in parallel.\n\n**Core principle:** Dispatch one agent per independent problem domain. Let them work concurrently.\n\n## When to Use\n\n```dot\ndigraph when_to_use {\n    \"Multiple failures?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Are they independent?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Single agent investigates all\" [shape=box];\n    \"One agent per problem domain\" [shape=box];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Sequential agents\" [shape=box];\n    \"Parallel dispatch\" [shape=box];\n\n    \"Multiple failures?\" -> \"Are they independent?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Single agent investigates all\" [label=\"no - related\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Can they work in parallel?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Parallel dispatch\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Sequential agents\" [label=\"no - shared state\"];\n}\n```\n\n**Use when:**\n- 3+ test files failing with different root causes\n- Multiple subsystems broken independently\n- Each problem can be understood without context from others\n- No shared state between investigations\n\n**Don't use when:**\n- Failures are related (fix one might fix others)\n- Need to understand full system state\n- Agents would interfere with each other\n\n## The Pattern\n\n### 1. Identify Independent Domains\n\nGroup failures by what's broken:\n- File A tests: Tool approval flow\n- File B tests: Batch completion behavior\n- File C tests: Abort functionality\n\nEach domain is independent - fixing tool approval doesn't affect abort tests.\n\n### 2. Create Focused Agent Tasks\n\nEach agent gets:\n- **Specific scope:** One test file or subsystem\n- **Clear goal:** Make these tests pass\n- **Constraints:** Don't change other code\n- **Expected output:** Summary of what you found and fixed\n\n### 3. Dispatch in Parallel\n\nIssue all three subagent dispatches in the same response — they run in parallel:\n\n```text\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts failures\"\n# All three run concurrently.\n```\n\nMultiple dispatch calls in one response = parallel execution. One per response = sequential.\n\n### 4. Review and Integrate\n\nWhen agents return:\n- Read each summary\n- Verify fixes don't conflict\n- Run full test suite\n- Integrate all changes\n\n## Agent Prompt Structure\n\nGood agent prompts are:\n1. **Focused** - One clear problem domain\n2. **Self-contained** - All context needed to understand the problem\n3. **Specific about output** - What should the agent return?\n\n```markdown\nFix the 3 failing tests in src/agents/agent-tool-abort.test.ts:\n\n1. \"should abort tool with partial output capture\" - expects 'interrupted at' in message\n2. \"should handle mixed completed and aborted tools\" - fast tool aborted instead of completed\n3. \"should properly track pendingToolCount\" - expects 3 results but gets 0\n\nThese are timing/race condition issues. Your task:\n\n1. Read the test file and understand what each test verifies\n2. Identify root cause - timing issues or actual bugs?\n3. Fix by:\n   - Replacing arbitrary timeouts with event-based waiting\n   - Fixing bugs in abort implementation if found\n   - Adjusting test expectations if testing changed behavior\n\nDo NOT just increase timeouts - find the real issue.\n\nReturn: Summary of what you found and what you fixed.\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n**❌ Too broad:** \"Fix all the tests\" - agent gets lost\n**✅ Specific:** \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\" - focused scope\n\n**❌ No context:** \"Fix the race condition\" - agent doesn't know where\n**✅ Context:** Paste the error messages and test names\n\n**❌ No constraints:** Agent might refactor everything\n**✅ Constraints:** \"Do NOT change production code\" or \"Fix tests only\"\n\n**❌ Vague output:** \"Fix it\" - you don't know what changed\n**✅ Specific:** \"Return summary of root cause and changes\"\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n**Related failures:** Fixing one might fix others - investigate together first\n**Need full context:** Understanding requires seeing entire system\n**Exploratory debugging:** You don't know what's broken yet\n**Shared state:** Agents would interfere (editing same files, using same resources)\n\n## Real Example from Session\n\n**Scenario:** 6 test failures across 3 files after major refactoring\n\n**Failures:**\n- agent-tool-abort.test.ts: 3 failures (timing issues)\n- batch-completion-behavior.test.ts: 2 failures (tools not executing)\n- tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts: 1 failure (execution count = 0)\n\n**Decision:** Independent domains - abort logic separate from batch completion separate from race conditions\n\n**Dispatch:**\n```\nAgent 1 → Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\nAgent 2 → Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts\nAgent 3 → Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts\n```\n\n**Results:**\n- Agent 1: Replaced timeouts with event-based waiting\n- Agent 2: Fixed event structure bug (threadId in wrong place)\n- Agent 3: Added wait for async tool execution to complete\n\n**Integration:** All fixes independent, no conflicts, full suite green\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter agents return:\n1. **Review each summary** - Understand what changed\n2. **Check for conflicts** - Did agents edit same code?\n3. **Run full suite** - Verify all fixes work together\n4. **Spot check** - Agents can make systematic errors\n"},{"name":"dataviz","path":"bundled:dataviz","content":"Base directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-502/bundled-skills/2.1.309/b686a53081079aed22896e5adfa24953/dataviz\n\n# Data Visualization\n\nA chart is **read by people and executed by you**. This skill turns \"make it look\ngood\" into a procedure with checks, so the result is right by construction rather\nthan by taste.\n\n**The method here is design-system-agnostic.** Nothing in the procedure, the form\nheuristic, the six checks, or the mark specs is specific to one product. A design\nsystem supplies a small set of *parameters* (its ramps, a categorical order, a\ndiverging pair, a status palette, a texture, its surfaces, its filter components);\nthe method consumes them unchanged. A **validated default palette** is the\nreference instance, fully specified in `references/palette.md`. To target your\nbrand, read that file's structure and substitute its values - touch nothing else.\n\n> The single most important habit: **the color part is computable, so compute it.**\n> Never eyeball whether a palette is colorblind-safe - run `scripts/validate_palette.js`.\n\n## The procedure - do these in order\n\nColor comes LAST. Most bad charts pick colors first.\n\n1. **Pick the form.** What is the data's job - magnitude, identity, polarity, a\n   single headline, change-over-time? The job picks the chart type, and sometimes\n   the answer is *not a chart* (a stat tile or hero number). -> `references/choosing-a-form.md`\n2. **Assign color by the job it does.** Categorical (identity), sequential\n   (magnitude), diverging (polarity), or status (state) - each has one rule.\n   Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled. -> `references/color-formula.md`\n3. **VALIDATE the palette - run the script, don't reason about Delta E.**\n   `node scripts/validate_palette.js \"<hex,hex,...>\" --mode light` (relative to\n   this skill's base directory - or load it as `<script type=\"module\">` in the\n   chart's own page, where it reads\n   `data-palette` off `<body>` and logs a `console.table` report). It returns\n   pass/fail on the lightness band, chroma floor, adjacent-pair CVD separation,\n   the normal-vision floor, and contrast. Fix anything that FAILs before continuing. Re-run for\n   `--mode dark` with that mode's surface.\n4. **Apply mark specs & spacers.** Thin marks, 4px rounded data-ends anchored to\n   the baseline, 2px lines, >=8px markers, a 2px surface gap between fills (stacked\n   segments and adjacent bars alike) and a 2px surface ring on overlapping marks,\n   selective direct labels. -> `references/marks-and-anatomy.md`\n5. **Add the hover layer - by default.** An HTML/SVG chart *is* interactive; ship\n   a crosshair+tooltip on line/area and a per-mark hover tooltip on bar/dot/cell.\n   The only form that skips it is a bare stat tile with no plot. Hit targets bigger\n   than the mark; filters in one row above the charts. -> `references/interaction.md`\n6. **Final accessibility pass.** For >= 2 series a legend is always present and <= 4\n   are also direct-labeled (a single series needs no legend box - the title names\n   it), so identity is never color-alone; a table view exists; dark mode is **selected** - its own\n   steps from the same ramps, validated against the dark surface, not an automatic\n   flip; texture is available for the CVD/print/forced-colors case.\n7. **Render it and look at it.** The validator checks color, not layout - open or\n   screenshot the output and eyeball it for label collisions, geometry, and overflow\n   before calling it done.\n\nThen check the result against **`references/anti-patterns.md`** - it is the catalog\nof what goes wrong. If your chart matches an entry, it's wrong.\n\n## Non-negotiables (true in every design system)\n\n- **Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled.** A 9th series is never a\n  generated hue - it folds into \"Other,\" small multiples, or composite encoding.\n- **One axis.** Never a dual-axis chart (two y-scales). Two measures of different\n  scale -> two charts, small multiples, or indexed to a common base. *(This is the\n  #1 chart mistake - see anti-patterns.)*\n- **Color follows the entity, never its rank.** A filter that changes the series\n  count must not repaint the survivors.\n- **Sequential = one hue, light->dark. Diverging = two hues + a neutral gray\n  midpoint.** Never a rainbow; never a hue at the diverging midpoint.\n- **Run the validator before shipping any categorical palette.** CVD Delta E >= 8 is the\n  target (OKLab ×100); 6-8 is a floor that is legal ONLY with secondary encoding. A\n  normal-vision floor below 15 is a hard FAIL - full-color readers can't tell the\n  pair apart; re-step it on the adjacent pairlist (secondary encoding does not excuse\n  this one); under `--pairs all` cut series or facet instead - see check 4. A contrast WARN\n  obligates visible labels or a table view - it is not dismissable.\n- **Thin marks; a legend always present for >= 2 series (none for one), with\n  selective direct labels (never a number on every point); recessive grid/axes.**\n- **Text wears text tokens, never the series color** - values, labels, and legends\n  stay in primary/secondary/muted ink; a colored mark beside them carries identity.\n- **Status colors are reserved** (good/warning/serious/critical) and never reused\n  for \"series 4\"; they ship with an icon + label, never color alone.\n\n## Plugging in a design system\n\nThe method is invariant; only these parameters change per system. The reference\ninstance - every value filled in - is `references/palette.md`.\n\n| Parameter | What the system provides |\n|---|---|\n| **Ramps** | the hue scales (named steps) the palette draws from |\n| **Categorical theme** | the fixed hue order (a named theme); default + alternates |\n| **Sequential hue** | the default single hue for magnitude |\n| **Diverging pair** | two warm/cool poles + a neutral midpoint |\n| **Status palette** | good / warning / serious / critical - steps distinct from categorical |\n| **Texture fill** | one directional hand-drawn fill, used at 45° / 135° |\n| **Surfaces** | light & dark chart-surface colors (the validator needs these) |\n| **Filter controls** | date-range & dimension controls (behavioral spec in `interaction.md`) |\n\nTo onboard a new system: fill those rows, feed its ramps to the validator, and let\nit snap each slot to the nearest passing step. Structure and rules stay as written.\n\n## Reference files\n\n| File | What it answers |\n|------|-----------------|\n| `references/choosing-a-form.md` | Which chart type / is it even a chart? |\n| `references/color-formula.md` | The four jobs, the six checks, snap-to-passing |\n| `references/marks-and-anatomy.md` | Mark specs, spacers, labels, figures, hero number |\n| `references/interaction.md` | Tooltips & hover, filters & time ranges |\n| `references/components.md` | The pieces a chart is made of - build each in plain HTML |\n| `references/anti-patterns.md` | **What goes wrong - check every chart against this** |\n| `references/palette.md` | **The reference palette instance** - every parameter, filled in; swap for your brand's |\n| `scripts/validate_palette.js` | Runnable six-checks validator (run it; don't eyeball) |\n"},{"name":"superpowers:test-driven-development","path":"plugin:superpowers:test-driven-development","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/test-driven-development\n\n# Test-Driven Development (TDD)\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.\n\n**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.\n\n**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**\n\n## When to Use\n\n**Always:**\n- New features\n- Bug fixes\n- Refactoring\n- Behavior changes\n\n**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**\n- Throwaway prototypes\n- Generated code\n- Configuration files\n\nThinking \"skip TDD just this once\"? Stop. That's rationalization.\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST\n```\n\nWrite code before the test? Delete it. Start over.\n\n**No exceptions:**\n- Don't keep it as \"reference\"\n- Don't \"adapt\" it while writing tests\n- Don't look at it\n- Delete means delete\n\nImplement fresh from tests. Period.\n\n## Red-Green-Refactor\n\n```dot\ndigraph tdd_cycle {\n    rankdir=LR;\n    red [label=\"RED\\nWrite failing test\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ffcccc\"];\n    verify_red [label=\"Verify fails\\ncorrectly\", shape=diamond];\n    green [label=\"GREEN\\nMinimal code\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccffcc\"];\n    verify_green [label=\"Verify passes\\nAll green\", shape=diamond];\n    refactor [label=\"REFACTOR\\nClean up\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccccff\"];\n    next [label=\"Next\", shape=ellipse];\n\n    red -> verify_red;\n    verify_red -> green [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_red -> red [label=\"wrong\\nfailure\"];\n    green -> verify_green;\n    verify_green -> refactor [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_green -> green [label=\"no\"];\n    refactor -> verify_green [label=\"stay\\ngreen\"];\n    verify_green -> next;\n    next -> red;\n}\n```\n\n### RED - Write Failing Test\n\nWrite one minimal test showing what should happen.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\ntest('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {\n  let attempts = 0;\n  const operation = () => {\n    attempts++;\n    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');\n    return 'success';\n  };\n\n  const result = await retryOperation(operation);\n\n  expect(result).toBe('success');\n  expect(attempts).toBe(3);\n});\n```\nClear name, tests real behavior, one thing\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\ntest('retry works', async () => {\n  const mock = jest.fn()\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');\n  await retryOperation(mock);\n  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\n});\n```\nVague name, tests mock not code\n</Bad>\n\n**Requirements:**\n- One behavior\n- Clear name\n- Real code (no mocks unless unavoidable)\n\n### Verify RED - Watch It Fail\n\n**MANDATORY. Never skip.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test fails (not errors)\n- Failure message is expected\n- Fails because feature missing (not typos)\n\n**Test passes?** You're testing existing behavior. Fix test.\n\n**Test errors?** Fix error, re-run until it fails correctly.\n\n### GREEN - Minimal Code\n\nWrite simplest code to pass the test.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn();\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (i === 2) throw e;\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('unreachable');\n}\n```\nJust enough to pass\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  options?: {\n    maxRetries?: number;\n    backoff?: 'linear' | 'exponential';\n    onRetry?: (attempt: number) => void;\n  }\n): Promise<T> {\n  // YAGNI\n}\n```\nOver-engineered\n</Bad>\n\nDon't add features, refactor other code, or \"improve\" beyond the test.\n\n### Verify GREEN - Watch It Pass\n\n**MANDATORY.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test passes\n- Other tests still pass\n- Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n\n**Test fails?** Fix code, not test.\n\n**Other tests fail?** Fix now.\n\n**\"Other tests\" means the project's suite, not just your file.** A\ngreen run of the test you wrote is not a green suite. Before you call\nthe change done, run the project's test command (bare `pytest`,\n`npm test`, `cargo test` — whatever the repo uses) even when your task\nnamed only one test file. A scope statement in your task bounds the\ndeliverable, not your verification. Any failure that run shows —\nincluding one you didn't cause — goes in your report by name; a red\ntest you watched scroll past and didn't mention is a report falsified\nby omission.\n\n### REFACTOR - Clean Up\n\nAfter green only:\n- Remove duplication\n- Improve names\n- Extract helpers\n\nKeep tests green. Don't add behavior.\n\n### Repeat\n\nNext failing test for next feature.\n\n## Good Tests\n\n| Quality | Good | Bad |\n|---------|------|-----|\n| **Minimal** | One thing. \"and\" in name? Split it. | `test('validates email and domain and whitespace')` |\n| **Clear** | Name describes behavior | `test('test1')` |\n| **Shows intent** | Demonstrates desired API | Obscures what code should do |\n\nWhen writing or changing any test, read [writing-good-tests.md](writing-good-tests.md) for the rules that keep tests honest:\n- Name the production change that would make the test fail — before writing it\n- Assert on real behavior, never on mock behavior\n- Keep test-only code in test utilities, out of production classes\n- Understand a dependency's side effects before mocking it\n\n## Common Rationalizations\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Too simple to test\" | Simple code breaks. Test takes 30 seconds. |\n| \"I'll test after\" | Tests written after pass immediately — which proves nothing. They may test the wrong thing, test the implementation instead of the behavior, or miss the edge case you forgot. You never watched it fail, so you never proved it can catch the bug. Test-first forces that failure. |\n| \"Tests after achieve same goals (spirit not ritual)\" | Tests-after answer \"what does this do?\"; tests-first answer \"what should this do?\" Tests written after are biased by the code you already wrote — you verify the cases you remembered, not the ones you'd have discovered. Coverage without proof the tests work. |\n| \"Already manually tested\" | Manual testing is ad-hoc: no record of what you covered, no way to re-run it when the code changes, easy to forget cases under pressure. \"Worked when I tried it\" ≠ comprehensive. Automated tests run the same way every time. |\n| \"Deleting X hours is wasteful\" | Sunk cost fallacy — that time is already spent either way. The real choice: rewrite with TDD (high confidence) vs. keep it and bolt tests on after (low confidence, likely bugs). Keeping code you can't trust is the waste. |\n| \"Keep as reference, write tests first\" | You'll adapt it. That's testing after. Delete means delete. |\n| \"Need to explore first\" | Fine. Throw away exploration, start with TDD. |\n| \"Test hard = design unclear\" | Listen to test. Hard to test = hard to use. |\n| \"TDD will slow me down\" | TDD IS the pragmatic path: catches bugs before commit, prevents regressions, lets you refactor without fear. \"Pragmatic\" shortcuts mean debugging in production — slower, not faster. |\n| \"Manual test faster\" | Manual doesn't prove edge cases. You'll re-test every change. |\n| \"Existing code has no tests\" | You're improving it. Add tests for existing code. |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP and Start Over\n\n- Code before test\n- Test after implementation\n- Test passes immediately\n- Can't explain why test failed\n- Tests added \"later\"\n- Rationalizing \"just this once\"\n- \"I already manually tested it\"\n- \"Tests after achieve the same purpose\"\n- \"It's about spirit not ritual\"\n- \"Keep as reference\" or \"adapt existing code\"\n- \"Already spent X hours, deleting is wasteful\"\n- \"TDD is dogmatic, I'm being pragmatic\"\n- \"This is different because...\"\n\n**All of these mean: Delete code. Start over with TDD.**\n\n## Example: Bug Fix\n\n**Bug:** Empty email accepted\n\n**RED**\n```typescript\ntest('rejects empty email', async () => {\n  const result = await submitForm({ email: '' });\n  expect(result.error).toBe('Email required');\n});\n```\n\n**Verify RED**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nFAIL: expected 'Email required', got undefined\n```\n\n**GREEN**\n```typescript\nfunction submitForm(data: FormData) {\n  if (!data.email?.trim()) {\n    return { error: 'Email required' };\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n**Verify GREEN**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nPASS\n```\n\n**REFACTOR**\nExtract validation for multiple fields if needed.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\nBefore marking work complete:\n\n- [ ] Every new function/method has a test\n- [ ] Watched each test fail before implementing\n- [ ] Each test failed for expected reason (feature missing, not typo)\n- [ ] Wrote minimal code to pass each test\n- [ ] All tests pass\n- [ ] Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n- [ ] Tests use real code (mocks only if unavoidable)\n- [ ] Edge cases and errors covered\n\nCan't check all boxes? You skipped TDD. Start over.\n\n## When Stuck\n\n| Problem | Solution |\n|---------|----------|\n| Don't know how to test | Write wished-for API. Write assertion first. Ask your human partner. |\n| Test too complicated | Design too complicated. Simplify interface. |\n| Must mock everything | Code too coupled. Use dependency injection. |\n| Test setup huge | Extract helpers. Still complex? Simplify design. |\n\n## Debugging Integration\n\nBug found? Write failing test reproducing it. Follow TDD cycle. Test proves fix and prevents regression.\n\nNever fix bugs without a test.\n\n## Final Rule\n\n```\nProduction code → test exists and failed first\nOtherwise → not TDD\n```\n\nNo exceptions without your human partner's permission.\n"},{"name":"superpowers:verification-before-completion","path":"plugin:superpowers:verification-before-completion","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/verification-before-completion\n\n# Verification Before Completion\n\n## Overview\n\n**Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n\n**Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n```\n\nIf you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n\n## The Gate Function\n\n```\nBEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n\n1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n   - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n   - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n\nSkip any step = lying, not verifying\n```\n\n## Common Failures\n\n| Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n|-------|----------|----------------|\n| Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n| Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n| Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n| Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n| Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n| Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n| Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP\n\n- Using \"should\", \"probably\", \"seems to\"\n- Expressing satisfaction before verification (\"Great!\", \"Perfect!\", \"Done!\", etc.)\n- About to commit/push/PR without verification\n- Trusting agent success reports\n- Relying on partial verification\n- Thinking \"just this once\"\n- Tired and wanting work over\n- **ANY wording implying success without having run verification**\n\n## Rationalization Prevention\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Should work now\" | RUN the verification |\n| \"I'm confident\" | Confidence ≠ evidence |\n| \"Just this once\" | No exceptions |\n| \"Linter passed\" | Linter ≠ compiler |\n| \"Agent said success\" | Verify independently |\n| \"I'm tired\" | Exhaustion ≠ excuse |\n| \"Partial check is enough\" | Partial proves nothing |\n| \"Different words so rule doesn't apply\" | Spirit over letter |\n\n## Key Patterns\n\n**Tests:**\n```\n✅ [Run test command] [See: 34/34 pass] \"All tests pass\"\n❌ \"Should pass now\" / \"Looks correct\"\n```\n\n**Regression tests (TDD Red-Green):**\n```\n✅ Write → Run (pass) → Revert fix → Run (MUST FAIL) → Restore → Run (pass)\n❌ \"I've written a regression test\" (without red-green verification)\n```\n\n**Build:**\n```\n✅ [Run build] [See: exit 0] \"Build passes\"\n❌ \"Linter passed\" (linter doesn't check compilation)\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n```\n✅ Re-read plan → Create checklist → Verify each → Report gaps or completion\n❌ \"Tests pass, phase complete\"\n```\n\n**Agent delegation:**\n```\n✅ Agent reports success → Check VCS diff → Verify changes → Report actual state\n❌ Trust agent report\n```\n\n## When To Apply\n\n**ALWAYS before:**\n- ANY variation of success/completion claims\n- ANY expression of satisfaction\n- ANY positive statement about work state\n- Committing, PR creation, task completion\n- Moving to next task\n- Delegating to agents\n\n**Rule applies to:**\n- Exact phrases\n- Paraphrases and synonyms\n- Implications of success\n- ANY communication suggesting completion/correctness\n"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3138b873-4029-4497-9f41-32ecf8e17bea","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:42:38.924Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\nPath: plugin:superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/dispatching-parallel-agents\n\n# Dispatching Parallel Agents\n\n## Overview\n\nYou delegate tasks to specialized agents with isolated context. By precisely crafting their instructions and context, you ensure they stay focused and succeed at their task. They should never inherit your session's context or history — you construct exactly what they need. This also preserves your own context for coordination work.\n\nWhen you have multiple unrelated failures (different test files, different subsystems, different bugs), investigating them sequentially wastes time. Each investigation is independent and can happen in parallel.\n\n**Core principle:** Dispatch one agent per independent problem domain. Let them work concurrently.\n\n## When to Use\n\n```dot\ndigraph when_to_use {\n    \"Multiple failures?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Are they independent?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Single agent investigates all\" [shape=box];\n    \"One agent per problem domain\" [shape=box];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Sequential agents\" [shape=box];\n    \"Parallel dispatch\" [shape=box];\n\n    \"Multiple failures?\" -> \"Are they independent?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Single agent investigates all\" [label=\"no - related\"];\n    \"Are they independent?\" -> \"Can they work in parallel?\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Parallel dispatch\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Can they work in parallel?\" -> \"Sequential agents\" [label=\"no - shared state\"];\n}\n```\n\n**Use when:**\n- 3+ test files failing with different root causes\n- Multiple subsystems broken independently\n- Each problem can be understood without context from others\n- No shared state between investigations\n\n**Don't use when:**\n- Failures are related (fix one might fix others)\n- Need to understand full system state\n- Agents would interfere with each other\n\n## The Pattern\n\n### 1. Identify Independent Domains\n\nGroup failures by what's broken:\n- File A tests: Tool approval flow\n- File B tests: Batch completion behavior\n- File C tests: Abort functionality\n\nEach domain is independent - fixing tool approval doesn't affect abort tests.\n\n### 2. Create Focused Agent Tasks\n\nEach agent gets:\n- **Specific scope:** One test file or subsystem\n- **Clear goal:** Make these tests pass\n- **Constraints:** Don't change other code\n- **Expected output:** Summary of what you found and fixed\n\n### 3. Dispatch in Parallel\n\nIssue all three subagent dispatches in the same response — they run in parallel:\n\n```text\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts failures\"\nSubagent (general-purpose): \"Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts failures\"\n# All three run concurrently.\n```\n\nMultiple dispatch calls in one response = parallel execution. One per response = sequential.\n\n### 4. Review and Integrate\n\nWhen agents return:\n- Read each summary\n- Verify fixes don't conflict\n- Run full test suite\n- Integrate all changes\n\n## Agent Prompt Structure\n\nGood agent prompts are:\n1. **Focused** - One clear problem domain\n2. **Self-contained** - All context needed to understand the problem\n3. **Specific about output** - What should the agent return?\n\n```markdown\nFix the 3 failing tests in src/agents/agent-tool-abort.test.ts:\n\n1. \"should abort tool with partial output capture\" - expects 'interrupted at' in message\n2. \"should handle mixed completed and aborted tools\" - fast tool aborted instead of completed\n3. \"should properly track pendingToolCount\" - expects 3 results but gets 0\n\nThese are timing/race condition issues. Your task:\n\n1. Read the test file and understand what each test verifies\n2. Identify root cause - timing issues or actual bugs?\n3. Fix by:\n   - Replacing arbitrary timeouts with event-based waiting\n   - Fixing bugs in abort implementation if found\n   - Adjusting test expectations if testing changed behavior\n\nDo NOT just increase timeouts - find the real issue.\n\nReturn: Summary of what you found and what you fixed.\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n**❌ Too broad:** \"Fix all the tests\" - agent gets lost\n**✅ Specific:** \"Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\" - focused scope\n\n**❌ No context:** \"Fix the race condition\" - agent doesn't know where\n**✅ Context:** Paste the error messages and test names\n\n**❌ No constraints:** Agent might refactor everything\n**✅ Constraints:** \"Do NOT change production code\" or \"Fix tests only\"\n\n**❌ Vague output:** \"Fix it\" - you don't know what changed\n**✅ Specific:** \"Return summary of root cause and changes\"\n\n## When NOT to Use\n\n**Related failures:** Fixing one might fix others - investigate together first\n**Need full context:** Understanding requires seeing entire system\n**Exploratory debugging:** You don't know what's broken yet\n**Shared state:** Agents would interfere (editing same files, using same resources)\n\n## Real Example from Session\n\n**Scenario:** 6 test failures across 3 files after major refactoring\n\n**Failures:**\n- agent-tool-abort.test.ts: 3 failures (timing issues)\n- batch-completion-behavior.test.ts: 2 failures (tools not executing)\n- tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts: 1 failure (execution count = 0)\n\n**Decision:** Independent domains - abort logic separate from batch completion separate from race conditions\n\n**Dispatch:**\n```\nAgent 1 → Fix agent-tool-abort.test.ts\nAgent 2 → Fix batch-completion-behavior.test.ts\nAgent 3 → Fix tool-approval-race-conditions.test.ts\n```\n\n**Results:**\n- Agent 1: Replaced timeouts with event-based waiting\n- Agent 2: Fixed event structure bug (threadId in wrong place)\n- Agent 3: Added wait for async tool execution to complete\n\n**Integration:** All fixes independent, no conflicts, full suite green\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter agents return:\n1. **Review each summary** - Understand what changed\n2. **Check for conflicts** - Did agents edit same code?\n3. **Run full suite** - Verify all fixes work together\n4. **Spot check** - Agents can make systematic errors\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: dataviz\nPath: bundled:dataviz\n\nBase directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-502/bundled-skills/2.1.309/b686a53081079aed22896e5adfa24953/dataviz\n\n# Data Visualization\n\nA chart is **read by people and executed by you**. This skill turns \"make it look\ngood\" into a procedure with checks, so the result is right by construction rather\nthan by taste.\n\n**The method here is design-system-agnostic.** Nothing in the procedure, the form\nheuristic, the six checks, or the mark specs is specific to one product. A design\nsystem supplies a small set of *parameters* (its ramps, a categorical order, a\ndiverging pair, a status palette, a texture, its surfaces, its filter components);\nthe method consumes them unchanged. A **validated default palette** is the\nreference instance, fully specified in `references/palette.md`. To target your\nbrand, read that file's structure and substitute its values - touch nothing else.\n\n> The single most important habit: **the color part is computable, so compute it.**\n> Never eyeball whether a palette is colorblind-safe - run `scripts/validate_palette.js`.\n\n## The procedure - do these in order\n\nColor comes LAST. Most bad charts pick colors first.\n\n1. **Pick the form.** What is the data's job - magnitude, identity, polarity, a\n   single headline, change-over-time? The job picks the chart type, and sometimes\n   the answer is *not a chart* (a stat tile or hero number). -> `references/choosing-a-form.md`\n2. **Assign color by the job it does.** Categorical (identity), sequential\n   (magnitude), diverging (polarity), or status (state) - each has one rule.\n   Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled. -> `references/color-formula.md`\n3. **VALIDATE the palette - run the script, don't reason about Delta E.**\n   `node scripts/validate_palette.js \"<hex,hex,...>\" --mode light` (relative to\n   this skill's base directory - or load it as `<script type=\"module\">` in the\n   chart's own page, where it reads\n   `data-palette` off `<body>` and logs a `console.table` report). It returns\n   pass/fail on the lightness band, chroma floor, adjacent-pair CVD separation,\n   the normal-vision floor, and contrast. Fix anything that FAILs before continuing. Re-run for\n   `--mode dark` with that mode's surface.\n4. **Apply mark specs & spacers.** Thin marks, 4px rounded data-ends anchored to\n   the baseline, 2px lines, >=8px markers, a 2px surface gap between fills (stacked\n   segments and adjacent bars alike) and a 2px surface ring on overlapping marks,\n   selective direct labels. -> `references/marks-and-anatomy.md`\n5. **Add the hover layer - by default.** An HTML/SVG chart *is* interactive; ship\n   a crosshair+tooltip on line/area and a per-mark hover tooltip on bar/dot/cell.\n   The only form that skips it is a bare stat tile with no plot. Hit targets bigger\n   than the mark; filters in one row above the charts. -> `references/interaction.md`\n6. **Final accessibility pass.** For >= 2 series a legend is always present and <= 4\n   are also direct-labeled (a single series needs no legend box - the title names\n   it), so identity is never color-alone; a table view exists; dark mode is **selected** - its own\n   steps from the same ramps, validated against the dark surface, not an automatic\n   flip; texture is available for the CVD/print/forced-colors case.\n7. **Render it and look at it.** The validator checks color, not layout - open or\n   screenshot the output and eyeball it for label collisions, geometry, and overflow\n   before calling it done.\n\nThen check the result against **`references/anti-patterns.md`** - it is the catalog\nof what goes wrong. If your chart matches an entry, it's wrong.\n\n## Non-negotiables (true in every design system)\n\n- **Assign categorical hues in fixed order, never cycled.** A 9th series is never a\n  generated hue - it folds into \"Other,\" small multiples, or composite encoding.\n- **One axis.** Never a dual-axis chart (two y-scales). Two measures of different\n  scale -> two charts, small multiples, or indexed to a common base. *(This is the\n  #1 chart mistake - see anti-patterns.)*\n- **Color follows the entity, never its rank.** A filter that changes the series\n  count must not repaint the survivors.\n- **Sequential = one hue, light->dark. Diverging = two hues + a neutral gray\n  midpoint.** Never a rainbow; never a hue at the diverging midpoint.\n- **Run the validator before shipping any categorical palette.** CVD Delta E >= 8 is the\n  target (OKLab ×100); 6-8 is a floor that is legal ONLY with secondary encoding. A\n  normal-vision floor below 15 is a hard FAIL - full-color readers can't tell the\n  pair apart; re-step it on the adjacent pairlist (secondary encoding does not excuse\n  this one); under `--pairs all` cut series or facet instead - see check 4. A contrast WARN\n  obligates visible labels or a table view - it is not dismissable.\n- **Thin marks; a legend always present for >= 2 series (none for one), with\n  selective direct labels (never a number on every point); recessive grid/axes.**\n- **Text wears text tokens, never the series color** - values, labels, and legends\n  stay in primary/secondary/muted ink; a colored mark beside them carries identity.\n- **Status colors are reserved** (good/warning/serious/critical) and never reused\n  for \"series 4\"; they ship with an icon + label, never color alone.\n\n## Plugging in a design system\n\nThe method is invariant; only these parameters change per system. The reference\ninstance - every value filled in - is `references/palette.md`.\n\n| Parameter | What the system provides |\n|---|---|\n| **Ramps** | the hue scales (named steps) the palette draws from |\n| **Categorical theme** | the fixed hue order (a named theme); default + alternates |\n| **Sequential hue** | the default single hue for magnitude |\n| **Diverging pair** | two warm/cool poles + a neutral midpoint |\n| **Status palette** | good / warning / serious / critical - steps distinct from categorical |\n| **Texture fill** | one directional hand-drawn fill, used at 45° / 135° |\n| **Surfaces** | light & dark chart-surface colors (the validator needs these) |\n| **Filter controls** | date-range & dimension controls (behavioral spec in `interaction.md`) |\n\nTo onboard a new system: fill those rows, feed its ramps to the validator, and let\nit snap each slot to the nearest passing step. Structure and rules stay as written.\n\n## Reference files\n\n| File | What it answers |\n|------|-----------------|\n| `references/choosing-a-form.md` | Which chart type / is it even a chart? |\n| `references/color-formula.md` | The four jobs, the six checks, snap-to-passing |\n| `references/marks-and-anatomy.md` | Mark specs, spacers, labels, figures, hero number |\n| `references/interaction.md` | Tooltips & hover, filters & time ranges |\n| `references/components.md` | The pieces a chart is made of - build each in plain HTML |\n| `references/anti-patterns.md` | **What goes wrong - check every chart against this** |\n| `references/palette.md` | **The reference palette instance** - every parameter, filled in; swap for your brand's |\n| `scripts/validate_palette.js` | Runnable six-checks validator (run it; don't eyeball) |\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: superpowers:test-driven-development\nPath: plugin:superpowers:test-driven-development\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/test-driven-development\n\n# Test-Driven Development (TDD)\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.\n\n**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.\n\n**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**\n\n## When to Use\n\n**Always:**\n- New features\n- Bug fixes\n- Refactoring\n- Behavior changes\n\n**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**\n- Throwaway prototypes\n- Generated code\n- Configuration files\n\nThinking \"skip TDD just this once\"? Stop. That's rationalization.\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST\n```\n\nWrite code before the test? Delete it. Start over.\n\n**No exceptions:**\n- Don't keep it as \"reference\"\n- Don't \"adapt\" it while writing tests\n- Don't look at it\n- Delete means delete\n\nImplement fresh from tests. Period.\n\n## Red-Green-Refactor\n\n```dot\ndigraph tdd_cycle {\n    rankdir=LR;\n    red [label=\"RED\\nWrite failing test\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ffcccc\"];\n    verify_red [label=\"Verify fails\\ncorrectly\", shape=diamond];\n    green [label=\"GREEN\\nMinimal code\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccffcc\"];\n    verify_green [label=\"Verify passes\\nAll green\", shape=diamond];\n    refactor [label=\"REFACTOR\\nClean up\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccccff\"];\n    next [label=\"Next\", shape=ellipse];\n\n    red -> verify_red;\n    verify_red -> green [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_red -> red [label=\"wrong\\nfailure\"];\n    green -> verify_green;\n    verify_green -> refactor [label=\"yes\"];\n    verify_green -> green [label=\"no\"];\n    refactor -> verify_green [label=\"stay\\ngreen\"];\n    verify_green -> next;\n    next -> red;\n}\n```\n\n### RED - Write Failing Test\n\nWrite one minimal test showing what should happen.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\ntest('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {\n  let attempts = 0;\n  const operation = () => {\n    attempts++;\n    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');\n    return 'success';\n  };\n\n  const result = await retryOperation(operation);\n\n  expect(result).toBe('success');\n  expect(attempts).toBe(3);\n});\n```\nClear name, tests real behavior, one thing\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\ntest('retry works', async () => {\n  const mock = jest.fn()\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');\n  await retryOperation(mock);\n  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\n});\n```\nVague name, tests mock not code\n</Bad>\n\n**Requirements:**\n- One behavior\n- Clear name\n- Real code (no mocks unless unavoidable)\n\n### Verify RED - Watch It Fail\n\n**MANDATORY. Never skip.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test fails (not errors)\n- Failure message is expected\n- Fails because feature missing (not typos)\n\n**Test passes?** You're testing existing behavior. Fix test.\n\n**Test errors?** Fix error, re-run until it fails correctly.\n\n### GREEN - Minimal Code\n\nWrite simplest code to pass the test.\n\n<Good>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n    try {\n      return await fn();\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (i === 2) throw e;\n    }\n  }\n  throw new Error('unreachable');\n}\n```\nJust enough to pass\n</Good>\n\n<Bad>\n```typescript\nasync function retryOperation<T>(\n  fn: () => Promise<T>,\n  options?: {\n    maxRetries?: number;\n    backoff?: 'linear' | 'exponential';\n    onRetry?: (attempt: number) => void;\n  }\n): Promise<T> {\n  // YAGNI\n}\n```\nOver-engineered\n</Bad>\n\nDon't add features, refactor other code, or \"improve\" beyond the test.\n\n### Verify GREEN - Watch It Pass\n\n**MANDATORY.**\n\n```bash\nnpm test path/to/test.test.ts\n```\n\nConfirm:\n- Test passes\n- Other tests still pass\n- Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n\n**Test fails?** Fix code, not test.\n\n**Other tests fail?** Fix now.\n\n**\"Other tests\" means the project's suite, not just your file.** A\ngreen run of the test you wrote is not a green suite. Before you call\nthe change done, run the project's test command (bare `pytest`,\n`npm test`, `cargo test` — whatever the repo uses) even when your task\nnamed only one test file. A scope statement in your task bounds the\ndeliverable, not your verification. Any failure that run shows —\nincluding one you didn't cause — goes in your report by name; a red\ntest you watched scroll past and didn't mention is a report falsified\nby omission.\n\n### REFACTOR - Clean Up\n\nAfter green only:\n- Remove duplication\n- Improve names\n- Extract helpers\n\nKeep tests green. Don't add behavior.\n\n### Repeat\n\nNext failing test for next feature.\n\n## Good Tests\n\n| Quality | Good | Bad |\n|---------|------|-----|\n| **Minimal** | One thing. \"and\" in name? Split it. | `test('validates email and domain and whitespace')` |\n| **Clear** | Name describes behavior | `test('test1')` |\n| **Shows intent** | Demonstrates desired API | Obscures what code should do |\n\nWhen writing or changing any test, read [writing-good-tests.md](writing-good-tests.md) for the rules that keep tests honest:\n- Name the production change that would make the test fail — before writing it\n- Assert on real behavior, never on mock behavior\n- Keep test-only code in test utilities, out of production classes\n- Understand a dependency's side effects before mocking it\n\n## Common Rationalizations\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Too simple to test\" | Simple code breaks. Test takes 30 seconds. |\n| \"I'll test after\" | Tests written after pass immediately — which proves nothing. They may test the wrong thing, test the implementation instead of the behavior, or miss the edge case you forgot. You never watched it fail, so you never proved it can catch the bug. Test-first forces that failure. |\n| \"Tests after achieve same goals (spirit not ritual)\" | Tests-after answer \"what does this do?\"; tests-first answer \"what should this do?\" Tests written after are biased by the code you already wrote — you verify the cases you remembered, not the ones you'd have discovered. Coverage without proof the tests work. |\n| \"Already manually tested\" | Manual testing is ad-hoc: no record of what you covered, no way to re-run it when the code changes, easy to forget cases under pressure. \"Worked when I tried it\" ≠ comprehensive. Automated tests run the same way every time. |\n| \"Deleting X hours is wasteful\" | Sunk cost fallacy — that time is already spent either way. The real choice: rewrite with TDD (high confidence) vs. keep it and bolt tests on after (low confidence, likely bugs). Keeping code you can't trust is the waste. |\n| \"Keep as reference, write tests first\" | You'll adapt it. That's testing after. Delete means delete. |\n| \"Need to explore first\" | Fine. Throw away exploration, start with TDD. |\n| \"Test hard = design unclear\" | Listen to test. Hard to test = hard to use. |\n| \"TDD will slow me down\" | TDD IS the pragmatic path: catches bugs before commit, prevents regressions, lets you refactor without fear. \"Pragmatic\" shortcuts mean debugging in production — slower, not faster. |\n| \"Manual test faster\" | Manual doesn't prove edge cases. You'll re-test every change. |\n| \"Existing code has no tests\" | You're improving it. Add tests for existing code. |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP and Start Over\n\n- Code before test\n- Test after implementation\n- Test passes immediately\n- Can't explain why test failed\n- Tests added \"later\"\n- Rationalizing \"just this once\"\n- \"I already manually tested it\"\n- \"Tests after achieve the same purpose\"\n- \"It's about spirit not ritual\"\n- \"Keep as reference\" or \"adapt existing code\"\n- \"Already spent X hours, deleting is wasteful\"\n- \"TDD is dogmatic, I'm being pragmatic\"\n- \"This is different because...\"\n\n**All of these mean: Delete code. Start over with TDD.**\n\n## Example: Bug Fix\n\n**Bug:** Empty email accepted\n\n**RED**\n```typescript\ntest('rejects empty email', async () => {\n  const result = await submitForm({ email: '' });\n  expect(result.error).toBe('Email required');\n});\n```\n\n**Verify RED**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nFAIL: expected 'Email required', got undefined\n```\n\n**GREEN**\n```typescript\nfunction submitForm(data: FormData) {\n  if (!data.email?.trim()) {\n    return { error: 'Email required' };\n  }\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n**Verify GREEN**\n```bash\n$ npm test\nPASS\n```\n\n**REFACTOR**\nExtract validation for multiple fields if needed.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\nBefore marking work complete:\n\n- [ ] Every new function/method has a test\n- [ ] Watched each test fail before implementing\n- [ ] Each test failed for expected reason (feature missing, not typo)\n- [ ] Wrote minimal code to pass each test\n- [ ] All tests pass\n- [ ] Output pristine (no errors, warnings)\n- [ ] Tests use real code (mocks only if unavoidable)\n- [ ] Edge cases and errors covered\n\nCan't check all boxes? You skipped TDD. Start over.\n\n## When Stuck\n\n| Problem | Solution |\n|---------|----------|\n| Don't know how to test | Write wished-for API. Write assertion first. Ask your human partner. |\n| Test too complicated | Design too complicated. Simplify interface. |\n| Must mock everything | Code too coupled. Use dependency injection. |\n| Test setup huge | Extract helpers. Still complex? Simplify design. |\n\n## Debugging Integration\n\nBug found? Write failing test reproducing it. Follow TDD cycle. Test proves fix and prevents regression.\n\nNever fix bugs without a test.\n\n## Final Rule\n\n```\nProduction code → test exists and failed first\nOtherwise → not TDD\n```\n\nNo exceptions without your human partner's permission.\n\n\n---\n\n### Skill: superpowers:verification-before-completion\nPath: plugin:superpowers:verification-before-completion\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/hotragn/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/7.0.0/skills/verification-before-completion\n\n# Verification Before Completion\n\n## Overview\n\n**Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n\n**Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n\n## The Iron Law\n\n```\nNO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n```\n\nIf you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n\n## The Gate Function\n\n```\nBEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n\n1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n   - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n   - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n\nSkip any step = lying, not verifying\n```\n\n## Common Failures\n\n| Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n|-------|----------|----------------|\n| Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n| Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n| Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n| Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n| Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n| Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n| Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n\n## Red Flags - STOP\n\n- Using \"should\", \"probably\", \"seems to\"\n- Expressing satisfaction before verification (\"Great!\", \"Perfect!\", \"Done!\", etc.)\n- About to commit/push/PR without verification\n- Trusting agent success reports\n- Relying on partial verification\n- Thinking \"just this once\"\n- Tired and wanting work over\n- **ANY wording implying success without having run verification**\n\n## Rationalization Prevention\n\n| Excuse | Reality |\n|--------|---------|\n| \"Should work now\" | RUN the verification |\n| \"I'm confident\" | Confidence ≠ evidence |\n| \"Just this once\" | No exceptions |\n| \"Linter passed\" | Linter ≠ compiler |\n| \"Agent said success\" | Verify independently |\n| \"I'm tired\" | Exhaustion ≠ excuse |\n| \"Partial check is enough\" | Partial proves nothing |\n| \"Different words so rule doesn't apply\" | Spirit over letter |\n\n## Key Patterns\n\n**Tests:**\n```\n✅ [Run test command] [See: 34/34 pass] \"All tests pass\"\n❌ \"Should pass now\" / \"Looks correct\"\n```\n\n**Regression tests (TDD Red-Green):**\n```\n✅ Write → Run (pass) → Revert fix → Run (MUST FAIL) → Restore → Run (pass)\n❌ \"I've written a regression test\" (without red-green verification)\n```\n\n**Build:**\n```\n✅ [Run build] [See: exit 0] \"Build passes\"\n❌ \"Linter passed\" (linter doesn't check compilation)\n```\n\n**Requirements:**\n```\n✅ Re-read plan → Create checklist → Verify each → Report gaps or completion\n❌ \"Tests pass, phase complete\"\n```\n\n**Agent delegation:**\n```\n✅ Agent reports success → Check VCS diff → Verify changes → Report actual state\n❌ Trust agent report\n```\n\n## When To Apply\n\n**ALWAYS before:**\n- ANY variation of success/completion claims\n- ANY expression of satisfaction\n- ANY positive statement about work state\n- Committing, PR creation, task completion\n- Moving to next task\n- Delegating to agents\n\n**Rule applies to:**\n- Exact phrases\n- Paraphrases and synonyms\n- Implications of success\n- ANY communication suggesting completion/correctness\n\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
